DELETE FROM unit_detail WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha';
DELETE FROM chapter WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>This book is a series of spoken sessions, delivered aloud to an audience and afterward set down as prose. It takes the opening chapter of the Quran, Surah Al-Fateha, and reads it slowly, almost phrase by phrase, drawing out of each word far more than a first hearing would suggest is there.</p>
<p>The series belongs to a larger conversation about knowing one''s master. It begins where the speaker says understanding must begin: not with theology but with friendship, and from friendship with love, because the name Allah reaches back to a root the sessions approach through the Quranic idea of love. From there the talks move outward — the linguistic meaning of Allah, of Rabb, of REHMA, and the striking image of the womb as the source of mercy. They ask what worship truly is, what it means that Allah is the Lord of all the worlds, and how being differs from attribute. They turn to the Day of Judgment, to the motives that move a person toward piety, and at last to the two petitions the surah teaches us to make: guidance to the straight path, and the steadfastness to remain upon it.</p>
<p>The speaker is the author of these sessions; he gave them, and they carry his voice throughout. What the reader receives is a patient, close reading meant to make a familiar prayer new again.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'introduction to the book';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block">‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ خَالِقِ الْوُجُودِ مِنَ الْعَدَمِ وَجَاعِلِ النُّورِ مِنَ الظُّلَمِ وَمُخْرِجِ الصَّبْرِ مِنَ الْأَلَمِ وَمُلْقِي التَّوْبَةِ لِلنَّدَمِ فَنَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى الْمَصَائِبِ كَمَا نَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى النِّعَمِ وَنُصَلِّي عَلَى رَسُولِهِ الْأَكْرَمِ لِلشَّرَفِ الْأَشَمِّ وَالنُّورِ الْأَتَمِّ وَالْكِتَابِ الْمُحْتَمِ وَكَمَالِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَالْخَاتَمِ سَيِّدِ وَلَدِ آدَمَ الَّذِي بَشَّرَ بِهِ عِيسَى ابْنُ مَرْيَمَ وَدَعَا لَهُ بِبِعْثَتِهِ إِبْرَاهِيمُ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ حِينَ كَانَ يَرْفَعُ الْقَوَاعِدَ بَيْتِ اللَّهِ الْمُحَرَّمِ فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ وَعَلَى أَصْحَابِهِ خَيْرِ الْأُمَمِ الَّذِينَ بَارَكَ اللَّهُ بِهِمْ كَافَّةَ النَّاسِ الْعَرَبِ مِنْهُمْ وَالْعَجَمِ</span></p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَصَلَّى اللهُ عَلَى أَمِيرِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ الْأَنْزَعِ الْبَطِينِ، وَقَائِدِ الْغُرِّ الْمُحَجَّلِينَ، غَالِبِ كُلِّ غَالِبٍ، مَطْلُوبِ كُلِّ طَالِبٍ، مَوْلَانَا عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَقُلِ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًۭا وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ شَرِيكٌۭ فِى ٱلْمُلْكِ وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ وَلِىٌّۭ مِّنَ ٱلذُّلِّ ۖ وَكَبِّرْهُ تَكْبِيرًۢا</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ، الَّذِي نَحْمَدُهُ، وَنَسْتَعِينُهُ، وَنَسْتَنْصِرُهُ، وَنُؤْمِنُ بِهِ، وَنَتَوَكَّلُ عَلَيْهِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنْ شُرُورِ أَنْفُسِنَا وَمِنْ سَيِّئَاتِ أَعْمَالِنَا، مَنْ يَهْدِهِ اللَّهُ فَلَا مُضِلَّ لَهُ وَمَنْ يُضْلِلْهُ فَلَا هَادِيَ لَهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولُهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْسَلَهُ اللَّهُ تَعَالَىٰ بِالْهُدَىٰ وَدِينِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ وَكَفَىٰ بِاللَّهِ شَهِيدًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيَّ بْنَ أَبِي طَالِبٍ وَصِيُّهُ وَوَزِيرُهُ، فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَعَلَىٰ آلِهِمْ وَسَلَّمَ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرًا</span></p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَا إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا، وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ، وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَعُ، وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ، وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ</span> Fa ammata adiyakunu <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> thikita bihil <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرِيم</span> baadanakhula <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span></p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ بِلَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ، وَاللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ، آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span></p>
<p>Once again, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>. Continuing our flow of conversation, we''ve discussed in detail the self, we''ve discussed the theme of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, right, those two categories on the website. Today, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, we''ll start a new group of topics that are categorized as, you know, know thy master, because I had know thy self, know thy <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, and now we''re going to look at know thy master.</p>
<p>So in these topics, we will take a closer look at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and His various attributes, a better understanding of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is. And then we''ll also look at His relationship and His expectations from us, according to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. And we''ll cover some very interesting topics, like His proof of existence, the concept of His mercy, the concept of Him being... the whole concept behind creating this universe, and then fate and destiny and things like that. So I find, you know, most people find these topics very interesting.</p>
<p>Now, the discussion of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, it usually begins with the understanding of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَه</span>, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٍ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. So it begins with the understanding of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَه</span>. In fact, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> itself comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَه</span>. But in order to properly understand the depth of this word, it''s interesting that we first need to understand the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآنِيٌّ</span> concept of love, right, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا</span>.</p>
<p>So, you know, and the Islamic concept of love is very different from the Bollywood and Hollywood concept of love. So, in order to understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>, we must first have a clear understanding of love itself, like the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآنِيٌّ</span> love. And then in order to understand love, we need to first understand friendship. Because love always begins with friendship. You cannot fall in love with someone unless and until you develop a friendship with that person first.</p>
<p>Real friendships develop into true love. And it''s interesting because if you think about it, we don''t need friendship for lust. This is why today in our society, we see these things like one night stands and casual, meaningless sex. It''s all around us in this society because it''s all based on lust. And it''s very confusing sometimes, especially when I talk to kids. I mean, it becomes very confusing between these terms.</p>
<p>I mean, what is the distinction between friendship, love, lust, all of these things? But lust, like we''ve talked about, it stems from this base animal instinct, which is full of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَوَانِيَة</span> that we studied in the earlier sessions. And I talked about this at quite length. And this is something that is related to animals, right? Because animals don''t need to first become friends in order to mate, right?</p>
<p>So I think that gives a good understanding of the difference. But love always begins with the sentiments of friendship. I mean, the Arabs have this beautiful saying that whenever the plant of love will grow, it will always grow in the soil of friendship. I really like that saying and I think it''s very true. So before we look at the term <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَه</span>, we must first look at the various shades of love, and before we can do that, we must first understand the different types of friendship <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> talks about.</p>
<p>In today''s day and age, it has become extremely difficult to create genuine and real friendships, especially for you kids. Today the world has moved towards virtual friendships, like Facebook, Twitter, Instagram, all this kind of stuff. And today friendships not only require, but they''re sustained by phones or some other electronic devices. I mean, we have become so enslaved to our phones and our devices that for us to have like a 15-minute conversation with another person without checking our phones or texting, it''s become impossible.</p>
<p>So the more connected we have become to our devices, the less connected we have become with each other, right? We are no longer able to have that genuine, real connections and conversations with one another. I mean, if you talk to your parents or your grandparents, I mean, they''ll tell you what those conversations used to be like. Right, so you know, instead of us owning these devices, they have started to own us. You know, our devices are now our masters and we are its obedient slaves, because you can actually see this in light, the second the phone goes ding, we have to drop anything and everything we''re doing in order to turn to our device, right? Oh, who texted me, right? So we turn to our devices in that abject obedience, right?</p>
<p>I hear and I obey. So it''s almost like, you know how these people who have asthma, they have to take an inhaler every few minutes because they have a problem in breathing? I literally see that has become pretty much the same things with phones today and Wi-Fi. I need Wi-Fi. I need Wi-Fi. Is there Wi-Fi in here? How can there not be Wi-Fi? So today we gasp for Wi-Fi just as asthma patients gasp for breath.</p>
<p>In fact, the other day I was telling my daughter about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَعْبَة</span> and I was talking like maybe we can plan to go to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُمْرَة</span> or something like that. So I asked her, what would you like to learn about Mecca and the whole <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُمْرَة</span> process and all that? You know what''s the first thing she asked me? All the things she would have asked me? Do they have Wi-Fi?</p>
<p>As it is, you know, this is what our lives have been reduced to. So anyway, so today I want to throw some light on this ironic concept of friendship. Okay, now we can go into this conversation with two different kinds of mindsets. We can approach this topic in two ways, basically. The first is that what kind of friends we should look for, like, you know, trying to find these qualities and characteristics in our friends or other people. That''s the first kind of mindset that we can have when we have this conversation. But that''s a selfish way of looking at this, according to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. Okay, the correct and the proper way of looking at this conversation is, what kind of friend should you and I be to others?</p>
<p>You see? Not what kind of friends we should have, but rather what kind of friends we should be. That''s the lesson we want to learn from this conversation. Because when you become the right kind of friend to others, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> Himself puts the right kind of people in your path. I mean, that''s just, we call it with different names, karma or whatever you want to call it, right?</p>
<p>That''s just how it works. So there are about 10 different words in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> for these different kinds and shades of friends. And each term presents its own nuances in terms of how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> brings it up in His different verses. So we''ll go through some of these terms and try to understand, you know, in the light of how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is using them, and basically what we learn from these different nuances about these various kinds of friendships.</p>
<p>So, the first of these words is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيّ</span>. Right? The first word of friendship that we''ll look at is the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيّ</span>. This word in the Arabic language, it comes from which is a kind of protection. So a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِي</span> is someone who takes care of your matters, it is someone who always has your back, basically. So a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِي</span> is a friend that in some sense, when trouble hits you, you know, no matter what kind of trouble it is, you know, he''s always there to guard you and protect you. It basically handles your situation for you.</p>
<p>So a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيّ</span> is used for a friend, but then not just any kind of friend. You see, it is used for a strong friend that you can always count on to not only get you out of any mess that you are in, but also protect you from getting into trouble in the first place. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> calls Himself <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيّ</span>, right? So there is this, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِي</span>, the protective friend of the believer.</p>
<p>And what does this protective friend do? He takes them out of darkness and brings them into light. And those who disbelieve, their protective friends are who? It''s the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيَاطِين</span>, it''s the devil. He takes them out from light and puts them into darkness. So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> Himself uses the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِي</span> to describe Himself, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِي</span> of those who believe. He is a protective friend to those who believe.</p>
<p>Now, another variation of the same root word is the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَى</span>. This is used for that same caring and concerned friend who has the ability to protect you. But now this person takes an authoritative role in your life. In other words, when we are 100% convinced that someone truly cares about us, that someone truly has our best interest at heart, we submit to the authority of this person.</p>
<p>Like children submit to their parents, students submit to their teachers. So in that sense, that authoritative figure who is in that position of protecting becomes the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَى</span>. Because if we listen to him, he will protect us from getting into trouble. Or he will help us get out of trouble when we''re in it. So when that person in that position of authority over our affairs, when that person takes that position, he is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَى</span>.</p>
<p>You understand? This is why we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِي</span>. That''s why we add the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَى</span> before his name. And we use that word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَى</span> not only to attest to his authority over our affairs, and this is a self-given authority, that we have given him authority. It''s not like a dictatorship, you understand? Because remember, the context is friendship. So we attest to his authority over our affairs, but also to substantiate the fact that we believe him to be our caring and our concerned friend who is there to protect us, whose teachings are there to protect us and keep us out of trouble.</p>
<p>So, we look at him as someone who always has our back, basically. You know, he''s someone whose teachings always have our best interests at heart. You understand? So by adding that word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَى</span> before his name, we testify for his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِلَايَة</span>, his genuine and sincere love, care and concern for us in order to protect us. Now, there''s an interesting adjective that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> uses for this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِي</span> in a verse, and that word is, that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> uses, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آمِينَ</span>.</p>
<p>Right? So let me find this for you. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِي</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آمِينَ</span>. So imagine you''re talking to this guy, right, this believer, and I think every one of us has sort of faced the situation. And so you''re talking to this guy really nicely and you''re trying to explain religion to him and, you know, Islam is such a good religion and all of that, and he''s responding back to you with the most aggressive language. Right? You''re being kind and respectful to this guy and he''s being a complete *** in response. Right? So Ashraf says, act with him as a protective friend, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيٌّ حَمِيمٌ</span>. Continue behaving with him as if you''re looking out for him.</p>
<p>In other words, respond in the most gentle way possible, like a protective friend. But not just that, then he adds the adjective <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آمِينَ</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آمِينَ</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمَا</span>, which means fever. It is a word that is also used to describe intense heat in the Arabic language. So in this verse, this word is used to describe the intimacy of friendship. You know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آمِين</span> is when you are that close with someone that there is heat in your friendship.</p>
<p>There is a closeness, right? An intimacy in that friendship. I''m not talking about heat in the romantic sense. I''m talking about it in the sense that you feel strongly towards someone to the extent that it heats you up in the, you know, makes you all warm inside. So a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حم</span> is a friend that when you are in their company you feel all warm and fuzzy inside. And the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is saying that you have to be like that towards who?</p>
<p>Towards someone who is being aggressive towards you. Someone who''s acting like a with you. So then imagine how are you to be with a believer. If that is how we are to deal with somebody who''s acting in that way with us, then imagine how we are to act with a believer. And that just sets the context, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So just look at the beautiful standards that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> sets for us as believers.</p>
<p>I mean, it''s really incredible. And then they say Islam is a violent religion, right? So this is the first kind of friend we''re supposed to be with each other, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيّ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَامِي</span>, a protective friend.</p>
<p>The second kind of friend is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَادِق</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَادِق</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدْق</span>, which means two things. It means sincerity and it means truthfulness, right? That''s the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدْق</span>. So a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَادِق</span> is actually an extremely sincere and truthful friend. So when you are a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدِفْ</span> to someone, what that means is that you are friends with that person for no reason other than a genuine intention of just wanting to be with that person.</p>
<p>You understand? You''re not friends with them because being friends with them will make you feel more popular at school or work, right? Or being friends with them will make other people think more highly of you. Or because they are really rich and you want to get some advantage out of that. Or they''re very knowledgeable, so hanging out with them is going to make you look like a scholar or something.</p>
<p>In other words, when you are a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَادِق</span>, you have no hidden agenda. Your friendship has no strings attached. It''s the most genuine and sincere kind of friendship. You are friends with them just for who they are, not because of the things they own or their social status or nothing artificial or superficial like that. But if you have a genuine friendship without all of these things, then that is truly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَتْكَال</span>.</p>
<p>You''re going to be there for that person no matter what. So you can just see, right, that being a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span> is not easy. Having a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span> is even more difficult in this day and age. I mean, it''s easy to have people that you can casually chat with or gossip with. I mean, that''s easy. But having a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُدَيْس</span> is a really, it''s very difficult, it''s very rare, because these are the kinds of friends that never give up on you no matter how many times you fail.</p>
<p>You know, people will, your friends will work with you a few times, one time, twice, three times, and at some point they''ll be like, you know what? But a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُدَيْس</span> will never give up. No matter how many times you fail, they will always be there. So that''s the first nuance, that''s sincerity, right? The second is truthfulness. So the Arab had this saying, you know, your friend is someone who speaks the truth to you, who is honest with you.</p>
<p>He''s not just someone who''s friendly with you. You know the difference between a friend and being friendly? A lot of people will be friendly, but they''re not your friends. All those people on your Facebook are not your friends, even if they are friendly with you. But these people, they''re completely honest with you. When you are falling off, they will say, listen, I care about you. I don''t want you to do this because I believe this is going to hurt you.</p>
<p>So you need to stop this. They''re just direct with you, honest with you. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span> is not there just to look good and give you that warm and fuzzy feeling that you crave. He tells you the harsh truth, the truth that you don''t want to hear. That''s why it comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدْق</span>, which means truthfulness. So now if you are a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span> to someone, then whatever it takes, no matter the consequences, you are going to be genuinely honest with that friend. As a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span>, you will not simply agree with your friend, knowing very well that the path that they have chosen is going to lead them to their destruction.</p>
<p>And you''re not afraid that if you say something to them or they say something to you, the friendship will come to an end. Well, I don''t want to say anything because, you know, he might get really upset. That''s not a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span>. Because, I mean, that''s a genuine fear. That''s why we usually lose our friends, right? I mean, we are honest to our friends and they don''t want to hear it. So that''s how the friendships end.</p>
<p>But that''s not what a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span> does. And that''s what we''re learning, that we should be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span> in life. We''ll never give up on our friends, no matter how painful it gets. And we never, and these are not mutually exclusive, right? So it''s not like, oh, I''m going to be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span> and I''m not going to be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِي</span>. The point is that these are all shades. These are like a rainbow. It''s like a rainbow with all these different colors. It''s one rainbow, but it has all these different shades. So we have to be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِي</span> and we have to be protective. We have to be caring, concerned, as well as truthful and sincere. So don''t look at these as, oh, I like this one, I''m going to stick with this one. No, that''s not the idea. Think of it like a rainbow.</p>
<p>It all has to come together to be the perfect friend. So you have to be all these nine, ten things in order to be qualified, from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآنِيٌّ</span> perspective, as the perfect friend. Now, interestingly, if we look at this word in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآنِيٌّ</span> context, it''s interesting how this word is used. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says that on the Day of Judgment there are some people who will find themselves on the wrong side of the fence. These are people who are heading towards hellfire. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, when he talks about them in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, he says, let me see. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says about them in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> that … So these people that are about to enter hellfire, they''re saying, it''s not our fault that we''re going to hell. It''s not our fault that we are in trouble on this Day of Judgment. We''re here because of these other criminals who misled us.</p>
<p>So it''s their fault. You know, these evil people misled us. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> describes, you know, what they say. They say, we have nobody on our side today to make a case for us. So is someone who likes you, and when you get into trouble they stand in the way and say, no, no, no, no, he''s good, he''s okay, he''s with me, right? Like imagine you''re in trouble with the principal and your teacher steps in and says, no, no, no, she''s a good girl, she''s a good student, don''t worry about it, you know, I''ll take care of her. You know, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِفَاءٌ</span>. So these people are saying, today nobody''s coming to back us up. Nobody had our back today. And then they say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا صَدِيقٍ حَمِيمٍۢ</span>. Now this is the only time the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدِيق</span> is used like this directly in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>.</p>
<p>They say, today we have no true, sincere and honest and truthful friends. We have no truthful, sincere friends that are close to us today. We have no loving friends today. And by saying this, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is reminding us that the converse is also true. You see, if people on the wrong side are complaining that they have no close friends on this day, that means the people on the right side of Judgment Day, you know, the ones going to heaven, they will actually have close friends to back them up. And it is because of their close friends that that''s where they''re heading, right?</p>
<p>And this is why, who is the ultimate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدِيق</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمِيم</span> that, you know, that us, that believers have access to? We have the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِفَاءً</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> qualify us all for that. But what are these disbelievers or people of hellfire complaining about? That on this day, they don''t have a what? A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدِيق</span>. Because in the world, when someone would give them a sincere and honest advice, they would turn them down. You see? They would mock them. They would make fun of them, ridicule them. They would break their friendships with them.</p>
<p>But today, these same people are wishing that they had those <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدِيقٍ حَمِيمٍ</span>. You see? And the scope of this is quite broad. I mean, look at... We reject the hierarchy of the elders because of what they''re saying, based on what we think is happening or the... the true or false propaganda that we receive and things like that. But it doesn''t change the fact that there''s still <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَادِق</span> and I mean what they''re saying is for our benefit. What they''re saying is the true honest truth. You know we...</p>
<p>We break our allegiance with them or we break our ties with them based on certain things or actions that we see happening or things like that. But that doesn''t change the fact about what they''re saying. They''re still being <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَادِق</span> to us. They''re giving us the sincere, honest truth about what is going to protect us. And on the Day of Judgment, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَة</span> says that there will be people who will search for them and ask for them and complain about not having them. But something really to think about.</p>
<p>And the point and the lesson is that we don''t be among these people. So if you have a true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span> in your life, if somebody fits this profile in your life, hold on to them and stick with them no matter how harsh and painful their truth feels. Because usually they''re going to tell you something you don''t want to do or they''re going to stop you from something that you do want to do. So that''s how you can identify a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span>.</p>
<p>Because in the long run, they will turn out to be your best friends in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخَا</span>. So that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span>, right? The third term that I want to look at is this term <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span>. This is a very comprehensive word in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> that is used for friend. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span> could be used in the more casual sense. You know, for people that you are sitting next to in a train or a bus during a journey. So if you''re on a train traveling to a destination and there''s this guy sitting next to you for the next four hours, technically he can be called your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span>. Right? So this word can be used for some very casual acquaintance. But then, interestingly, if you spend a lifetime with someone, that person is also your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span> because it means companion.</p>
<p>But whether you look at it short term or long term, there is one thing that is generally common for someone to qualify as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span>. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span> is someone who always means well for you. They will always want something good for you. So if there is a problem, they will always try to help you in whatever capacity they can. And the more they know you, the more they possess the ability to help you, the more genuinely concerned they are going to be for you. You see? This is why one of the names of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> uses this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span> for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا صَاحِبُكُم بِمَجْنُونٍۢ</span>. Your companion is not insane. And check this out, right? People of Mecca are calling him insane.</p>
<p>And what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> saying? This companion of yours, this companion, this man who is actually genuinely concerned about you, who''s actually concerned about your well-being, who cares about you, he''s not insane. He didn''t say my messenger is not insane, my prophet is not insane. Right? He didn''t say the one I sent is not insane, the one I love the most is not insane. I mean, this is the greatest man who ever walked the face of this earth, right? And they''re calling him insane. But look how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is explaining his position to them. He doesn''t say that this amazing man is not insane. No, he says your companion, the one who genuinely cares for you, is not insane.</p>
<p>So that''s the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَى</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. I mean, we don''t really pick these things up when we''re reciting it or going through translations, but the ancient Arabs did. And it is statements like these, like, look at the size of the statement, right? But this one statement converted so many people because they could see the depth of what is being said here. And they could realize that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمَا</span>n beings cannot talk like this.</p>
<p>So the takeaway for us is that we need to learn to interact as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَصْحَابُ</span>. See, this is, and not just <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> was called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span>, even what were the companions called? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَصْحَاب</span>, right? Same, same word. Because they also genuinely cared about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, they also genuinely did everything they could to help him. So what we need to learn is to interact with each other as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَصْحَابُ</span>, as companions, right? This is again something that is gradually disappearing at a societal level. You know, the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُحْبَة</span>, it works at an individual level, but it also works at a societal level, because remember, it''s long term and short term, right?</p>
<p>The companions during the time of the Prophet, they celebrated normality. You know, they did not create fake standards of living. Today we don''t care about this kind of companionship. You know, if I buy an expensive phone, you know, I will make sure I flash it in your face every five seconds. Oh, you don''t have, you still have an iPhone 6? Poor guy. It''s okay, don''t worry, I''ll still hang out with you. You know, but only when we''re alone, because I don''t want to be seen with you when you''re, you know, iPhone 5 or whatever. So that goes against the whole concept of being aside. It''s not that I shouldn''t, I cannot own a phone.</p>
<p>But it''s just about not flashing it in the face of people who I know will start feeling bad about it. Because as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span>, I''m supposed to always be concerned about my companion''s feelings. And I''m not going to go out of my way to show off just to make him feel inferior. So, you know what I mean? So it''s not that you cannot have good stuff. That''s not what it''s being... The idea is not that.</p>
<p>You know, &quot;Oh, I bought this cheap bag, you know, but this whole thing, it''s just, it''s just Gucci, you know, I just got it on a sale for like 800,000.&quot; You know, so that shows you''re not concerned about your companion''s feelings. Especially if you know that, you know, he or she could not afford it. I said &quot;he,&quot; but I hope no, he is not buying Guccis, but who knows. Anyway, so my point is that it''s a very delicate term in that respect.</p>
<p>Okay, so that was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span>. This is pretty interesting, right? The way of all these different shades of friendship. So we have to be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيّ</span>, and we have to be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَادِق</span>, and we have to be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span>.</p>
<p>Now the next one is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيجَة</span>. This term occurs once in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيجَة</span>, which comes from the verb in Arabic and it means to penetrate deeply. Right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> knows what goes into the earth and what comes out of it. So a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيجَة</span> is a friend who is all up, basically he''s all up in your business. They know all about what is going on in your life, and they are deeply involved in your affairs. And you trust this person so much that you can share anything and everything with them. And you don''t just share it at an information level, they''re actually involved in your life on a day-to-day basis. Now, this is a very special kind of friendship. It''s not your typical friendship. It''s not someone you are nice to, or you sometimes hang out with, or you text back and forth. This is a very, very close kind of friendship.</p>
<p>And it''s very rare to find such a friend in this day and age. I mean, you know, this is someone that you can trust with your bank account. This is someone you can trust with your children. This is someone you can trust with all your gold and jewelry without a second thought. You know, you can trust them in any affair of your life. So these are people that you give signed blank checks to, because you''re traveling for a month and you know, &quot;I''m traveling for a month, could you please write out these checks for my bills,&quot; or whatever. And you don''t have to think twice about it. You''re not worried at all. And that''s why this kind of friendship is very reckoning.</p>
<p>Now, it''s interesting how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> uses this word in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, and in fact He makes it a condition that you can only have this level of friendship with three specific entities. Right? So what does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيجَة</span> mean? You know, a deep, penetrating friendship. Right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, do you think that you shall be left alone while <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has not yet tested them among you, you know, who have made an effort and fought and things like that, and who have not taken <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيجَة</span> besides <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, His messenger, and the believers. Now what does that mean? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is calling Himself a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيجَة</span> to us. Why is that? You and I will go through trials in our lives, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will test to see how deeply <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s love and His commands have penetrated your most important personal affairs. What is His role in your family decisions? What is His role in your personal decisions, in the kind of financial decisions you make, the kind of business decisions you make, you know, the kind of career and education decisions you make? What is His role in your life? You know, how deeply has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s love penetrated your life? That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيجَة</span>. You know, is He <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيجَة</span> to you? Do you have that kind of friendship with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> or not? Right? So that''s the tone of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَة</span>.</p>
<p>Then He added the messenger, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, like, he, how much do you consult the legacy of your Prophet in the most personal matters of your life? When you and I are having trouble in life, how much do we consult the life and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِيرَة</span> of the Prophet and his example? Now when you and I are facing doubts and trouble in Islam, how much do we consult the advice of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>? How deeply involved is he in our most intimate matters? Right?</p>
<p>See, today we talk about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span> of the messenger. You know, we celebrate and we emphasize the most outwardly aspects of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span>. There''s the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span> of prayer, there''s the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span> of keeping a beard, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span> of, you know, being the first to say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ</span>, you know, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span> of these mannerisms and these etiquettes that we have learned from our beloved messengers. And it''s there, we do that. But in most aspects, these are still public and outward facing. But the most intimate matters of our lives, when it comes to the most private internal matters, like should I enter this financial transaction? You know, should I buy these types of clothes? Should I go out with these kinds of friends to this kind of place? Should I be involved in the kinds of things they''re doing? So that''s when this question becomes important. Like, have I involved the messenger in those decisions?</p>
<p>That have to do with my most private affairs. I mean, where is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span> in those matters, right? Even in the way we talk with each other, I mean, in our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحْلَاق</span>, in the way we carry ourselves, do we follow the Prophet? I mean, just look at the way we talk to each other today. I mean, it''s so messed up, right? There''s no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span> in it, and that''s an outward form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span>. Today there''s no respect for the elders, there''s no humility in mutual conversations with each other, or when interacting with each other, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is telling us in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> that we are supposed to talk respectfully and kindly and as a protective friend to our enemies, you know. But we do not even behave that way with each other. I mean, the second we disagree about something, look what happens, right? We start beating them with evidence, we curse them out, we mock them.</p>
<p>Even in something as simple as a conversation, there is a major deviation from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِيرَة</span> has not penetrated our lives at all. It hasn''t penetrated our conversations, right? So that''s the second one. And then he says, you know, and then finally he includes the believers. In other words, if you find <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> intimately connected in your life, and you find the teachings of the Prophet intimately connected in your life, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will give you the best kinds of believers in your life. He will give you the gift of those kinds of special friends that you can trust to the depth of your personal lives. And what a gift it is to have, because trust is something that has completely disappeared in our times, right?</p>
<p>We cannot trust anybody today. We cannot trust the people we work with. We cannot trust our closest friends. That kind of trust is gone. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيجَا</span> kind of trust is gone. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is giving us the secret to finding those kind of friends. If you let <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in your lives, if you let the messenger in your life, then the next step will be that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will give you the gift of true believers. And we will be able to trust like that. That is another freedom that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> gives us.</p>
<p>There''s one thing for a person to be trustworthy, and then for us to have that freedom to be able to trust like that. So that''s, it''s an amazing gift, right? Another kind of friendship is, this type of friendship is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِهِتَنَا</span>, which comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُهْتَانًا</span>. Now what does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُهْتَانًا</span> mean? It means stomach. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِهِتَنَا</span> is actually a friend who keeps your secrets. So the other one was a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيجَة</span>, who was involved in all your personal matters.</p>
<p>This one keeps and protects your secrets. And remember, this is all inclusive, right? So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيّ</span> should be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span> should be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِدِّيهْ</span>, and a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيجَة</span> and a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>. So it''s all coming together. So when you tell this person about something personal that happened today in your life, you can trust them to take the secret to their graves. Which is not, again, something that is rare today, right?</p>
<p>What happens when you tell a friend a secret today? You tell them not to say it to anyone, and what happens? They put you on speaker and they start texting their friends, &quot;Hey, listen up. Don''t tell anyone, but you know what? And don''t tell them because it''s a secret. Shh.&quot; And then they do that thing to the next one and the next one and the next one.</p>
<p>So that''s not a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِطَانَة</span>. We all have friends who have a very light stomach, and they just cannot keep a secret. And the lesson here is that we should not be that way.</p>
<p>And now the context of this verse is interesting, because it''s a very specific context. You know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, so, you know, this verse is actually in the context of the Battle of Uhud, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ آلِ عِمْرَانَ</span>. For those of you who have studied the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِيرَة</span>, you know that the Battle of Uhud is a very difficult military situation for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِمُونَ</span>, right? You know, there are all these different communities that would live in, that lived in Medina, Christians, Jews, all of them, right? And some of them, you know, they knew are cooperating with the disbelievers, because it''s all family members, right?</p>
<p>You know, one person in the family converted and became Muslim. The remaining are Jews and all, Christians, or things like that. So in that context, in this context of this Battle of Uhud, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> saying, don''t tell your secrets to your Jewish and Christian friends, because you don''t know who''s going to spy on you in the state of war. Be careful, because a lot of them are not going to leave any stone unturned, and they will want to harm you, because it''s battle.</p>
<p>They want to win. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not talking about everyone, right? He''s talking about those who have this propensity, who have this potential to harm you like this. So just be on the careful side in that context. So again, the context of this verse is a state of war, right? A tense situation where leaking intelligence could cost lives. You know, so one might casually talk and say that, yeah, you know, I met the Prophet today, and he''s going to be at this place under that tree at this time.</p>
<p>Now for you it might be a not big deal, but for someone who''s trying to assassinate the messenger, this information is gold. Or maybe that person then shares that secret with somebody else, and then that person tries to harm it. So it can perpetuate like that. So what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is saying is that keep your close secrets to the closest among you. That''s the context of this verse.</p>
<p>But what we learn from this verse is the description of what this friendship is supposed to mean. Like, so there''s another flavor or another shade of this friendship that we learn, which is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِطَانَة</span>, a friend who knows your deepest and darkest secret, right? So that''s the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِطَانَة</span>.</p>
<p>Next one is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>, right? And it''s the most beautiful word that is used for a friend in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>. And this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>, it comes from, which is again a very comprehensive word, because one of, you know, you hear this word again when we talk about love, because is also a form of love. But in this context, what I want you to understand is that, and what will help you understand, is th' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'quranic friendship';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'at this another word that comes from the same root, which is, you know, and some of you know what this means, right? Is flossing. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَالَ</span> is when something goes into or in between something else.</p>
<p>Like when you floss, you put that thread in between your teeth. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَالَ</span> in Arabic. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَالَ</span> is someone whose friendship penetrates deeply in your heart. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيجَة</span> was somebody who penetrates deeply into your affairs in terms of trust. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span> is someone whose friendship penetrates deeply within your heart. This is very, you know, this shade is very mixed with love. You know, you love someone that deeply, that''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>. You have this deep-seated love for this person, that you constantly think about this person. They put it in those terms, you know, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span> is the one that you constantly think about, that you constantly miss them when you''re not with them. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>.</p>
<p>That''s how you know that this person is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>. You''re always thinking about them, and you wish that you were with them right at this moment. That kind of a friend is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>. This is again a very rare kind of friendship. And this is the kind of friendship that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had with Ibrahim, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> took Ibrahim is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>. So it''s not just, you know, when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, uses the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span> for, it''s not just like we think about friendship. It''s a very different kind of friendship.</p>
<p>So, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> uses the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span> in very interesting ways in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. You know, sometimes in very scary ways, and this is something that we have to really take seriously. So let''s look at this verse, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, you know, believers, spend from that which we have provided for you, you know, help others, or in the way of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, before a day comes when there will be no bargaining. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمٌۭ لَّا بَيْعٌۭ</span>, no bargaining. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا خُلَّةٌ</span>, there''s going to be no friendships. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا شَفَاعَةٌ</span>, and there''s going to be nobody to have your back, right? And in another verse he says, um, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْأَخِلَّآءُ يَوْمَئِذٍۭ بَعْضُهُمْ لِبَعْضٍ عَدُوٌّ إِلَّا ٱلْمُتَّقِينَ</span> ‎, that really close friends. Look at this, right? Now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>, what did I say? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span> was, this is not just any ordinary friendship, right? It''s a very deeply deep-seated love that you have for your friend. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is saying that these close friends who have so much love for each other, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَى</span>, which is the plural of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says that really, really close and intimate friends who are extremely close to each other in this world, when the day of judgment comes, they turn into each other''s enemies.</p>
<p>And the only exception that he puts there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَّقِينَ</span>, the pious ones. And grammatically, it''s a very strong kind of exception, which means that the only possible exception to that is people who protected themselves from making the wrong kinds of friends, from choosing the wrong kinds of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>. You know, everyone else that we think, you know, today we have all these best, you know, bestest friends and God knows what, right? But you know, but all these kinds of friends, as in, you know, over here in the Americas they say like, &quot;That''s my dog.&quot; I mean, that kind of friends, on Judgment Day, that''s not, he''s no longer going to be your dog, right?</p>
<p>So in other words, make friends based on how conscious they are of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, how conscious they are and how responsible they are with their religion. I mean, don''t let the wrong kinds of friends be your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>, you understand? Because when the Day of Judgment comes, it''s a very scary situation, right? And these wrongdoers, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> talks about it, like what they say, and what it says is, let me see, there''s this verse in which he says, you know, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبِيل</span>, it means generally a path, right, which is, in Arabic, especially in ancient Arabic, it''s a very beautiful linguistic illustration of a lifestyle, right? So the path is the path of life, basically. You know, if this person says, if I only had chosen the lifestyle of the Messenger, I would not be in this mess today, right?</p>
<p>And this is a very powerful verse in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, because if you look at the next <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَة</span>, it says, this is really interesting, I mean, I find this really interesting, you know, and I really don''t know if there''s any other verse in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> that has more despair in it than this one, because it combines two words of regret: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا وَيْلَتَا لَيْتَنِي</span>. I mean, this is exaggerated <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَا</span>, you know, because, you know, just <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْتَنِي</span> is an exaggerated form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَرَ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِلَى</span>, sorry, and then after that it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْتَنِي</span>, to describe even more <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَرَ</span>, even more despair, even more regret, right? So it''s like regret on top of regret.</p>
<p>So now, what is this about, and what is he saying, what''s so interesting? It''s not, it doesn''t mention a name. It says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُلَانًا</span>. You know, in Urdu we say, oh, right, like we don''t use an actual name, we just say, you know, that person, and make it anonymous. And what''s so interesting is that this verse is actually saying, I wish I did not take so and so as my friend, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُلَان</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُلَانًا</span>. Now tell me something, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span> is someone that you have a lot of love for, isn''t it?</p>
<p>And if you have so much love for this person, you would at least know their name, wouldn''t you? But Judgment Day comes along and you have so much regret on top of regret, and so much hate fills your heart because of the path that this person has put you on, that you cannot even remember his name. So the verse says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُلَانًا خَلِيلًۭا</span>. I mean, this is the level of regret that this person has. So you can see that, in itself, as beautiful as it may be, but if you choose the wrong person to be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>, it could end you up, you know, it could end up in, you could end up in really regretful things.</p>
<p>Okay, so that''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>. But it''s interesting how the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> takes such a beautiful meaning word and then shows you, shows you that how you could choose the wrong person, you know, for that position basically. Next word, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَفِيق</span>. Now the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَفِيق</span> in Arabic, it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِفْقَة</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيف</span>, and what it means is comfort. So you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِرْفِقَا</span>, these are the pillows that you place behind your back when, you know, when it''s hurting, and also the small cushions on the couch, they''re also called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِرْفَق</span>, because you lean on them and they take the pressure off your back and they help you relax, right?</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَفِيق</span> is a friend that when you are stressed out, when you are in serious trouble, when you''re going through a difficult time, you turn to them for comfort. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَفِيق</span>. They''re like a pillow that you can lean on at any time and find comfort in their company. And then people who are reclining, are they sitting formally or informally? Always informal, right? So they''re relaxed. So this is a friend that you don''t have to act in a certain way with or behave in a certain way.</p>
<p>You don''t have to walk on eggshells with this person. You can just be yourself. If you feel like being happy, good, evil, you know, whatever, whatever you want to say, you can say, and without being judged, without the fear of being judged or, you know, somebody taking that and holding that against you for the future. And this is, again, oh my God, such a... You know, if you have someone who fits this profile, all these words, you should really do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِجْدَة</span> and do a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْرًا</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for giving you such a friend, because such a friend is so rare and you need to just cherish this friend like nothing else.</p>
<p>Just look at the qualities of these friends, right? And you can only appreciate all of this if you have seen the inverse or experienced the inverse in your life. If there are people that you have or had in your life that you have to walk on eggshells with, then you can really appreciate a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَفِيق</span> when you find one. So that''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَفِيق</span>, somebody that your soul makes you that comfortable.</p>
<p>Now in terms of usage, it''s beautiful. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ النِّسَاءِ</span>, here this one, right? Right, those, those who obey <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and obey the Prophet, they will receive special gifts, special favors from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And what are those? What is that special gift? That they will be counted among the elite categories. They will find comfort in the company of prophets, those who did righteous deeds. Those who gave up martyrs, who gave up their life in the way of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, right?</p>
<p>And the righteous. The truthful, the martyrs, and the righteous. And he ends the verse by saying... And what an awesome group of comforting friends these people are. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> praising these four categories. ... What an awesome group of comforting friends to be amongst. Can you imagine being in the company of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> and not feeling nervous? Can you imagine being comfortable around <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِي</span>, around the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَئِمَّةٌ</span>, right?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَفِيق</span> is another kind of friend who we should all strive to be like, right? A very comforting friend. Okay, two more. Let''s get through this. Next one, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span>.</p>
<p>Now this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span> is a very cool type of friend. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span> is actually used in Arabic for a rope that is used to hold two camels together. You know, when two camels are standing side by side and you tie them with one single rope, that rope is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span> is someone who is always side by side with you. He''s joined to the hip, as they say. You know, one moves, the other moves, the other is forced to move. They''re always moving together. So your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span> is the friend that you are always hanging out with, you''re always on the phone with, you''re always on WhatsApp with. You know, you''re constantly, you know, putting likes on their Facebook. They put a picture of a donkey and you put like a like on it. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span>. You know, you''re constantly with this person. When you''re happy, he or she becomes happy. When he or she is sad, you become sad. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span>. Because you move together.</p>
<p>You see, you''re tied together. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span> is also somebody that you have a lot in common with. Very compatible. Because you cannot tie two camels together where one is very tall and the other is very short, right? It wouldn''t work. Right? You cannot tie two camels together in which one is really fast and the other is really slow. Wouldn''t work. So a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span> is someone that you pretty much, you''re very compatible with.</p>
<p>You pretty much share a brain and a heart with. You think like them, you feel like them. You know, they think like you, they feel like you. So that kind of relationship is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span>.</p>
<p>Now, interestingly, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span> can also mean people of the same community. Like, we are all a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span> right now, right, in the sense that we''re all tied together by the same rope of the session, right? We''re all of the same mindset, right? As a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَمَاعَة</span>, as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُلَيْمَانِيس</span>, we are tied together by the rope of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدِي شَرِيف</span>. So you see, so it can be used in that broad sense as well. We all belong to the same creed. So in that respect, we''re all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَالِم</span>, we''ll all move together.</p>
<p>And like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> presents this word in a very scary way as well. So if you look at in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الصَّافَّاتِ</span>, let me share this with you. Yeah, this one, right? Okay. So in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الصَّافَّاتِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> talks about some of us, who may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> make all of us from that, but basically as a group, some members of the group, they make it to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>, and in there we inquire about other peoples in our families and communities, right? So that''s this conversation, that''s the context of these verses. And then they turn to each other and they start questioning each other. See, one of them, a speaker within that group, they say, indeed I used to have this companion, this person that I was really compatible with, and you know, he used to ask me, he used to mock me, he used to make fun of me in the life. And you know, even though he was a friend and we always are now together and we did everything together, he would always make fun of my religion, and he would say, you know, are you among the believers? Are you one of those who testify? Are you among these idiots who call themselves <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِمُونَ</span>? I mean, are you among those fools who say that when we die and we become dust and bones, we shall receive reward and punishment? Are you among those fools?</p>
<p>In response, it will be said to them, this guy is questioning about this friend who used to mock him, right? And so the other members are going to say, they said, look down. Have you looked down? Have you looked at the other category of Hellfire? And so he looks down and he sees that person in the midst of Hellfire. So may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> protect us from being one of them.</p>
<p>So the point of this is, be careful who you choose as your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span>. Your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span> can make you a shining star, or a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرِيم</span> can destroy you, right? So be very careful who you constantly hang out with. In fact, there''s another verse, what is it .....</p>
<p>And whoever turns away from the remembrance of the intensely merciful, right, whoever turns, no, we shall appoint for him a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَأْن</span>, and he is going to be a to him, right? So this is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> telling us that, you know, if you turn away from the remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, if you disregard <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, if you reject <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> despite all the evidence, despite everything that he has done for you, given you, shown you, all of that, if you still turn away from him, he is going to, as a punishment, give you a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span>. And what that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span> is going to be, it''s going to be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>. You will end up hanging out with these kind of people all your, the rest of your life, right? So that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span>.</p>
<p>Again, I want to compare this with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>. You know, what a beautiful word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span> is, what a beautiful word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span> is, but then look how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is using it in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, right? It''s very interesting.</p>
<p>And last one. Okay, so so far we''ve looked at the good friends that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> describes, even when used in a bad, in a negative context. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَزُول</span> is something that is like bad through and through, a bad friend. So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> also talks about bad friends.</p>
<p>And the word he uses to describe these evil friends is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَزُول</span>. Now, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَذَلَ</span> in Arabic, it''s close to the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَدَا</span>, which means deception. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَيْرُلْ</span> is a friend who will only act like your friend until the moment of truth arrives. And then you see his true colors. So they will be with you, you know, while they were with you, they''re just like you and they''re right by your side, you know, they help you, they''re all of these things. They pretend to be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيجَة</span> and all of these things, right? When times are convenient, they always hang out with you, so they were your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span>. But then when time became tough, all of a sudden they disappear on you. So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَزُول</span> is the one that you will — that will reveal himself or herself in times of difficulty or at the final moment.</p>
<p>You know, you were relying on them to be there for you and then they are nowhere to be found. This is the kind of friend that is called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَذُولٌ</span>, and it is an extremely dangerous friend, right? You can see why, right? Because if he''s gonna, or she''s gonna, pretend to be your all of these other things, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span>, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَافِع</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>, all of these things and you''re like, oh my God, this is like... But then, right at the moment, you find out that this person turns out to be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَذُولٌ</span>.</p>
<p>And, you know, some of us can even relate. We''ve gone through some of these experiences. And it''s... I mean, so this is the point of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَذُولٌ</span>, it''s not just any ordinary deception. It has to do with extreme kind of deception. You know, an extremely disappointing friend who leaves you hanging at the worst possible time. You know, this is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيجَة</span> that you trusted with all your gold and jewelry, you wrote him a blank check, and the next morning, gone. This type of deception is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَزُلْ</span>, and it takes years of consistent effort to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَذَا الْفِي</span> someone, right?</p>
<p>And this is actually the description of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> is described as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَزُول</span>, because — and what is his ultimate deception? To prevent us from the remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That''s his game, to distract us and divert our attention towards <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> says, right, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَذُول</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمَا</span>n beings.</p>
<p>Why is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> saying that? Because man, is he going to act like your friend in this life. He is going to be your best friend, your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَفِيق</span>, your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>, all of that stuff, and he''s going to get you into things that you do not want to be in, and he will convince you that those things are good for you and they''re all for your entertainment. And this is the, you know, forget Islam and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and all that, evolution.</p>
<p>Don''t reject your forefather monkeys just for this religion. This is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَزُول</span>. He will make you watch. He will put you on a path to watching movies and just enjoying filth. He will make you buy stuff. He will make you go places. He will say things and you will build the wrong kinds of friendships. All of it. And you will think, man, this is so good, I''m living it up. This is because when the Day of Judgment comes, this guy is nowhere to be found. And when, on Day of Judgment, when you complain about him, he''ll be like, hey, hey, hey, wow, I was never an authority over you and you were never an authority over me. You know, don''t, don''t complain. I did not have any authority over you. I suggested all of these things to you, but you''re the one who did it. I didn''t put a gun to your head, right? You were the one who chose to do all of these things. So now don''t blame me, blame your own self for the situation that you''re in. So you know what?</p>
<p>Look, what''s done is done. You did whatever you did. I told you. Now let''s just enjoy the barbecue together. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَدٌّ</span>. That''s what''s going to happen on Judgment Day. So you can see how dangerous this is, right? And it builds with small things. And that''s the question we have to ask ourselves. Are we these kind of people? Are we the kind of friends who abandon our friends in their time of need?</p>
<p>Oh, you called? Oh, I didn''t know. My phone was not even charged. It was dead. I just saw my phone. It was like, oh man, I wish I''d— you knew he called. You were just <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَازُولِينْغ</span> it. But you see my point. So don''t be that kind of friend. If you''re going to be a friend with someone, be a reliable person, right? Reliability is an extremely important quality in friendship, and especially in difficult times.</p>
<p>And not being reliable is one of the easiest ways to lose friends. You must have some really good friends in your life and you do not want to mess that up, because friendship is so rare today. So these are the different kinds of friendships that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> talks about, which gives us a really good understanding of the kind of friends we should be. Like I said in the beginning, you can look at this two ways.</p>
<p>You can make a list of your friends and say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَفِيق</span>, no. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>, no. Okay, sorry, you can''t be my friend. You''re missing these qualities. You can do that and just eliminate your friends based on these qualities. Or you can look at it the other way and say, okay, let me look at myself and say, am I all of these things to my friends or not? And where do I lack?</p>
<p>Do I need to be a better <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>? Do I need to be a better <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَفِيق</span>? Do I need to be, you know, turn ourselves into this perfect friend that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> portrays? And again, see friendship, if you understood anything about these words, friendships are not about being good, it''s not about being liked, right? Because that''s usually today what friendships, what dictates, you know, what governs friendships, right? To be liked. I want to be cool, I want to be liked, I want to be loved. And if you really understand the meanings of these words that we talked about today, have nothing to do with like. You could be the most hated person to your friend and still be the best friend to them, right? So it''s really interesting how the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآنِيٌّ</span> perception of friendship, you know, versus the societal concept of friendship. But I hope, anyway, I think this is really, I hope this helps you in setting the grounds because this friendship, because you will see, right, it is this kind of person, this kind of friend that you fall in love with. So you can see how this friendship leads towards love, right? If somebody had these qualities, that''s the person you''re going to love, right? And who has these amazing qualities greater than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, which then leads to the conversation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>. So I hope, you know, that was the idea of bringing this up at this point.</p>
<p>Okay, so let''s....................</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'quranic friendship';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ خَالِقِ الْوُجُودِ مِنَ الْعَدَمِ وَجَاعِلِ النُّورِ مِنَ الظُّلَامِ وَمُخْرِجِ الصَّبْرِ مِنَ الْأَلَمِ وَمُلْقِي التَّوْبَةِ عَلَى الْعَدَمِ فَنَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى الْمَصَائِبِ كَمَا نَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى النِّعَمِ وَنُصَلِّي عَلَى رَسُولِهِ الْأَكْرَمِ وَوَلِيِّ الشَّرَفِ الْأَشَمِّ وَنُورِ الْأَعْظَمِ وَالْكِتَابِ الْمُحْكَمِ وَكَمَالِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَالْخَاتَمِ سَيِّدِي وَلَدِ آدَمَ الَّذِي بَشَّرَ بِهِ عِيسَى ابْنُ مَرْيَمَ وَدَعَا لَهُ بِيَاسَتِهِ إِبْرَاهِيمُ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ حِينَ كَانَ يَرْفَعُ قَوَاعِدَ بَيْتِ اللَّهِ الْمُحَرَّمِ فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ وَعَلَى أَصْحَابِهِ خَيْرِ الْأُمَمِ الَّذِينَ بَارَكَ اللَّهُ بِهِمْ كَافَّةَ النَّاسِ الْعَرَبِ مِنْهُمْ وَالْعَجَمِ وَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَى أَمِيرِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ الْأَنْزَعِ الْبَطِينِ قَائِدِ الْغُرِّ الْمُحَجَّلِينَ غَالِبِ عَلَى كُلِّ غَالِبٍ مَطْلُوبِ كُلِّ طَالِبٍ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيِّ ابْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَنزَلَ عَلَىٰ عَبْدِهِ ٱلْكِتَبَ وَلَمْ يَجْعَل لَّهُۥ عِوَجَاۜ</span> ‎</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًا، وَلَنْ يَكُونَ لَهُ شَرِيكٌ فِي الْمُلْكِ، وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ وَلِيٌّ مِنَ الذُّلِّ وَكَبِّرْهُ تَكْبِيرًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي نَحْمَدُهُ وَنَسْتَعِينُهُ وَنَسْتَغْفِرُهُ وَنُؤْمِنُ بِهِ وَنَتَوَكَّلُ عَلَيْهِ، وَنَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنْ شُرُورِ أَنْفُسِنَا وَمِنْ سَيِّئَاتِ أَعْمَالِنَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ يَهْدِهِ اللَّهُ فَلَا مُضِلَّ لَهُ وَمَنْ يُضْلِلْهُ فَلَا هَادِيَ لَهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولُهُ، أَرْسَلَهُ اللَّهُ تَعَالَى بِالْهُدَى وَدِينِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ وَكَفَى بِاللَّهِ شَهِيدًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَا إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَعُ وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَأَمَّا بَعْدُ، يَقُولُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى فِي كِتَابِهِ الْكَرِيمِ بَعْدَ أَنْ نَقُولَ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ، بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِيمِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ بِلَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ، وَاجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ</span> Once again, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>.</p>
<p>You know, we''re talking — the direction in which I''m taking these discussions is towards the topic of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَه</span>. But as I said, that you cannot really understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَه</span>. In order to truly understand and appreciate the meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَه</span>, you need to understand what love means, especially in the Islamic sense, in the Quranic sense, and you cannot understand love, you cannot fall in love with someone without being friends.</p>
<p>So friendship is what we covered in the previous session, and we looked at the various qualities that make up a perfect friend. Right, so there is the quality of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيّ</span>, protection, being a protective friend; a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span>, a sincere and true friend; a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span>, which is a companion; a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَجَ</span>, a trustworthy; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِطَانَة</span>, which is a confidant; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>, an intimate friend; there was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَفِيق</span>, a comforting friend; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِين</span>, which was a compatible friend.</p>
<p>So we looked at all these different traits, and like I said, think of it like a rainbow, that all of these traits come together in a perfect friend. A perfect friend from a Quranic standpoint is not one of these things, it is a combination of all of these traits. But when it comes to love, the Arabs have what they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِرْآةِ بُلْشَبْدْ</span>, which is the stages of love.</p>
<p>So what they say is that love increases in 10 stages. And basically, it starts at level 1, then goes on to level 2, level 3. They pretty much describe love like a fever, so it just keeps getting warmer and warmer. So they describe love in ten stages, which they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَرَثَ بُلْهُوجْ</span>, and these are the ten levels of love.</p>
<p>And now, generally, you can look at these stages in the worldly sense. It''s definitely applicable in the worldly sense, at least to a certain level, but the idea and the goal of this discussion is, you know, it''s more divine love, rather seeing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> as the beloved. So, you know, with that I''ll begin this topic. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>, the first level of love is what they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَقَ</span>, okay, and what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَقَ</span> is, it''s, right, the connection of the heart with the beloved. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَقَ</span> is basically a connection.</p>
<p>Now in Arabic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَّقَ</span> is a hanger, you know, what you use to hang your clothes on. Now a hanger has a hook and, you know, by definition a hanger means something to hang upon, so that''s how they get this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَّقَ</span>, which is basically, you know, a connection of the heart. So the heart gets connected, it gets hooked, it gets attached to the beloved. This is the first stage.</p>
<p>So in our terms, this is what we call attachment, or, you know, generally we say what we call a crush. In our terms, right, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَقَة</span>. So when you''re at this first stage, that''s when you say, like, you know, I''ve got a crush on this person or that person, or I''m attached to this person or that person. So that is basically <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَقَة</span>. And this type of attachment could either mean something or it could be very temporary. It could just be like an instant, you know, attachment and attraction, and then it''s over. Or, you know, it could progress. But basically that hook, that first connection, is what they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَاكَ</span>.</p>
<p>The second level is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِرَادَة</span>, which is willful love. This is where you actually begin looking for the beloved. So it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تُرِيدُهُ</span>, you know, you make an effort to seek out the beloved. And at this stage, the lover is called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرِيد</span>, because he sets out with a goal or a purpose to find the beloved. You see? The first level was, you know, a simple connection of the heart, right?</p>
<p>But at this stage, there is a pursuit of what the heart desires with a particular intention, with an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِرَاضَة</span>. So it''s basically considered the beginning of the journey, right? So that''s the next stage. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرْضَة</span> is the stage where you begin to desire the company of the beloved, and it''s not just an attraction, you actually want to meet the beloved. So there''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَقَ</span>, there''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِرَاضَة</span>. The third one is what they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبَبًا</span>.</p>
<p>Now, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبَبًا</span>, it comes from an Arabic expression which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّبْعُ الْمَثَانِي</span>. The Arabs use this term to describe a waterfall. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّبْعُ الْمَثَانِي</span> is, right? It''s a waterfall. So we can think of this stage, this third stage, in English as falling in love, right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبَبًا</span> is the stage where you begin to feel like you''re falling in love. It''s where you begin to lose control, and that''s the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">انْصِبَابٌ</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبَبًا</span> is sort of, you know, you begin to lose control, and you actually know that you are falling in love with this person, and you start feeling all weird inside. Okay? So, all right, so that''s the third state, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبَبًا</span>, right, falling in love. Then you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَرَامٌ</span>.</p>
<p>Which is a, it is a sort of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُلَازَمَة</span>, you know, like the punishment in the Hellfire. That''s, you know, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱلَّذِينَ يَقُولُونَ رَبَّنَا ٱصْرِفْ عَنَّا عَذَابَ جَهَنَّمَ ۖ إِنَّ عَذَابَهَا كَانَ غَرَامًا</span> ‎. So indeed the punishment in the Hellfire is never going to end, right? The punishment doesn''t stop there. So the idea of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَرَام</span> is sort of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا يَنْفَكُّ</span>, it''s sort of, it''s never ending. So this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَرَامٌ</span> is the state where, you know, the Arabs, they refer to it as, like, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُغْرَمٌ بِهِ</span>, which means that they just want to be with the beloved, right? So it''s a very strong desire to just be with the beloved all the time, and distance actually becomes painful at this stage.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَرَامٌ</span> is where it''s like an obsession, it''s a non-stop obsession about the beloved. You just can''t stop thinking about the person. You know, they say a sign of people at this stage, at this fourth stage, is when you find them constantly, like, staring at the beloved''s pictures. Have you ever seen that, like, you know, where people just constantly stare at the beloved''s picture, or they keep going back and reading their letters over and over, and the same e-mail, the same text messages, over and over and over again? Right? That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَرَامٌ</span>. Because what it does is it gives them a sense of being close to the beloved, and it reduces the pain of the distance from them. So, you know, this is the state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَرَامٌ</span>, when this person, the only way he feels comfort is when he actually gets some sort of that physical connection with the, you know, either through a picture, letters, whatever, right? But it''s a non-stop feeling of obsession.</p>
<p>So that''s the fourth state. So you can see how it increments, right? It starts with the connection, then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِرَادَة</span>, there is the intent of pursuit joins that connection. Then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبَبًا</span>, where, you know, it begins to feel like they realize that they''re falling in love. And then it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَرَامٌ</span>, where this obsession begins to develop.</p>
<p>So that''s the fourth one. The fifth one is what they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُدٌّ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُود</span>, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَدُودٌ</span>. And the lover is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَدُود</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُدٌّ</span>, it means pure love, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُدٌّ</span> is, it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَفْوَةُ الْمَحَبَّة</span>, it''s the purity of love. It is love that is now pure from the inclinations of the self. A love that is pure from the inclinations of the self. You know, in these previous states where you had, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَقَة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبَبًا</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَرَامٌ</span>, all of these states, there''s always still some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> involved.</p>
<p>There was still some selfish reasons for loving the beloved, for wanting to see them, for desiring them, for thinking about them. There''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> involved. In these previous stages, it''s really more about you than it is about the beloved. But when you move into the stage of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُدٌّ</span>, it starts becoming pure. It starts becoming selfless. Right? So, this is why the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, he said, there''s a Hadith in which he said, what was it?</p>
<p>He said, ... Marry a fertile woman who is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَدُودٌ</span>. You know, marry a woman who has this type of pure love. A woman whose love is not selfish. And obviously in Arabic, there is always, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَفْهُومُ الْمُخَالَفَةِ</span>, which means that the opposite is also true, that a woman should also look for a husband with that quality. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">األْوَدُودُ</span> is this pure kind of love, in which there is no inclination of the self.</p>
<p>Now, interestingly, if you know, some of you might have realized this, that this is also one of the names of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, right? He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">األْوَدُودُ</span>. In the Quran, He calls Himself <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">األْوَدُودُ</span>. You know, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱسْتَغْفِرُوا۟ رَبَّكُمْ ثُمَّ تُوبُوٓا۟ إِلَيْهِ ۚ إِنَّ رَبِّى رَحِيمٌۭ وَدُودٌۭ</span> ‎. Ask forgiveness of your Master and turn to Him in repentance. Indeed, my Master is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, He is the Most Merciful, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَدُودٌ</span>, and He is most loving, His love is pure, because He is selfless in His love.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَدُودُ</span>, basically from a linguistic perspective, it has two meanings. You know, it''s both ways. It''s like ... <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the one who loves His servants, and He is the one who His servants love. So it''s a relationship of pure love both ways, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has a pure selfless love for His creation, and then the creation has a pure selfless love for their master. That''s ... That''s how they define <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوُدُّ</span>.</p>
<p>It''s a two-way street. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَادُّ فِي عِبَادِهِ وَالْمَوْدُودُ</span>. Right. So, you know, so that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُدٌّ</span>. And interestingly, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَدُودٌ</span>, and He describes the true purpose of a man and a woman coming together, you know, also in the same way, like, there is ... ... ... ... So there''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَدَّة</span>, right? Amongst His signs is that He created for you wives from amongst yourself. Why? ... ... so that you may find <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُكُون</span>, so that you may find repose in them.</p>
<p>And He has put between you pure love and mercy. Right? So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says that He put <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوَدَّة</span> between a man and woman, which is supposed to be at least pure love. And like I said, pure love is a love that is pure from the inclinations of the self. This is the type of love where the husband will not divorce his wife when she gets sick. A husband will not divorce his wife when she''s no longer beautiful.</p>
<p>You know, when she becomes fat. So if you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوَدَّة</span> for your wife, even after she gets fat, you''ll still love her. Seriously, if she gets sick, you''ll still love her. Because she''ll still love you even when you get fat and ugly. I mean, she''ll still love you. Because a woman''s love is quite amazing that way. A woman''s love, it doesn''t change the way a man''s love changes.</p>
<p>Because that is not love that changes. I love this poem of Shakespeare in which he says, &quot;Love is not love which alters when it alteration finds, or bends with the remover to remove. Oh no, it is an ever fixed mark that looks on tempests and is never shaken.&quot; That is real love. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوُدُّ</span>. And what Shakespeare meant by that is that love is not love that when things change or when the situation changes, your love changes.</p>
<p>You know, your wife is beautiful today so you love her, but if she''s no longer beautiful one day, it doesn''t change your love. If it does, then that was not love, that was just lust, right? I know this woman who got into this accident where the side of her face got burnt off pretty bad, you know, on one side. And the husband just divorced her just because she didn''t look beautiful anymore.</p>
<p>So he just divorced her and sent her back to her parents. I mean, that''s not love. That is sick, right? But that is the state of some people. But that is not real love. If you think you''re in love with someone, the best way for you to find out if you really love them is just ask yourself this question. If she gets burned today, if that beautiful face gets burned today, if she loses her beautiful voice, would you still love that person?</p>
<p>And if you cannot answer that question, then you really are not in love. You''re just hormonal. That''s what it is. Because women, if you look at women, they have a much deeper capacity for love than most men do. I mean, that''s just a fact, right? If that same accident happened to this guy where his face got burnt off, she would have still spent the rest of her life with him.</p>
<p>She would have taken care of him, she would have loved him the same way. Because that is real love. And women have a much greater capacity to love in this way than men do, right? And this is why our Prophet is so amazing. You know, he said, I was made to love women. That statement has nothing to do with lust. I was made to love women. And I was made to love perfume and to love flossing.</p>
<p>What''s the connection between women, perfume and flossing? Perfume just gives, right? Perfume dissipates its own existence for others, for us. When you put on perfume, it diffuses and it gives up its own existence to make you smell nice. Right? A floss goes into a filthy mouth and it cleans it at the expense of ruining its own existence. It sacrifices itself to clean our mouths. What does the floss get out of that experience?</p>
<p>Nothing. What does the perfume get out of that when we apply it? Nothing. That is the quality of women that our Prophet loved. It was that sacrifice he loved that came so natural to a woman and men find it so difficult. You know, and this is why, you know, we learn love from our mothers, right? You know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> said, Paradise is under the feet of mothers, under the feet of mothers.</p>
<p>Because that''s where we learn it best, right? In our mothers, that love is where we see it most embodied. And this is why in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثًا قُدْسِيًّا</span>, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> speaks to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> and he says that I have derived, you know, I have derived the womb, you know, literally means to split, like, you know, separate it, split it out of something. He says, I have split the womb from My name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰنِ</span>, the Merciful.</p>
<p>That is unique to women. Men don''t have wombs. Right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> gave that only to women. And that womb is such a beautiful, is that space in the center of a woman that is the source of all mercy in this world. I mean, that was our first abode, all of us. We were all initially there in that abode of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, of mercy. And what''s amazing is that, you know, that womb, it grows, it grows big to facilitate what''s in it.</p>
<p>That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, that is mercy. It is that emotion that grows with the need of the beloved. Right? That it expands itself to satisfy the need of others. That''s what mercy is. That''s what love is. And this is why our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةً لِلْعَالَمِينَ</span>. We have not sent you but as a mercy to all of humanity. Because that is what he did.</p>
<p>He was that womb. He expanded for the needs of others. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُدٌّ</span>. Pure love. Selfless love. It is not that, you know, this utilitarian kind of love that, you know, a love that is tainted by one''s own selfish needs. It''s not the type of love that we know of. I''m going to get something out of this. What''s in it for me? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُود</span> is a love that transcends all of that.</p>
<p>It''s the kind of love that when there is beauty, that love is there. But then when the beauty goes away, the love is still there. If the service is no longer there because of a stroke or because of some incapacity, the love is still there. That is real love. You know, it''s not really love when there is just usefulness from me, you know. That''s not love, that''s just something else.</p>
<p>And you know, we are a society, we have no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَدَّة</span>, we have no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحَمَ</span> between ourselves. Look at what we have become today, right? We are a society, we are a community that is completely... Forget <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> as a Muslim, just us as a community. I mean, there''s no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, there is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُدٌّ</span>. You know, we are void of this kind of love. Everything, everything in our lives, in our relationships, you know, what''s in it for me?</p>
<p>You know, why should I help you? Why should I go out of my way? You know, after that battle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِفِّين</span>, you know, when that battle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِفِّين</span> was over, this water man, you know, he narrates that he was on the battlefield searching for dying soldiers. In those days, it was, I mean, you must have seen this in movies, that, you know, they had this man whose only duty was to carry around this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَشْكِيزَا</span>, this water container.</p>
<p>And his only job was to quench the thirst of these dying soldiers because, you know, they''re wounded and they say that right at the moment of death, one becomes extremely thirsty. And so water helps with the transition. So anyway, this man, he narrates that he was, after the Battle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِفِّين</span> was over, he was carrying around this water on the battleground, searching for thirsty soldiers who are about to die.</p>
<p>And he came upon this one soldier, this man whose lower part of his body, it had been severed from, you know, his body. So just basically his torso was sitting on the scorching ground, and he was sighing for water, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَطَشُ، الْعَطَشُ</span>. He was just constantly saying, you know, water, I''m thirsty, I''m thirsty. So, and they say that that''s an extreme form of thirst. And so this man, he says that he, you know, I walked up to him. And this guy, he brought the water, that cup, he poured the water in a cup and he brought the cup of water close to his lips, of this dying man.</p>
<p>And they heard the cry of another man close by saying the same thing. So he says that this dying man, right, in that state, torso half cut up, he sealed his lips. And he refused to drink that cup of water, only so that I could quench the thirst of his brother first. He says, I rushed towards this other man so that I could give him water, and as I brought the cup close to his lips, they again heard another man sighing, and this man sealed his lips so that I could first go and quench the thirst of this other person.</p>
<p>So he said, I rushed to this third person to give him water, but by the time I got there, he had already died. So then I returned to the second person and he had died, and I returned to the first one and he had died. By, I mean, this was love, this was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُدٌّ</span>, right, that these people had for each other. And where is that love today? And how do we compare to these, how do we, how do we measure up to these people?</p>
<p>I mean, what were these people made of? But that is, you know, this is the standard these people have set for us. I mean, our love is just, you know, just this Bolly<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُدٌّ</span> nonsense of chasing a girl around a tree singing a song. I mean, that''s what, you know, we''ve reduced love to, just.</p>
<p>Fifth stage was what? The next stage is what they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَقَفْ</span>. So, this is the stage of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَقَفُ الْقَلْبِ</span>, which is basically, the representation is the pericardium.</p>
<p>It''s the stage where the heart is just surrounded by this love. It encompasses the heart. This is the state of... It''s just, I don''t know how to describe this. It''s a state of obsession, but it''s a state of pure, like there is no self, there''s no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> involved. It''s like constantly thinking about the beloved. Right? You know, wherever you are, whatever you see, it just reminds you of the beloved.</p>
<p>It''s that stage where you see the beloved everywhere, sunrise, sunset, everything beautiful, everything amazing reminds you of your beloved, the flower, the rose, the birds, the music, everything. It just reminds you of the beloved, and it''s a very... it''s a very pure — remember, these are all incremental stages, right? So a person who is at the level of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَغَف</span>, he has already crossed the stage of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُدٌّ</span>, so his love has become extremely pure. Now it''s just that he''s constantly engulfed by the beauty, and it''s not for his own self, it''s just, you know, in love with the person itself, with the beloved itself.</p>
<p>Then comes the next stage. After <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَغَف</span>, the next stage is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">العِشْق</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">العِشْق</span>. This is a very dangerous state of love. You know, people who go in this state of love, like for someone or for God, they end up going insane. This is an insane love. That''s the only way to describe it. And it''s a very dangerous phase. It''s where they literally, like a person who is at the level of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> will appear insane to me. It''s like, are you insane? Who does that? It''s that kind of love.</p>
<p>This stage of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> is where the lover enjoys pain and suffering for the sake of the beloved. It''s that state of intense love, right? It''s that state of insane love, I should say. Somebody''s mic is on. Right? So that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">العِشْق</span>.</p>
<p>It''s very interesting. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>, it comes from a vine called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَشْقَى</span>, right? And this vine, you know, when it surrounds a tree, it wraps itself around the tree, it basically sucks out the life of that tree and eventually kills it. That''s where they get this term <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>, because it''s from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَشْقَى</span>. It''s that vine that wraps itself around the tree and sucks the life out of it. And imamate in that is that the tree, it sacrifices itself to nourish and feed this vine. You know, it kills itself so that his beloved can survive. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>.</p>
<p>But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> is this type of love where there is total constriction, and the lover actually begins to suffer in the love of that beloved and enjoys that suffering. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>. And the Arabs say there is no cure to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> except that... the only cure for this insane love is meeting the beloved. That''s the only cure.</p>
<p>And people have, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span>, during the time of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span>, there was this woman who had turned Muslim, but she did not disclose her Islam to anyone. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span> eventually found out, and he summoned this woman to his court. And so she was arrested and she was brought to court along with her two daughters. And the older one was about 10 years old and the younger one was only a few months old.</p>
<p>And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span> said to this woman, I have heard that you have turned Muslim. You now believe in the God of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span>. So I give you two choices. The first choice is that you reject the God of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span> and you accept me as your God. If you do that, I will grant you and your daughters a palace in which you and your daughters will reside as royalty. You will have all the comforts of this world. Anything that you can imagine, you will have for the rest of your life, for the rest of your generations. All you have to do is reject this God of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span> and accept me as God. And the second choice is that if you reject my offer, I will burn both your daughters alive in front of you, and I will burn you alive as well.</p>
<p>And so this woman, who was at the stage of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>, she rejected his offer. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span> ordered his men to fill this large cauldron with oil, right, and it was heated till it started boiling before her eyes. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span> asked her again, do you accept my offer? And she said, I am already in love with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span>''s God. How can I leave the one I love for you? How can I reject him for you? This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>.</p>
<p>So by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span>''s command, his soldiers, they dragged her 10 year old and threw her in the boiling oil, right in front of her eyes. I mean, there are mothers in this group, you know, only a mother can understand what falls on her heart when she sees her child burned to death in front of her. But this woman, she saw that entire thing, she did not say a word.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span> asked her again, do you accept my offer now? And she again refused. So they snatched that few month old daughter from her arms and threw her in the boiling oil. And she still did not say a word. And they say that, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> has narrated this and he says, right then, the veils of the sky were removed before her eyes and she saw both her daughters in paradise saying to her, you know, mother, just be patient a little while longer, we''re all soon going to be united in heaven. And then came this woman''s turn, and so she turned to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span> and she said, you know, I have one last request, that after you killed me, bury my bones and the bones of my daughters together in one place.</p>
<p>And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span> said, your wish will be granted. And so they finally threw this woman in the burning oil as well, and she was united with her children. And her last wish was fulfilled, and their bones were buried together. 2000 years later — there''s about a distance of 2000 years between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span> — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> 2000 years later is being taken on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِعْرَاج</span>, and when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البُرَاق</span> flies over <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَيْتُ الْمُقَدَّسِ</span>, the Prophet smells this fragrance of paradise.</p>
<p>And he asks <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِبْرِيل</span>, where is this fragrance coming from? And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِبْرِيل</span> narrated this entire incident to the Prophet. And he said, since the day the bones of that woman and daughter were buried, this fragrance has been rising from their graves, and all the angels rejoice and enjoy this fragrance. I mean, we don''t possess the sense to smell that kind of fragrance, but you know, this was our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> who sensed that fragrance because he was an angel in human form, and so he asked that question. But the point is, this is the kind of suffering, that the kind of sacrifice, right? I mean, this is the story of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>, of how this woman loved <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> loved her back.</p>
<p>I mean, 2000 years later, the messenger <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> is sensing the fragrance of paradise rising up from their graves. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرْبَلَاء</span>, the story of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرْبَلَاء</span>. That''s the — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرْبَلَاء</span> is the story of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>. That is the most amazing story of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامُ حُسَيْنٍ</span>, you know, he''s the leader, but every single person in that story, you know, every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَحْرَم</span> we listen to these tales of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>.</p>
<p>And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرْبَلَاء</span> is a tale of insane love. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرْبَلَاء</span> is. And you must have heard that story of that woman, right, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامُ حُسَيْنٍ</span>s camp, where, you know, whose son goes to battle and returns back to her, comes back and is completely covered in blood and wounds. And he comes back and he calls out to his mother and he asks her and he says, mother, are you pleased with me now? What mother could bear watching her son covered in wounds from head to toe and covered in blood and bleeding from, you know, everywhere? No mother could watch that. But this woman, she tells her, you know, and he''s asking her, are you pleased with me? And she says, no, not yet. She says, not yet my son, I will not be pleased with you until you sacrifice yourself for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامُ حُسَيْنٍ</span>. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>. And he goes back into battle and he fights till he''s martyred, and they cut off his head and they toss it towards the camp, and his mother comes out, she takes his head in her — puts it in her lap, and she begins kissing his forehead, and then she gives out this scream and dies. But you know, that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> is a very, very dangerous stage of love, and this is where there is just no extreme that the lover will not go to. I mean, there''s just no limit here when it comes to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>. We just throw around this word, right, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجھے عِشْق ہُو گَیا</span>, but people don''t really understand the depth of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> and what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> means.</p>
<p>Then the next phase is what they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَتَيُّم</span>, right? The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَيْم</span> is a word used for a slave, but it''s still not at the level of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span>, right? But it''s a higher degree of love where there is a type of servitude that goes with it. This is where you are now in the service of the beloved. You''re constantly in the service of the beloved. The love reaches the stage of what can I do for you?</p>
<p>How can I serve you? How can I make you happy? That''s all that matters to the team. Pleasure comes from servitude. How do I make the beloved happy? What can I do for you? Just saying I love you is not enough. Tell me what I can do for you. I want to do something. That need of doing something for the beloved, anything, just to make the beloved happy, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَتَايَا</span>.</p>
<p>And the next stage after that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعَبُّد</span>. This is the stage where the lover becomes a complete slave of the beloved. At this stage, at the stage of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اهْدِ</span>, there is complete obedience in addition to servitude, right? In the previous one there was just servitude; here there is complete obedience. This is basically where, you know, when the beloved says jump, the lover, you know, asks how high. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعَبُّد</span>.</p>
<p>It is a stage of unquestioned obedience of the beloved. Right. You know... A poet says... You claim to love your God and yet you disobey Him? This kind of love just does not make any sense to my imagination. Right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span> is the stage of complete obedience in love. And this is the honorific title of our Messenger, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> called him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span>. This is the greatest honor that was bestowed upon the Prophet.</p>
<p>You know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Glorious is the one who took his slave for a journey in the night. Not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ الَّذِي أَسْرَى بِرَسُولِهِ لَيْلًا، بِنَبِيِّهِ لَيْلًا، بِحَبِيبِهِ لَيْلًا</span>, no. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَنَ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَسْرَىٰ بِعَبْدِهِۦ لَيْلًۭا</span>. Not messenger, not prophet, not beloved, nothing, just <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span>. Glorious is the one who took his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span> on the night journey. This is the title of the Prophet. He is, his title of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آب</span> is greater than his title of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُول</span>, because of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّدِيَا</span>.</p>
<p>Right, so this is the level of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعَبُّد</span>, which is, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعَبُّد</span> is adoration basically, right? You know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَابِد</span> is the one who adores. It''s adoration, it''s adore, it''s from that Latin word adore. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span> is where you move into the realm of adoration, where there is complete stillness and unconditional obedience of the Beloved. The next stage after that is what they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلَّة</span>, and this is the level of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>.</p>
<p>The Prophet said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> took me as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>, just as he took Ibrahim as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>. Right? And this is the highest level of love. You know, it''s an extreme, it''s that, you know, like what they say about the state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span> is... That''s how they describe it. Where the love completely permeates every cell of the body. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَخْلِيل</span>. No, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَخْلِيل</span> is where, when you penetrate deeply, like, you know when you do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَخْلِيلُ الشَّعَرِ</span> in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحْكَام</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غُسْل</span>, when we do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غُسْل</span>, you have to get to every hair follicle, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلَّة</span>, and it''s a very high degree of love where, you know, the love has permeated the entire soul, every cell of the body is in love with the beloved.</p>
<p>I mean, how do you explain that? It''s just something that just transcends words. You cannot really describe <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيل</span>, but that''s what it is. It''s like, you know, you love the beloved with every cell, every pore of your body. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلَّة</span>.</p>
<p>And the final stage of love is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَبَّة</span>. There''s really no word in the English language that, you know, that can encompass the depth of this love. I mean, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَبَّة</span>, it comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبًّا</span>, actually <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَبَّة</span>, which means seed. Right?</p>
<p>And this, it''s never, the idea of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَبَّة</span> is that it''s never ending, it''s eternal kind of love. It will never end. You know, what happens when you sow a seed? You get a harvest. Right? You get an exponential amount of return on your investment. And what happens after you harvest the crop? It grows back again. You harvest again, grows back again. That''s hope. That''s eternal love. And that is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَآمْ</span> of the Prophet.</p>
<p>He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَبِيبُ اللَّهِ</span>. He is the one that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> loves in that way, eternally. You know, can you just take a moment to appreciate the station of our Prophet? I mean, Adam is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفِرُ اللَّهُ</span>, you know, the one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> sent. Noof is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَجِيُّ اللَّهِ</span>, the one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> saved. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَلِيمُ اللهِ</span>. Ibrahim is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيلُ اللهِ</span>, the one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> befriended. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَلِيمُ اللهِ</span>, the one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> conversed with. Isa is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُوحُ اللهِ</span>, the one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> breathed His spirit into.</p>
<p>But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span>, he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَبِيبُ اللَّهِ</span>. He is the one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> loved. And this is extremely astonishing if you think about it. We just say that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> loves him, but just think about who is loving who. I mean, what a personality that the Creator is in love with His created thing. I mean, what does that say about our Prophet? Where the Creator falls in love with him. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>Where, you know, the entire creation is worried about pleasing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We are all worried about praising <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is worried about praising <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span>. And indeed, your Master will give you so much that it will please you. You will be well pleased. You are the one I want to please. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is promising His Messenger that He will give him and keep giving him so much until he is well pleased.</p>
<p>I mean, that is the kind of love, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has for His Messenger. And that''s the kind of love that the Messenger had for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. This is why to love the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is to love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And to love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is to love the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Right? Say, if you love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> then follow me, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will love you. I mean, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> give us the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَام</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَبَّة</span>, and may He increase us all in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَبَّة</span> and make us people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَبَّة</span>.</p>
<p>You know, the Prophet said, you know... You will not enter paradise until you believe, and you will not believe until you love one another, until you spend on one another, until you take care of one another, right? You will not achieve any good until you spend from that of what you love. I mean, how much do we spend in the way of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>? Because, you know, those who believe and they love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, they love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> more than their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>.</p>
<p>Those who claim to believe and love <spa' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'the stages of love';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'n class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, they love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> more than their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. Those who believe, they love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> more than they love their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. And we should think about what people are out there doing for their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, because it''s amazing. Look at how much people love their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. Look what they are doing to possess their beloved. Look how they are running after their beloved. We really have to ask ourselves if we are doing one tenth for our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span> as what they are doing for their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>.</p>
<p>Just one tenth. They''re working 12 hours a day, 7 days a week, you know, amassing all of this stuff. People learn about, you know, they invest in learning about the most bizarre things because they can trust them, you know, they learn about it. They''ll spend all this time learning about the most ridiculously stupid stuff because they love what they''re doing. We don''t learn out of it to understand our Creator''s book.</p>
<p>I mean, look at that. Have you guys seen that advertisement about this guy, this advertisement for learning Italian? I mean, they show this farm boy and he''s in love with this Italian celebrity, right? And so the idea is that she''s going to be in his town for one evening, just one evening. So he has this one shot, this one chance where he can meet her, right? And if this is a TV advertisement, because they know they are talking to people who understand that when you are in love, you will do anything for the beloved.</p>
<p>You''ll do anything, right? So this farm kid, he gets this Italian software to learn Italian in one night just so that he can speak with that celebrity visiting his town, right? This is what love will do. This is what a lover will do for his beloved. That''s a lover. I mean, we are loveless people. We just want to talk about love. We don''t want to do anything.</p>
<p>We don''t want to make any effort in the name of love, right? We don''t learn Arabic, we don''t take the time to... This guy spends one night learning the entire language so that he could speak with his beloved. We don''t take the time to understand the language our beloved speaks to us in. And how old are we? We don''t want to make the effort to understand the language in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> speaks to His creation.</p>
<p>We are lazy people. We claim love, but none of it is true. We are just fake lovers. We are just fake lovers. But there are lovers. There are true lovers. So if we are not in love, if we cannot be true lovers, we should at least, at the very least, be in awe of these lovers like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> , <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِيّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامُ حُسَيْنٍ</span>. At least we should wish to be in love with them, right?</p>
<p>You know, the Arabs, they say that sometimes the ear falls in love before the eyes do. You know, you sometimes hear about someone and you just fall in love with them. It''s such a beautiful thing. I can''t tell you how beautiful. At some point I hope we all experience true love, really. I hope we all, you know, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> made this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>, this love in this world, a sign, an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَة</span>, to show us what divine love is. He made <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>, muhabba, a sign in this world between people for us to understand what divine love is supposed to be like, right? It''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُجَّة</span> against us. Romeo and Juliet, that''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُجَّة</span> against us.</p>
<p>Laila and Majnun, that''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُجَّة</span> against us, because if this is the love of a created thing for another created thing, then what kind of love should a creature have for his creator? I mean, if these are the depths, if these are the limits that you will go to in love for another human being, right? What should be the depth of your effort for your Creator? So this love is...</p>
<p>pure love is a beautiful thing. I mean, people long for love. There are people who want it so bad. They keep falling in love over and over again, keep having their heart broken, but they keep falling in love over just so, so that, for that experience of love. And these are, you know, foolish people. These are shock, because, like I said, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> is a disease. But then, but then, you know, cure, there is a cure. It''s meeting the beloved. You know, you will want to meet your beloved so bad that you will go to the ends of the earth to meet this person, right?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلْمَانُ الْفَارِسِيُّ</span>, look at his love. Look at his love. Look what he did. You know, this is a man who was sold in slavery unjustly, right? And he was a slave. He traveled all over just to find the messenger. If he heard of any righteous man, right, he would go up to him, he would ask him, tell me about the Prophet, tell me what he looks like, tell me how he speaks.</p>
<p>I mean, his journey is an amazing journey to the Prophet . I mean, how many of you even know the story, right? I mean, this is the kind of lovers we are. But you should really study his life, his journey to his Prophet, his sincerity. You know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> gave him the signs and he knew what to look for because of his sincerity. So the first time he met the Prophet, he brought him dates and he said, this is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقَة</span>, and then he watched the Prophet not eat from it, because he knew, right, he was told that the Prophet will not eat from charity. And then the next time he came, he brought him some dates and he said, this is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِدَايَة</span>, it is a gift, and then the Prophet ate from it, and Salman knew, ah yeah, just like I was told. And then he became Muslim.</p>
<p>And look at the love of the Prophet for Salman. I mean, look where love gets you. Salman is from my family, he is part of our family. Amazing, you know, these are lovers. Amazing, amazing people of love. You know, when this, when the Prophet came to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدِينَة</span>, right, and he let the reins of his camel go, because everybody, everyone wanted the Prophet to stay with them. So, you know, and the Prophet was an extremely diplomatic person. He did not want to upset anyone, he did not want to offend anyone. So what he said is that, you know what, here''s what I''ll do, I''ll leave the reins of my camel and I''ll just let it walk, and wherever she stops, I will live with that person. So the camel, he let go of the reins, and the camel stopped in front of the house of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَضْرَتْ أَبُو أَيُّوبَ الْأَنْصَارِيّ</span>, and he rushed forward and he just lifted the bags of the Prophet, and the Prophet said, a man is with his luggage, right? So he took the Prophet in the house and he put him on the bottom floor, because, because, you know, he wanted to honor him. You know, somebody on the bottom floor is much, it''s easier to live on the bottom floor than the top floor, because you don''t have to walk up the stairs, you can easily go outside, you have easy access.</p>
<p>So he wanted to give the Prophet the utmost ease. So he housed him on the bottom floor, and he and his wife, they took the floor above. But then he became extremely constricted. He couldn''t walk on the floor, because &quot;I don''t want to put my foot where the Prophet might have rested his head.&quot; I mean, this is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَدَبٌ</span> we don''t have. I mean, look at that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَدَبٌ</span>. We don''t have any <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَدَبٌ</span> anymore.</p>
<p>So he went and he begged the Prophet, you know, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, we can''t stay above you, it''s just...&quot; And so the Prophet changed places with him, right? So his wife would take food upstairs for the Prophet, and after he ate, when his wife brought the food back, he would ask her, &quot;Show me, show me where the Messenger''s fingers ate from,&quot; and then he would eat from that place, right? So this is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَتَبُّعُ الْأَثَرِ</span>, right, following the traces. This was love. Amazing, the kind of love these people have for the Messenger. Amazing.</p>
<p>Maimuna, right, his wife, the Prophet''s wife, thirty years later, after she married the Prophet, right, when she was nearing death, you know where she wanted to go? To the place where she spent the first night with the Prophet. That is where she''s buried. I mean, just look at that love. Look at that love. Amazing. Today we just want to take love out of Islam, and we want to make it into something ugly, I mean?</p>
<p>This religion is nothing if it''s not about love. This religion is nothing if it''s not love. No one is a believer until he loves his brother. You know, he did not use any other word. Ten stages of love, and he uses the last stage, you know, . That''s the only word you use, love, until you love. Say, if you love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, then follow me, and God will love you.</p>
<p>Because the sign of love is that you follow the beloved. You do what the beloved says. I mean, our religion, our history, our story, our legacy is a legacy of love. I mean, we have such amazing lovers in our history. I mean, look at this man''s love. I mean, when he heard the Prophet, you know, the Prophet''s tooth got struck in the Battle of Uhud, he went insane.</p>
<p>You know, what did my Messenger feel? How much pain did my Messenger feel when they struck? Just to experience that same pain, he pulled out his own tooth. Just to experience what his beloved experienced. But then that was not enough, you know. What if it was not this tooth? What if it was this tooth? So he pulled out another. What if it was this one? And he pulled out another. You know, what if it was this one? He pulled out his entire — he pulled out all of his teeth. This is love. This is insane love. This is — I mean, where do we stand? I mean, I ask myself that question. I mean, where do I stand? It''s just, how do you compare to these lovers, right?</p>
<p>And this is why I feel that if we cannot love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, we should at least try to love the ones who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> loved, at least the ones who loved <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And if we cannot love them, we should love the ones who love those that loved <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And love is a struggle. When you love, you struggle, you sacrifice. That''s the nature of love. You sacrifice for the one you love. If you''re not sacrificing, that''s not love. You know, that''s not real love, and it''s just, you know, me, me, me, me. People go to extremes, you know, we, you know, we go to...</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمُرُّ بِالدِّيَارِ، دِيَارِ لَيْلَى</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمُرُّ بِالدِّيَارِ، دِيَارِ لَيْلَى</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَقْبَلُ الْأَمْ جِدَارُ وَلَا الْجِدَارَ</span>. The poet says, you know, didn''t you ask me about that place, about that place that''s no longer there? Didn''t you ask me about that place where Laila used to live? And then he said, yeah, indeed I did ask, but how do dead stones give me answers? You know, where did everybody go? And then he says, you know, I go by the stones, I go by the dwelling places of my beloved.</p>
<p>This is Majnun, right? He says, I go by these stones, the dwelling places where Laila lived, and I kiss this wall, and I kiss that wall. And he says, you know, it''s not the love of the walls that has enraptured my heart, you know, but the love of the one who used to live in these walls. This is why we go to these places. This is why we go to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِيَارَة</span>. It''s not places. It''s the Beloved. He was there. This is why we go there. Because He walked there. He slept there. He ate there. He prayed there. That''s why we go there. Our religion is a religion of love, and everything we do in our religion, it has to be out of love. I swear, everything, it should be out of love. I mean, I''m talking about the most idealistic situation, and I realize that, you know, but it''s good to know these things, right? It''s at least good to know what the goal is. But everything in our religion is supposed to be out of love, not out of fear. We make religion out of fear. That''s so sad, what we look, what we''ve turned Islam into. Look how religion is portrayed to us, how we portray our religion to others.</p>
<p>I mean, we should do everything in our religion out of love, not fear. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is worthy of fear, but even that fear of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, it should be rooted in love, right? It''s like the fear that a child has of upsetting or disappointing the parent, right? Even though the child is afraid, but that fear is rooted in love. They love the parents so much that they are afraid of upsetting them.</p>
<p>That should be our fear of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Should be rooted in love. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>, He is greater than anything that can be said about Him. We can''t praise you, as the Prophet said, we cannot praise you, you are as you praised yourself. So anything I say about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will be deficient. And, you know, we need to, we need to think about our lives. We all need to think about our life. This is our time, you know. This is our time, and what we do with this time is going to determine what happens when we leave this time.</p>
<p>Because we''re all going to depart from this world. So it is really important that I ask myself, you ask yourself, we all ask, how are we spending our time? What are we doing with our time? Are we doing useful, worthwhile things, beneficial things? Are we getting closer to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>? Are we coming any closer to knowing our Prophet any better? Because He is worth knowing. I swear, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللَّهِ</span>, He is worth knowing.</p>
<p>He is worth knowing beyond, above and beyond anything else that exists in this world. He is worth knowing. He is worth studying. The stories about Him are worth knowing. His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحَادِيث</span>, His family, His character, His etiquettes, they''re all worth knowing. His family members are worth knowing. Those who loved him are worth knowing. You know, who are there and what they went through, and that gives us sort of a compass, a direction of where we need to head. So, you know, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> guide us all. May He open up our hearts and put us all on this path of love. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ، وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ الْخَاتِمِ الْخَاتِمِ الْخَاتِمِ</span></p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'the stages of love';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ خَالِقِ الْوُجُودِ مِنَ الْعَدَمِ وَجَاعِلِ النُّورِ مِنَ الظُّلَمِ وَمُخْرِجِ الصَّبْرِ مِنَ الْأَلَمِ وَمُلْقِي التَّوْبَةِ عَلَى النَّدَمِ فَنَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى الْمَصَائِبِ كَمَا نَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى النِّعَمِ وَنُصَلِّي عَلَى رَسُولِهِ الْأَكْرَمِ لِلشَّرَفِ الْأَشَمِّ وَالنُّورِ الْأَتَمِّ وَالْكِتَابِ الْمُحْكَمِ وَكَمَالِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَالْخَاتَمِ سَيِّدِ وَلَدِ آدَمَ الَّذِي بَشَّرَ بِهِ عِيسَى ابْنُ مَرْيَمَ وَدَعَا لِبَعْثَتِهِ إِبْرَاهِيمُ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ حِينَ كَانَ يَرْفَعُ قَوَاعِدَ بَيْتِ اللَّهِ الْمُحَرَّمِ فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ وَعَلَى أَصْحَابِهِ خَيْرِ الْأُمَمِ الَّذِينَ بَارَكَ اللَّهُ بِهِمْ كَافَّةَ النَّاسِ الْعَرَبِ مِنْهُمْ وَالْعَجَمِ وَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَى أَمِيرِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ إِمَامِ الْمُتَّقِينَ وَقَائِدِ الْغُرِّ الْمُحَجَّلِينَ غَالِبٍ عَلَى كُلِّ غَالِبٍ مَطْلُوبِ كُلِّ طَالِبٍ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي أَنْزَلَ عَلَى عَبْدِهِ الْكِتَابَ وَلَمْ يَجْعَلْ لَهُ عِوَجًا الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًا وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ شَرِيكٌ فِي الْمُلْكِ وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ وَلِيٌّ مِنَ الذُّلِّ وَكَبِّرْهُ تَكْبِيرًا الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ، الَّذِي نَحْمَدُهُ وَنَسْتَعِينُهُ وَنَسْتَغْفِرُهُ وَنُؤْمِنُ بِهِ وَنَتَوَكَّلُ عَلَيْهِ، نَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنْ شُرُورِ أَنْفُسِنَا وَمِنْ سَيِّئَاتِ أَعْمَالِنَا، مَنْ يَهْدِهِ اللَّهُ فَلَا مُضِلَّ لَهُ وَمَنْ يُضْلِلْهُ فَلَا هَادِيَ لَهُ، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولُهُ، أَرْسَلَهُ اللَّهُ تَعَالَى بِالْهُدَى وَدِينِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ وَكَفَى بِاللَّهِ شَهِيدًا، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيَّ بْنَ أَبِي طَالِبٍ وَصِيُّهُ وَوَزِيرُهُ، فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَعَلَى آلِهِمْ وَسَلَّمَ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَا إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ، اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا، اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَعُ وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَأَمَّا بَعْدُ، يَقُولُ اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى فِي كِتَابِهِ الْكَرِيمِ بَعْدَ أَنْ قُلْنَا كُلَّنَا أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ، بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِيمِ، رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي، اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ بِلَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ، وَاللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ</span></p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span> In the previous session, we took an Islamic view of the terms friendship and love. So we looked at the different types of good and bad friends that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ks about, and we studied the various stages of love, in how it grows and develops from attachment to, you know, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l-inclusive form, which is hope. Hope is basic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly pure love, and it includes within it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l the previous stages of love.</p>
<p>So, you know, hope is sort of a union of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l these ten stages of love. And so in the light of these terms and definitions, you know, we''re going to move that conversation forward to understand the linguistic meanings of words like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَٰن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>. We <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l have some sort of an image of, you know, like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, we think it''s just a name. It''s like we have names, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has a name, and that name is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, right? But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, we <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l understand it to be, you know, sort of, we think of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l these different names as just one entity, basic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly names of the same entity. But actu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly it''s quite different, and each word actu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly gives us a window into understanding, you know, what that entity means.</p>
<p>From <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l these sessions that I''ve t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ked about, and you''ve looked at the power of the Arabic language, that''s one thing that it does which no other language can do, is that each linguistic meaning of the word, it gives you sort of a perspective of what that entity truly means. And that''s why we''re going to look at these different words linguistic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly, to get an idea of exactly what is expected to us, what should be our perspective, what should be our understanding when we use the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, what should be our understanding when we use the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>, what exactly are we t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>king about, right?</p>
<p>So that re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly gives us, you know, more power when we execute our various forms of worship. It sort of gives us a more clear understanding of what to do. And as we understand these words, you know, keep the stages of love in mind, and then that''ll help us determine how much <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> loves us. And this is another benefit of linguistic studies, that not only do we understand how we''re supposed to love Him, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>so how He loves us. Because the thing about love is that love inspires love, right? You have to receive love to be able to give it, and then you have to give love in order to receive it. And the more this give and take takes place, the more that love increases. So that''s how it works, right? Love engenders love.</p>
<p>So today we''re going to begin by looking at this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, it comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>. Okay, now gener<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly we use this term <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> to mean God, like I said, but in re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ity <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> is a very generic term in the Arabic language, like a book or a car. You can have many types of books, and you can have many types of cars, right? It''s not specific. Sim<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>rly, there can be many types of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>.</p>
<p>There are many types of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>. In the English language, we have two types of articles. We have the indefinite article, which is a book, a car, and then there is the definite article, which is the book, the car. Right? Now, what''s the difference between a book and the book? A book is a generic term, right? It could mean any kind of book, any book. But when I say the book, I''m no longer t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>king about just any book.</p>
<p>I''m now referring to a very specific book. Right? So it''s the exact same principle with the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> is a generic indefinite term that can be used for many things. There can be many types of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>, just as there can be many types of books and many types of cars. But when I apply the definite article the to it, what is that definite article in Arabic?</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/87/1b3198d2-bb3f-48d3-ac49-05f97e515554.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>It is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَلِفُ وَاللَّامُ</span>, right? When I apply that definite article to the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>, when I make it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> plus <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>, that combination generates the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. You understand? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is basic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلْإِلَه</span>. In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the specific form of the generic word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is actu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلْإِلَه</span>. And that''s why, based on the rules of Arabic grammar, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْ</span>if of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is dropped when it joins with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْ</span>, and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْ</span> merges with it to become <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Right? So basic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly that''s where <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> comes from. When you apply the definite article of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْ</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>, it becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, and this is why, in order to understand the linguistic meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, we must first understand the meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>. Right? Okay.</p>
<p>Now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span> is a composite word and it has a slew of meanings. So today the first thing that we need to understand is what I mean by a composite word. It''s re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly important to understand, you know, how composite words, these words, how they work in Arabic before we can proceed, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l of these terms that we''re going to t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>k about, like, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَٰن</span>, these are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l composite words. So once you understand how it works, then it''ll make it easy to understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l these different words.</p>
<p>So think of a composite word like a jigsaw puzzle, right? Every piece of the puzzle has a separate independent meaning. Okay, every piece of the puzzle has a separate independent meaning, but then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l these meanings come together to create a composite image of what that word means, just like each individu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> piece of a jigsaw puzzle has a certain image on it, but then when you join it together properly, the fin<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> image comes forth, right?</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/87/3187e204-def2-4bcb-bfbf-e9a3c6e4224c.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>And you can see the entire picture. It''s the same concept of a composite word. So let''s say if the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> has four different meanings and, you know, for example, it has four meanings A, B, C, and D, then the sum of those meanings will give us the complete understanding of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>. You understand? The meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> is not A or B or C or D.</p>
<p>It is rather the sum of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l those meanings. It''s A plus B plus C plus D. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l meanings of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> apply together at the same time. That''s how this works. That''s what a composite word is. In English, you know, one word can have multiple definitions and they can have their own context. Same thing in Arabic. Right? Each individu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> variation of the root word can have its own meanings, but then when you combine it together, it forms one single meaning, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span> this will become clear as we go <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ong.</p>
<p>Now let''s look at the different linguistic meanings of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>. The first and the primary meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> comes from the variation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَهَ يَأْلَهُ</span>. And the verb <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span>, it liter<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly means to worship. So the first meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span> is the entity that is the focus of our worship and obedience. That''s the first meaning. What is worship? It is an extreme form of gratitude.</p>
<p>We covered this in the previous sessions, that worship is the extreme form of gratitude. It is that highest form of that sense of gratitude where the benefactor becomes so desirable, so important in your life, that you are forced down on your knees in humility. So when you completely succumb and surrender your will to the will of the beloved, right, when that happens, that beloved becomes your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>. That is the first meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span>, of that variation <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span>. So for instance, if you are obsessed about your job, if you are obsessed about your career to the degree that you put it above and beyond everything and everyone else in your life, then you''ve made your career your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>.</p>
<p>You understand? If your video games, sports, TV shows, children, we<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>th, if these are the things that commands and rules your heart, right? If those are the things that make you bow down before them, then by definition, these things are your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>. If you are madly in love with someone to the point where that person controls and dominates your every thought, every action, every decision, then that beloved is now your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>.</p>
<p>Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> is the one you worship, the one you submit and obey to without question. So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> says jump and you say how high, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَاهَسْ</span>. So that''s the first meaning. I''m sorry, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَلَا</span>. Right? So what I just explained is the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَلَا</span>. Now let''s look at the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. So just to rephrase, because I think I used the wrong definition. Defining <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَعْلَى</span> and thinking of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَعْلَى</span> is the one who is the object of worship, is the one you liter<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly worship and who dominates your thoughts, right? So that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَعْلَى</span>. Sorry about that. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span> is another variation of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> and it is used for a child that is thirsty for the milk of its mother. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span>. Liter<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامَةٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span> is used for a child that is thirsty for the milk of its mother and is desperately crying for it.</p>
<p>Parents can understand what I''m t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>king about. Have you seen a baby crying for milk? And if you remember the desperation on that baby''s face, and what does the baby do when the mother offers it her breast? It turns to it with this hungry passion, this hungry thirst. It just latches on. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. It is the entity that you desperately and inevitably turn to with a passion, with a desperation.</p>
<p>It is what you desperately and uncontrollably turn to in your every single moment of happiness and grief. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span>. So, you know, for example, some people have re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly bad sugar addictions. Right? If there is a... I think I''m one of those. You know, you put a chocolate in front of me, that''s it. Right? Some are addicted to smoking, some to drugs, some to parties, some to movies.</p>
<p>So anytime you feel happy or depressed, the thing that you desperately and uncontrollably turn towards for comfort, by definition that becomes your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>. Because you find peace and comfort in the arms of these masters. And we become their obedient slaves. So when these masters command, we have to obey. When the desire commands certain people to smoke, they have to obey. So, when you can''t stop yourself from eating that last stub of ice cream or watching that episode of your TV show and you must watch a movie every night or you must play a video game for hours every day at the stake of no matter what, then you are completely and loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly devoted to these things.</p>
<p>Because you just feel that you absolutely have to do these things in order to feel happy and satisfied again. And so you turn to them in this form of desperation that a baby turns to its mother out of its thirst for milk. And, you know, this applies to each and every one of us in some way or the other. You know, we <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l have such <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span> in our lives.</p>
<p>It doesn''t make us horrible people, just makes us human, right? So, and what we''re doing today, you know, by learning these meanings, we''re in a sense waking up to see these masters for what they re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly are. And I think that''s the first step towards any change, right? It''s just waking up to see the re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ity, not be deluded. So don''t feel overwhelmed by these definitions because, you know, once I put it into the context of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l of this will make sense.</p>
<p>But for now, just understand that these are the linguistic meanings of these words. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَلَا</span> means that entity of worship, the one that you worship that you feel extreme gratitude for. And then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span> means is that emotion of a child turning, crying for the breast or the milk of its mother. So that desperation, that deep-seated loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ty, that''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span> comes from. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامَةٌ</span> is so beautiful because of this child, right?</p>
<p>When it''s crying for milk, there is nothing else that will satisfy the child. You cannot distract that child. You cannot sing to it. You cannot give it toys. You cannot do anything. That child is going to keep crying until it gets what he wants. And that is how we are in our lives. There are certain things in our lives we are so addicted to that nothing will satisfy us until we have that, whatever, we fulfill that desire. So that''s why I think the imagery that these words create is so beautiful, because it re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly answers every possible question of what the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span> means. You want to understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span>? Just look at a baby crying for milk. That''s it. And everything that that child or that baby is facing at that point is the meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span>. This is why I find this language so beautiful. Okay, so that was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span>.</p>
<p>Now let''s look at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَاهَا</span>, right? Another variation. This <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَاهَا</span> is someone you are passionately in love with, someone you cannot stop thinking about, someone you are obsessed about. Right? Now, this derivative <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَاهَا</span> is used when someone goes insane by the loss of a beloved. That''s the imagery. That''s the image in front of you. Someone who loses his mind by the loss of a beloved. The mother who loses her child is c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>led <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَلَّى</span>, derivative of the same root, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَلَّى</span>.</p>
<p>This is a mother who is suffering that extremely excruciating pain and grief over the loss of her child. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَلَّى</span>. It is that kind of passionate and obsessive love. In Urdu <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>so we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا هَنَا مَحَبَّتْ</span>, right? Mad love, crazy love. So it''s the same root, it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَاهَا</span>. So if there is someone in your life that you are obsessed about, that you are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ways thinking about, that you''re madly in love with, you know, being apart from this person drives you insane, you cannot stop texting, you cannot stop c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ling, you''re constantly looking at their pictures, you know, reading their emails, texts, whatever, missing them with a passion, then you are in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللَّهَ</span>, and by definition that person is your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>.</p>
<p>Okay, so that''s another meaning of this variation of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>. The next one is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهَا</span>, right? So there''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span> and there''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰهًا</span> is liter<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly — not linguistic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly, liter<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly — used for something that you lean on.</p>
<p>Something that you put your entire weight on. So for example, you know, when you''re exhausted and you lean on something and you put your weight on that thing, then that thing that is supporting you is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰهًا</span>. Right? So old people, they w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>k with a cane. That cane is c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>led <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰهًا</span>. So in your life, if you''re banking on something, you know, for example, you''re just, you''re banking on your finances, your property, your houses, your cars, your jobs, you''re depending, you know, you just completely depend on your soci<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> status, your friends, your neighbors, your children, then any of those things that you lean on are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>.</p>
<p>So if you''re putting <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l your eggs in one basket, metaphoric<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly speaking, then that basket, that thing, that person is your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهَا</span>. Another derivative of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالِهَا</span>. We saw <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَهَا</span>, now this is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالِهَا</span>, which means to become astounded, to become astonished, to become mesmerized. And you can see how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَهَا</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَهَا</span> are connected, strongly connected with each other, right? Because the one that you are completely astounded by is the one you will passionately f<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l in love with. So that, it''s amazing how these words are so interconnected. You cannot separate one from the other. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالِهَا</span> means to become astounded, become astonished.</p>
<p>So you know how kids create role models, and actors and musicians and these hip-hop artists, right? How kids today want to be like them, live like them, think like them, dress like them, t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>k like them, w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>k like them. So you know when this movie a few years ago with Aamir Khan, because most people here in this group today, in the session today, watch Indian movies, so you know when this movie Ghajini came out with Aamir Khan and he had that haircut with the line going across in the top, right? Do you know how many kids actu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly shaved their heads with the exact same style? So when a kid, you know, when someone feels that they have to go out and shave their head to look like Aamir Khan because that''s how he looked in the movie Ghajini, then they''ve made Aamir Khan their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>, because they are now astounded and astonished by him.</p>
<p>So by definition, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> is the one or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَهَا</span> is the one that you''re completely and utterly impressed by, the one you wish to imitate, the one you want to be like. Now that could be a person, it could be a thing, it could be a profession, it could be anything. Anything that mesmerizes you, grips you, takes complete control of your decisions is another derivative of the same root <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْ</span>right. Let''s see. So these are the various meanings of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> that we looked at. Now let''s go over them again. Just a quick review of who and what an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> is. The one we worship and obey is our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>. I''m just looking at the root word. I''m not looking at the variation, but they <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l tie in with the same root. So the one that we worship and obey, the one we are completely devoted and loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> to is our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>.</p>
<p>The one that we turn to in utter desperation to find comfort is our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>. The one we are passionately in love with, the one we obsess about is our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>. The one we lean on for support, physic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly, ment<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly, emotion<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly, psychologic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly, in whatever shape or form, that is our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>. The one we are astonished by, mesmerized by, the one we are utterly impressed by, we want to imitate, that''s our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/87/21ac5722-564f-4aed-beeb-4f61c600508f.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>Now if you combine <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l these definitions together, you get the meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> means the one that we worship. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> means the one that we are devoted and loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> to. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> is the one that we turn to for comfort. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> is the one we''re passionately in love with. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> is the one we use for our support, we turn to for support for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l our needs.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> is the one we are mesmerized by. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْ</span>l of this combines together. This is what I meant by a composite word. The variations have their own independent meanings, but when you join <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l these variations together, it gives you the complete picture of the root word. Okay? So this is what the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> means. And they''re <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l interconnected, like I said. Because the one you are passionately in love with is the person you will be completely devoted to.</p>
<p>That is the person that you will lean on. That is the person you will turn to for comfort and support with utter desperation. Right? Separation or distance from that person will drive you crazy. You will liter<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly submit to obey, you know, you will obey every single thing they want, every single desire they have, you will fulfill. In other words, you will worship that person. So you can see how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l these meanings, they merge together to create a complete picture.</p>
<p>So that is how a composite word works. So now based on these meanings of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>, you can see that we <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l have numerous <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>, right? We personify and objectify these definitions in some way or the other. Some have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> in the form of people, family, friends, children, parents, actors, musicians. Some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span> exist in the form of objects, houses, cars, bodies, jewelry, food, video games, movies, right?</p>
<p>And then some have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span> in the form of jobs, careers, recreation, drugs, sex, whatever. So we <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l have hundreds if not thousands of entities in our lives that meet the definition of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>. I hope everybody can see this. Right? Now, here is something that I want you to think about and this is where the conversation gets re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly interesting and a bit scary. I said that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l these people and things in our lives that meet the definition of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span> is basic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly what they''re doing is that they give a physic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> form to one of the definitions of the word, right?</p>
<p>So for example by definition <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> is the one we are loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> and devoted to. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> is the one we turn to for comfort. And so now if smoking or movies or video games is what I turn to for comfort, then in a sense, in that sense, in that context, these activities are taking on a physic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> form of that definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>. You see that? It was origin<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly the definition is a concept and then we give it a physic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> form.</p>
<p>In the form of, you know, smoking or movies or whatever. So for example, you know, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the one that astounds and astonishes you, you know, if Shah Rukh Khan or Sylvester St<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>lone or, I don''t know, Lady Gaga, these are the ones who astound you, impress you to the point that you want to be like them, then, you know, you''ve given the definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> a form. You''ve personified that definition in the form of Shah Rukh Khan or St<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>lone or whoever.</p>
<p>You understand? Now here is my question. What is the definition of an idol? You know, we t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>k about idol worship, right? What is an idol? An idol is an image or representation of a god used as an object of worship. That''s the standard definition of an idol. It is the personification or the objectification of someone or something, isn''t it? Who creates idols? We create idols. Idols are man-made creations, aren''t they?</p>
<p>Man creates idols, and who objectifies and personifies the definitions of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>? We do. We decide that we are going to turn to smoking, movies, drugs, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>cohol for comfort. We decide that we are going to be completely loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> and devoted to our jobs, our careers, to Facebook, to Instagram. We choose to f<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l passionately in love with we<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>th, power, fame, soci<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> status. Nobody does that for us. We do that.</p>
<p>In other words, we are the creators of our own <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>, just as idols are man-made creations. Do you see the sim<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>rity there? You see where I''m going with this? Because gener<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly we think we are Muslims, right? And therefore we have nothing to do with idol worship. But the scary re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ity is that we are liter<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly surrounded by thousands of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> which are self-created idols, and we worship them loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly on a daily basis.</p>
<p>Every person, every object, every concept, every idea that we personify or objectify, in a sense, is an idol that we made for ourselves with our own hands. So in that sense, we are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l idol worshippers, right? It''s a painful re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ity, but it''s true. So if you re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly look, understand the word, the meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>, then the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>, it doesn''t seem so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ien now, does it?</p>
<p>This is why there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> in which he says — let me see if I can share this with you — he says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشِّرْكُ فِي أُمَّتِي أَخْفَى مِنْ سَيْرِ النَّمْلِ فِي لَيْلَةٍ ظُلْمَةٍ عَلَى صَخْرَةٍ سَوْدَاءَ</span>. That <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>, idol worship, or associating partners with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is more hidden in my nation than the movement of a black ant in a dark night on a black stone. It''s that subtle.</p>
<p>This is how unaware we are of our own idol worship. Now in the fin<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> an<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ysis, who is at the center of my idols? I am. Now why did I create these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>? Why did I create these idols? What motivated me to build my idols? My desires, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْ</span>l my idols, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l these idols that I have created, are basic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly a product and a creation of my own passions and my own caprice.</p>
<p>My idols exist because of my wants. Now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, &quot;Have you seen those who take their desire as their god,&quot; as their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>? The one who takes his own desires as his god. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is basic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly asking the messenger, do you not see these people who take their desires, you know, their cars, their houses, their jobs, their we<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>th, their fame, their pleasures as their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>?</p>
<p>Do you not see this person who has taken smoking, drugs, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>cohol, movies, video games as their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>? Who has taken, you know, Shah Rukh Khan, S<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>man Khan, Adele, St<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>lone as their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span>, and then he says... Do you re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly want to be responsible for these people on the Day of Judgment? Because that is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> wanted, right? He wanted to convert as many people as he wanted, these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفَّار</span>, these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُشْرِكِينَ</span>, these rejecters, disbelievers, hypocrites, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l of them.</p>
<p>He wanted to get everybody into the fold of Islam. And so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is actu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly asking him, don''t you see these people, how they''ve taken their desires as their god? They''re not going to accept me as their God. They''ve taken their own... their desires are their God. And he''s asking, do you want to be responsible for these people on the Day of Judgment? May <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> protect us from being amongst those.</p>
<p>So worship of the self is the worst form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> that there is. When gratitude turns into self-gratification, that is the worst kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>. Idolatry has nothing over selfolatry, if that is a word. And so we are constantly committing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> by taking our desires and making them our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>. So human beings are in a constant state of self worship, which is exactly what prevents them from truly worshiping <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>But that is the default state of mankind, and here is where Islam comes to the rescue. By the negation — listen to this carefully — by the negation and the destruction of these self-created idols emerges the understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. By the negation and the destruction of these self-created idols comes the understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Who is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>? He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. You see? He is everything other than what you take for your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>.</p>
<p>If we break and destroy each and every idol that generates from the desires of ourselves, when the Kaaba of our hearts is empty of these idols, what we will find left is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. What is the first thing that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> did after the conquest of Mecca? And here, re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly, if you understand it, we think that these are just random events, right? But just look at the — forget the history of Islam from the way it occurred.</p>
<p>Look at it from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s strategy. Just think about it in the sense that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> planned it this way. There''s going to be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفَّار</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُشْرِكِينَ</span>, Mecca is going to be surrounded by these people, dominated by these people. My messenger is gonna come, he''s gonna spread the message of Islam, it''s gonna take a long time for it to come through, 23 years of hard work, but then he is going to conquer it and Islam is going to prevail and it''s just gonna, you know, flood the entire city. So think about it as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s strategy, right? And now look at this event of what happened in the conquest of Makkah.</p>
<p>What is the first thing that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> did after the conquest of Mecca? He cleansed the Kaaba of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l idols. That''s the first thing he did. There were 365 idols in and around and on the top of Kaaba, and he destroyed each and every one of them one by one. He had so many people, they could have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l worked together to break <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l the idols together. No, but he uses <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِي</span> as his sword to break each and every —</p>
<p>He doesn''t break it himself. He makes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِي</span>. He liter<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly uses <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِي</span> as his hand, as a sword, to break each and every idol, one by one. And this is the exact same exercise that we <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l have to go through in our lives. You know, just as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span>, just as he conquered Mecca, we have to conquer our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>. And then just as he cleansed the Kaaba of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l idols, one by one, gradu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly, we have to gradu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly cleanse our Kaaba, our hearts, of these idols, one by one.</p>
<p>And the greatest and the most powerful idol that stands in the Kaaba of our hearts is our own <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>, our own passions. And this is why I think our creed<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> formula is so incredibly amazing, because it attacks the very root of this problem. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. There is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> except <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. There is no one, no person, no thing, no concept, no principle, no ideology worthy of giving form to except the one who is free of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l form, and that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. And so by the negation of our self-made idols, by the elimination of our self-created <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>, emerges the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>. What happens when you add the definite article to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>? You get the ultimate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>So our creed<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> formula of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span> is the idea of negating the multiplicity of our self-created <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> to affirm the one true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span>, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. It is the exercise and the practice of making <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلَه</span> by taking away the form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span> and applying it to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. And this exercise, this practice of seeing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> as the one and only, the ultimate target of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l these definitions of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>, by making him, eliminating these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span> will make him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلَه</span>, it will make him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>So in essence, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span> is the exercise of isolating the various definitions of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> that we learnt about and making them specific to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Right? So this means that now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only one I worship and obey. Right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span> now means, in a sense, I can say <span class="' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'linguistic meaning of allah';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا يَعْلَى إِلَّا اللَّهُ</span>. I will not worship anyone but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. If any other <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> conflicts with the commands of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, then I will disobey that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> to obey the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْإِلَٰه</span>, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>This does not mean that I cannot enjoy life, right? The difference is now that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is at the front and center of my worship and obedience. And as long as nothing else takes the form of worship for me, it does not conflict with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s worship and obedience. I can enjoy my life. I can do whatever I want. There''s so much I can do, I can enjoy to the fullest degree.</p>
<p>Right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span> now means that, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is going to be the only one that I will be completely devoted and loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> to. Above and everyone else in my life. Even my own parents, my own children. You know, I will only serve <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and no one else. But then I''m happy to serve my parents because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has commanded me to do that. But I''m serving them because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has commanded me.</p>
<p>I''m taking care of my children because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has commanded me. You see? You see how when we change our thinking like that, how we apply the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْ</span> to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>. Parents can be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>, children can be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>, but when I actu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly do it because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has commanded me to do so, now I have destroyed them and made... I now have given a specific form <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلَه</span>, and I''m now doing it because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has commanded me to do so.</p>
<p>And so now I''m happy to hang out with my friends, I''m happy to go to parties, I can watch movies, I can do whatever I want, as long as my ultimate loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ty and devotion lies with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. If anything or anyone ch<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>lenges that loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ty, then I must choose <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> over them. If there are certain kinds of parties that, if I go to it, would be in disobedience of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, then I should not go to them.</p>
<p>If there are certain kind of movies that I should not watch because they will conflict with what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> wants, then I should not watch. But other than that, there are so many other movies, so many other parties that I can go to. Right? So you understand the difference? This is how we make him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلَٰه</span>. That yeah, I am willing to be loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> to the various <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> in my life, but not at the risk of upsetting the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلَٰه</span>.</p>
<p>That is what the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is. So you can see how you cannot understand what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> means until you understand what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهٌ</span> means. You see that? So now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span> means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. That <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only one I will turn to in utter desperation for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l my needs and comforts. This does not mean that I cannot ask help from my family and friends.</p>
<p>This does not mean that if I''m hungry and out of a job, I should sit on my **** and not do anything because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the one who''s supposed to feed me. No, absolutely not. I will turn to whoever I need to for whatever I need. The difference is that in the back of my mind I will remember that these people are simply a means to an end.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the one providing for me, and he is using these people as the means, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْبَاب</span>, to get the job done. And therefore my ultimate ask is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ways going to be of him. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا اللهُ</span>, I need this, I turn to you for the fulfillment of my needs. Provide me with the means and resources that will fulfill my needs in the way that pleases you, because it is you and only you who is the source of my provision, nobody else. But then if I have this in the back of my mind, I can turn to Ayaz and say, hey, I''m out of a job, is your company hiring? Can you help? Can you put in a good word for me? Can you give my resume to your HR department? But not with the understanding that Ayaz is the one who''s giving me the job or helping me, but understanding that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the one who will eventu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly help me. But I can use, you know, I can turn to, I ask for help, there''s nothing wrong with that.</p>
<p>So understand the difference? Because in this case, yes, Ayaaz is becoming my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> because I''m turning to him with a need, but he''s not my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the one who is in the back of my mind, that I see him as the specific one who''s going to provide my provision, and Ayaaz is just going to be the means for it. So I hope you can understand the difference.</p>
<p>I hope I''m being clear enough, because this is a very difficult concept, and this is why you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l need to re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly reflect on this for yourself, because it''ll re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly help you bring out the understanding of these meanings. So now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ الْ</span>so means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the one I am passionately in love with. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the one I''m completely obsessed about. From the moment I wake up to the moment I go to sleep, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the one that I think about.</p>
<p>This does not mean that I have to sit on my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَمَس الْ</span>l day. Or, you know, I spend my entire day doing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَذْبِيح</span>. No. I can do whatever I want. But everything reminds me of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> now. Everything in the horizons and my soul becomes a sign for me, pointing me towards <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, reminding me of Him. Isn''t that what love does? You look at the flower, you see the beloved.</p>
<p>You look, you know, in life. If you''re in love with another person, everything reminds you of that person. The flower, the rainbow, the sunset, the frog in the pond, right? They <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l remind you of your beloved. Love is an obsession. It is a constant connection with the beloved. So now what happens if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is at the back of my mind, even my beloved reminds me of the blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>That <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the one who put this love in my life. You know the story of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَضْرَتْ أَيُّوب</span>? He was put through these great tri<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>s to test his faith. So according to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثُ رَسُولِ اللَّهِ</span>, once <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> mentioned the patience and gratitude of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَضْرَتْ أَيُّوب</span> to his angels. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْلِيس</span> came and he spoke to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and said that the only reason this man is grateful to you is because you have blessed him with so much.</p>
<p>This is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> said. He put this question right before the angels and he said that. That you have given him so much, that is the only reason he is grateful. If you hadn''t given him that, he would no longer be grateful. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> started taking things away from him. He took his possessions away first, right? Then his house, it collapsed right in front of his eyes, killing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l his sons.</p>
<p>Can you imagine that? Just think of that. Standing in, you know... God forbid. A person who is standing in front of his house and he watches his children are sleeping in that house, he watches the entire structure collapse on his children, killing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l of them, what would he go through? Do you know what came out of his mouth, of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَضْرَتْ أَيُّوبَ</span> mouth, when he saw that? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>That is what he said. That is his awareness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, that is his awareness of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is, and that is how he''s made, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span> specific in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. And if that wasn''t enough, he got this horrible disease where his body got infected with worms that would eat his flesh. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> put <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَضْرَتْ أَيُّوب</span> through this extreme form of tri<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>s to test his faith and love for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>And he succeeded with flying colors. And this was not just to prove <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> wrong, it was to prove... You see, you can look at this two ways. How can <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> be so cruel? If re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَضْرَتْ أَيُّوب</span> loved <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> so much, why would He put him through this? So did he re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly need to prove <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> wrong that desperately? No. But by putting Ayyub through this test and seeing him...</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> knows what''s going to happen. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> knows that Ayyub is truly, you know, truly faithful to him. But by putting him through this test, he just set a precedent for the entire world. And then when he completed, when he succeeded in this test, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> took it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l away. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> asked him to bathe in the spring water that he set out for him, so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَضْرَتْ أَيُّوب</span> took a bath in it and he was completely cured. And what''s interesting is that years later someone asked him, you know, how is your he<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>th now, Ayyub? And you know what he did? He sighed and he said, you know, the days of my illness were better than my days today.</p>
<p>The days of my illness were more beloved to me than my days of he<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>th today.&quot; And that man was shocked. He said, &quot;How come, Ayyub?&quot; And he replied that when I was ill, every single day <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> would ask me once, &quot;How are you doing today, Ayyub?&quot; And now that I''ve been cured, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> no longer asks me that. So I miss that love and care that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> showered upon me during my days of illness.</p>
<p>That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِهَا</span>. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَلَّى</span>. He doesn''t care about his own pain and suffering. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْ</span>l he cares about is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is no longer t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>king to me and asking me how I''m doing. I''m not feeling that love from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. That is extreme passionate love, in which a person is driven to insanity in the absence of his beloved. Right? So now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span> has become <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا وَلَهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>Then you can keep applying this to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l definitions. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span> now means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. That <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is now the only one I will lean on for support emotion<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly, psychologic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly, financi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly, physic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly, in every way, shape or form. This doesn''t mean that I''m not going to go see a doctor if I''m sick. It doesn''t mean that I''m not going to ask my boss for a pay raise if I deserve it.</p>
<p>Doesn''t mean I''m not going to ask my family and friends for help if I need it. I will continue to do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l of those things. But my ultimate support is going to be from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. He is the one that I will ultimately lean on. He is the one who will cure me of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l my illness, not the doctor. He is the one who will increase me in my provision with my pay raise, not my boss.</p>
<p>He is the one who will fulfill <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l my needs, not my friends and family. Can you just imagine how liberating that would be? How many of our complaints with people and life would just disappear? We would no longer have to blame someone that we trusted for disappointing us, because he was not meant to be. I never leaned on that person for support to begin with. Therefore, there is nothing he can do to disappoint me.</p>
<p>You see, you cannot have a disappointment without expectation, right? And if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l my expectations are from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, then nobody in this world can disappoint me. Not even <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, and we learn this in much more detail in the session on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, that everything He does is for my benefit, whether He takes from me or He gives to me. Because He loves me. So if I got what I wanted, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> was the one who gave it to me.</p>
<p>If I did not get what I wanted, it was not meant for me. Who knows if getting what I wanted would end up being the worst thing for me in the long run. I mean, we <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l say, right, be careful what you wish for. Because sometimes we desire something so bad and that turns out to be the worst thing in our life. Right? So that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهَا</span>.</p>
<p>Now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span> fin<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا وَلِيَهَ إِلَّا اللَّه</span>. That <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only one that astounds and impresses me. This does not mean that I should not appreciate anyone in my life. I should not praise anyone or not feel impressed by anyone in my life. No. What this means is that I must see <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> behind the praise. If my child does re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly well in school, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, praise for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> that He made my child intelligent.</p>
<p>There are many children who aren''t. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> made my child intelligent. If I''m mesmerized by the sunrise and the sunset, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, praises for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> for creating these beautiful things for me and giving me the eyesight, the time and the willingness to appreciate it. You know how many people in this world are so burdened by their problems that they, you know, the last thing they will think of is going and appreciating sunrises?</p>
<p>That''s a gift from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> to me. And what I''m doing is I''m taking the praise for the beauty of the sunset and attributing it to the creator of that beauty. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا وَلِهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ</span> is that nothing will impress me except that which impresses <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Who impressed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> the most? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> I have the example of what impresses <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. And so if I want to impress Him, what should I do?</p>
<p>Try to become like Him. Isn''t that true? Right? I mean, why would I try to imitate Aamir Khan and Shah Rukh Khan and dress like them and t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>k like them? Why? Because the idea behind that is impressing the world. What is that going to get me in the end? The same <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, the same people when I die, what are they going to do? They''re going to throw me in a ditch and throw dirt in my face, right?</p>
<p>So what am I working so hard to impress them for? Shouldn''t I just impress the one where it actu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly matters? And we know exactly who impressed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. It was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span>, it was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِي</span>, it was... This is why we study <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِيرَة</span>. We don''t just study <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِيرَة</span> from a historic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> point of understanding how the events of history took place. No. We study history to study their character, to understand how these people live so that we could emulate and try to be like them, because the more we are in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامَةٌ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> the more we will be loved by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>So what happens through this exercise is that we get to the true concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>. The concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> is not that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the ultimate deity in a hierarchy of deity. The concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> is not that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the ultimate God in a hierarchy of gods. No. The concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> is that there is no other deity. There is a complete elimination of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l other deities.</p>
<p>Except the one and only deity, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. It is the transference of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهِيَ</span> from its generic form to one specific being that has no form. That is the ch<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>lenge and the go<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. Right? And how do we do this? Slowly, gradu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly, one by one, one idol at a time. Just with the sword of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span> we keep striking at the idol. We''re not going to break every idol in one strike.</p>
<p>It might take a lot of strikes to quit a habit. It might take a lot of effort to reject the desire. We will fail. You know, every time you strike an idol and it doesn''t break, that''s a failure. Every time you try to leave a habit and you fail, that is just one strike. Try again, try again, try again. Our problem is... and this applies to me.</p>
<p>I can see me t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>king to myself right here. Like I give up, you know, I try, I try, and then when I fail, it''s like, oh, you know what? It''s not gonna happen. Why? Just keep trying. So what? Eventu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ly it''ll happen. So, you know, when people learn about these ideas, one of the reactions is, you know, oh my God, you know, I cannot do this.</p>
<p>Why not? You know, and that is the wrong concept, because the conquest of Makkah should be in the front of our eyes when we''re trying to de<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> with the situation. Look at how the Kaaba was not cleansed in one strike. It took a lot of effort. So it''s gonna take a lot of effort on our part. But the thing is, when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is at the front and center of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>l of these efforts, it becomes so easy.</p>
<p>Because there''s no grudge. There''s no, oh my God, why do I have to do this? You know, this is terrible. Because the idea is now that I''m doing this to please <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, I''m doing it happily. I want to do it, and that makes it so much easier. The more you want, the more power it gives in your strength. Right? And the most easiest way of doing this, the first step of doing this, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاة</span>.</p>
<p>Our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاة</span> has the concept of breaking idols within its very nature, because look at the times of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاة</span>, the most busiest times that we <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ways have: you know, waking up from our deep sleep, right smack in the middle of the day when we''re busy with work, right at the time when we are leaving for work, came home tired, sunset, you know, that''s when we have to do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْرِب</span>, and then now we''re sleepy and tired after a long day of work.</p>
<p>We''ve eaten so much that I can barely get off the chair and now I have to do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشَاء</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاة</span> has been placed at this, you know, right at the center of the most uncomfortable times, and in order to do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاة</span> every single time you have to break an idol. Because as soon as it''s time for prayer, the first thing you will have to do every single time is stop doing whatever you''re doing.</p>
<p>So now if I am gluttonous, if I eat a lot, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشَاء</span> will stop me from eating a lot because I know I have to do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشَاء</span>. So I will not let that food make me so heavy and feeling so, you know, full that I am not able to do my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاة</span>. So this is the beauty of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاة</span>. It forces you to leave whoever you''re with and turn your face towards <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاة</span> is the very concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span> in action. You want to see <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span> in action in your life? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاة</span>. Because that is exactly what you''re doing, breaking idols. And so this is the journey of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. It''s a difficult journey, and every journey requires provisions, right? That help you get through the journey. And the provision for this journey is the knowledge of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, which is what we''re doing in these sessions.</p>
<p>The more knowledge we have of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, the more we will be amazed by His glory. The more amazed and astonished we are, the more passionate we will become in our love for Him. The more passionately we love Him, the more we will be obsessed about Him. The more we''re obsessed about Him, the more we will worship Him and submit to His commands.</p>
<p>The more we worship Him, the more we will turn to Him seeking His refuge. The more we find ourselves under His powerful shelter, the more we will lean on Him, the more we will put our weight on Him for support. Because in the fin<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span> an<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ysis, love engenders love. The more we re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْ</span>ize how much He loves us, the more we will love Him back. The more we love Him back, the more intense He will get in His blessings and mercy. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذْ تَأَذَّنَ رَبُّكُمْ لَىِٕن شَكَرْتُمْ لَأَزِيدَنَّكُمْ</span>. And your master has said that if you are grateful, I will give you more. I will increase in my blessings. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ، وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَأَوْصِيَائِهِ لَا سِيَّمَا ابْنِ أَبِيهِ الطَّاهِرِ وَصِهْرِهِ مَوْلَانَا الْإِمَامِ، ذُرِّيَّتِهِ الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ، صَلَوَاتُ اللَّهِ وَسَلَامُهُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>
