<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/87/6c9d2189-31d6-4688-b40f-1861bf91cdfe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'linguistic meaning of allah';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِيمِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ صَانِعِ الْخَلْقِ عَلَى أَكْمَلِ نِظَامٍ وَأَحْسَنِ قِوَامٍ الْوَادِي عَلَى أَشْرَفِ أَجْدَرِ الْمَقَامِ وَأَرْفَقِ مَسَامٍ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى الْمُؤَيَّدِ أَبْلَغِ الْكَلَامِ مُحَمَّدٍ الْمَبْعُوثِ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ لِهِدَايَتِكَ فَاتِحِ الْأَنَامِ إِلَى سُبُلِ السَّلَامِ وَإِلَى مَا دَلَّ يَالِي وَلَا يَمِّ وَالْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًا وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ شَرِيكٌ فِي الْمُلْكِ وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ وَلِيٌّ مِنَ الذُّلِّ وَكَبِّرْهُ تَكْبِيرًا وَالْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي أَنْزَلَ عَلَى عَبْدِهِ الْكِتَابَ وَلَمْ يَجْعَلْ لَهُ عِوَجًا الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي نَحْمَدُهُ وَنَسْتَعِينُهُ وَنَسْتَغْفِرُهُ وَنُؤْمِنُ بِهِ وَنَتَوَكَّلُ عَلَيْهِ وَنَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنْ شُرُورِ أَنْفُسِنَا وَمِنْ سَيِّئَاتِ أَعْمَالِنَا مَنْ يَهْدِهِ اللَّهُ فَلَا مُضِلَّ لَهُ وَمَنْ يُضْلِلْهُ فَلَا هَادِيَ لَهُ وَنَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولُهُ أَرْسَلَهُ اللَّهُ تَعَالَى بِالْهُدَى وَدِينِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ وَكَفَى بِاللَّهِ شَهِيدًا وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيَّ بْنَ أَبِي طَالِبٍ وَصِيُّهُ وَوَزِيرُهُ فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَعَلَى آلِهِمْ وَسَلَّمَ عَلَيْهِمْ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرًا اللَّهُمَّ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَا إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَعُ وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ</span> Faamma baadi akkullu <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى في كِتابِهِ الكَريمِ</span> baada akkulla <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ بِلَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَاللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span></p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>. In the last session, I t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ked about the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> and its various flavors and meanings. We learned that when the definite article the or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> in Arabic, when it gets applied to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span>, it becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلٰه</span>. And both of those words then fuse together to form <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l the various meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> then become exclusive for the one that we c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. That''s what the Al does. So now, if I take the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> as a powerful entity that demands submission, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> becomes the one, the only one for me.</p>
<p>Right? He becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلَٰه</span> for me. The only one I deem worthy of my submission. He becomes the only one I must be completely loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> to. Because there is no one more powerful than Him. And it is He who created the heavens and the earth in re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ity. And the day He says be, it becomes. His word is the ultimate truth and He has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l dominion on the day the trumpet will be blown. He is the knower of the unseen as well as the seen. And He is wise and He knows everything. And then the same formula applies to the rest of the meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰه</span>. If we take the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰه</span> as seeking protection and refuge, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only one I exclusively turn to for protection and refuge in desperate times.</p>
<p>If I take <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> in the meaning of love, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> becomes the only one I choose to be passionately in love with. He is the only entity, the only deity that I choose to be completely obsessed about. For <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l the infinite favors that He bestows upon me, knowing well that I am an extremely ungrateful and unworthy creature, He does not hold back in bestowing His favors upon me. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآتَىٰكُم مِّن كُلِّ مَا سَأَلْتُمُوهُ ۚ وَإِن تَعُدُّوا۟ نِعْمَتَ ٱللَّهِ لَا تُحْصُوهَآ ۗ إِنَّ ٱلْإِنسَنَ لَظَلُومٌۭ كَفَّارٌۭ</span> ‎. And He gave you of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l that you asked for. And if you try to count the blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, you will never be able to count them. Indeed, no doubt about it, man is an extreme wrongdoer, a disbeliever, an ungrateful being.</p>
<p>Now, if I take the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> in the sense of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللَّهَ</span>, of astonishment, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> becomes the only one whose glory and whose magnificence astonishes me. I re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ize that even when I set out to understand Him, when I venture to recognize Him, I do so with the re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ization that His loftiness will exhaust my limited capabilities of comprehension and understanding. I will be dumbfounded every single time I attempt to approach Him.</p>
<p>Because there is just nothing that is comparable to Him. And there are no words that can express His glory. If the sea were to become ink, in order to write the words of my master, indeed the sea would be exhausted before the words of my master would be finished. Even if he brought something similar to the oceans to assist, the words of my master cannot be captured. The praise of my master cannot be captured. So that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْحَمْدُ</span>.</p>
<p>Fin<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly, if I take <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> in the meaning of the one that I lean on for support, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> becomes the only one that I choose to lean on. He becomes the only one I choose to put my entire weight of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l my spiritu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> burden on for support. Because His support is the only support that matters. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن يَنصُرْكُمُ ٱللَّهُ فَلَا غَالِبَ لَكُمْ</span>. If <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> helps you, no one can overcome you. And if He forsakes you, then who is there after Him that can help you? And in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, let <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>one the believers put their trust.</p>
<p>So, in other words, there are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l these different meanings, these various rivers of knowledge that flow out of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span>, and they <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l then become exclusive. They <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l converge into one entity when it reaches the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلَٰه</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. But the re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> question is, what do we get out of the union of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l these meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span>? So if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only powerful entity deserving submission, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only one capable of providing refuge and protection, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only one worthy of passionate love and obsession for my thoughts, and if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only one who astonishes me, astounds me, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only one I choose to lean and depend on, then the union of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l these meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> with its exclusivity of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, making Him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, what does the union of these meanings produce for me? What does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l of this mean to me? What does this make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> to me? They make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> worthy of my worship. They make Him worthy of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>.</p>
<p>This is why He created me. And I did not create jinn or the humans except that they should worship me. And this is why He says that. Because the sum of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l these meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span>, they make Him worthy of my worship. This is why every single day, in every single <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَمَاز</span>, I say, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎. It is only you that we worship, and it is only you that we seek help from. This concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلٰه</span>, what it leads us to is the idea of worship, the idea of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. And the one we worship is c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>led <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْبُود</span>. We are the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْبُود</span>.</p>
<p>Now, what is worship? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is commonly translated as worship, right? But linguistic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly speaking,</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/154/60534835-e6af-4fc4-8820-31a58bca68fb.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> actu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly means a combination of two things. It means to worship and it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>so means to obey, both at the same time. They''re not interchangeable like many other words in the English language. No. Both meanings of worship and obedience are embedded inside this one word of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> in the Arabic language, in classic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> Arabic. Together, they both mean <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. You cannot take one meaning out of this word. It is worship and it is obedience.</p>
<p>But gener<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly, when we think of the word worship, what comes to our mind? The five prayers, fasting, paying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>, performing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحَجّ</span>, right? These are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l different forms and the ritu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>s of worship.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/152/5e2c8227-d756-43cf-9baa-783172ee5603.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>So it is worship. But these are the ritu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>s of worship. But what happens is that we think that''s it, and we stop at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l of those activities. And that''s the point that I want to highlight today. The problem with this translation of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> exclusively in the meaning of worship is that it does not necessarily include the concept of obedience. Especi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly an obedience that stems out of love. That is critic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> to understand. That is extremely important to understand. Worship is a love-based obedience. Because the word obedience, it can have a very negative connotation to it, right? Because people can be forced into obedience. Children are often bullied into obedience by the use of either threats or violence.</p>
<p>So it is possible for obedience to have a very negative emotion attached to it. But this is not the kind of obedience that we''re t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>king about here when we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is an obedience that comes natur<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly from the appreciation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l these various meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> that we learned. When we choose to love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> passionately, when we choose to become obsessed about Him, when we are completely devoted and loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> to Him, when we completely trust Him and lean upon Him, When we''re tot<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly impressed and amazed by His loftiness, His greatness, His magnificence, when we know that we can <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ways uncondition<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly turn to Him for protection and refuge, then the natur<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> outcome of the sum of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l of these love-based emotions is obedience. So remember that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is an obedience that stems out of love. It is a lover''s obedience to make the beloved happy. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> created us. And this is why He gave us this ability to love. He did not create us and desire disobedience from us. He <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>lows disobedience. His magnificence enables this capability of disobedience within His creation because disobedience is a product of free will.</p>
<p>which is c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>led <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِذْلَان</span>. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is not content with it, because disobedience is completely contrary to the concept of love. You know, there''s a poet by the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محمُودٌ الوَرَّاقُ</span>, and in one of his poems he says, You disobey <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and then you feign the love of God? This is an impossible and unknown kind of logic. Had your love been sincere, you would have been obedient. Because a lover is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ways obedient to the one he loves. So, it is the nature of love that we want to obey the one that we love. In another one of his poems, he says, . You know, a woman once asked someone about the state of this person who loves her with this burning passion. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَاشِق</span> is the one who is burning up with passion. Right?</p>
<p>They say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَوَاءُ الْعِشْقِ الِاتِّصَالُ</span>. The only cure for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> is to be connected with the beloved, to be in the embrace of the beloved. So, she''s t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>king about this man, she''s asking about this man who loves her, and she''s inquiring about his love for her. And this is something that lovers do, right? They want to know, they inquire from others about how much their beloved loves them. So, she asks this man, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لِلَّهِ صِفْهُ، وَلَا تَنْقُصْ وَلَا تَزِدِ</span>. For <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> sake, describe him to me and don''t stint in your description and don''t exaggerate. In other words, you know, I want to know the truth. I want to hear the truth. So, this man, he says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَوْ كَانَ رَحْلِيلُ الْمَوْتِ بِالدُّمَاءِ</span>. If your lover thought that he would die from thirst, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَقُتِيَ قِيفَانَ وُرُودَ الْمَاءِ لَمْ يَرِدِ</span>. And if you said don''t touch that water, he wouldn''t touch it.</p>
<p>So her lover knows that he is going to die of thirst, but if the woman said don''t touch that water, he wouldn''t touch it. He''d rather die than disobey. So the point of that, what he''s emphasizing there is that the nature of love is obedience. So the more you love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, the more you will want to obey Him. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has these two concepts embedded within it: worship and obedience. Both of which stem out of this passionate love for Him and not from some kind of forced compulsion or coercion. And to put this in practic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> terms, to give you an example, let''s say, you know, we are about to pray in 15 minutes, right? Let''s say it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> time. And so we have to pray. We have to do our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. We have to go through the ritu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> of prayer.</p>
<p>Now, what kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is prayer, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>? Is it worship or is it obedience? It''s worship, right? But then from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر الْفَاتِحَة</span>l the way up till <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظُهْر</span>, we will not be worshipping him through this ritu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> of prayer, but if we are sincere in our love for him, we will be doing what? We will be obeying him. We will be in a state of obedience with him. Because it is the dream and the desire of every lover to make their beloved happy. And so the beauty of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is that it covers the entire spectrum of love, putting us in a constant state of worship. You see, when we are performing the ritu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> acts of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, we are in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. But then when we are in between two prayers, by being in a state of obedience, we are still in the state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. You see, this is why</p>
<p>The concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is so comprehensive in Islam. We are constantly in a state of worship, if not through the form<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> ritu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>s, then through obedience. So what this means is that if you go in the morning and earn a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span> income through your job, that is an act of obedience, that is an act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. When you go to school and you sincerely accomplish the purpose of education, you''re in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, you''re worshiping. When you love and respect your elders, when you are kind to your siblings, when you''re generous to the needy, when you''re courageous in the face of temptations, no matter what you do, as long as you''re in a state of obedience and servitude to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, anything and everything that you do becomes an act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. It becomes worship. It turns into worship.</p>
<p>Going to the toilet becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. Imagine that. When you go to the toilet to relieve yourself, if you obey the commands of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> to cover your head and feet and seek His protection and recite <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l those <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءٌ</span>, you''re in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. So you can see that this concept of worship, of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, is re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly incredible in Islam. And may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> give us <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l the strength and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> to become His obedient slaves and to fulfill the purpose of our creation.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِلَاءً</span>...</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتَمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ، وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ، وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِمَا الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ، وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'what is worship?';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ خَالِقِ الْوُجُودِ مِنَ الْعَدَمِ، وَجَاعِلِ النُّورِ مِنَ الظُّلَمِ، وَمُخْرِجِ الصَّبْرِ مِنَ الْأَلَمِ، وَمُلْقِي التَّوْبَةِ عَلَى النَّدَمِ فَنَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى الْمَصَائِبِ كَمَا نَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى النِّعَمِ وَنُصَلِّي عَلَى الرَّسُولِ الْأَكْرَمِ، لِيسَ الشَّرَفِ الْأَسْمَى، وَالنُّورِ الْأَتَمِّ، وَالْكِتَابِ الْمُحْكَمِ، وَكَمَالِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَالْخَاتَمِ، سَيِّدِ وَلَدِ آدَمَ، الَّذِي بَشَّرَ رَبُّهُ عِيسَى ابْنَ مَرْيَمَ، وَدَعَا عَلَى بِعْثَتِهِ إِبْرَاهِيمُ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ، حِينَ كَانَ يَرْفَعُ الْقَوَاعِدَ إِلَى بَيْتِ اللَّهِ الْمُحَرَّمِ فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ، وَعَلَى أَتْبَاعِهِ خَيْرِ الْأُمَمِ، الَّذِينَ بَارَكَ اللَّهُ بِهِمْ كَافَّةَ النَّاسِ الْعَرَبِ مِنْهُمْ وَالْعَجَمِ وَصَلَّى اللهُ عَلَى أَمِيرِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ، الْأَنْزَعِ الْبَطِينِ، وَقَائِدِ الْغُرِّ الْمُحَجَّلِينَ، غَالِبٍ عَلَى كُلِّ غَالِبٍ، مَطْلُوبِ كُلِّ طَالِبٍ، مَوْلَانَا عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ</span></p>
<p class="ar-block">‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَقُلِ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًۭا وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ شَرِيكٌۭ فِى ٱلْمُلْكِ وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ وَلِىٌّۭ مِّنَ ٱلذُّلِّ ۖ وَكَبِّرْهُ تَكْبِيرًۢا</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي نَحْمَدُهُ وَنَسْتَعِينُهُ وَنَسْتَغْفِرُهُ وَنُؤْمِنُ بِهِ وَنَتَوَكَّلُ عَلَيْهِ وَنَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنْ شُرُورِ أَنْفُسِنَا وَمِنْ سَيِّئَاتِ أَعْمَالِنَا َنْ يَهْدِهِ اللَّهُ فَلَا مُضِلَّ لَهُ، وَمَنْ يُضْلِلْهُ فَلَا هَادِيَ لَهُ وَنَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولُهُ، أَرْسَلَهُ اللَّهُ تَعَالَىٰ بِالْهُدَىٰ وَدِينِ الْحَقِّ، لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ وَكَفَىٰ بِاللَّهِ شَهِيدًا وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيَّ بْنَ أَبِي طَالِبٍ وَصِيُّهُ وَوَزِيرُهُ، فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَعَلَى آلِهِمْ وَسَلَّمَ عَلَيْهِمْ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرًا</span></p>
<p class="ar-block">‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَالُوا۟ سُبْحَنَكَ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَآ إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَآ ۖ إِنَّكَ أَنتَ ٱلْعَلِيمُ ٱلْحَكِيمُ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ، وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَعُ، وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ، وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ فَأَمَّا بَعْدُ يَقُولُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى فِي كِتَابِهِ الْكَرِيمِ بَعْدَ أَنْ نَقُولَ أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَمَاتِ عَلَى لَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ، وَاللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span></p>
<p>Once again, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>. So, in the previous session, we studied a couple of composite words like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَه</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرَب</span>.</p>
<p>I explained that the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَه</span> is a composition of several different meanings. You know, it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَهَ يَأْلَهُ</span>, the one who is worthy of worship. Then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span>, which is used for a child that is thirsty for the milk of its mother. Basically, the one who we desperately turn to for the fulfillment of our needs. That''s basically <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهَ</span>. Then there''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَهَا</span>, the one that we are passionately in love with.</p>
<p>There''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهَا</span>, the one we lean on or we put our weight on. There is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِهَا</span>, which is to become astounded by the glory and the magnificence of someone. Right? So when we combine all of these meanings together, we get an understanding of what the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَه</span> means. And then we looked at our credal formula, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>, which is basically designed to eliminate the multiplicity of these generic <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَه</span>, right, and to identify that one specific <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَه</span>. So basically we apply the definite article of &quot;the&quot; to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَه</span> and we make him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلَاه</span>, and you know that the combination of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلَاه</span> is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> basically is.</p>
<p>So, you know, we looked at that in the previous, we compared that to the concept of idol worship and breaking idols and things like that. Then we looked at the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, which is also a composite word. You know, it''s a combination of these six meanings: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, being the absolute owner; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيَّاد</span>, being the one who has complete authority; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبِّي</span> is the one who nourishes, takes care, ensures growth, right; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْشَد</span> is the which comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُشْد</span>, which means the guide and the one who puts to purpose; the benevolent benefactor, the one who showers with undeserving gifts; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيِّم</span>, the maintainer, the sustainer.</p>
<p>So you combine all of these six meanings, these six images, these six concepts, and you understand what the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> means, which we translate as Master. Then we looked at the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ</span>, which is the Quranic perspective, and it means, you know, a free slave, as opposed to forced slavery. The Quranic concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَادٌ</span> is a free slave. And in order to understand that relationship, we looked at this master-slave relationship that we find in the world, and then we compared that relationship to the master-slave relationship that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> talks about in the Quran.</p>
<p>And after that analysis, we concluded that even though <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is a master, He is unlike any other master that we have or will encounter in this world. So that''s pretty much the things that we talked about in the previous sessions. Today we''re going to look at the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, which is the primary attribute of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. It is actually an attribute that is the most dear to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. In fact, this quality is so fundamental to Him that we learned that He has actually made it obligatory on Himself. Okay, so we know that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has imposed certain obligations on all Muslims, right? As soon as you become a Muslim, there are certain obligations that... There are certain actions that become compulsory for Muslims.</p>
<p>You know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, prayers, fasting, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجٌّ</span>, right? These are all, you know, a bunch of actions that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has made compulsory on all Muslims. But there is one action that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has actually made compulsory on Himself. And that is the action of His mercy. It is the action of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. So, there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> says that .................. So, he says that indeed when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> established all of creation, right, when He was done creating everything, He wrote a statement on His throne. He wrote a statement on His throne.</p>
<p>And what was that statement? Indeed, My mercy for all My creation will always overcome and overpower My wrath and My anger. In other words, in My dealing with My creation, My mercy will always supersede My anger. My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> will always supersede My anger. Mercy is not... Well, I''ll talk about that in a bit. But basically He says that My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> will always overpower My anger. And this is a rule that our all-powerful, almighty Creator imposed upon Himself in His dealings with His creation, with us, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ</span>.</p>
<p>Now of course this is not literal, right? You understand that there''s not like an actual physical throne that He sits on and then there is this, you know, the statement written on that throne. It''s just a graphic representation of an emotion, right? It''s a visual representation of an emotion that depicts the love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for His creation. So we know that, why do we write down things, and why do we, and why would... So imagine if somebody has a throne, a king has a throne, and he is writing something on that throne. What''s the idea behind that, right? In the human context, it''s a constant reminder. You know, a king is about to sit on a throne and the first thing he looks at is that statement, and he''s like, oh okay, so now I have to deal with my subjects with mercy, right? So that''s the physical human representation of that emotion. It''s not...</p>
<p>So this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> is not literal, but you get the idea that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has basically imposed this rule upon Himself, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ رَحْمَتِي تَغْلِبُ غَضَبِي</span>, my mercy is always going to take precedence over my anger and my wrath. So it''s a beautiful <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> that depicts this emotion of love that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has for His creation. So now let''s look at the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> itself. It''s a very powerful word that we will study over the course of the next few sessions.</p>
<p>It''s too much to cover in, you know, it''s too broad to cover in one session. We''ll start gradually. Today we''ll look at the general definition of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. Okay, in the next session we''ll look at a more graphic representation of the word, and that''ll help us visualize this concept properly. Because we talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> all the time, we talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s mercy all the time, but do we have a clear image of what that word means?</p>
<p>We don''t, actually. In most cases, we don''t. And if you look at the translation of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> in English, you know, what do we, how do we translate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> in English? Mercy. Now the problem with that translation is that it actually distorts the meaning of the word. I''m not saying that it''s the wrong translation, but I am saying that it''s a weak translation. Why? Because the word mercy is generally used when you are being spared from a punishment.</p>
<p>So when you hear the words &quot;the soldier showed mercy,&quot; what does that mean? Right? It means that they had the power to do something terrible, but then they chose not to do it and to spare everyone. Right? &quot;The criminal begged the judge for mercy.&quot; So the whole begging thing and the whole, you know, begging for that mercy, it creates this image. In other words, he asked the judge to be spared, to not be punished, basically, right? So when we generally think of the word &quot;mercy,&quot; the idea is that something bad or something painful was about to happen, and then somebody decided not to go ahead with that painful action. In other words, you know, showed mercy. You understand?</p>
<p>So there is this subconscious association with punishment when the word &quot;mercy&quot; is used, followed by the idea of somehow escaping that punishment. But nevertheless, the default position is pretty much punishment when we think of mercy. So now what happens is that when we constantly use this word &quot;mercy&quot; for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> with that subconscious taint of escaping punishment, and when we constantly keep saying it, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, everywhere in the Quran, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, that word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> is being constantly used, you know, He''s extremely merciful, extreme.</p>
<p>When we keep repeating it over and over again, it creates this idea in our minds that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> wanted to do something bad to us, like He has this power to do bad to us, but then He decides not to do it to us, right? So that''s how He showed us mercy, by not punishing us. So you see what I''m saying? It''s subtle, but it creates the subconscious image of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> being the tyrant in some sense, who then chooses not to go ahead with His act, right?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعُوذُ بِاللهِ</span>, it creates that sort of... it taints the image of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in that sense. Now, in reality, it is absolutely not true. That is not the true intent of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> has absolutely nothing to do with punishment. It has no concept of the word punishment in it. So I think it''s really good to linguistically understand the proper meaning of this word as the ancient A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>s understood it, so that we can really appreciate the next time we talk about or we hear the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, we really get an image, a clear understanding of what that word means.</p>
<p>So I''m going to share this image. I hope everybody''s logged into the website. Okay, so in classical A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>ic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> has four meanings. It''s a composite word, and these four words or these four meanings come together to explain the meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. The first is unconditional love. It is care, genuine concern, and mercy. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> means. It''s a combination of these four things: unconditional love, extreme care, genuine concern, and then mercy.</p>
<p>Mercy is actually the last piece of the puzzle, not the first. Unconditional love is the first. Then comes care, then comes concern, and then finally mercy. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. Now, these four words are quite self-explanatory, right? Unconditional love is affection without conditions. We know that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> loves His creation unconditionally, like the mother who loves all her children equally regardless of whether they are obedient or disobedient. Doesn''t matter, right? It doesn''t really matter to the mother in terms of her love for them. She still loves them. It''s the same way <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> loves us, the same, you know. And why not?</p>
<p>We''re all His creation after all. I mean, He created us with His own hands, He tells us in the Quran. He didn''t set up a factory, send us off, or, you know, outsource the job of human creation to angels or anything like that, right? The Quran tells us that &quot;I created you with my own hands.&quot; In the story of Adam, we know that He created Adam with His own hands.</p>
<p>So there''s that, you know, that closeness. Because what do we do, right? When we get disconnected and we want to create something in bulk, we set up a factory, we outsource it, we do things like that. Nowhere in the Quran does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> say that He outsourced the job of creating this universe. He did the whole thing by Himself. So that establishes that closeness, that emotion of, okay, this is very personal, you know, human beings are a very personal creation to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>And so there''s no surprise that He loves them unconditionally. So that''s unconditional love. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is caring. He looks after us, provides for us, fulfills all our needs, right? He''s genuinely and sincerely concerned for our salvation, for our happiness, for our well-being. That''s why He sends the Quran, the messengers, constant reminders, constant teachings, constant wake-up calls, right? Because He''s concerned, He wants us to be happy, He wants us to achieve eternal happiness. So there''s that genuine concern there. And finally, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is merciful in that He shows compassion towards our weakness, right? He graciously forgives our sins and our transgressions, and He accepts our weak and feeble acts of worship, even though they lack the sincerity and the honor that He deserves.</p>
<p>I mean, we pray, but half the time we''re on Amazon shopping while we''re praying, right? So, you know, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> could just throw that prayer away and He could say, you know, I don''t want this kind of a connection, but He''s merciful in that He accepts that, He wholeheartedly accepts anything that we offer to Him. So, you know, so He''s merciful in the fact that He shows that compassion towards our weakness.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is a combination of these four words put together: unconditional love, extreme care, genuine concern and mercy. But what''s interesting is that, linguistically speaking, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> means to have these four things in order to benefit someone. That''s the primary condition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. It''s not just, you know, love for the sake of love, or care or concern for the sake of it. It actually has to have an end result, and that end result is to benefit someone. So the ancient A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, for him, he would define <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> as, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is a kind of love, it is a kind of tenderness that is intended to benefit the beloved. In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is precisely targeted towards benefiting someone. It has to translate into some sort of a beneficial action, otherwise it''s not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. You see, it''s service. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> manifests itself in a form of service, otherwise it''s not really <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. So that''s the most important thing, that, you know, you can have all these emotions, but if it doesn''t actually, you know, how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says that if you love me, then show me?</p>
<p>He imposes the same rule on Himself. This is the most amazing thing about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, that He''s extremely fair, not just with us, but with Himself. So whatever He imposes on us, He imposes on Himself too. You know, He says that, you know, just saying that you love me, just having love for me is not enough. Just saying it is not enough. I need to see it in action.</p>
<p>He imposes the same rule on Himself. He''s like, I have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> for you. I have love for you. I care about you. I''m concerned about you. I will have mercy on you, and I will show it to you. So that you know that that benefit manifests in the form of that benefit. Now, today, what I want to focus on in today''s session is these two commonly used words of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>.</p>
<p>So there are two common flavors or manifestations of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> that we find in the Quran that is used over and over again, right? And those words are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>, and both those words, they originate from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. So let''s first look at how both these words are commonly translated, so that we can appreciate when we learn its real meaning. We''ll go into a lot of depth into these words today.</p>
<p>But just to start off, if you look at the translation of these words in English, right? ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎. How is that normally translated? In the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. Or you will find, in the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the Most Beneficent, the Most Merciful. The Most Merciful, the Most Kind, the Most Merciful, the Beneficent, the Beneficent, the Beneficent. The problem with these translations is that, you know, these words are very difficult to process and visualize in English. I mean, when do we ever use the word beneficent in our normal day conversations? Like, if somebody helps you with something, do you say, oh gee, you are so beneficent?</p>
<p>No, you don''t do that, right? The purpose of translation is to understand the text better, but if we start using words for translations that we ourselves do not use in normal conversations, or words we cannot relate to, then it kind of defeats the entire purpose, and then we sort of just form some sort of a concept about that word. So today I want to focus on these two words so that we really appreciate how do we visualize <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> when we call Him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>.</p>
<p>So as I said, both these words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, they both manifest from the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. And I just explained what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is. It is a combination of four things that are intended to benefit someone: unconditional love, care, concern, and mercy. So when we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>, we are essentially acknowledging these four qualities about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Okay? That''s the implicit acknowledgement in any word that derives from the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>.</p>
<p>And this is something that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> states in the Quran over and over again. There are so many verses that talk about His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. The Quran in itself is a manifestation of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, right? Out of His love He created night and day. Out of His love, He made your tasks easy for you, right? Out of His love, He sends you winds that deliver good tidings, so that you can taste, you can recognize His love for you, you can recognize His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> for you. When <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> opens the door of His love for someone, nothing can close them.</p>
<p>So there are numerous verses in the Quran that talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, and the purpose of all such verses is to explain one thing to us. In the last session we talked about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> being our master, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, right? And when we think of the word master in the worldly sense, when you think of the word authority, what is the last thing you think of in a master-slave relationship?</p>
<p>Unconditional love. Right? But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is reminding us right away, right at the beginning of every recitation. We start every recitation with ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎. So every time we begin reciting His message, He is reminding us that He''s a different kind of master. He''s reminding us that we will never ever find a master in this world who is as powerful as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and yet who has the kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> that He has.</p>
<p>Because no other master in this world will show unconditional love to his slaves. They might take care of their slaves once in a while, right, but they will never ever love them. We know in this world masters will always show love to their own, never to their slaves, because it is beneath them. Because they enjoy a much higher social status than a slave, and therefore they will never associate themselves with the slave.</p>
<p>Forget unconditional love. But this master, our master, what is the comparison between... We''re not talking human to human. We''re talking creator to creation. Right? And that''s where He shines so bright, in which... in there, that He is a master, a powerful master, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. But the very first emotion that He exhibits towards us is unconditional love. No other master will care for us the way <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> cares for us.</p>
<p>No other master will be more genuinely concerned for us than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is concerned for us. No other master will show us mercy and compassion when we disobey them, not do the task assigned to us, do not fulfill the task to their standards. No master will show the kind of compassion and mercy and forgiveness to us in the way <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> does it for us. So that''s the first thing to understand.</p>
<p>The whole purpose of these verses of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is for us to realize what an incredible master we have, for us to appreciate that. So now let''s understand these words linguistically. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span> this will blow your mind, because once you understand these words you can really appreciate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> like nothing else. So I''ll begin with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, okay? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, it follows a particular phonetic pattern that represents intensity, ferocity, you know, severity.</p>
<p>It''s a pattern that represents extreme force or power. Now, any word in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>ic that follows this phonetic pattern of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آنَ</span> has these characteristics. So you know, words like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَدْبَاًن</span>, which means extremely angry, intensely angry; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَوًّا</span>, extremely hungry, ferociously hungry, right; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْشَان</span>, extremely thirsty. So it''s this pattern that have this common sound, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضْبَانَ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَوًّا</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْشَان</span>. So on the same phonetic pattern we have the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, extremely merciful, intensely merciful. You understand? Now, what''s really interesting is that any word in the Quran, in ancient A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>ic, that follows this phonetic pattern, it has to have three — it will always have these three characteristics.</p>
<p>It''s going to be extreme, it''s going to be immediate, but it''s going to be temporary. All words that match this pattern will always possess these three characteristics. They will be extreme, they will be immediate, and they will be temporary. So now if you look at the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, it means love, care, concern and mercy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. But not just love in the sense we understand love. It is a very extreme and intense form of love.</p>
<p>It is the kind of love that is beyond human imagination. It is care and concern beyond human imagination. It is mercy and forgiveness and compassion and all of those things beyond human imagination or example. And you will not find any example in this world, in human existence, that can compare to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. So that''s the first characteristics of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, that whatever you expect from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in terms of love, care, concern and mercy, first thing to understand is that it is going to be beyond your imagination and expectation.</p>
<p>You will never be able to find an example of that kind of love and concern in this world. Right? That''s the first thing. The second characteristic of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> is that it is something that is happening immediately. You don''t have to wait for it. It is happening right now. So let''s think about this for a second. In English, what is the difference between someone who is patient and someone who is being patient?</p>
<p>Which of the two is showing patience right now? The one who is being patient, right? Like, for example, if I say that so-and-so is a patient woman, you know, she might — she is a patient woman, but I can''t say that if she''s going crazy right now with her kids or not, you know, maybe she''s yelling at them, maybe she''s throwing them against the wall, I don''t know. When I say so-and-so is a patient woman, what I''m basically saying is that she is generally a patient person. That does not necessarily mean that she is acting patiently right this very second. But when I say that, you know, this woman is being patient, I''m talking about her right now.</p>
<p>So you understand the difference? The love and the care of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> is not something that is happening generally or at some distant point in time. That extreme and intense form of love is happening right now. In other words, through the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, the Quran is teaching us that we don''t have to wait for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to show us love at some future point in time. We don''t have to wait for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to care for us, be concerned for us, show us compassion at a later point in time when some event occurs or when we are in the middle of some calamity.</p>
<p>No, no, no, no. All of that is happening right now. You understand? He loves us, cares for us, concerns for us, is compassionate towards us, extremely, intensely. When? Right now. He is being extremely compassionate to us right now, every single waking moment. All of that is happening in its extreme form right now. So the first characteristic of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> is that it is extremely intense, the second is that it is immediate.</p>
<p>What was the third one? The third is temporary. The love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> is extreme, it is intense, it is immediate, but it is temporary. It''s not permanent. Now, this third part is a bit hard to take in when we hear it for the first time. You know, it sort of kind of feels wrong, you know, how can that be? But I''ll explain that, so just bear with me for a bit and you''ll understand the context of why I say it''s temporary. But for now, just understand that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> has these three qualities: intense, immediate and temporary. And as I said, all words that follow this phonetic pattern, they have these characteristics. So let''s understand it this way. If you look at the word of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَذَابًا</span>, it means extremely angry, intensely angry, fuming with anger.</p>
<p>It''s not like just regular anger. It''s like, you know, it''s where you end up killing someone. It''s that kind of an anger. But the thing is with that extreme anger, it has to be temporary. Think about it. It has to be temporary. Why? Because someone who is extremely angry, he cannot possibly sustain that anger physically forever. He cannot. His head will explode. He will die. Right? Even the Hulk chills out eventually.</p>
<p>So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبًا</span>, even though it''s an extreme state, it''s an immediate state, it cannot last forever. Either he''s going to burst out, he''s going to do what he needs to do and then calm down, or he will blow a fuse in his head and die basically. But it cannot last. That state, that intense state cannot last. Similarly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَوْهَنْ</span>, extremely hungry. And hungry when? Right now. Like you crazy teenagers, right?</p>
<p>But temporary. Because you will eat something in that state, and once you do your hunger goes away. Or if you don''t eat, you starve to death. And that''s how... that your hunger goes away. In either case hunger goes away. It does not permit... that intense hunger does not permanently stay there. Same with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَكْشَنْ</span>. You know, extremely thirsty right this very moment, but temporary. You either have a drink and then you''re no longer thirsty, or you die of thirst.</p>
<p>In other words... in either case the thirst is not going to last forever. So now why is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s intense mercy temporary? Because we would not be able to handle the intensity and the severity of His love for a long period of time. Just as I gave you the example of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَوَان</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَشَان</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبًا</span>, we would not be able to, we would go insane. I''ll give you an example. I mean, kids will be able to understand and relate to this much better, but have you ever had those moments where your mom, you know, where they''re in this cuddly and huggy kind of mood, and they wrap their arms around you and squeeze you till you just cannot breathe anymore, and all made-up, but you know, right, until your face is dripping wet? You know what I''m talking about. Now that is a manifestation of what?</p>
<p>It''s a manifestation of intense love. It is a burst of love, a burst of this energy that your mother feels, and she just has to shower that love on you. And that''s a beautiful thing. It''s an extremely beautiful thing when you''re feeling down or when you''re depressed and you just need some serious TLC. That is great. You actually appreciate that intense love. Right? But now imagine that every time you walk by your mother, that''s what she does to you.</p>
<p>Every time you walk past, she goes, &quot;Oh, Mera Bachan.&quot; Right? Every single time. How would you feel then? Would you be able to take it? No. You''d get annoyed. You''d get irritated. It''s like, &quot;Mom, leave me alone. Stop marinating my face with your saliva every time I walk by.&quot; Right? So you get the idea. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s intense love is extreme, it''s immediate, but it has to be temporary, because if it was constant, if He burst that love out on us constantly, it would drive us insane, and there are examples of people who have gone insane.</p>
<p>So that''s the idea behind, because anything that is intense, it cannot be sustained for a long time. I mean, if you''re jogging, if you''re on the treadmill, and if you put the treadmill to the highest speed and you get this burst where you''re just running, great. You can expend a lot of energy when you do that, but you cannot just keep going on. You eventually have to slow down, right?</p>
<p>That same principle that applies to any word that basically follows this phonetic pattern. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, three things to understand about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> is that it is intense, it is immediate, and because of that it is also temporary. Right? Okay. The next word is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيم</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s love, care, concern, and mercy, it also manifests through the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>. And there are two characteristics that you need to know about the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>.</p>
<p>And once again, this applies to all words that follow this phonetic pattern, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرِيم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلِيم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَكِيمٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَمِيل</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَلِيل</span>. They all have these qualities, the same two qualities. What are they? The first is that they are permanent. They are not temporary. The second quality is that it is not necessarily happening right now. It is not immediate. It is long-term. For example, if I say, you know, my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَا</span> loves me or my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَا</span> is loving to me, that''s a long-term quality, right?</p>
<p>But does that mean that she''s being loving to me right now? No, not necessarily. She may not be showering me with love this very moment, but I know for sure that it''s always there for me. That''s the difference between these two words. Now, what''s really interesting is that everywhere in the Quran we find <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> mentioned first and then it is followed by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيم</span>. Right? Now why is that?</p>
<p>And why are the two always used together? And this is where it gets really interesting. See, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had only said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, then His love, His care, His concern, His mercy would have been extreme, it would have been immediate, but it wouldn''t have been permanent. If <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had only said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>, His love and His mercy would have been permanent, but it would not have been extreme. So how do I talk about the love, care, concern and mercy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> so that it is extreme and it is beyond expectations and it is immediate and it is permanent and it is long-term and it is all of that at the same time? The only way to express that is through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>, together, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ</span>.</p>
<p>So you cannot understand, we''re taking an intangible emotion and we''re trying to confine that in words. To express what, to visualize again an understanding of what that really means. So, you know, there could be no better way of expressing these qualities of love, care and concern of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> better than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>. Let me give you an example so that you understand this better. You are at work, right?</p>
<p>Today is fading. Your boss is not there at the office and you have a flight booked, you know, to Timbuktu, let''s say, for 6, you know, 6 o''clock in the evening, 6 p.m., which means that you need to leave the office exactly at 5 o''clock or you will not be at the airport. It''s already 4:45, right? You''ve got 15 minutes left and you still haven''t received your paycheck, and you desperately need your paycheck because that money is for this trip. You know, you were counting on this paycheck for the expenses on this, for this trip. So now you''re getting desperate.</p>
<p>It''s 4:50 now and your boss still isn''t there. So you''re getting extremely anxious. And your co-worker comes up to you and says, &quot;Don''t worry, our boss is reliable. He''ll be here. You''ll get your paycheck. Don''t worry, relax.&quot; 4:55, you''ve got five minutes left and you''re totally freaking out. Your co-worker is still telling you, &quot;Don''t worry, don''t worry, he''s reliable, he''ll be here.&quot; Now what would you say to that co-worker at that point? You''ll say, &quot;Listen, I know he''s reliable, but I need him to be reliable right now, right this very minute. I need his quality of reliability right now.&quot; You see, when I''m in serious trouble, I need <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s help. When? Right now. So I need the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> that gives me that help that I need right now.</p>
<p>That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>. But then I also need the other name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span> because that takes care of my future. When <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>, it is permanent, right? Which is a guarantee for what? The future. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> is a guarantee of His love, His care, His concern and His mercy for the immediate, for the present, whereas <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span> is a guarantee of His love, His care, His concern and mercy for the future.</p>
<p>In other words, both short term and long term are covered by the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>. I''ll give you another example. I come home, I''m really, really hungry, I''m starving. I didn''t have time for breakfast, I was too busy at work, couldn''t have lunch. So now I come home in the evening and I''m dying, right? I sit at the dining table, you know, my wife comes up to me and says, &quot;So what do you think, what should I cook for next week?&quot; I''ll be like, &quot;Woman, who cares about next week?&quot;</p>
<p>Give me something right now. Right? When you''re extremely hungry, the only thing you can think of is what? Right now. And then when my right now is taken care of, once I''ve finished eating my dinner, right? Once I''ve taken care of my right now, I will say only then I will be interested in what I''m going to eat next week, right? Only after you''ve paid the bills for this month you will start worrying about the next month.</p>
<p>Only after you''ve paid the fees for this semester you will start worrying about the fees for next semester. You understand? So we only think about our future... we can only think about our future when our immediate concerns are taken care of. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, I am taking care of your immediate needs right now, but when your immediate concerns are taken care of, I am also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>.</p>
<p>I will also take care of all your future concerns. Right? So I hope that gives... You know, somebody asked Ibn Abbas once about the difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, and I think he gave a very beautiful explanation. He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> to us in this life and he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span> to us in the afterlife, because we know that the love and care of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, it goes away for disbelievers on the Day of Judgment. Right, until that day he is, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> for both the believers and the disbelievers. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> for all of us, every human being. In other words, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, he does not only care about Sulaimanis or about Muslims, he cares about everyone.</p>
<p>His love, his care, his concern for every single human being that he ever created, you know, plants, animals, humans, whatever. No matter what religion, color or race they belong to, he actually loves and cares for them. He even loves and cares for those who hate him, who deny his existence. His doors of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> are always open to everyone, because he''s always hoping that they will choose to turn back to him. His invitation never expires. His invitation, his arms, you know, he extends, he opens his arms for us, and that invitation never expires, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> is a universal quality.</p>
<p>My mercy extends to all. The Quran says the rain comes down from the sky for all creation. Human beings drink from it, we all draw our sustenance from it, right? The good drinks, the wretched drinks, the sinner drinks, the pious drinks, the friends of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> drinks, the enemy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> drinks. So everybody drinks. This is a universal mercy of his, right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span>, is open for everybody, and he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span> in that same sense, that he takes care of everybody, their long-term concerns. He has a plan for everyone. You know, eventually we know that everyone is going to come into the fold of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْلَام</span> and everybody will eventually enter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>, because that is what he wants, that is what he''s designed this everything to work with. So his mercy is just incredible. I mean, there''s just no words to explain it.</p>
<p>There''s this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>. It''s such a beautiful <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>. I think, to me, personally, I think it''s one of the most... it''s one of those things that really brings home to me this concept of his mercy. There''s a... <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> tells us that there''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which he says... He say' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'linguistic meaning of rehma';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 's that not a single day passes that the ocean asks its master, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا اللَّهُ</span>, give me permission. Let me drown these disbelievers.</p>
<p>Let me, you know, drown these disobedient creatures. They enjoy your blessings and they rebel against you. Give me permission. Allow me. Let me teach them a lesson. The earth begs its master, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا اللَّهُ</span>, allow me to swallow them alive. Allow me to eat them up right where they stand. They walk on your earth, they eat from its produce, that you grow from them, and then they do not prostrate to you, they do not do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَجْدَة</span> before you in abject humiliation.</p>
<p>Let me teach them a lesson, let me remove them from the face of this earth. Angels say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا اللَّهُ</span>, give us permission, let us destroy them, let us annihilate them. They don''t deserve to live after what you do for them, the kind of love, the kind of care, the kind of concern and mercy you show them, the way they respond back to you, they do not deserve to exist.</p>
<p>Let us annihilate them. Right? The ocean, the earth, the angels, what are they? They''re creations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, just like us. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created them. They are watching us, they are watching our sins, they are watching our rebellion, and they''re getting furious. And they''re just like creations like us, but the Creator that we are all rebelling against, what did He say? How did He respond to them? Leave them, let them be. I love my creation way more than how you are imagining it. If these are your creation — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is talking to the ocean, the earth and the angels — if these are your creation, then do whatever you want with them, go ahead, if they are your creation. But if they are my creation, then just hand them over to Me and step out of the way.</p>
<p>I will deal with My creation, My way. And in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَسْأَلُ الْمَلَائِكَ، سَلِمَ ذَا عَقْدُهُ</span>, the angels ask him, &quot;What are you waiting for?&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَسْتَجِيبُ اللهُ، إِنَّنِي أَتَلُوَ إِلَّا تَوْبَةً</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> responds to them and says, &quot;I am waiting for their repentance.&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْ أَتَانِي نَهَارًا قَبِلْتُهُ، إِنْ أَتَانِي لَيْلًا قَبِلْتُهُ</span>. If they approach me during the daytime, I will accept them. If they approach me in the nighttime, I will accept them.</p>
<p>If anyone takes one step, if anyone will take one step towards me, I will run towards them. And if anyone runs towards me, I will bring him closer. And if anyone walks away from me, if anyone turns away from me and walks away from me, I will go after him, I will chase him. I mean, how beautiful is that? If anyone takes one single step towards me, I will run towards him. And if somebody walks away from me, I will chase him. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> speaking to him. You know, this is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> dealing with his creations. It''s not just, you know, it''s incredible. Does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> need to do that? If anyone walks away from me, I will chase them.</p>
<p>I will go after him in order to bring him closer to me. And I will ask him, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوَلَا كَرَبُّ غَيْرِي</span>? Do you have another master who is more loving than me? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوَلَا كَرَبُّ غَيْرِي</span>, do you see another master that can compare to me? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَسَنَةُ عِنْدِي بِعَشْرِ أَمْثَالِهَا وَأَزِيدُ، وَالسَّيِّئَةُ عِنْدِي بِمِثْلِهَا وَأَعْفُو وَأَغْفِرُ</span>. Do you not see that I take one good deed, one tiny good deed, every single good deed that you do, and I reward you ten times for it, in fact even more, but I count your single sin as a single sin? And when you repent, I even forgive that and reward you for your repentance. Then where are you going?</p>
<p>I personally feel that this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> really portrays the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِيَّةُ</span> of our Creator. You know, His love, His emotion is the kind of... I mean, there is... You will not find anyone more humble than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And does He need to be humble? I mean, He is the Creator, right? I mean, look at this humility. I mean, this statement just... you know... You take one step, I will run towards you. You walk away from me, I will chase you. I mean, that is...</p>
<p>That is... you know, if He is this humble being a creator, then how are we so arrogant being creation? And you know, His love is just not a word that could be measured in words, and it can only be judged through His actions, and all we see is His actions. So I hope that, you know, I''ll end it here. I hope that I was able to explain the difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, and give everybody a really good understanding of what the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> means, and how perfect <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span> come together to capture that emotion. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَغَرَضُنَا الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتَمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ وَآلِهِ وَعَلَى عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِ الطَّاهِرِينَ وَسَلِّمْ</span>.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/79/9da9df6f-f8e4-4a81-9768-38023d7a120f.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" style="--img-h:369px" /></figure>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/79/40b93a03-5882-4ae6-9177-d9f5d781701c.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/79/bd725d5f-a767-46d9-b83c-e8e7d526de0a.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/79/0946d4dd-bfcb-45eb-be4c-2bebdaa9f080.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'linguistic meaning of rehma';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللهِ الرَّحْمٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلّٰهِ الَّذِي لَا يَبْلُغُ مِدْحَتَهُ الْقَائِلُونَ، وَلَا يُحْصِي نَعْمَاءَهُ الْعَادُّونَ، وَلَا يُؤَدِّي حَقَّهُ الْمُجْتَهِدُونَ، الَّذِي لَا يُدْرِكُهُ بُعْدُ الْهِمَمِ، وَلَا يَنَالُهُ غَوْصُ الْفِطَنِ، الَّذِي لَيْسَ لِصِفَتِهِ حَدٌّ مَحْدُودٌ، وَلَا نَعْتٌ مَوْجُودٌ، وَلَا وَقْتٌ مَعْدُودٌ، وَلَا أَجَلٌ مَمْدُودٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَطَرَ الْخَلَائِقَ بِقُدْرَتِهِ، وَنَشَرَ الرِّيَاحَ بِرَحْمَتِهِ، وَوَتَّدَ بِالصُّخُورِ مَيَدَانَ أَرْضِهِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَىٰ سَيِّدِ الْأَنْبِيَاءِ وَالْمُرْسَلِينَ، خَاتَمِ النَّبِيِّينَ، السَّيِّدِ الْمُمَجَّدِ، الْبَشِيرِ الْمُصَدَّقِ، النَّذِيرِ الْمُؤَيَّدِ، الْمُصْطَفَى الْأَمْجَدِ، الْمَحْمُودِ الْأَحْمَدِ، أَبِي الْقَاسِمِ مُحَمَّدٍ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَىٰ وَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ، أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ وَأَمِيرِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَىٰ سَيِّدِنَا الْإِمَامِ الطَّيِّبِ، صَاحِبِ الدَّعْوَةِ النَّبَوِيَّةِ وَالصَّوْلَةِ الْحَيْدَرِيَّةِ وَالْعِصْمَةِ الْفَاطِمِيَّةِ وَالشَّجَاعَةِ الْحُسَيْنِيَّةِ وَالْعُلُومِ الْبَاقِرِيَّةِ وَالْمَعَاقِلِ الْجَعْفَرِيَّةِ وَالْحِكْمَةِ الْإِسْمَاعِيلِيَّةِ وَالْجُودِ الْمُحَمَّدِيَّةِ، فَصَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَعَلَىٰ آلِهِمْ وَسَلَّمَ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰهُمَّ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَا إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ، وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَعُ، وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ، وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ دَعْوَةٍ لَا يُسْتَجَابُ لَهَا، وَمِنْ مُنْكَرَاتِ الْأَخْلَاقِ وَالْأَعْمَالِ وَالْأَهْوَاءِ</span> Fa amma baadi, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَكُلُّ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى فِي كِتَابِهِ الْكَرِيمِ</span>, bada naqula <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span></p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِيمِ، رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي، وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ إِنِّي أَعُوذُ بِكَ أَنْ أَضِلَّ أَوْ أُضَلَّ، أَوْ أَزِلَّ أَوْ أُزَلَّ، أَوْ أَظْلِمَ أَوْ أُظْلَمَ عَلَيَّ، وَأَجْهَلَ أَوْ يُجْهَلَ عَلَيَّ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ بِلَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ، آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span> Once again, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>.</p>
<p>In the previous session we started this conversation about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, and, you know, I said that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is generally weakly translated as mercy. But in reality, in Arabic linguistics, it is a much more powerful word. And it basically is a combination of a few things. We talked about it as a combination of four different things, four different emotions. It''s a combination of unconditional love. It is a combination of constant care, genuine concern.</p>
<p>And then finally, it''s mercy, it''s compassion. So we just generally think about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> as mercy, but then it is a much more complicated word. And it has to be geared towards the benefit for someone. These four emotions, they do not necessarily translate as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> unless it actually results in benefiting somebody. So as the Arab would say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةٌ رِرْكَةٌ تَقْتَلِي إِحْسَانُ إِلَى الْمَخُومِ</span>. So it is a love or it is a kind of tenderness that is specifically intended to benefit the beloved.</p>
<p>So that is what I explained the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> means. We looked at its two derivatives in the previous session, which is very commonly used. These are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>. And we looked at the words that kind of follow the phonetic pattern of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>. In Arabic, there is a certain linguistic pattern in which different words, they kind of follow this rhyme. So all words that follow this phonetic pattern of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آنَ</span>, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانُ، قَضْبًا، حَشًّا</span>, all of these, they have three things in common: they are intense, they are immediate, and it is temporary. Right?</p>
<p>So the mercy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> is intense. It is happening right now, but it is temporary, only because a constant supply of that intense love would actually drive the human being <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَان</span>e. And then there''s a story of Hazrat Isa, I believe that. So I was saying that there was this story of Hazrat Isa that he was walking by a temple and he saw this old man who was in a state of meditation, and he sensed that in his state of meditation he sensed that, you know, somebody extremely wholly pious is walking by. He sensed the vibe, he sensed that energy, and he broke his meditation and he said, you know, he asked who you are, and Hazrat Isa explained that he is the Prophet of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. And, you know, so Hazrat Isa asks this man, this old man, that why, you know, what are you doing in this wilderness sitting outside this temple? And he says that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رُوحَ اللهِ</span>, you know, since 70 years I''ve been asking for one thing and one thing alone from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and I have not yet received it, and you are the prophet of me, you are the beloved prophet of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, so will you please intervene on my behalf and ask him to give me what I have been seeking?</p>
<p>And so Hazrat Isa asks him, you know, what is it that you are looking for? What is it that you''re seeking? And he says, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا عِيسَى</span>, it''s been 70 years asking for the love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. So what, you know, I want you to ask him on my behalf to grant me, you know, a portion of that love so that I can experience that intense love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>Because I''ve heard about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and I have heard about how intense his love is, and I want to experience it physically. So can you ask him to give that to me. So Hazrat Isa raises his hand in prayer and he asks <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> to fulfill the need of this old man, and then he walks away because this temple was walking towards a certain destination and this temple happened to be on the way.</p>
<p>So he walks away, and then when he returns on his way back, he goes back through the same path and he sees that, you know, where the temple has crumbled, it has completely been destroyed as like an earthquake had just taken place. And there''s this big hole, you know, in the ground, and in there that man is standing there with his eyes closed, just staring towards the sky in a state of complete, you know, shock.</p>
<p>And he''s not responding to anything, he''s not... he cannot hear anything, he cannot say anything. And so Hazrat Isa, he asks <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, he says, what just happened here? And, you know, I just asked you for, you know, for you to fulfill the need of this man, and now I see him in this completely shocked state. So what just happened here? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> responds to Hazrat Isa and he says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رُوحَ اللهِ</span>, you know, you asked me for something that this man was not, no human being is capable of withstanding.</p>
<p>And he said that you asked me to give, grant him my love. And I gave him, you know, from the intensity of my love, I reduced it down to 70,000. So I gave him one portion from 70,000 portions of my love, of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span>, I made him experience that, and look what has happened to him. Now you tell me if I had given him a little bit more, what if one portion from that 70,000 levels of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> has brought him down to this condition.</p>
<p>You just imagine what would have happened to him if I gave him some more. So even the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> that we experience, the intensity of his love that we experience in this world, is just a minute fraction, not even one of that level, because just one level of that intense love can drive an extremely pious man. This is not, we''re not talking about any average human being, right?</p>
<p>This is a person who spent 70 years of his life in meditation. So you can imagine the level of piety, and he could not withstand one percent of that, not one percent, one fraction of that love. So, you know, for us human beings, you know, we don''t, we probably, you know, what we experience is not even that much, and yet, you know, there are people, there are so many stories in which people go <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَان</span>e.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> is basically intense love, it''s immediate love, and because of that intensity, it is temporary. And then we looked at the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>, which is a long-term permanent kind of love, and the reason <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span> are used in combination is to cover all these different aspects. Because if a love is immediate and it''s, you know, if it was just a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, it would be intense, it would be immediate, but then it would be temporary.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> takes, is used, is there, or the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is there to take care of the immediate concerns of the human being, whereas <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span> is there to take care of his long-term concerns and long-term care and unconditional love and mercy. So that is where we, and I said that all words that fall on this pattern, of this pattern of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, also has the same qualities, and many of the names of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> actually fall on this pattern.</p>
<p>So we know most of them, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرِيم</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَلِيل</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَمِيلٌ</span> and all of that. So they all have these same, and between this intensity and, you know, immediate attention versus, you know, permanent and long-term attention, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has covered basically all grounds. And a human being is basically all, not just human being, but all of creation is basically wrapped up and engulfed in this constant supply of love in one form or the other.</p>
<p>So that is what we talked about in the previous session. In today''s session, what I want, I want to continue this conversation further, and I want to actually — we looked at the two derivatives of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, right, which was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>. Today I actually want to talk about where the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> itself comes from. So just keep the definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> in mind, which is the combination of those four things: unconditional love, constant care, genuine and sincere concern, and unconditional mercy, right? So there is...</p>
<p>Keep the definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, you know, before you, so that you can properly understand where... when I explain the origin, the root of this word, it will help you really appreciate the kind of love we''re talking about. And also that, you know, it is obviously for the benefit. It is always targeted towards the benefit of someone, otherwise it''s not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. So today we''ll look at the root word for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, which actually comes from the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْم</span>.</p>
<p>Okay, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, it comes from the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْم</span>, and the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْم</span> in Arabic, it actually means the womb of a mother when she''s carrying a child. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَن</span> is the place where the baby lives inside the mother, where the fetus develops for nine months before the child is born in this world. Now, why do you think <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> chose a word for mercy that derives from the womb of a mother?</p>
<p>Because the womb of a mother, it actually describes the relationship between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and His creation. And this is what makes it so beautiful. Now, what I find interesting that, you know, both parents love their children, right? You know, father and mother all love the child. But what I find interesting is that in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, whenever <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> compares His mercy for His creation, He never uses the example of a father.</p>
<p>Never. He always uses the example of a mother. So in order to truly understand and appreciate the meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, right? In order to truly understand and appreciate the kind of relationship <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has with us, we must first understand the relationship of a mother with her child, when she''s carrying that child in her womb. There is a famous <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is actually speaking directly to the womb.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> tells us that there''s this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> actually talks to the womb. And obviously, you know, the womb here means the archetypal womb, because the womb of a mother is how all of humanity exists in the world today. Right? We all came as a result of the womb of our mothers. And so, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> spoke directly to this womb and He said, I named you from one of my names.</p>
<p>In other words, I am a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, I am a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, and so I am deriving the name of my unconditional love, my constant care, my genuine concern, and my infinite mercy, right? I am naming my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> based on your womb, your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَن</span>. So I am giving you one of my names. I am a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, I am a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, I have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, and that is why I am calling you, the womb, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَن</span>. I''m making you a symbol of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> and my unconditional love, because every single human being that will exist through time will exist because of you. They will come in this world through you, and you will remind them of my love for them. Your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> is the very first thing, womb is the very first thing that they will experience before they come in this world, and my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is what they will experience once they are born in this world until the day they return to me. That is the conversation that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> had with the womb, which is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَن</span>, right?</p>
<p>I have named you from one of My names. Now, what is it about the womb of a mother that is important for us to understand? Well, there are a few things. The very first question that I want to place before you is that when a child is inside the womb of its mother, is it being punished? Is there any association with the concept of punishment for the baby when it is inside the womb of its mother?</p>
<p>Remember, we talked about this in the previous session, that generally mercy is related to punishment, that there was some kind of punishment that was going to happen and then the person decides not to go ahead with that punishment, right? So if we are now comparing, if we''re trying to understand the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> through the womb of a mother, let''s put these questions to actually answer some of these or rather strengthen some of these concepts.</p>
<p>That a child in the womb of a mother, there is no concept of punishment there. Right? Absolutely not. What''s the relationship of the mother with the baby when it''s in the womb? It''s pure love. Right? And what is that a reminder for? It is a reminder of the very kind of relationship that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has with His creation. That it is based on pure love. It has nothing to do with punishment.</p>
<p>The moment a woman finds out that she is pregnant, she falls in love with that baby. It doesn''t matter, even if she never planned on having a child, never planned on having children, every woman, the second she becomes pregnant, the first emotion she feels for the baby is love. She hasn''t even seen the baby yet, has she? She hasn''t met the baby yet, but she still falls completely and unconditionally in love with her baby the moment she finds out she''s pregnant.</p>
<p>So that''s the first thing that I want to put forth when we discuss this word mercy, that it has nothing to do with punishment. It has everything to do with love. And secondly, did the womb exist before the mother conceived? Or does the body just create the womb when she gets pregnant and then discards it later? It doesn''t, right? The womb exists within every woman, baby or no baby, the womb is always there, right?</p>
<p>Similarly, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ رَحْمَة</span>, His unconditional love, His care, His concern, His mercy, it''s always there. His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> does not exist because we exist or because humanity exists or, you know, because creation exists. No! His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is always there and will always remain there, even when we no longer exist, even when all of humanity no longer exists, even when this universe no longer exists, just as the womb exists even after the child is born. You understand?</p>
<p>I''m drawing that parallel over there. So just by examining the womb we get answers to some really interesting questions, you know. It really helps us understand some of these, you know, it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, the way <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has designed everything is just incredible, that, you know, it gives us so many lessons just by examining how certain things work and how He has named them after some of His own emotions and principles.</p>
<p>Now, when the child is inside the womb of its mother, does the child even know that it is being taken care of? Does it even know that the mother exists? It doesn''t. Right? So it is being taken care of in ways that it cannot even imagine. I mean, forget realizations and appreciations. I mean, that comes all later. The fetus in the womb of its mother cannot even imagine what role the womb is playing in its life to maintain its existence. In the same way, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is taking care of us in ways that we cannot even possibly imagine or realize.</p>
<p>He is taking care of us in such a subtle manner that, you know, we forget His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, even His presence becomes invisible to us. You know, He''s taking care of us in such a subtle manner. That is why one of His names is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَطِيف</span>, right? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَطِيف</span> that comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لُطْف</span>, it means something that is extremely subtle, something that is there, that is constantly working behind the scenes for you, but you don''t feel its presence. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَطِيف</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is constantly there, He''s constantly working 24/7, never takes a break, never sleeps, never gets tired, all of that is in place and we forget His existence because He''s working in such a subtle manner behind the scenes. But what''s amazing isn''t that in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> every time the attribute <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَطِيف</span> is used, what other attribute comes with it?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَبِير</span>, the All-Knowing. You see, your eyes cannot see Him, but He is aware of what your eyes are seeing. Your eyes cannot see Him, but He is aware of what your eyes are seeing. He is extremely subtle, but at the same time He''s also All-Knowing. Right? So you forget. You become unaware of His presence, but He knows everything. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So the child doesn''t even know that the mother is taking care of it, just as we don''t even realize how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is taking care of us.</p>
<p>The next thing that comes to mind when you think of the womb is protection. The mother is constantly protecting the baby. Right? Have you ever seen a pregnant woman walk? I mean, she watches the corners of the table when she walks, right? She always tries to be safe. She sleeps on her side, right? She watches what she eats. She''s constantly caressing her tummy, the baby, right? She loves the baby.</p>
<p>She cares for the baby. And that''s why she''s extremely protective of it. This is exactly what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is doing for us constantly. I mean, you kids, you know, you guys, you don''t just make it to school just like that. I mean, can you even imagine how many things can go wrong, the horror stories that we hear? I mean, in just the time you leave the house to the time you enter your school, there are millions of things that can go wrong. I''m not even talking, you know, back from school, the duration of the school and the time that you return home. I''m not even talking about that. Just the time between you leave and you reach school, a million things can go wrong. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is protecting them. He''s protecting all of us. He''s keeping us safe. Same for us when we leave for work or whatever, right? Every single day people walk out of their homes, and but not everyone returns that night. Do these people who die in car crashes or accidents, did they know that they''re not going to return home tonight?</p>
<p>When the time comes, that''s it, right? But until that time comes, what is constantly protecting them from accidents and all kinds of harm? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ رَحْمَة</span>. So just as the womb is constantly protecting its creation, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is constantly protecting His own creation. That is what the womb of a mother is reminding us. Then, so I hope this is helping you draw, understand that the whole idea of this conversation is to take these concepts of a mother and the baby in her womb and apply it to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> with us. So it''s that microcosm, macrocosm thing that''s going on here, right? It''s a perfect parallel. So then, if we look at this relationship further, this mother puts up a lot of difficulties, you know, she puts up with a lot of difficulties for her baby, doesn''t she?</p>
<p>A mother deals with constant morning sickness. Some have to go through horribly painful tests, you know, get all of these injections and God knows what, right? Some have to take extremely nauseating medicines for the health and growth of the baby. They have to get blood work done constantly. It''s one painful thing after another. A mother goes through, you know, all these stages when the baby is developing, and in that process she goes through a lot of pain, and for the sake of what?</p>
<p>Her child. Which again, by the way, the child is completely unaware of. The child takes the mother completely for granted, just as we take the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> for granted. And technically, if you think about it, the child in the womb of its mother is doing nothing for the mother, and yet the mother is doing everything for the child. The baby eats her food, right?</p>
<p>The poor woman just, you know, ate, and within minutes that damn baby takes up everything, and she''s like, you know, seriously, stop it. Right? She just ate this huge meal, and minutes later she''s hungry again. And then the husband has to run in the middle of the night for ice cream, because the baby is taking everything from her, and it''s not giving anything back to her in return. And when he does actually thank her, when the baby does that, what does it do?</p>
<p>It kicks the mother from the inside. I mean, that''s like, thank you. You know, it just decides to stretch from time to time inside. It decides to do this in the middle of the night, it decides to do this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَانِي</span>ty workout just whenever it feels like it. But every time the child does something, who goes through pain? The mother. She silently but happily endures it. Now that is incredible.</p>
<p>Think about that for a second. That is incredible. The mother is in a constant state of discomfort because of this baby, and yet she is happily enduring it. We are doing absolutely nothing for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is doing absolutely everything for us. Right? I mean, with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> it''s so weird, because we cannot do anything for Him even if we wanted to, even as mature adults, even after understanding this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. What is it that we can do for Him? We cannot add anything to His kingdom, we cannot take anything away from His kingdom. Our good deeds do not benefit Him, our bad deeds do not harm Him. We cannot make Him any more wealthier than He is, any more richer than He is. We cannot make Him poor, you know. We cannot praise Him in any way or any more than He has already praised. Everything in the heavens and the earth is in constant praise for Him, right? So what are we going to do? We cannot do anything for Him. I mean, just take a moment to appreciate that there is absolutely nothing that we can offer <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, and yet He is constantly doing things for us without us even realizing His presence, and in many cases even without acknowledging His presence. Right? He is constantly working for us. Every moment He''s busy in work. He''s constantly keeping this universe and everything within this universe functional.</p>
<p>How many blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> can you possibly deny? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. I hope we can, you''re appreciating that mercy, that concept of mercy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, that nobody, you know, tell me one person you know who''s gonna do that. You constantly do for somebody in your life who does absolutely nothing for you. How long is that relationship gonna last? No matter who you are, I don''t care if you''re Mother Teresa, right? It just doesn''t work that way.</p>
<p>But look at when we look at our relationship with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, it''s like, whoa. So then the womb, it reminds us that all the needs of the child are being met by its mother. The child is completely surrounded, right? But it''s completely enveloped by the love and the care of its mother. That''s the idea of the womb, right? It''s a circle, it''s like a ball, it''s like a bubble.</p>
<p>So he''s completely engulfed in that mercy, in that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَن</span>. His food, his drinks, all of that is taken care of. Even when the child excretes, all of that is taken care by the body of the mother. Everything that that child needs is completely provided for. Right? The rent is paid, housing and boarding is free, does not have to pay for the utilities. All meals are included. In other words, the baby is completely wrapped up in the love and the care of its mother, right?</p>
<p>In the same way, we are completely wrapped up in the unconditional love, the care, the concern and the mercy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. He provides us with our sustenance. He gives us the luxuries of a good life. He makes the outside world functional for us. He''s keeping the inside world functional for us, right? My heart is beating. My organs, they''re working. My breath is coming in and out.</p>
<p>I can walk. I can speak. And He''s making all of this happen for me. He gave me parents. He gave me teachers. He gave me knowledge. He gave me my own family. So He surrounded me with all comforts of my life. You know how we complain about paying the bills? You know, like our mortgage and utility expenses and school fees and things like that? I mean, just think for a second, how much do we pay for our sight?</p>
<p>How much do we pay <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> for our ability to hear, our abilities to speak, our ability to breathe? Forget everything else. How much are we paying for each and every breath that we take? Let me ask you a different question. How much would you pay for your breath right now? If just right now, if you couldn''t breathe and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> asked you to pay for the next breath, what would you be willing to pay for that next breath? What would that breath be worth to you? Can you just appreciate how we take things for granted?</p>
<p>I mean, when you ask these questions, it really puts things in perspective for us, right? So then what do we pay for our hands, for our ability to walk? I mean, we are going through life that is a life that is immensely blessed with uncountable bounties. We are immensely blessed with the riches and provisions He created for us, all of which we take completely for granted, right? And He is okay with that. He still continues to serve us, just like the mother keeps serving the baby. What is, if the mother stops doing what she''s doing for the baby in its womb, what is the baby capable of doing? Can he say, oh fine, if you''re not going to do it for me, I''ll do it myself? Can the baby say that? How helpless is that baby inside the womb of its mother for its sustenance and its development, for its continued existence?</p>
<p>Completely. So are we. Do we realize that? Do we appreciate that? Are we grateful for that? And so far, you know, we''ve only talked about the benefits of the child. Let''s talk about the motivation for this mother. So I said the baby is not, you know, doing anything for the mother. It''s not paying the mother in any way. It''s not giving anything back to the mother. So then what is the motivation for this mother to carry on?</p>
<p>Despite all the discomfort this child is putting her through, what could be possibly motivating this mother to carry on doing what she''s doing? If you think about that question, if you really reflect on that question, the only motivation you''ll see that the mother has for the baby is that of an extreme form of unconditional love. That is how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> looks at us. As the child grows in the womb of its mother, it becomes more and more of a problem for the mother.</p>
<p>Right? From a physical standpoint. As time passes and the embryo grows in size, it becomes more and more of a problem for its mother. But what happens to her love? She falls more and more in love with her baby. She constantly increases in her love. Now that is incredible. And we know that when that baby comes out of her, we know that it almost kills her. She goes through this immense pain of childbirth, which obviously men, we cannot understand.</p>
<p>Right? But we see it. I mean, if nine months of torture wasn''t enough, this baby causes this incredibly intense pain at childbirth. And the emotions are incredible. You know, I''ve been in the delivery room twice, so I have seen what that pain is like. You know, when Nazleen was in labor, she was in labor I think for eight hours at the time of Hira, and I''m sure there are mothers in the group that have gone through much worse, you know, but personally I''ve, you know, at least in my life, I''ve never seen anyone go through so much pain for that extended period of time. But and I remember when Edam came out, you know, the doctor tried to hand her to me, right?</p>
<p>All excited and just like that, just directly. And she was covered up in all of this gooey stuff. And I was like, you know, what is this? Can you please clean this thing first? Right? So that was my emotion. But I remember when they brought Edam to Nazleen, right? So with this incredible love, she touched her cheeks, I remember, and she said, Hi, baby. And then she passed out.</p>
<p>I mean, that was an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَان</span>e moment, at least in my life. And I''m never going to forget that. And I still don''t get it. And probably never will. I mean, I thought she would hate this baby who caused her so much pain. I thought she would scream at her, yell at her, curse her out. &quot;You almost killed me. I hate you. I never want to touch you again.&quot;</p>
<p>That''s not what happened. Instead, the very first time they brought Adam to her, she gave her a big hug and kissed her. I''ve never seen anyone in so much pain and this happy at the same time. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Anyone else who causes so much pain to you, anyone who makes you bleed this way, that a child makes the mother bleed at childbirth, you would not give them a hug.</p>
<p>I promise you, you would not give them a hug. We made our mothers bleed almost to death in certain cases, and as soon as we come out of her womb, she showers us with love. And that love increases for us after going through that process. She falls more in love with — our mothers fell more in love with us than they ever did before. And this is seconds after we tried to kill her.</p>
<p>So you tell me, what kind of relationship is that? It''s a completely illogical relationship. This is not a relationship you and I can make any sense of. It''s beyond our logic, because this does not happen with any other relationship. There is no other relationship in your life or my life that works this way. You know, where you just never give, you never give, you only receive, you only take, and still all you get back in return is love, more love.</p>
<p>You keep demanding and she keeps giving, right? You know how certain people in life, they just feel like, &quot;Oh, I''m a terrible person. Why would <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> ever love me? And why would He care for me? And He''s never gonna forgive me. I''m doomed, that''s it, right? It''s the end of the road. I''ve done so many bad things, now forget it. I''m never gonna ever be worthy of His mercy. That just doesn''t make logical sense. You know why? I would never forgive anybody like me, so, you know, why would <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> forgive me?&quot; Well, ask the baby who just tried to kill the mother in childbirth, how does the mother feel for that baby, and you''ll have your answer. You know, we cannot even humanize <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love. Like, forget, you know, you reduce <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says that I love you 70,000 times more than a mother loves the child. Now imagine that. We cannot even understand the love of a mother for the child or really appreciate it. How are we gonna, you know — why, how dare you blame <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> in that way, that He''s not going to forgive you?</p>
<p>How dare you? After what a child does to her mother when she comes out of the womb, look at how the mother treats that child. And if a mother can do that, you think <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> cannot do that? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. And by the way, your mother bled so much to give you life. She just went through childbirth or surgery to give you life, and what happens right after you are born? What does she have to do now? She has to feed you. And feeding, as we know, is also a very painful thing for some women.</p>
<p>Anyone else in this world, anyone, right, who goes through surgery or goes through any kind of intense pain, they say, &quot;I need six weeks off, I need two months off, I need a break, I need a vacation.&quot; Does a mother get a two-month break after she gives birth to you? No. You break a leg and you get six months off from school. She just almost died giving birth to you, and she''s on the clock right away.</p>
<p>The moment you are born, she is working full time after going through that childbirth. And what do babies give back to the mother in return? I mean, she gives the baby milk. She gives it life. She gives it love. She gives it care. She''s constantly concerned about it. She''s constantly protecting it. She bathes it, oils it, changes diapers like every five minutes. And she does all of this through countless sleepless nights, month after month, year after year.</p>
<p>What does the baby do in return? Does the baby say thank you? Does it show gratitude? Does it sing praises for the mother? No. Does the mother still keep loving and caring her baby over and over again? Yes, she does. The only reward the baby offers is what? By urinating on her? Pooping on her? Throwing up on her? And even when the baby does that, how does the mother respond?</p>
<p>Toss it out of the window? No, she actually finds that cute. Oh, so cute. And then she cleans the baby up. Oh, it''s okay, my little baby. Don''t worry, here, you know, I''ve changed you, now you''re gonna feel all better. I mean, that''s the conversation I''ve seen moms have with the child after they just urinated and pooped on them. Isn''t that an incredibly beautiful relationship? But at the same time, isn''t it also an extremely <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَان</span>e relationship?</p>
<p>But this is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is doing for us. This is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is doing for us on a much more larger scale. And that also doesn''t make sense, because it''s, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love for us doesn''t make sense. It''s beyond logical reasoning. We just cannot limit <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love to common sense, because yes, we do not deserve this kind of love from Him. That is what common sense would tell you. If all of us, we honestly judge our actions, you know, if we look at our ingratitude towards <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, you know, there''s absolutely no reason for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> to continue taking care of us.</p>
<p>I mean, we... we don''t even take the time out to remember Him five times in a day. That''s... what is that, two hours? If you do all the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَمَاز</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْض</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفَلْ</span>, what is that, two hours out of 16 hours? And that''s all it takes, right? We''ll watch a three-hour Indian movie, see this idiot chase this girl around a tree. We''ll do that. We''ll watch a five-hour soccer match.</p>
<p>We''ll watch an all-day cricket match, right? Rewind and play over and over again. Look at that shot. Look how amazing, right? Yeah, it''s okay. It''s only been four hours. So what? You know, it''s okay. We have time for all of those trivial activities, but we will not take out two hours for our creator. Not even two hours, 30 minutes, just a five. We don''t even do that much to just say thank you.</p>
<p>We constantly disobey Him after everything He does for us. We do everything He tells us not to do. And some of us, we don''t even acknowledge His existence. I mean, who knows if there''s an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> or not, and who cares if there is? And yet He keeps giving us, He keeps feeding us, He keeps providing for us, He keeps forgiving us. It''s something truly incredible. And I''m mostly just thinking of myself right now.</p>
<p>I mean, despite my constant ingratitude, my constant disloyalty, you know, my constant defiance to His every command, I am still waking up every morning. I am still breathing oxygen. I am still going about my day, you know, eating and drinking all this delicious food that He provides for me. I''m still enjoying life to the fullest. He takes none of that away from me. And yes, that doesn''t make sense.</p>
<p>It just doesn''t make sense. But that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ رَحْمَة</span>. And, you know, just... And you know, you want to see how ungrateful human beings are? Just look at what we do with food. Look at the amount of food that we waste. His greatest blessing for mankind, the very means of sustenance. Look how we disrespect that food. I mean, have you ever studied the etiquettes of eating in that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">چَہَل سَبَق</span>?</p>
<p>The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he said, sit before your food like a slave sits before its master. Cover your head before you eat out of respect for that food. Do not place your plate on the floor, elevated from the ground, in order to honor it. When you''re done eating, find those grains and those crumbs that have fallen off your plate and put them in your mouth. Suck the food off your fingers. That''s the kind of respect and honor we should have for food in our lives.</p>
<p>Why? One, because without it, we cannot even imagine what our lives would be reduced to. And second, just think about the amount of effort it took behind the scenes to get that food on your plate. I mean, can you appreciate the time, the effort, the protection, the management, the planning that goes into taking a seed, growing it into a plant, having the animal eat it, taking care of the animal, feeding it, protecting it until it reaches the proper age, getting that animal into the hands of the one who is going to do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَبِيحَة</span> in order to make it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَال</span> for us, getting that dead animal or that food into the supermarket or the meat shop so that you end up buying it, giving you the provisions to buy it, providing you with the tools to cook it until that food finally makes it to your plate.</p>
<p>That is years and years and years of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> labor that sits before you in your plates. But do we care? No, we take a few bites and toss it away. It wasn''t warm enough, wasn''t fresh enough, not enough salt, right? Kids, you guys do projects for school, right? Imagine you worked on a school project for months and months, and the second you present it to your teacher, he just tosses it out of the window or steps on it or breaks it or whatever.</p>
<p>How would you feel? And then that professor tells you, okay, for next month I want you to work on this project. You tell me how you will respond. Will you go through that effort again? Look what we do with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> effort, and look what He does. Does He stop that food from us? Right? Some people say, why can''t we waste food, we can afford it? Well, you should not waste food out of respect.</p>
<p>It doesn''t matter how rich you are, how much money you have, how much food you can buy. It''s not about that. It''s not about the quantity. It''s about looking at the effort behind everything that took place to get that food to you. It is about respecting that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, that love of the Creator. Why do you think <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> placed all these etiquettes in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>?</p>
<p>If you read <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَائِمُ الْإِسْلَامِ</span> or, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">چَہَل سَبَق</span> and all these rules, what do you think those rules are there to remind you of? All rules of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> are a constant reminder for us because we are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَان</span>, right? We are forgetful creatures. We constantly, you know, need reminders because we are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَان</span>. So somebody who constantly forgets, he needs constant reminders. Reminders of what? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> mercy. His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>.</p>
<p>All of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> is basically just a reminder of that. Reminder of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> mercy. Because as soon as any decent human being is presented and reminded of the love, care, concern and mercy of another person, his natural response is praise and gratitude. That''s his natural response. If that person is a decent human being. If it''s not a decent human being, then it''s just similar to an animal. You feed an animal.</p>
<p>Even an animal actually is grateful. That is what makes us so terrible, because you put a food in front of an animal. Have you ever seen a thirsty dog? If you ever have a thirsty dog and you put a bowl of water in front of it, after it has taken its drink, it''s going to look up to you and you will see that gratitude in its eyes.</p>
<p>Even an animal in many cases is better than human being. But we unfortunately are ungrateful people. We continuously disrespect and disregard <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> efforts, and He constantly keeps providing for us. He gives us food, He gives us shelter, He clothes us one better than the next. Just like the mother keeps feeding her baby regardless of what it does. Does the baby care about the months and months of intense effort that went into letting it grow from a drop of liquid into this fully developed child, then from a child to an adult?</p>
<p>No. Does the mother stop loving the child ever? No, because this is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَانِي</span> love. There is absolutely no rhyme or reason for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> to love us this way, but He does. He still does, always will. Why? Because He has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> for us. Because this is the kind of relationship He has with us, of unconditional love, constant care, genuine concern and constant mercy. Constant mercy. What happens to the womb as the baby grows in size?</p>
<p>It expands. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, it expands to accommodate the baby. It expands to accommodate the need of the baby. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love, His mercy, His compassion, His forgiveness, it constantly expands to accommodate the needs of His creation. This is the love of our Creat' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'womb - the source of mercy';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'or for us. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. If you want to understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, understand and appreciate the womb of a mother. Oh womb, I have named you after my names.</p>
<p>I have named you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَن</span> to remind you that I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>. I have made you a symbol of my love so that every human being recognizes and appreciates my unconditional love for them, through you and by your example. Because none of us, no creation in this world, is going to come in this world without that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَن</span>. Nobody can miss it, you see. Do you see the beauty of this design?</p>
<p>And just as we will never be able to understand the love of a mother for her child, we will never be able to understand and appreciate the love and care of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> for His creation. But if there''s any semblance of decency in us, the least, the very least we can do is acknowledge it and be grateful for it. Right? So that''s the understanding of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, that He gave us mothers so that we can taste one drop of that endless ocean of love that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has for His creation.</p>
<p>So, you know, while you are, after the session, while you guys are hugging your mothers, don''t forget to be appreciative of the One who gave you that mother and who loves you the same way, more than your mother will ever love you. And be grateful to Him. And the best way to be grateful and express that gratitude to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is to leave whatever you''re doing when He calls you in the form of those five prayers, to show that we are all decent human beings, we are not ungrateful people. Right? To show Him that, you know, to show that sleep is not as important to me as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is, you know, my work is not as important, my homework, my schoolwork is not as important to me as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is, you know, my own tiredness is not as important, that I''m never going to be tired enough to not be grateful to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, because He never gets tired giving me anything. He never stops working for me, not providing my food because He was tired.</p>
<p>And He is constantly working 24/7. I don''t have to work that. He gives me eight hours of good sleep. So the least I can do to show Him that, express that gratitude to Him, is to complete my five days of, you know, my five times <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَمَاز</span> every day. That''s the least I could do for Him, that I could just drop whatever I''m doing, break any idol that I am worshipping, and just stand before Him and fall in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَجْدَة</span> before Him to say, thank you. ............................... <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/101/69b7af1e-c0ca-4a3f-9149-ff599bc840cc.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'womb - the source of mercy';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ صَانِعِ الْخَلْقِ الْأَكْمَلِ نِظَامًا وَأَحْسَنِ قِوَامًا الْوَاضِعِ الشَّيْءَ فِي أَجْدَرِ مَقَامٍ وَأَرْفَقِ مَسَامٍ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي لَا تَنْقُصُ خَزَائِنُهُ وَلَا يَبِيدُ مُلْكُهُ وَلَا تَرَاهُ الْعُيُونُ وَتَعْزُبُ عَنْهُ الْحَرَكَاتُ وَالسُّكُونُ وَلَمْ يَزَلْ وَلَا يَزَالُ أَبَدًا وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى الْمُؤَيَّدِ بِأَبْلَغِ الْكَلَامِ مُحَمَّدٍ الْمَبْعُوثِ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ لِهِدَايَتِهِ كَافَّةَ الْأَنَامِ إِلَى سُبُلِ السَّلَامِ وَإِلَى مَنْ ضَلَّ آلَهُ وَلَا يَؤُمُّ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًا وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ شَرِيكٌ فِي الْمُلْكِ وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ وَلِيٌّ مِنَ الذُّلِّ وَكَبِّرْهُ تَكْبِيرًا</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَنزَلَ عَلَىٰ عَبْدِهِ ٱلْكِتَبَ وَلَمْ يَجْعَل لَّهُۥ عِوَجَاۜ</span> ‎</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ، اَلَّذِي نَحْمَدُهُ، وَنَسْتَعِينُهُ، وَنَسْتَغْفِرُهُ، وَنُؤْمِنُ بِهِ، وَنَتَوَكَّلُ عَلَيْهِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنْ شُرُورِ أَنْفُسِنَا، وَمِنْ سَيِّئَاتِ أَعْمَالِنَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ يَهْدِهِ اللَّهُ فَلَا مُضِلَّ لَهُ، وَمَنْ يُضْلِلْهُ فَلَا هَادِيَ لَهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولُهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْسَلَهُ اللَّهُ تَعَالَىٰ بِالْهُدَىٰ وَدِينِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ وَكَفَىٰ بِاللَّهِ شَهِيدًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيَّ بْنَ أَبِي طَالِبٍ وَصِيُّهُ وَوَزِيرُهُ، فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَعَلَىٰ آلِهِمْ وَسَلَّمَهُمْ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْ لَنَا عِلْمًا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَعُ وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَللَّهُمَّ آتِ نُفُوسَنَا تَقْوَاهَا وَزَكِّهَا فَأَنْتَ خَيْرُ مَنْ زَكَّاهَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْتَ وَلِيُّنَا وَمَوْلَانَا وَنِعْمَ الْمَوْلَىٰ وَنِعْمَ النَّصِيرُ وَلَا حَوْلَ وَلَا قُوَّةَ إِلَّا بِاللَّهِ الْعَلِيِّ الْعَظِيمِ</span></p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَّا بَعْدُ أَقُولُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى ذِي كِتَابِهِ الْكَرِيمِ بَعْدَ أَنْ أَقُولَ أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِيمِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ بِلَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَاللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span></p>
<p>Once again, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>. In the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>st couple of sessions we learned about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>I hope I was able to provide a clear understanding of what that word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> means and what it represents. Just to quickly reiterate, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is a combination of unconditional love, constant care, genuine concern, and an unlimited supply of mercy and compassion. And the two frequently used derivatives of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>, right? And the attribute <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> highlights the intensity, the urgency, promptness, but also the interim and the temporary state of that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, whereas the attribute <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>, it highlights the long-term and permanent qualities of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>.</p>
<p>We then studied the root word of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> itself in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>st session, which comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحَمْ</span>, and it literally means the womb of a mother. We saw some, you know, magnificent examples that the womb provides in order to depict and demonstrate the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. You know, we saw, we drew some parallels to understand the role that the womb p<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ys in development of the fetus and the role that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> p<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ys in the development of a human being. And we, like, for example, we know that how the womb takes a shapeless drop of liquid and by its <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> it transforms it into a living child. And so we can draw the analogy that, you know, it is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> that shapes and transforms our spiritual existence, our spiritual being, the same way, right? His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> takes human beings in their raw and ignorant form, a form that really has no goal, no purpose outside the confines of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, and then he transforms them into these incredible personalities that, you know, we do not even mention their names without adding <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةُ اللهِ عَلَيْهِ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span>, right?</p>
<p>And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةُ اللهِ لِلْعَالَمِينَ</span>. That''s one of his titles, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةُ اللهِ لِلْعَالَمِينَ</span>. He is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> for the entire world because he took on the role of a mother''s womb in shaping and nurturing the spiritual existence of humanity. Not just for the people of his time, not just for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span>, the companions, but for all of humanity till the end of time.</p>
<p>We know how a mother suffers in order to provide comfort to her child. You know, how she protects them, how she protects it, nurtures it, feeds it, how she goes through various hardships, pain and suffering in order to transform that shapeless mass into that living, breathing child. So in the same way <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, through his various intermediaries, you know, his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَئِمَّةٌ</span>, his prophets, his messengers, he shapes our spiritual existence in the same way. And historically, just like the pregnant mother, historically we see that, you know, these people suffered various hardships as well to deliver the message of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> to their nations, in order to, you know, in strive and change the human condition. Just as a mother suffers and endures pain for the development of her child, in the same way these prophets and messengers, they''ve suffered at the hands of humanity for their own benefit. Just as the fetus becomes the reason for their hardship, nations become a source of hardship for these messengers, but they endure all of it for their development. So there''s a perfect analogy between the two.</p>
<p>So today I want to talk about some of the manifestations of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> and hopefully share a different perspective with you.</p>
<p>Because one of the most amazing qualities of the womb, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحَمْ</span>, is that it expands. Right? The womb expands to accommodate the needs of the fetus. And that provides us with a beautiful reminder of the fact that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> expands to fulfill not only the needs of His creation, but also to forgive their shortcomings, to forgive their sins and transgressions. His immense forgiveness for us is one of the manifestations of His infinite compassion towards His creation.</p>
<p>Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> knows that we are people of extremely weak character. We are people who are ens<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ved by our worldly desires, our passions, our caprice. He knows that, but then He also knows that we are trying our best in our efforts to move towards Him. We''re all trying, doing whatever we can to move towards Him, whether it''s, you know, centimeter by centimeter or inch by inch, in whatever way we can. We are making an effort somehow, however small it is. You know, we are trying to move towards Him, so He compensates for that weakness, and He strengthens our shortcomings through His forgiveness. So there''s a beautiful verse in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> that I want to share with you. It says:</p>
<p>‏ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ يَعِبَادِىَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَسْرَفُوا۟ عَلَىٰٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ لَا تَقْنَطُوا۟ مِن رَّحْمَةِ ٱللَّهِ ۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَغْفِرُ ٱلذُّنُوبَ جَمِيعًا ۚ إِنَّهُۥ هُوَ ٱلْغَفُورُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ</span> ‎ So He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ يَا عِبَادِيَ الَّذِينَ أَسْرَفُوا عَلَى أَنْفُسِهِمْ</span> O my s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves who have transgressed against themselves by their sins. Now, what s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves is He talking about? Right? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدٌ</span> — now you should all know what that means, right? He''s not talking to the disbelievers or the polytheists or the idol worshippers.</p>
<p>No, He''s talking to us, those who have submitted, who have developed a certain awareness of their master and they have willingly submitted themselves to His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>. That''s an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدٌ</span>, right? So He''s talking to these s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves. He''s talking to us Muslims, as believers, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا عِبَادِي</span>. Now, linguistically this form of calling someone implies an extreme closeness. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا عِبَادِي</span>, this form of addressing someone implies an extreme form of closeness and it implies extreme love, extreme intimacy.</p>
<p>Right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا عِبَادِي</span> implies a deep connection or an intense love in that statement. If it was just a casual call, like a master calling their servants, He would have said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَيُّهَا الْعِبَادُ</span>. And that would have meant just like, all s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves. But there would be no intimacy or love in that statement. But in this verse He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا عِبَادِي</span>, which represents an extreme closeness. Oh my beloved s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves, my revered s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves, my cherished s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves.</p>
<p>You see? You understand the difference? So there''s a deep intimacy in that call. And what type of s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves is He addressing? Faithful s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves? You know, trustworthy s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves, obedient s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves, exemp<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ry s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves? No. The ones who have crossed all limits and all boundaries of transgressions against themselves, the ones who have caused severe harm to their souls by living a life of sin, by living a life of hopelessness, by living a life of disobedience to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, by living a lifestyle that is harmful and detrimental to their spiritual progression.</p>
<p>These are the type of s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves He is addressing. But there is something so beautiful that the scho<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>rs point out about this verse, which is again the use of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا عِبَادِي</span> that precedes this statement. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is still calling them my s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves, you see. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is still saying that no matter who you are and no matter what you''ve done, you are still My s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves. So you can see that there is absolutely no abandonment on the part of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>Just as the womb does not abandon its fetus if it''s deformed, or you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> constantly maintains that re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>tionship of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> with us, even though we may be the worst of the worst. We may be the most sinful people on the face of this earth who are constantly walking on very thin ice, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, He still maintains that re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>tionship and that connection with us through the use of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا عِبَادِي</span> — my s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves, my beloved s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ves, right?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. The ones who have done severe harm to their souls and to themselves. Never ever lose hope in the mercy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, not even the slightest bit. I mean, I won''t get going, I mean there''s a lot of technical details here, I won''t go into them, but I just want to talk about the use of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span> at the beginning here. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span> here right at the beginning, it indicates unconditional absolutism, you know, unconditional absoluteness, like without the shred of a doubt. You know how we have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>? That also begins with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>, because that''s what it indicates, right? It has that absolute finality in that statement, that there is absolutely no God except <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Right?</p>
<p>That''s when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span> is used at the beginning of a statement. So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is saying that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَقْنَطُوا۟ مِن رَّحْمَةِ ٱللَّهِ</span>. Under no circumstances, with complete certainty, I''m making it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> on you. I''m making it impermissible. I''m forbidding you to lose hope in the mercy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Ever. Why? And this is something amazing about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> never just tells us something and then not give us a reason to believe in it.</p>
<p>So why should we not lose hope in the mercy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>? Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is capable of forgiving all your sins and transgressions. How is that possible? I have spent an entire lifetime in his disobedience. How is it possible for Him to forgive all my sins? Because indeed, without the shred of a doubt, He and only He, most definitely, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَفُورُ</span>, the constantly forgiving. This word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَفُورُ</span>, it follows the same phonetic pattern as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>.</p>
<p>So He''s not just forgiving, He''s constantly forgiving. He''s constantly merciful. That''s why both are used at the same time, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَفُورُ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيم</span>, constantly forgiving, constantly merciful. Nothing is beyond His capacity, and there is nothing that is out of His reach. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> can and will forgive all your sins.</p>
<p>So the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَفُورُ</span> is so amazing. It really gets to the essence of this concept of forgiveness in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. When <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> talks about His ability to forgive, He uses three different attributes for Himself in terms of forgiveness, and they all come from the same root word, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَرَ</span>. Okay, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَرَ</span>, it literally means to cover something up, to put a cover on something. The Arabic word for helmet, you know, the word for helmet in Arabic is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِخْفَر</span>, comes from the same root. When you put a helmet on your head, can somebody see your head? No, it''s covered, right? It''s hidden. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَكْفِرَة</span> means to forgive or to cover up the sins so that it is completely out of sight. Nobody can see it, like it never existed.</p>
<p>Now, like I said, there are three different forms that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> uses in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> to highlight basically his, which are all derivatives of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِرَة</span>. The first is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِر</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> calls himself <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِرِ الذَّنْبِ</span>, right? There is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَة</span> actually called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِر</span>, in that He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَنْزِيلُ الْكِتَابِ مِنَ اللَّهِ الْعَزِيزِ الْعَلِيمِ، غَافِرِ الذَّنْبِ وَقَابِلِ التَّوْبِ شَدِيدِ الْعِقَابِ</span>. So linguistically the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِر</span> means the one who immediately forgives.</p>
<p>Someone who does not hesitate in forgiving. Who does not have to think about, you know, let me think about it, if I, give me some time, I''ll forgive you. No. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِر</span> is someone who will instantly forgive without any kind of hesitation. He does not have to think about it before he forgives. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِر</span>, right? The second variation that he uses in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَّار</span>.</p>
<p>Ask forgiveness of your master, indeed he is a perpetual forgiver. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَّار</span> is the one who forgives abundantly. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِر</span> was the one who forgives instantly, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَّار</span> is the one who forgives abundantly. The one who has no limitations in terms of what he is going to forgive and what he''s not going to forgive. The one who can continue to forgive regardless of how much sin exists or what variation or what type of sin it is.</p>
<p>You know how we say, you know, I will forgive everything but I can never forgive betrayal. Or I can never forgive lies, or I can never forgive rudeness. We all have certain things that we consider unforgivable, right? Okay, I''m going to forgive all of these things, but there are these things that I will not forgive, there is, right? But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَّار</span>, is someone who has no limitations in what he can and cannot forgive. He can forgive everything if one truly repents. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَّار</span>.</p>
<p>The third variation is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفُور</span>, which, as I said, is on the same phonetic pattern of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, and this is the highest and the most intense form of his forgiveness, because it is somebody who''s constantly or permanently forgiving. You know, even a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَّار</span>, who is, even if I am, let''s say, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَّار</span> and I''m able to forgive everything, how many times will you do the same thing before I say, you know what, enough is enough, now I''ve forgiven you 10 times, 20 times, 50 times, 100 times, that''s it, now I''m not gonna forgive you, right? So even a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَّار</span>, with his ability to forgive everything, could have a limitation in terms of, he could have a number. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not just <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِر</span>, he''s not just <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَّار</span>, he''s also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفُور</span>, which means linguistically that he is permanently forgiving, right?</p>
<p>So you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِر</span>, you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَّار</span>, and then you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفُور</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفُور</span> is the one, again it''s on the same phonetic pattern of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, which means constantly or permanently forgiving. And this is the form that we are talking about in this verse. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, Indeed he is constantly forgiving and constantly merciful. So some scho<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>rs say that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفُور</span> is kind of a combination of all the other forms as well, because you can be, they could be somebody who is instantly forgiving, somebody who''s abundantly forgiving, but then how do you deal with a creature that constantly makes mistakes, that constantly transgress, who is insan, who forgets, who constantly needs reminders, who constantly slips and falls? How do you deal with such a person? Because in that situation both the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِر</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَّار</span> fall short. So that''s why it is complemented with this final form, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفُور</span>, which is constant.</p>
<p>So no matter how many times, what type, everything about forgiveness is covered between these three forms. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So unflinching loyalty, provision of infinite hope, a never-ending permanent re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>tionship, an intense, immediate, abundant forgiveness. All of these things are essential manifestations of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. So forgiveness is one of the beautiful manifestations of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. Another manifestation of that expansion or that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, it manifests in the form of his infinite rewards for trivial acts.</p>
<p>You know how we have these holy nights in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> offers like a ridiculous amount of reward? So some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَشِفَاء</span> is coming in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَعْبَان</span>, right? 14 <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَعْبَان</span>, and I think it''s like what? 20 <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجّ</span>? Right? What is the amount of... people have done <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجّ</span> in this group, right? What kind of effort does it take to do a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجّ</span>? Those of us who''ve done <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجّ</span>, we know that, right?</p>
<p>It''s like... it kills you, it drains you completely by the time you know everybody falls sick, everybody in that sickness they have to do all of these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْكَان</span>, by the time you come home you need like a one month vacation, right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجّ</span> is an extremely physically tiring act and it has an immense reward for it, but then there are nights in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> just offers 20 times that reward for a single amount.</p>
<p>How much effort does a thumb washer take compared to a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجّ</span>? Nothing! There''s no comparison, yet you get the... if you do it with the right intention, you get it... you get a reward of 20 <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجّ</span>. So today I want to share with you a perspective of why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> offers such rewards and hopefully you''ll get a real appreciation for the intention behind these rewards, and to exp<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>in that I want to, you know, narrate a story with you.</p>
<p>The, you know, the holy month of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> is around the corner, right? And we know that it is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> to do a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تِكَاف</span> in this month. And what is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تِكَاف</span>? It is the act of retiring oneself to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> for a short period of time exclusively for the remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> in silence. In India, Pakistan, people spend like at least three days. I think the those four bashikhs that we have, I think up till <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْلَةُ الْقَدْرِ</span> they spend those nights and days in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span> in which they constantly pray and they recite <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, they do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَزْبِي</span>, or they reflect on their lives, whatever it is, just retiring for the sake of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ تَعَالَى</span>. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> loves that, right? And for that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> offers a huge reward. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he stated in a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> that the reward of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>, he says ...............</p>
<p>So, whoever stays in the state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اتَّقَاهُ</span> for one single day, just one day and one night, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will distance him from the Hellfire by three trenches, and each trench will be the distance between east and west. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. That''s for spending one day in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> in the state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span>. What is that reward again? He will be distanced from the Hellfire by three trenches, and the length of each trench will be the distance between East and West.</p>
<p>That illustrates how beloved this act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَاف</span> is to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, right? So just keep that reward in mind. So one day during the month of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ عَبَّاسٍ</span>, who was the cousin of the Prophet, he enters a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> with the intention of spending a day in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَاف</span>, right, in order to earn that immense reward that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has promised. Soon after he begins his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span>, he sees a Muslim brother in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> looking very sad.</p>
<p>So he walks up to him and he inquires about his sadness. And the man says, O cousin of the Prophet, I owe a financial debt to someone that I need to pay back but I am not able to pay it because I don''t have any money, and the weight of that debt, that obligation, is crushing my soul. People in those days were different than us. You know how we get burdened by the financial obligation that, oh my God, I gotta pay mortgage every month, I gotta pay the bills every month and everything. We look at the money, they looked at the obligation itself, the fact that I owe this man money. That is how, you know, it was very different perspective in those days. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ عَبَّاسٍ</span> says to this man, would you like me to go to your lender and talk to him on your behalf?</p>
<p>Maybe I can convince him to forgive your debt or at least reduce it or maybe extend the timeline. So, would you like me to do that? The man says, &quot;Of course, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا ابْنَ عَبَّاسٍ</span>. I''d be delighted if you did that.&quot; So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ عَبَّاسٍ</span> immediately gets up, walks out of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> and begins putting on his slippers. Now, what happens when if you''re in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِتْقَاف</span> and you step outside the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span>, what happens? Your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَاف</span> is broken, right?</p>
<p>So suddenly this man, he realizes and he says, &quot;Oh my God, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ عَبَّاسٍ</span> was here for his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَاف</span>. Now he''s breaking his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَاف</span> for me. Oh my God, what have I done? He came to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> for an act of worship and I am now the reason for him to break his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَاف</span>. That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>st thing I wanted.&quot; So he gets up and he rushes towards him and he says, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا ابْنَ عَبَّاسٍ</span>, don''t you realize what you''ve done? Don''t you remember what you were here for? You''ve just broken your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَاف</span>.&quot;</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ عَبَّاسٍ</span>, he looks at this man and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> narrates that tears begin to roll down from his eyes. And this is something I find really interesting, because anyone who, if you look at, if you study <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, right, anyone who narrates the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, this is something that you will see. Like after the death of the Prophet, if any <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> is narrated after that, you''ll always see that tears begin to roll down their eyes, because that was the connection they had with the Prophet, right? The second they would remember him, they would cry.</p>
<p>So he says, with tears rolling down from his eyes, he says, &quot;I remember my Prophet telling me that if one of you was to strive for the need of his Muslim brother, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> rewards him with the blessings of 10 years of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَاف</span>.&quot;</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Ten years of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَاف</span>, not one day, not one month, not one year, 10 years of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَاف</span>. What did I just tell you? What was the reward of one single day of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَاف</span>? A distancing from Hellfire by three trenches, each trench having a length of the distance between the East and West. That''s the reward for one day of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَاف</span>. Are we able to do that? Can we do a week of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَاف</span>? Seven days? When was the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>st time we did a week of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَاف</span>? We can''t. No, one day is challenging enough. And what''s the reward for striving for the needs of a Muslim brother? Ten years of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِتْقَاف</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>Now when you hear this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, after hearing this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, what do you naturally think of? Right, from now that I''ve shared this reward with you, what do you think? You''ll be like, &quot;Oh my God, I should never miss an opportunity to help anyone.&quot; Right? This is such a great opportunity to gain the closeness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, which is true. Sometimes the fastest way to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s blessing is creation itself. No doubt about that, right? Other people, doing good to them. So generally when people hear this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, they rush to help others. When they find out that somebody is in need, they will do whatever they can to help that person out. They keep their ears open for an opportunity to help someone.</p>
<p>And that is an extremely beautiful thing. But what I want to point out today is something much higher and much deeper in this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>. I want to switch your perspective and focus it on the intention of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. This <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> is actually amazing, and I''ll exp<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>in why, and I''ll give you an example so that you can understand that.</p>
<p>So let''s say I walk into a room, all of you are there, and I see that one of you in this room is very sad, okay? Let''s say my niece Maryam is very sad, right? I find out that for some reason Mimi is looking really sad today. So I say, &quot;Look, I''ll give $10 million, $10 million to anyone who makes her smile. I''ll give 10 million dol<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>rs to the one who makes Mimi smile.&quot; Now what do you think is going to happen? Ten million bucks? Everyone in the room is going to try to make her smile, right?</p>
<p>Everyone is going to flock around her, throwing their best jokes at her to try to make her smile. That''s what would happen. Now, most people will look at that gesture, will look at my gesture, and say, &quot;Oh, you''re so rich. I mean, you must have loads of money that you''re willing to give 10 million dol<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>rs just to make her smile. You''re just throwing away 10 million dol<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>rs for a smile.&quot; But then there are a very few who will get to the essence of that gesture and say, &quot;Wow, you must love her so much. You must love her so much that her one smile is worth 10 million dol<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>rs to you.</p>
<p>She must mean the world to you.&quot; You see the difference? Because that is the reality. The reality is that I love her so much that I''m willing to pay 10 million dol<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>rs to the one who makes her smile. So when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, &quot;I''ll give the reward of 10 years of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِكَافٌ</span> to the one who fulfills Asif''s needs,&quot; even though the general understanding of that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> becomes a motivation to do it for that reward, what most people fail to see is that how much <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> loves me.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا اللهُ</span>, You love me so much that You''d give that much reward to anyone who fulfills my needs and removes my constrictions for me. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. You see that? So another beautiful way to interpret this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, and the true interpretation of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, is: O you who is feeling low, O you who is feeling depressed, O you who is feeling burdened and constricted because of your troubles, it is you I am looking at, it is you I am focused on, it is you I am concerned about, it is you that I am opening doors, because I want to see you happy, because I love you, and for your happiness I will reward anyone with this amount of ridiculous <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَوَاب</span> if he strives to bring you ease.</p>
<p>This is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> that He has for each and every one of us. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. And this transforms my perspective entirely when I see another person in need. Because now I see this person as the one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> loves. So much that He''s willing to reward me with such a generous amount of blessing just to help him out. It''s no longer about me and my reward, it''s about his ease.</p>
<p>So then how beloved would I become in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s eyes for helping him out? Let''s say you love a person. If somebody finally does make Mimi smile and it makes her happy, reward or no reward, that person will become closer to my heart because I love Mimi. If someone did something really nice for a person that you love, how would that make you feel about that person? He would come closer to your heart, isn''t it?</p>
<p>So after understanding this perspective, that closeness towards <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is what motivates me now to help another person, and not so much the reward itself. Because now I see that reward as a gesture for the one in suffering. So I hope you really begin to appreciate the reason for these immense rewards for the most simple acts. Right? It is the love He has for us that He''s willing to shower us with blessings for the most trivial acts of kindness.</p>
<p>You know, He''ll give us the reward of 10, 20 <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجّ</span> for a couple of hours of worship, not because of the worship, but for the love of the fact that we are taking the time out to stand before Him in remembering Him. You see, it''s not the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُكُوعٌ سُجُودٌ</span> that He cares about. It''s that love. So I hope this example, and, you know, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> and this narration of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ عَبَّاسٍ</span>, it transforms our perspective in our future worships and things like that, because we just do not really see the love behind the reward. We just look at the reward and we sometimes do it for that, or we just, you know. So that''s another example of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s immense <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. And another one is His response in afflictions, and I''ll give you another example.</p>
<p>Some of you might know this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> in which he tells us that when you visit a sick person, ask him to pray for you and your forgiveness. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> says that when you visit a sick person, ask that sick person to pray for you and your forgiveness. Now, if you think about it, that sounds completely backwards, doesn''t it? I mean, shouldn''t you be the one praying for that sick person?</p>
<p>He''s the one in need, so we should be praying that may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> grant him help, because he seems to be in need of my prayers. But the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> says ask him to pray for you and your forgiveness. What is that about? Let me give you an example to make this more re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>table to you. Alright? I''ll use the example of my daughter, but, you know, there are many parents in this group, we can all substitute, right?</p>
<p>So imagine my daughter falls very ill... God forbid. May <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> protect all our children from all kinds of trials and hardships. But just as an example to exp<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>in this better, to help you understand this concept, let''s say my daughter falls very sick and she is admitted in the ER. The doctors come and tell me that she is in a lot of pain. She''s not being able to breathe properly.</p>
<p>Now, as a father, I can''t even imagine what state I would be in if that ever happened. There is nothing more painful for a parent than to see her own child suffer, whether it''s physical, mental, spiritual, doesn''t matter. So the doctor tells me that she cannot breathe and she needs to be put on a venti<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>tor. Now, how would I feel about that? How would I react to that?</p>
<p>At that moment, when she is there lying in bed, in that state, is there any other p<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>ce in this world that I would rather be? Of course not, right? I would not leave my daughter''s side even for a second. I''d sit right next to her holding her hand because I love her so much. So I''ll constantly be by her bedside praying for her. Now finally <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> answers my prayers and she wakes up.</p>
<p>And she wakes up, she looks at me and she says, &quot;You know, Babu, I want ice cream.&quot; Now, what do you think I''ll do? You want ice cream? Of course. What f<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>vor do you want? If I can''t find it there, I''ll drive anywhere I have to to get that f<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span>vor of ice cream for her, right? Isn''t that what I would do? Can you imagine how close I''m feeling to her right now at this point, to see her wake up from that state?</p>
<p>What else do you want, sweetheart? Is there anything else I can do for you? Is there anything else I can get for you? You see? And it so happened that right before she fell sick, we had a big fight. She disobeyed me. She was very rude to me. She said some very harsh things and I was really angry with her when this whole thing happened. Now she wakes up from that state and she remembers what she did and she says, &quot;Babu, I''m sorry for what I did.</p>
<p>Please forgive me.&quot; What do you think I''m gonna say? Forgive you? Sweetheart, there''s nothing to forgive. I''m not upset with you at all. I don''t care about the fight. I don''t care about what you said. Don''t even think about it, right? Isn''t this what we would do as parents? Is there anything unnatural about what I''m saying? Now, if this is how much we love our children, can you even imagine how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> must love His creation?</p>
<p>According to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> holds the love of 70 mothers for every single one of His creation. If in this kind of affliction I feel so close to my daughter, when a creation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> goes through his trials and afflictions, can you imagine how close <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is with him at that point? Look how ready I was to respond to anything that my daughter asked for in that moment, instantly.</p>
<p>I am extremely limited by my capabilities. But when a human being, a creation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, when he is in pain, and he asks for something from the depth of his heart, whether it is for himself or for somebody else, do you think <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> would refuse him in that moment? Because when we are in a state of affliction, in that moment <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is closest to us, more than any other human being. In that moment, when we turn to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for forgiveness with true repentance, there is nothing that He would not forgive. No matter what sin he or she may have committed, no matter how horribly that person might have behaved, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will forgive that instantly.</p>
<p>I mean, I do not hesitate for a second in forgiving my child regardless of what she did in that state. Can you even imagine <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s mercy? The one who identifies himself with love, with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, who holds the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> of 70 mothers in His heart for His creation, do you think He would refuse to forgive? If I was mad at my son and my daughter after waking up says to me, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَابَا</span>, please forgive my brother,&quot; do you think I would refuse?</p>
<p>Oh my' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'manifestations of rehma';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || ' God, you are in such a difficult state, you are in so much pain, you are suffering right now. And even in this state you are concerned about your brother? You want me to forgive him? Absolutely. Your request is granted. Of course I forgive him. I''ll give you anything you want right now. All you have to do is ask. Tell me, what else can I do for you?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, that is a human state. A parent''s love for a child. What would be the love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for the creation? So can you appreciate the beauty of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> now? Why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> said that when you see somebody, when you visit a sick person or somebody in affliction, ask them to pray for you? Because as I''m going through my affliction, when I raise my hands and I ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to fulfill the needs of another person, or if I ask for my own forgiveness, or if I ask for, you know, somebody else''s forgiveness, there is no way <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> turns down my request.</p>
<p>Because I am extremely close to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in my moment of trial. He is right by my bedside, holding my hand. This is how His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> manifests. And this is why trials become the perfect opportunity for us to ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for our needs. Trials and afflictions could become one of the best experiences one can have, you know, an experience of awareness and the closeness of the love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. May <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> grant us all the wisdom and the true perspectives of seeing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and His true intentions behind these acts of rewards and forgiveness and things like that. So I hope, you know, this gave you a good perspective of how His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> manifests.</p>
<p>I mean, I just took two examples of afflictions. There are so many, but I just wanted to talk about the most important, the most common ones that we come across in our daily life. So I hope this perspective helps us all in our efforts in moving towards Him.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'manifestations of rehma';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ، بِسْمِ اللهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ، اللَّهُمَّ رَبَّنَا لَكَ الْحَمْدُ كُلُّهُ، وَلَكَ الْمَجْدُ كُلُّهُ، وَبِيَدِكَ الْخَيْرُ كُلُّهُ، وَإِلَيْكَ يَرْجِعُ الْأُمُورُ كُلُّهُ، عَلَانِيَتُهُ وَسِرُّهُ، سُبْحَانَ ذِي الْعِزَّةِ وَالْجَبَرُوتِ، سُبْحَانَ الْحَيِّ الَّذِي لَا يَمُوتُ، سُبْحَانَ الْحَيِّ الْحَلِيمِ، سُبْحَانَ الْبَاعِثِ الْوَارِثِ، سُبْحَانَ الْعَظِيمِ الْأَعْظَمِ، تَبَارَكَ اللهُ أَحْسَنُ الْخَالِقِينَ، الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي أَنْزَلَ عَلَى عَبْدِهِ الْكِتَابَ وَلَمْ يَجْعَلْ لَهُ عِوَجًا، وَالْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًا وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ شَرِيكٌ فِي الْمُلْكِ وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ وَلِيٌّ مِنَ الذُّلِّ وَكَبِّرْهُ تَكْبِيرًا، وَالْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي نَحْمَدُهُ وَنَسْتَعِينُهُ وَنَسْتَغْفِرُهُ وَنُؤْمِنُ بِهِ وَنَتَوَكَّلُ عَلَيْهِ، وَنَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنْ شُرُورِ أَنْفُسِنَا وَمِنْ سَيِّئَاتِ أَعْمَالِنَا، مَنْ يَهْدِهِ اللهُ فَلَا مُضِلَّ لَهُ، وَمَنْ يُضْلِلْهُ فَلَا هَادِيَ لَهُ، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُ اللهِ وَرَسُولُهُ، أَرْسَلَهُ اللهُ تَعَالَى بِالْهُدَى وَدِينِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ وَكَفَى بِاللهِ شَهِيدًا، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيَّ بْنَ أَبِي طَالِبٍ وَصِيُّهُ وَوَزِيرُهُ، فَصَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَعَلَى آلِهِمْ وَسَلَّمَ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرًا، اللَّهُمَّ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَا إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ، اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا، اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَى وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ فَأَمَّا بَعْدُ يَقُولُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى فِي كِتَابِهِ الْكَرِيمِ بَعْدَ أَنْ قُلْتُ أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ بِلَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَاللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span> Once again, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, today we''re going to look at the linguistic meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>. The root for the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> is actually <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَّنَا</span>. Okay, the root word for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> is actually <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَّنَا</span>. And so what the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرَب</span>s do is that they combine the two bars at the end and they put a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَدَّة</span> on top of it, right? That crown at the top, that''s called the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَدَّة</span>. So they just put that.</p>
<p>And then it basically becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>. So that is just how the root word works for this. Now, if you look at the English translation of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>, you''ll find that it is generally translated as Lord. And I don''t really understand what the word Lord means. The only Lord I know of is the Lord of the Rings. So Lord is not a good translation for the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَبٌ</span> in the eyes of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرَب</span> linguist, because it does not reveal the true meaning and the power of this word.</p>
<p>So let''s begin by understanding <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> from a linguistic perspective. The first thing to know is that the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> is a composite word, just like the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰه</span> that we studied in the previous session. In the previous session, we learned that the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰه</span> is not exclusive to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, right? It can be used for many other entities in our lives. Sim<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>rly, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرَب</span> is also not exclusive to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, okay?</p>
<p>It has several linguistic implications. This word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرَب</span> is used for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, but it can also be used for people, you know, human beings, and things other than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Just like the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span> can be used for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, is used for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, but then it can be used for other things as well. Sim<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>rly, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرَب</span> can be used for people, it can be used for parents, for teachers, for guardians, you know. So there are many different ways in which the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرَب</span> is used in classical <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرَب</span>ic. So that''s the first thing I want you to understand, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> is not specific to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/78/b8244e3f-11ba-4e9b-a182-4022d0ce982f.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>It can be used for human beings and it can be used for other things as well, okay? Now let''s understand what does the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> mean. The classical definition of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّبُّ هُوَ الْمَلِكُ وَالسَّيِّدُ وَالْمُرَبِّي وَالْمُرْشِدُ وَالْمُنْعِمُ وَالْقَيِّمُ</span>. A true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> is someone who is six things. Or I can say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> is six things. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيَّاد</span>, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبِّي</span>, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْشِد</span>, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">منعم</span> and He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيِّم</span>.</p>
<p>And the interesting thing here is that He is all these six things at the same time. This is the part that really is important. The meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> is the combination of all these six meanings at the same time. And this will become clear. So let''s look at each of these meanings individually. Let''s look at the first one. The first meaning is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, which means owner.</p>
<p>Someone who has ownership of something or someone is called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, and that thing that he owns is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَٰلِكِ</span>. Okay, so there''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, and what he owns is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَٰلِكِ</span>. This is not the English <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَٰلِكِ</span> that cows produce. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرَب</span>ic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَٰلِكِ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلْقِيَا</span>, it means property, and the owner of that property is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>. Okay, now do we own things in life? Do we own phones, iPads, computers, cars, houses?</p>
<p>Yes. So that makes us <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> as well, right? And because owner is one of the definitions of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, so by virtue of that ownership it makes us <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> as well. You understand? So I am the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> of my car and the car is my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ملقيا</span>. You see? So do teachers have some ownership over their students? Sure. Do parents have ownership over young children? Yeah. I mean, despite how the kids might feel that, you know, it''s my life, Mom.</p>
<p>You can''t tell me what to do. You don''t own me. Well, despite all of these sentiments, you know, that children have today, I mean, and to be fair, the sentiments we had as kids growing up, the truth is that parents have an ownership over their children until they become adults. But do parents have ultimate and absolute ownership? No. Because even though parents do give birth to their children, they do not create them.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created them. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created the child and the parent. Therefore ultimate and absolute ownership belongs only to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. So you see where, how the generic sense of ownership becomes specific when it comes towards <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Right? So even though parents, teachers, they''re all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> in the sense of the word, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the absolute owner and the absolute <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> of His creation. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>. And if He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, what does that make us?</p>
<p>It makes us His property. That makes us <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ملقيا</span>. Okay? So that''s the first meaning. The second meaning of the word can be understood by the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيَّاد</span>, which complements the previous meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> meant owner, whereas <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيَّاد</span>, it means someone who has authority. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيَّاد</span> is the one who is in charge. You understand the difference? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> is someone who has ownership. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيَّاد</span> is the one who has authority.</p>
<p>So why are we... why am I using... why do they separate out these two things? Is it possible to own something and then not have complete authority over it? Sure, it''s possible. For example, I own my car, but do I still have to get it registered? Do I still have to get it inspected every year? Do I still have to drive within speed limits? Do I have to drive it on the road?</p>
<p>Can I just drive it on the sidewalk if I, you know, feel like it? No. Not unless I''m in Pakistan. So even though I own my car, I do not necessarily have complete authority over it. You understand? And the reverse of this is also true. I may have complete authority over something, I may be able to do whatever I please with something, but I may not have complete ownership of it.</p>
<p>Like for example, the computer in my office. I can do whatever I want with the software that is installed on it. I can install new stuff. I can uninstall old stuff. You know, nobody''s going to stop me or question me about that. But do I own that computer? No. My company owns it, right? So you understand the difference? I can own things but not have complete authority over it, or I can have complete authority over something but not necessarily have ownership of it.</p>
<p>Right? But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>, He has absolute ownership and He has complete authority. He has both at the same time. He can do whatever He wants, whenever He wants, you know, with this property, because we are all His property. If He wants to burn it, He can burn it. If He wants to tear it apart, He can tear it apart. If He wants to drown it, toss it, discard it.</p>
<p>He can do anything he wants at his will. You and I do not get to question him about it. Why? Because he has complete ownership. And he has complete authority over us and everything else in the universe. And even if we do complain and cry about it, guess what? It makes no difference to him. He does not care. Because he is owner, he and we are his property.</p>
<p>You know, when parents ground their children, if I take my daughter''s phone away, I mean, she can cry and complain as much as she wants, but she''s not getting it back because I am her <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>. And in that context, she and her phone are my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلْقِيَا</span>. I bought her that phone. And so if our crying and grumbling doesn''t get us anywhere with our own parents who are limited in their capacities as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>, so it surely will not make a difference to the one who is the absolute and ultimate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>.</p>
<p>Right? So this is something we really have to understand, that we are creation. He is the creator. He is the owner. We are property. And what we constantly seem to forget is that He is not the one on trial. We are. I mean, we''ve got it completely backwards. Today mankind has put <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in the stands and taken the seat of the judge, and we shamelessly question his every move. You know, if you are so powerful, then why don''t you eliminate evil from this world? If you are so loving and merciful, then, you know, how could you do this to me? I prayed to you. Why did I not get that job or that promotion? Right, I''m a good person. I pay my taxes. I pay my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>. I brush my teeth twice every day.</p>
<p>Why did you do this to me? You see? So we''ve got it completely backwards. The reality is that he is the judge. He is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>. He is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّد</span>. We are his property and his subjects. He has every right to question us. We have absolutely no right to question him. This is just based on the principle of ownership and property. Nobody can argue with this. And this is why I find this so amazing about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, is that He actually allowed us to understand the wisdom of His design.</p>
<p>You know, through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> and His messengers. He did not create us as robots. He did not create us like, I don''t know, a tree. He gave us intellect. So I feel that is an incredible act of mercy and generosity on His part, because He is not obligated to do anything for us. The one who subjugated the entire solar system is definitely capable of, you know, subjugating us, but he did not do that. And this is what I find so amazing about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, as this powerful, you know, being that has complete authority and ownership of us, right? He''s not there to service, but he does it anyway, because that is what a true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> does.</p>
<p>Anyway, so I hope it''s clear, the difference between, you know, these distinctions between ownership and authority, right? So that''s the first two things, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّد</span>. Okay, the third one is, now this is a very powerful word, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبِّي</span>. This is someone who basically ensures growth. Alright, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبِّي</span> is someone who ensures growth. Someone who takes care of something, who nurtures something so that it can grow, that''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبِّي</span>.</p>
<p>Now, is it possible for us to own something, have authority over it, but not take good care of it? Sure. You know, backyards in many houses. That''s a good example. People own cars but they never get them serviced. We own our bodies and yet we abuse it. We have ownership and authority over our bodies but rarely do people take good care of their bodies. So it is possible to have ownership and authority over something, but that does not necessarily mean that we will also take good care of it.</p>
<p>Understand? A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> is someone who not only owns, who not only has complete authority over what he owns, but then he also takes complete care of it. He ensures its proper growth. He nurtures it, and this process of taking care of one''s property and nurturing it is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span>. It''s called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span>, and this is a phenomenon we find everywhere in the world. This is not just specific to humans.</p>
<p>You know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> placed this design in His creations. There''s... a poet says... ... The depth of the ocean nurtures the white clear pearl. So he calls the ocean a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> because it helps develop that pearl within its shell. A tree is also called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> because it nurtures the fruit it bears. So there are all these different kinds of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> that we find in the world. And the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span>, and this is really important, the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> is all about setting limits, setting boundaries.</p>
<p>You cannot have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> without setting boundaries. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> is. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> of a plant would be to take care of it, you know, watering it at the right time, making sure it gets enough sunlight, protecting it from too much sunlight, protecting it from insects, from bugs. Right? So in other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> is a bunch of do''s and donts. It''s a bunch of do''s and donts that apply until the plant matures into a tree and starts bearing fruit.</p>
<p>You understand? If you just leave the plant to grow by itself, that''s not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span>. Because you''re not setting limits, you''re not setting boundaries. Sim<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>rly, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> of children is taking care of their physical needs. Their diets, their clothing, their mental needs, educating them, protecting them. It''s a bunch of do''s and donts that are applied to a child for their nurturing. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> is basically monitoring their progress from birth until they mature into a healthy, sound, decent human being.</p>
<p>From a physical point of view, from a psychological point of view, from a social point of view, that''s all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَةُ الْأَوْلَادِ</span>. Now, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَّة</span> a quick and easy process? Or is it a very long, very time-consuming process? It is very hard work, isn''t it? In fact, it is the most difficult process there is. It''s a never-ending, thankless, full-time job. Right? And parents do that, and this is why when we recite <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التَّحِيَّاتُ</span> in every prayer, what do we say at the end of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التَّحِيَّاتُ</span>?</p>
<p>We say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ اغْفِرْ لِي</span>, O <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> forgive me. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِوَالِدَيَّ</span>, and my parents. And have mercy on them just as they had mercy on me during my period of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> when I was young. Right? And this is such a beautiful statement, because in this statement we find the primary ingredient of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ اغْفِرْ لِي</span>, forgive me, forgive my parents and have mercy on them just as they had mercy on me.</p>
<p>So what we''re learning is that the primary ingredient of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>, of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span>, of nurturing is mercy. The primary component of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> is mercy. If there is no mercy, then there can be no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>. And what is mercy? It is a combination of love, care, and concern. It''s a combination of love, care, and concern. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> is made of. Have you ever seen people, you know, who shower all this TLC on plants?</p>
<p>Like they''ll actually spray each individual leaf with water and then clean them gently with a soft cloth. Right? That kind of love and that kind of care with that level of gentleness is imamate of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>. It is not harshness, it is not beating, it is not, you know, psychological, emotional, physical abuse, it is not guilt tripping into submission, it is love, it is patience, it is kindness.</p>
<p>That''s the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْلَام</span>ic way of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>. I mean, this is contrary to what we see in movies and society today, you know, but the reality is that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْلَام</span>ic way of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> has always been <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, it has always been mercy, and the proof of that is the life of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>. I mean, just think about this, right, our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he took the most vicious people, he took the most barbaric people, and without a single slap, without a single taunt, without a single sarcastic remark, without a single insult, he turned them into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللهُ عَنْهُ</span>, he turned them into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةُ اللهِ عَلَيْهِ</span>, the greatest titles possible for a human being, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span>.</p>
<p>I mean, we don''t mention the name of these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> without saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span>, without saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةُ اللهِ عَلَيْهِ</span>, and our Prophet did that. How? With his love, with his gentleness, with his kindness, forgiveness, patience, with his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>. And sometimes harshness is necessary in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>. There''s no question about that. But behind that harshness, the sentiment is still <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. It''s not frustration, it''s not anger, it is still <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>.</p>
<p>It is to guide the person. Because you have tried love, you have tried kindness, it''s not working, but you have to make sure that this person does the right thing, so now you resort to harshness. But even behind that harshness your primary ingredient is still love. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولِ اللَّهِ</span> life is the perfect example of that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>. And you know, and what did he ask in return is very interesting.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولَ اللَّهِ</span>, after devoting 23 years of his life to our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> — have you guys ever heard the last <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُطْبَة</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجَّةُ الْوَدَاعِ</span>, the last sermon of the Prophet when he was returning from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجّ</span>? That is something really incredible. In that he says, he says many things, but one of the things he says towards the end, when he''s ending his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُطْبَة</span>, he says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أُمَّتِي لَا تُسَوِّدُوا وَجْهِي يَوْمَ الْقِيَامَةِ</span>. O my people, do not embarrass me before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> on the Day of Judgment.</p>
<p>That''s what he says in the end. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أُمَّتِي لَا تُسَوِّدُوا وُجُوهَ الْقِيَامَةِ</span>. Do not embarrass me before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> on the Day of Judgment. You know, when a result does not meet expectations, if majority of the students fail in a class, doesn''t the principal question the teacher? What''s going on? Why are there so many failures in your class? And our Prophet, after 23 years of service and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span>, asked us for one thing in return.</p>
<p>Do not embarrass me before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> on the Day of Judgment. And so may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> make us among those who makes our, you know, who make our Prophet proud on that day. So that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>, right? And which is, comes from the act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span>, and the main ingredient of that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> is love, care and concern, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, right?</p>
<p>The fourth meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرَب</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْشِد</span>. He is the one who sets things in the right direction. The one who sets us on the right path, puts us to proper use. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْشِد</span>. The guide, right? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْشِد</span> comes from the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُشْد</span>, which in classical <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرَب</span>ic means to ripen. You know, when a fruit ripens, it''s the final stage for a fruit. That''s why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُشْد</span> also means rationality, it also means reason, it also means maturity, because those are the final stages of intellect.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْشِد</span> is the one who takes something in its raw form, works on it with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, with love, continues working on it, it gets involved in its <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> until it ripens, till it matures, and then he puts it to proper use. He sets it in the right direction. This is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْشِد</span>. So let''s say if you have a teacher who trains you, let''s say in computer sciences, and then also finds you a job so that you can now apply what you''ve learned, that would be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْشِد</span>.</p>
<p>And a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْشِد</span> is not someone who just trains you to maturity and then leaves you to rot. He actually designs a path for you. He guides you forward from your point of maturity. You know, he provides you with the opportunity to practice what you''ve learned. So that you and others can benefit from what you''ve learned. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْشِد</span>. You understand? So what we''re learning here is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> is not only someone who owns us, who has complete authority over us, who nurtures us with love, care and concern, but he is also the one to create opportunities for us so that we can put our training to use. He trains us by putting people in our path that will help us grow and develop. He''s the one who find the perfect friend for you. That friend who is going to help you grow and mature.</p>
<p>And then he will use that friend to guide you in putting that training to use. By becoming good friends to other people. That''s what a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْشِد</span> does. And this is something even parents and teachers cannot do at times. You know, they will complete the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَةٌ</span> of their children, but then they cannot always put them to proper use. You know, kids have to find their own path. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>, He goes the extra mile and He sets us on the right path after our training has been completed.</p>
<p>That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُشَدّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Alright, so that''s the fourth meaning. The next meaning comes from the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْعِم</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْعِم</span> is used for the one who bestows gifts, the one who gives blessings. It comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَم</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>, which means gift or blessing. We don''t have slavery today, but imagine for a second that if we owned a slave, right? Now, it is one thing to own a slave and it is an entirely different thing to take care of that slave, to nurture that slave, to put that slave to proper use, and then on top of everything bestow gifts upon the slave.</p>
<p>And the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَة</span> suggests gifts and benefits that are on top of what he deserves. Remember, gifts are not deserved things. A gift is not something you deserve. A gift, by definition, you know, they''re always beyond what is deserved. Isn''t that true? It is an expression of the love and mercy of the benefactor. If you deserved it, then that is salary. That is a pay. That is an earning. It''s not a gift. You understand the difference?</p>
<p>Now, interestingly, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرَب</span>ic word for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعَمْ</span>, which means yes'', also comes from the same root word as blessing. Why? Because what''s the natural human response to receiving a blessing? It''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعَمْ</span>, yes''. Right? Isn''t that interesting? And this is why I love this language. Every word is so perfectly connected. Every variation, every derivative is so perfectly connected to its root.</p>
<p>Anyway, so now imagine that you own a slave or an animal. Let''s say a goat, right? You own a goat and so you take care of it, right? Because it''s your property. But you don''t buy gifts. You''re not going to buy your goat jewelry, right? Why? Because you don''t need to. It''s a goat. It''s part of your property. So in the old days, slaves were no different than a goat or an animal, right? They were barely taken care of, let alone given gifts.</p>
<p>Who is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>? He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> and He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّد</span>, right? He is our absolute owner with complete authority over us, which makes us His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلْقِيَا</span>, His property. You see that? He is the shepherd, we are the sheep. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> is an owner who not only takes good care of His property, who not only has complete authority and control over it, who not only ensures its growth to maturity with love, kindness, concern, who not only looks after it, protects it, sustains it, puts it on the right path, but then on top of all of that He bestows numerous undeserving, generous gifts upon it.</p>
<p>Think about that for a second. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> as our absolute master does not owe us anything, and this is a very difficult concept for a lot of people to grasp, because we just think <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> owes us a lot. For some reason we just believe that, you know, He is supposed to do all of this stuff for us. No, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المَلِكِ</span>. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّيَّاد</span>. He doesn''t need to do anything for us. He does not owe us anything. Therefore anything and everything He gives us, it has to be a gift. He is not obligated to give us anything.</p>
<p>I did not do anything to deserve my eyesight. I did not do anything to pay for my hands. How much would you charge if I asked you to sell both your eyes to me? Forget both eyes. How much would you sell me one eye for? How much would you sell your hands for? How much is your ability to walk worth to you? We just cannot put a price on these things. These are priceless gifts.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> gave us all these precious gifts, but He did not just give them to us. Gifts, even though are an act of generosity, there is a whole philosophy behind gifts, which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span> we''ll talk about when we talk, you know, when we discuss <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>. But gifts, you know, there is a purpose for a gift, and He says that He will question us about these gifts on the Day of Judgment. On that day we will be asked about the greatest gift of all. What is the greatest gift of all? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>. Right? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعِين</span> is the superlative form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعَمْ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمْ</span>, and it means the greatest gift of all. What is that greatest gift of all that has been given to us?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, faith. That is the ultimate gift. On the day of judgment, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will ask us about our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, which is the greatest blessing of all. Otherwise, what was the purpose of giving us all these gifts and making us different from animals? Right? If I give you a gift, I expect you to use it for the purpose that I gave it to you for. So, if that was not the case, animals have eyes, animals have ears, right?</p>
<p>But they don''t have the intellect that we have. They don''t have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>. So the idea of all these gifts, and intellect most of all, is so that we can develop <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>. So that we can become different from animals. So that we can see creation, reflect upon it and then recognize him and try to get closer to him. You understand?</p>
<p>So now we''re learning that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> is someone who not only owns us, who not only has ultimate authority over us, who not only trains us, nurtures us with love, care and concern, kindness, patience until the point we reach maturity, who not only puts us on our chosen path, but then he also showers us with tons and tons of undeserving gifts.</p>
<p>Basically he helps us with all these gifts so that, to make our path easier, to make us comfortable, to make it pleasu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>le. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>. I mean, how powerful is this word? I mean, just think about that, right? Our understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> is so limited until we actually get into the linguistics of the word. So I hope this is really giving you the power of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>.</p>
<p>And the final meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيِّم</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيِّم</span> is the one that makes sure that things stay together. He makes sure that things do not fall apart. The one who has his constant attention and focus on things. Because when you are not constantly focused on something that is important, what happens? You drop the ball. Right? Things fall apart. People who enjoy gardening, they know that some plants are extremely delicate. If you don''t take care of them for even one day, they will die. Some people have fish for pets.</p>
<p>I mean, if you forget to feed them even for a single day, what happens? Nemo kebab for dinner, right? Right. I mean, ladies, if you leave your house unattended for one day, what happens? Right? So you understand what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيِّم</span> does. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيِّم</span> is the sustainer, the maintainer, the one who constantly focuses and keeps his attention on things. The one who never drops the ball, who cannot afford to drop the ball. That meaning is also embedded in the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>.</p>
<p>That''s why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱللَّهُ لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا هُوَ ٱلْحَىُّ ٱلْقَيُّومُ</span> ‎. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the only <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰه</span> who is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَيُّ</span>, who is the giver of life, and he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَيُّومُ</span>, the one who then also maintains that life by his constant attention on things. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيُّوم</span> is again the superlative form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيُّوم</span>, the one who constantly sustains, who constantly maintains, who never takes a break. Right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيُّوم</span>, you know, the superlative form, it''s actually showing that there is no break, there''s never a loss of attention.</p>
<p>So in other words, I''m not alive because I take care of myself. You know, one breath to the next is not because I eat healthy and because I exercise, or if because I follow a good diet, or because I live in a healthy environment. That is not why I''m alive. The only reason I am alive is because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> allows me to be alive, because he sustains me, not because of my efforts. You understand? There are plenty of ways I can cease to exist in a second.</p>
<p>Just crossing the road. One little earthquake, one snow blizzard, that''s it. You know that Indian actress, Sridevi, who just died? I mean, perfectly healthy woman, drowned in a bathtub. She probably had lots of plans for the next day, just as I do, just as you do. I''m sure she had a lot of short-term goals, long-term goals, right? All gone, ****. We exist because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيِّم</span> allows us to exist.</p>
<p>So we are completely and constantly dependent on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Another thing in this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَة</span>, you know, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱللَّهُ لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا هُوَ ٱلْحَىُّ ٱلْقَيُّومُ</span> ‎, we see two attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> that are mentioned, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَيُّ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَيُّومُ</span>. The one who gives life and the constant maintainer. Now why are those attributes used together? Every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتٌ</span> in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> when two attributes are used together they''re very connected. So why are these two used together?</p>
<p>Because the one who gives life also knows how to sustain it. In other words, only the one who gives life knows how to sustain it. He knows how long to sustain it, what stages and steps to take it through, how and when to end it, what happens after it ends. That entire plan of action for each individual creation, not just for human beings, all creation, all of creation.</p>
<p>That entire blueprint from beginning to end is already and completely planned out by the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيُّوم</span>. That is the difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيُّوم</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيُّوم</span>, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيُّوم</span> has it all planned out. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not making this up as He goes. The destiny of every single creation, the plan of every single creation''s progress has been already determined. He''s already thought about everything from beginning to end. That''s part of the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيُّوم</span>, to have a complete, perfect plan.</p>
<p>You can''t start something or maintain something without a plan, can you? When you decide to start a business, you don''t just choose what business you''re going to do by playing, I don''t know, you know, like rock paper scissors. I mean, you have to have an entire business plan, right? What your initial investment is going to be, how will you keep the business afloat, how will you grow the business, cost analysis, expense analysis, budget allocations, right?</p>
<p>You plan the whole nine years and you do all of this before you actually even begin the business. So you think <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> just created all of this without a plan? Not possible, right? So those are the six meanings that come together to give us the definition of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>. So now let''s look all of this together. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> is someone who is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, who has absolute ownership over his property.</p>
<p>At the same time he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّد</span>, he has complete and unconditional authority. At the same time he is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبِّي</span>, he ensures growth, takes care of us, nurtures us, protects us with love, care and concern and mercy. At the same time he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْشِد</span>, who brings us to physical and spiritual maturity and then sets us on his straight path, puts us to proper use, gives us the opportunity to practice what we''ve learned.</p>
<p>At the same time He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">منعم</span>, who showers us with undeserving and numerous gifts and blessings. And at the same time He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيِّم</span>, who sustains us for the extent of our duration. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> as our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> is the sum of all of these meanings all at the same time. He is not one of these things at different times of our lives. He is all of these things and he is all of these things at the same time.</p>
<p>His ownership includes his authority, which includes his nurturing, which includes his guidance and direction, which includes his blessings, which includes his sustenance, his provisions, his maintenance, all at the same time. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>So now I hope you have a much clearer understanding of what the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَرَبَ</span> means, and the point of learning these words linguistically, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰه</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَرَبَ</span>, all of this, is to start seeing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in a different light. Because we all have our own understanding of, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, but what these words do is that they help us remove the fog and, you know, make things clear for us. Okay, so this is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is and this is what He''s doing for us. And what that does is it helps us determine what we have to be like, right?</p>
<p>And so, you know, to conclude, I want to tie this understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> that I explained today with one of the earlier sessions in which I talked about this man of pure essence, if you remember, someone from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُولِي الْأَلْبَابِ</span>, who is deeply reflecting on the creations of the heavens and the earth. Right?</p>
<p>And he''s deeply reflecting on the conflict between the night and day, right? So, in that, he says, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ فِي خَلْقِ السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضِ وَاخْتِلَافِ اللَّيْلِ وَالنَّهَارِ لَآيَاتٍ لِأُولِي الْأَلْبَابِ</span>&quot; Right? That indeed in the creation of the heavens and the earth and the conflict between night and day there are clear signs for the people of pure essence. And I explain each of these words in a linguistically in quite a few depth in those sessions. And these are the people that are constantly reflecting upon the creations of the heavens and the earth, whether they are sitting down, whether they are standing up, whether they are lying down, basically all the time. And they are in deep reflection upon these creations of the heaven and the earth.</p>
<p>And then he says, at the end of his conclusion he says, after recognizing that there has to be a creator for the heavens and the earth and for himself, look at this. The word he uses for that creator is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>. You see that? Because when he reflected on the creations of the heaven and the earth, that is what he saw. He saw an absolute owner. He saw somebody who has complete authority, somebody who is nurturing and developing his creation with love, care, kindness and mercy, who is setting everything to its purpose, who is showering every creation with numerous undeserving gifts and blessings, and who is sustaining, maintaining, providing for them continuously without losing focus on them through the entire timeline of their existence.</p>
<p>This is why he said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَّنَا مَا خَلَقْتَ هَذَا بَطِلًۭا</span>&quot; Our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>, we did not create all of this without a purpose. You did not create all of this without a purpose. And this was an extremely beautiful and a very appropriate conclusion drawn by this person of intellect, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> validates that conclusion in his revelation. What is the very first <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَة</span> that was revealed to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>? ‏<span class="ar-inline" di' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'linguistic meaning of rabb';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'r="rtl" lang="ar">ٱقْرَأْ بِٱسْمِ رَبِّكَ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ</span> ‎</p>
<p>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱقْرَأْ بِٱسْمِ رَبِّكَ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ</span> ‎ Recite with the name of your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> who created you. Not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اقْرَأْ بِسْمِ اللهِ الَّذِي خَلَقَ</span> He could have said that, right? But look at this. In this very first verse, just think about this, the very first verse is being revealed to the Prophet and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is introducing himself to his messenger for the very first time. And what does he say? When you introduce yourself, how do you do that? Do you use your name or do you use your attribute? If I met you for the very first time in your life and I said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ</span>, I''m a teacher,&quot; or &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ</span>, I can do 200 push-ups,&quot; what would you say?</p>
<p>You''ll be like, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَعَلَيْكُمُ السَّلَامُ</span>, you''re also a weirdo,&quot; right? Because that''s not normal. When you introduce yourself, you always use your name. So, I would imagine that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> should have said, read with the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> who created you. Right? No, but He says, read in the name of your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> who created you. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> chose to introduce Himself as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>. Now, what does this tell you?</p>
<p>When I introduce myself as a teacher, what am I telling you? How am I characterizing myself? I''m characterizing myself as a teacher. In other words, what I''m saying is that my being a teacher is way more important to me than all my other attributes, including my name. Now, does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> have shortage of attributes? No. But even though <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is glorious beyond comprehension, he is magnificent beyond comprehension, he is powerful beyond comprehension.</p>
<p>What he''s saying to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, and to us by extension, is that when I interact with you, the most important relationship that''s going to exist between us is that of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>. That is how I want you to think of me. Not as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَلِيل</span>, not as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَظِيم</span>, not as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَوِيّ</span>, not as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَبِير</span>, not as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَكَبِّر</span>, but as what? As <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. I mean, we just take this for granted, you know, we just assume a lot of this stuff, but I just want you to take a moment to really let this sink in, just reflect on this.</p>
<p>When a king introduces himself, does he introduce himself in a humble way? Does he say, oh, I''m your neighbor, I got this, you know, big house next door. I''m your dad''s friend. I''m your caretaker. Does the king say that? No. He says I''m your king. Kneel before me. Bow down to me, right? That''s how a king introduces himself. If <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> chose to do that in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, let''s say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> introduced Himself in that way, would it befit Him?</p>
<p>Would that have been okay to do so? Would that have been acceptable for us slaves and properties? Absolutely! But what word does He choose to use instead? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>. And now that you actually understand the meaning and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> and the love behind this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, can you imagine how important this word must be to him? And so after understanding the meaning, the real meaning of this word, can you appreciate the kind of love, the kind of loving relationship he wishes to have with his creation?</p>
<p>And this is very important to understand. I mean, you know that it was not a coincidence that he introduced himself as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> in the first verse. This is very important from a thematic, you know, point of view as well, because if you look at the sequence of revelation, in the very first verse he says, right, recite in the name of your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> who created you. But what is the last verse that was revealed to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>?</p>
<p>Sequentially. On this day I have perfected your religion for you, and I have completed my blessings on you, and I have selected <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْلَام</span> as the way of life for you. ... and then he says, like, you know, that''s the completion of the verse, but then he ends the verse by saying ... have no doubt about it, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is all forgiving and all merciful. Here he does not use the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, you see that?</p>
<p>Because the entire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is an explanation of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is, all his attributes and everything, but he ends it by using his name. Of course he''s used his name everywhere else in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, but it''s really interesting to me that in the first verse sequentially he starts with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> and ends with ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَإِنِ ٱنتَهَوْا۟ فَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ غَفُورٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ</span> ‎. That''s the last statement that was revealed to the Prophet: have no doubt about it, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is all forgiving and all merciful. Begins the revelation with the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, and he ends it with his name <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, right, which is, which, which interestingly exactly matches the pattern of ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, in that you have the name <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> followed by the two attributes of mercy, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>. And how does he end revelation? The constantly forgiving, the constantly loving. He begins his message with his intense and constant love and mercy.</p>
<p>He ends his message with his constant forgiveness and his constant mercy. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. ... ... ...</p>
