<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/78/7a08c24b-14c5-4e03-baea-0851a90c77f9.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'linguistic meaning of rabb';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي لَا يَبْلُغُ مِدْحَتَهُ الْقَائِلُونَ، وَلَا يُحْصِي نَعْمَاءَهُ الْعَادُّونَ، وَلَا يُؤَدِّي حَقَّهُ الْمُجْتَهِدُونَ، الَّذِي لَا يُدْرِكُهُ بُعْدُ الْهِمَمِ، وَلَا يَنَالُهُ غَوْصُ الْفِطَنِ، الَّذِي لَيْسَ لِصِفَتِهِ حَدٌّ مَحْدُودٌ، وَلَا نَعْتٌ مَوْجُودٌ، وَلَا وَقْتٌ مَعْدُودٌ، وَلَا أَجَلٌ مَمْدُودٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَطَرَ الْخَلَائِقَ بِقُدْرَتِهِ، وَنَشَرَ الرِّيَاحَ بِرَحْمَتِهِ، وَوَتَّدَ بِالصُّخُورِ مَيَدَانَ أَرْضِهِ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَىٰ سَيِّدِ الْأَنْبِيَاءِ وَالْمُرْسَلِينَ خَاتَمِ النَّبِيِّينَ، السَّيِّدِ الْمُمَجَّدِ، الْبَشِيرِ الْمُصَدَّقِ، النَّذِيرِ الْمُؤَيَّدِ، الْمُصْطَفَى الْأَمْجَدِ، الْمَحْمُودِ الْأَحْمَدِ، أَبِي الْقَاسِمِ مُحَمَّدٍ</span></p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى وَصِيِّ ابْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ، أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ وَأَمِيرِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا الْإِمَامِ الطَّيِّبِ صَاحِبِ الدَّعْوَةِ النَّبَوِيَّةِ وَالصَّوْلَةِ الْحَيْدَرِيَّةِ وَالْعِصْمَةِ الْفَاطِمِيَّةِ وَالشُّجَاعَةِ الْحُسَيْنِيَّةِ وَالْعُلُومِ الْبَاقِرِيَّةِ وَالْمَعَاقِلِ الْجَعْفَرِيَّةِ وَالْحِكْمَةِ الْإِسْمَاعِيلِيَّةِ وَالْجُودِ الْمُحَمَّدِيَّةِ، فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَعَلَى آلِهِمْ وَسَلَّمَهُمْ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَا إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا، إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَى وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ دَعْوَةٍ لَا يُسْتَجَابُ لَهَا وَمِنْ مُنْكَرَاتِ الْأَخْلَاقِ وَالْأَعْمَالِ وَاللَّهْوِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَأَمَّا بَعْدُ، أَقُولُ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى فِي كِتَابِهِ الْكَرِيمِ بَعْدَ أَنْ نَقُولَ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ، بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ بِلَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span> Once again, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>.</p>
<p>So, in the previous session, we looked at the linguistic meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, right? We learned that linguistically the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> is a combination of six attributes. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span> is someone who is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, who has absolute ownership, and at the same time he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّد</span>, meaning he has complete authority over us. And then, within that space of complete ownership and unrestricted authority, he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبِّي</span>, he is the one who ensures growth, right? He takes care of his creation, nurtures them, protects them. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْشِد</span>, which means...</p>
<p>He not only nurtures His creation till they become mature, He then also sets them to purpose. He sets each creation on the path of its destiny, and then He supports them through it. And while doing so, He is also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْعِم</span>, He showers His creation with numerous undeserved gifts and blessings. And then finally He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَائِم</span>, the one who sustains and maintains all of His creation for the extent of their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُرّ</span>ation.</p>
<p>So all these meanings come together to define the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>. And we also learned that the primary ingredient in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>s recipe of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> is the ingredient of love. All six meanings are grounded in that one fundamental emotion of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, which is intense and constant love, care and concern for creation. Right, and this is why we have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰنُ عَلَّمَ الْقُرْآنَ</span>, right? The intensely merciful taught the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. That''s what we learned. It''s not,</p>
<p>you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْجَبَّارُ وَعَلَّمَ الْقُرْآنَ</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is the most essential element of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>. And this is why, when we saw that journey of that person who was reflecting on the creations of the heavens and the earth, then naturally the word that came to him when he called out to his master was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَّنَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَّنَا</span>. So he used the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> because he saw all of creation reflecting the various attributes of his creator''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>.</p>
<p>That''s what he saw. He saw creation reflecting the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>. And this is like, for example, if you meet a very skilled electrician, right? If you see this guy working, and just by observing his knowledge and his skill, you can conclude that he actually went to a really good electrical engineering school, or at least he had some very skilled teacher who taught him this electric work.</p>
<p>Now, you haven''t actually seen the teacher, nor do you know the name of the school that he went to, but you are still able to conclude that just by observing his work. How is that? Because this electrician is his teacher''s creation, you see, and by his knowledge and his skill he is manifesting the attributes and the training of his teacher. And this is exactly what creation is doing. All of creation, it is constantly manifesting the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> of its creator. And that principle holds true at a micro cosmic level, and it certainly holds true at a macro cosmic level, right? That this universe, being a creation, is constantly reflecting the attributes of its creator.</p>
<p>The seven heavens and the earth and everything that exists between them, they are all constantly glorifying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. And there is not a thing in this world that does not sing His praise. It''s just that you do not understand their glorification. It is you that do not understand their glorification, but everything else is in a constant state of glorification. So in other words, the signs and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَات</span> of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is has been placed in the very fabric of His design.</p>
<p>And the more we reflect on creation, the more we will be able to appreciate the beauty of His desire. So that is pretty much what we talked about so far, right? So just to recap. Now, in the last session, I said that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> is commonly translated as Lord in English, but linguists agree that a more suitable one-word replacement for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> is actually master. Because a master not only represents ownership, it doesn''t just represent authority and control over something or someone, it also represents perfection of knowledge and skill, which is essential for nurturing, for guidance, for maintenance, right? So in the Arab linguists'' opinion, the word master is much closer in meaning to the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> than Lord. Okay?</p>
<p>So from moving forward, whenever I want to use the English word for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, I will use the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, I will use the English word master. Now, once we understand the meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, the next thing to understand is that the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, it actually implies a relationship. There are certain words that just inherently imply relationships.</p>
<p>They need a counterpart. So for example, when I say teacher, who does a teacher have a relationship with? With students. It automatically implies that relationship to exist. I cannot be a teacher if I don''t have any students to teach. I cannot do the sessions if nobody attends the sessions. So it''s a two-way street. When I say parent, what relationship is implied? Children, right? When I say employer, it implies a relationship with employees.</p>
<p>So there are certain titles that necessitate the existence of a relationship. It doesn''t work without, you know, the counterpart. But when I say intelligent, it doesn''t necessarily have any personal relationship with anyone. Right? It''s generic. When I say tall, it''s a generic attribute. It''s not a personal relationship with someone. Right? So in the same way, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has certain attributes, certain names that do not necessarily imply a personal relationship.</p>
<p>For example, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَعْلَى</span>. He is lofty. Now that''s a powerful attribute, but it''s a generic state in relation to creation. It does not imply a personal relationship. But then there are certain attributes which do establish relationship. For example, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَالِق</span>, creator. If He''s a creator, there has to be what? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَخْلُوق</span>, there has to be creation. You cannot be a creator if you have no creation. You can be powerful by yourself, you can be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْآلَاء</span> on your own, but not a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَالِفْ</span> until you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَخْلُوع</span>. You understand?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَفُورُ</span> is another one. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is extremely forgiving, which means there has to be someone to forgive. You know, there has to be sin to exist, otherwise forgive what? So there are some attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> that are generic in nature, and there are others that imply a very personal relationship. Therefore, when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> begins His revelation with the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, which I briefly talked about in the last session, that when He introduced Himself, when He said, read in the name of your master who created you.</p>
<p>So when He introduces Himself as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, and then by extension to us, in doing so what He''s essentially saying is that when you think of me, I want you to put all my other attributes aside, and I want you to think of me first and foremost as your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>. Right? That''s the whole idea behind introducing Himself as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>. So He''s saying that the one relationship that you should never let go of, even if you forget everything else, even if you forget every other relationship I have with you, the one relationship that you always need to be mindful of, every single waking moment of your life, what is that?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>. That I am your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> and my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> will take precedence in all my dealings and interactions with you. And the primary ingredient of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. It is love, it is care, and it is concern for you. But then what''s interesting is that if you carefully observe the statement, He did not just say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, He said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّكَ</span>, your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>. I mean, isn''t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> master of all creation? Right? Isn''t He <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيَّاد</span>, one who has absolute ownership and complete authority over everything?</p>
<p>So basically He could have just said to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span>, &quot;Read in the name of the master who created everything.&quot; But no, He makes it personal: &quot;Read in the name of your master who created you.&quot; Right? As if He''s saying that I''m only your master and nobody else''s. He''s saying that He''s only my master and nobody else''s. Like when He deals with me, He''s gonna make it that personal, as if He only created me and He''s only my master. And that applies to each and every, you know, human being in this world. And so He makes His relationship that intimate with each and every one of us, just as an introduction. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>Now, does the relationship of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> require a counterpart? It does, right? If <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>, you know, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is Master, what does that make me? And what does that make you? What completes the relationship with a Master? Slave, right? If <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is Master, that makes all of us slaves. And the Arabic word for slave is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ</span>. So when we accept <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> as our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>, just by virtue of us accepting that relationship, we are also accepting the fact that we are all His slaves.</p>
<p>Which is okay if you think about it. Because, you know, we''ve already established that He''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, so He owns us. We already accept that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has complete authority over us because He''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّد</span>. So we already know we are in submission to His training, His nurturing, because He''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبِّي</span>. We know that we need His guidance because He''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْشِد</span>. We know that we''re constantly enjoying the countless undeserved gifts that He has bestowed upon us because He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْعِم</span>. And we know that He is our ultimate sustainer and provider.</p>
<p>We believe that we exist because He allows us to exist, because He''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيِّم</span>. So therefore it''s not the most unimaginable thing to accept this notion that we are slaves to this gracious master. And the truth is that whether we accept it or not, it really doesn''t matter, does it? I mean, we are His property and we are His slaves regardless. I mean, my children can fight with me and they can tell me, you know, &quot;We don''t accept you as our father. You are not our father from this day on.&quot; And, well, you know, tough luck. Because guess what? I am. So you understand? So we can reject it, we can deny being <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>s slave and everything, but by definition, we are property, so too bad.</p>
<p>So anyway, I just wanted to set the stage by making sure everyone understands that, you know, our relationship, that we are slaves of this master, whether we like it or not, and whether we want to accept it or not, that that''s just what the relationship is. Now, having said that, today what I want to actually show you is how beautiful this relationship is with this master. Because unless and until you appreciate the beauty of this relationship with Him, we will not be able to reap the benefits of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>.</p>
<p>This is really important to understand, because this is how relationship works. Every relationship — you can think of any relationship. A teacher cannot benefit a student until the student acknowledges and submits himself to the teacher, to the learnings of that, to the teachings of that teacher. A student will only benefit from the teacher when he accepts that relationship and submits to it. Right? A doctor cannot help a patient until what? Until the patient accepts and submits to the prescribed treatment of that doctor.</p>
<p>If a patient has diabetes, let''s say, and the doctor is asking him to stop eating candy, but every time he sees candy he says, &quot;Oh, you know, I know the doctor asked me not to do it, but what does he know anyway? You know, it''s only a little bit. I mean, it''s not like I''m eating the whole bag. I mean, he''s never gonna find out anyway. Besides, everyone else is eating candy, so why can''t I?&quot; You know, now if a patient does that, he''s not truly accepting the doctor-patient relationship, is he? I mean, doesn''t matter if he calls him, if he addresses him as, you know, Doctor So-and-so. It doesn''t matter how many visits and appointments he keeps with that doctor, he''s simply not going to benefit.</p>
<p>Why? Because despite what he''s saying, he is not really accepting himself as a patient. Only after he completely accepts that relationship and submits to the doctor''s treatment, only then will he be able to gain the benefits that the doctor wants him to have. So it''s a two-way street. Now this is what''s really interesting that I want you to think about. The doctor is completely helpless in this relationship until the patient completes that relationship, despite his vast knowledge and skill.</p>
<p>The doctor has the knowledge to benefit the patient. The doctor really cares about the patient. He wants to benefit the patient. But despite all of that, all of that knowledge, all that will, all that desire to help and benefit the patient, he is helpless. He cannot do anything. Why? Because the patient has not yet completed the relationship, you see? Now I''m not really talking about doctors and patients, am I?</p>
<p>We all claim that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>. We all know about His infinite <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. We all know He wants the best for us. We all know the treatment He has prescribed for our souls. Right? We all know the do''s and donts. We even pray and fulfill our religious obligations, but then in our daily lives we do everything that goes against His prescribed treatment. So then how is our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span> supposed to help and benefit us?</p>
<p>Isn''t that interesting? Despite the infinite powers of our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>, we render Him helpless by not completing this relationship. So if I truly want to gain the benefits that my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span> has to offer, what do I need to do? I need to complete the relationship. Right? And how do I complete the relationship? By submitting myself as His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ</span>. This is how I can complete my relationship with Him, you understand?</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/82/cfa7ce2f-61a4-4cb1-9479-ff4021731839.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>His relationship with me is that of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبِّيَّة</span>. I complete that relationship with my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبِّيَّة</span>, with my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, my submission, my servitude, and my worship. So now let''s understand this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span> properly. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is generally translated as worship, which is an action. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span> is servitude that acts as the driving force behind my various acts of worship. You understand? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is the various forms of worship.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span> is that feeling, that driving force of servitude that makes me execute those forms of worship. Now what''s interesting is that linguistically the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, it means two things. It means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّةٌ وَطَاعَةٌ</span>. It means worship and it also means obedience, both at the same time. So let''s break it down. What is worship? When we think of worship, what generally comes to mind? The five prayers, right? Fasting, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>, those things.</p>
<p>These are all different forms of worship. And we think that''s it. We stop at those activities. And that''s the problem with this translation of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> meaning just worship, because it does not necessarily include the concept of obedience. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> actually includes obedience, and it''s the kind of obedience that actually comes out of love, not force. Because you know the word obedience can have a very negative connotation attached to it, because people are forced into obedience, we know that.</p>
<p>Children are often bullied into obedience by the use of force or beatings or whatever, right? Historically slaves have been beaten and tortured into obedience like animals. So the word obedience can have a very negative emotion attached to it, but this is not the kind of forced obedience we are talking about here. That would not be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. The word for that kind of forced obedience in Arabic is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِتْكَا</span>, which is equivalent to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غُلَامِي</span> in Urdu, right?</p>
<p>So you understand the difference? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِكَا</span> is forced obedience. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span> is willing obedience. Worship is actually a manifestation of that willing obedience. So you know if you think about it, the seven forms of worship that we know of, right, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِلَايَة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجٌّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَاد</span>, these are all manifestations of willing obedience. We do that because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has commanded us to do that. So we execute all these various forms of worship in obedience to His command, right?</p>
<p>But if you are obeying willingly and happily, then you are in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span>, which is the desired state. If you are doing it with a grudge, if you''re not actually happy to do it, you are in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِكَا</span>, which is not the desired state. It''s the same action, it''s the same forms of worship. I mean you''re performing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span> in both cases, but if you''re praying unwillingly, you know if you''re like...</p>
<p>you know if you''re doing a hit-and-run <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span> because you just want to get it over with, that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِفْقًا</span>, that is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is where you actually are willing, you want to do, you''re looking forward to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. That makes the difference. So when we talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, always keep in mind that we''re talking about willing obedience, we''re not talking about forced obedience. And therefore in the final analysis, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is nothing but a willing obedience of the beloved generated from the emotion of passionate and unconditional love.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/82/281db322-3bb2-41ea-8b21-09f2524569b1.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>That is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is. It is a willing obedience of the beloved that comes from the emotion of unconditional love. This is why when Imam Jafar Sadiq was asked: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا إِمَامَ خَالِدَ الدِّينِ</span> — Oh Imam, what is the essence of religion? It is nothing but obedience. And then the man asked: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَالَةُ طَاعَةٍ</span> — What is obedience? And he said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَّا الْحُبُّ</span> — Nothing but love. So the essence of all forms of worship is willing obedience, and the essence of willing obedience is love.</p>
<p>You understand? We learned in the previous sessions, earlier sessions, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلَه</span>, He is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>, right? He is the one who takes precedence over all forms of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span> in our lives. But what kind of relationship does He have with us? He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, right? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> describes who He is and what He is generally supposed to mean in our lives, but the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> describes a personal relationship with us.</p>
<p>In other words, He is the entity that is to be worshipped because He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. And He is also the entity that is to be willingly obeyed because He is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>, right? So to put this in practical terms, why are these two things important to understand? Let''s say we''re about to go for prayers, right? Let''s say in 15 minutes it''s time for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span>. And we have to do our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>.</p>
<p>Now what kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is praying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span>? Is it worship or is it obedience? It''s worship, right? But then from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> until <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظُهْر</span>, we may not be worshipping Him, but what should we be doing at that time? Obeying Him. You understand? If we truly consider Him an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰه</span> and we truly accept His relationship with us as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>, then between two prayers, you and I might not necessarily be worshipping Him, but we still need to be in obedience to Him.</p>
<p>This is why the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is so comprehensive in Islam. All forms of obedience are considered <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> in Islam. Therefore if you really want to understand the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, I prefer the word obedience over worship, because unconditional willing obedience is worship. Unconditionally willing obedience is worship. You go and earn a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span> income at your job, that''s an act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. You go to school and sincerely accomplish the purpose of that learning, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. You obey, love, respect your parents and teachers, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. You are kind to your siblings, generous to the needy, respectful to your elders, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>.</p>
<p>You wash your hands before you eat, you cover your head before you go to the toilet, you smile at someone, you do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُضُوء</span> before you sleep. These are all acts of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> because they are being done in the willing obedience and out of sincere love for the beloved. So you can see how powerful the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is in the Islamic terminology. And it''s an extremely comprehensive word, which is not just restricted to the forms of worship.</p>
<p>So I hope I made that clear. So now that we understand the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span> and this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, let''s understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ</span>, which means a slave. Now what is a slave? A lot of people, you know, kids and generally Muslims, even many that I have talked to, they take offense to this word slave because it creates a very negative image in their mind, right? Based on how historically masters have treated slaves, the black slavery and then the, you know, the Jewish slavery and all that.</p>
<p>And fair enough. So what I want to do today is use those same negative examples to draw out a comparison between the kind of masters we know, or the kind of masters the world has seen, and the kind of master our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span> is, and then you decide whether this word, you know, the word slave in the context of Islam, is it an ugly word or a beautiful word? I''ll leave that up to you, right? So but, but I think it''s really important to look at this comparison because it really gives us an idea of what we''re talking about and what relationship we''re actually trying to understand.</p>
<p>So let''s begin with this term &quot;slave.&quot; What is a slave? What is the most defining characteristic of a slave? A slave is someone who does not have free will. That''s the definition of a slave. A slave does not have a choice in any decision that his master makes. He must obey the will of his master without question. In other words, a slave has no existence of his own other than fulfilling the desires of his master.</p>
<p>Isn''t that true? A master completely denies and prohibits free will for his slave, which is the ultimate form of humiliation. Taking away an intelligent person''s freedom of choice is the worst form of humiliation. Because it says, I do not think you are capable of making the right choices. It says, I do not respect you enough to believe that you can make the right choice. It says, you are no different than an animal to me. Just as I choose and make decisions for my cattle and my dog, I''m going to make your decisions for you. Denying the freedom of choice is the ultimate form of humiliation and disgrace for a human being.</p>
<p>We all know that. Now when it comes to our Master, when it comes to our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>, what is the most precious gift that He gave us? The ultimate gift that distinguishes us from all other creations in this world. What is that? It''s free will. Did He need to do that? No, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, He''s owner. He''s the owner of every single creation in this universe. That fact alone makes every single creation His property, His slave, doesn''t it?</p>
<p>We already established that. That means being His property or His slave has nothing to do with being Muslim or not, because everything is His property and slave. The sun is His property, the moon is His property, the galaxies are His property, this entire universe and everything within it, including you and I, are His property. Does the sun have free will? Does the moon have free will? Does the night and day have free will?</p>
<p>No. They''re all in complete submission to the will of their Creator. They''re all floating in their orbits. They''re all bound by gravity. They have no choice but to float in their orbits, right? And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has bound them and taken away their freedom of choice. We see that. Could He have done that to us? Could <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> have bound us? Could <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> have forced us into submission? Could the One who has compelled and enslaved the sun, the moon, the solar systems, the galaxies, could He have forced us into submission?</p>
<p>Absolutely. But did He? No. He gave us an intellect and He gave us freedom of choice. Something He did not give to any other creation. The most powerful of creations, He did not do that. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So right off the bat we can see that indeed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is our Master. You know He is our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>, but His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> is unlike any other master we know of. The very first thing every master we know of will try to do is take away our freedom of choice.</p>
<p>Teachers, parents, bosses, judges, doctors, anyone, any worldly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> you can think of will try to control us by restricting our free will. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is the only <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span> who has granted us complete freedom of choice. Now, we said that denying freedom of choice is the ultimate form of humiliation. So my question is then, what is allowing complete freedom then? If denying freedom is humiliation, then allowing complete freedom of choice is what?</p>
<p>It''s the ultimate honor. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> honored us above and beyond every other creation by granting us freedom of choice. And say that the truth has been revealed by your Master. Whoever chooses, let him believe, and whoever chooses, let him disbelieve. Complete freedom of choice. Now what kind of gifts do masters give their slaves? Nothing, right? They barely feed them, they barely clothe them, they barely pay them. And when they do feed and clothe them, they give them the bare minimum, just enough to keep them alive, right?</p>
<p>And for the very little they give them, how much do they expect back in return? A lot! Every master we know of gives the bare minimum and expects the maximum from his slaves, right? What has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> given us? We talked about the numerous undeserved gifts that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْعِم</span> has showered upon His creation, right? Precious gifts that are above and beyond the reason and any logic of economics.</p>
<p>Any company that gives its employees this much, the way <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> gives us, that company would just bleed red. Now, in comparison to the quantity and the quality of the gifts that our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span> bestowed upon us, how much does he expect in return? The bare minimum. The very least. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. I mean, of all his creations, our Master gave us the most. Right? The rest of creation is constantly in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُوذِيَّة</span> to him.</p>
<p>They''re all working round the clock in constant obedience to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> without taking a moment''s break. But then, despite giving us the most, he expects the least from us in return. I mean, what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>? Count the entire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ دُرّ</span>ing the day, what? One hour? One month of fasting out of 12? That too only from sunrise to sunset? Two and a half percent of wealth in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>? The wealth that he gave us in the first place.</p>
<p>Incredible! And even then, you miss <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, no problem, do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَضَى</span>. You cannot find water, no problem, do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَيَمُّم</span>. You''re sick, traveling, no problem, do your fast later on. You don''t have a saving that has been left untouched for a year, no problem, no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span> for you, you don''t have to pay <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>. You don''t have money for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجّ</span>, traveling is not safe, no problem, I''ll lift your obligation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجّ</span> from your shoulders. I mean, this is crazy. How easy has he made it for us?</p>
<p>And what kind of master does that? Do you know of any master in the world who would make it that easy for his slave? Do you know of any other master in this world who will give so much and ask for so little in return? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, we''ve never heard of such a master. On top of that, right, if that wasn''t generous enough, he then sent us revelation and messengers to explain his reasons to us.</p>
<p>Why you should do this? Does a master ever, ever, ever explain his reasons to his slaves? Can any slave turn to his master and say, hey, explain the purpose of this command to me? Can a slave negotiate with his master? Can a slave say, you know, I''ll obey your command only if and when you explain it to me? Can any slave do that? No. But did this master explain things to us in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>?</p>
<p>Did this master, through the life example of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, show us how to practically execute our obligations? Did this master make it compulsory, mandatory on us to understand before we do something? Did this master make it actually obligatory on us to understand the reasons of his command? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>! Why would he do that? No master ever does that. He did that to make it easy for us to accept his relationship.</p>
<p>To make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span> easy for us. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ بِكُمُ ٱلْيُسْرَ وَلَا يُرِيدُ بِكُمُ ٱلْعُسْرَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> desires ease for you. He does not desire hardship for you. Now here''s another question. Who are the acts of worship meant to benefit? Who is slavery always designed to benefit? The master or the slave? Always the master. But this master, he designed the universe for us, he gave us the most, he asked the least in return from us.</p>
<p>He explained the reasons of his command to us. But the question is, everything he asked us to do, who does it benefit? It benefits us and only us, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Every slave in the world is bought and put to work to benefit the master. Every command issued to a slave is for the master''s benefit. So what kind of a master is this whose every single command is meant for the benefit of his slaves?</p>
<p>Not one single command for his own benefit. If that is not love, if that is not care, if that is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, if that is not ease, if that is not mercy, then you tell me what is. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. He is in constant service to us, and what he asks from us in return is also to benefit us, not him. Is the work of a slave judged according to the capabilities of the slave or according to the expectations of the master?</p>
<p>A slave''s work is always judged according to the level of the master''s standards and expectations. How does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> judge our actions? According to our individual level of knowledge and capabilities. Nowhere in the entire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> demand perfection. Nowhere, not a single place. The only thing He asks for in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is effort and sincerity. That''s it. Who does that? What kind of a master does that?</p>
<p>Do you know of any master in this world that rushes to fulfill the needs of his slaves? Do you know anyone like that? Have you ever watched a movie, read a book in which a slave calls out to his master in need and the master just drops everything and runs to his aid? Do you know of any <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span> like that? But what does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّبُّ</span> do? When my slave...</p>
<p>Look at that, My slave, when my slave asks, he''s telling <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> this, he''s saying when my slave asks you about me, I am near him in that very moment. I immediately respond to the call of my supplicant when he calls upon me, immediately... the moment he calls me. No master ever does that. From what history tells us, slaves are always rushed to fulfill the needs of their masters, never the other way around.</p>
<p>So then what kind of... master is this? What kind of a merciful master is this who rushes to fulfill the needs of his slaves? Who rushes on the call of his slaves? So the more we reflect upon the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> of our master, the more we realize that His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, His mercy is absolutely incredible. We find that he is extremely loving, extremely caring, extremely merciful. He''s unlike any other master that we know of.</p>
<p>He''s unlike any other master we will ever come to know of. No master does for his slave what our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> does for us. But the problem is that because he is so subtle in his love and mercy that we forget our place. We forget that we are property. We forget that he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, he is owner. Because if he''s owner, then that, just by that definition, that makes me property, which means he can do whatever he wants with me. He can break me, he can rip me apart, he can burn me, drown me, he can do whatever he wants with me. And when he chooses to do that, there is absolutely nothing that I can do, or you can do, or all of us put together can do, or everything in the universe combined can do, to protect me against that wrath.</p>
<p>If the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> decides to harm me, there is absolutely nothing in existence that will be able to help me. But even look at that, right? If the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> decides to harm you, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Not in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُرِيدُ الْجَبَّارُ</span>, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُرِيدُ الْقَهَّارَ</span>, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُرِيدُ الاِنْتِقَامَ</span>, right? If the conqueror, if the powerful, if the avenger decides to harm you, no, no, no, no. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُرِيدُنِ الرَّحْمَٰنُ</span>, if the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> decides to harm you, even in his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُرّ</span>, even in his harm, he is merciful.</p>
<p>Even in his affliction that he puts on us, even in that there is a hidden benefit. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, look at the words he uses. And yet we constantly disobey him, we constantly fail in our obedience and servitude to him, but he still allows us to exist. I mean, how grateful should we just be for that? Just think about that. You know my phone? It was causing problems.</p>
<p>It was acting up. It was freezing up. I had to restart it every now and then, right? You know what I did? I went to the store. I got a new phone. I got rid of the old one. Why? Because occasionally, not all the time, occasionally a few times it acted up on me, so I got rid of it. Because that''s what we do with property that is no longer doing its job.</p>
<p>We discard it. We are all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>s property and we are not doing our jobs. Not occasionally, not sometimes, in some cases constantly, all the time. So now what should my owner do with me? Now, what should my owner do with such a useless property as myself? Discard it. But does he do that? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. I mean, how grateful should we be just for that? So you can see that even though words such as, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ</span>, master and slave are used, he is unlike any other master we can imagine in this world.</p>
<p>This is a master who loves us intensely, constantly. And so when we realize, when we realize that, what is our natural response to him? To love him back. And this is yet another unique relationship if you think about it. There is not a single master in this world where a slave would love his master. In fact, historically, slaves have always hated their masters. The first chance they get, they want to run away from their masters.</p>
<p>Isn''t that true? But we wholeheartedly love our master. And so the only direction that we want to run in is towards him, not away from him. And how do we do that? By submitting ourselves to his love and mercy through our willing obedience. Not a forced obedience as we experience in the world, a willing obedience. An obedience that is based on the highest stages of love that we learned earlier.</p>
<p>An obedience that manifests in the desire of service and servitude. Like the service of a passionate lover, an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَشَقُّ</span> for his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَشُوقٌ</span>, for his beloved, right? That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو ضِيَاء</span>, that is worship. And therein lies our greatest challenge. Now listen to this carefully, I''m going to wrap up. The greatest challenge, the greatest difficulty, the greatest hardship that you and I constantly face in life, every single waking moment, is to accept <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>.</p>
<p>I''ll say that again. The greatest and constant challenge you and I and everyone else in this world faces is not accepting our creator as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. Everyone is fine with that part, right? Even the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَهْرِيّ</span> Arab, they accepted him as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. There is no one in this world, there''s no one in this group here that has a problem with accepting him as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. We all believe he''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>.</p>
<p>The real problem, the real challenge is in accepting him as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, as Master. You know why? Because if you accept him as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, if I accept him as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, that means we become what? We have to become his slaves. We have to accept his relationship. We have to ' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'master slave relationship';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'give up our freedoms of our passions and our desires and to put the collar of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span> around our necks and become his slaves.</p>
<p>And that is where we all struggle, because we all live in times of absolute and unrestricted self-indulgence. We live in the times where I am in charge, I am in control. You know, I am the master of my own destiny. I am powerful, I am wealthy. I don''t have to listen to you, you''re not the boss of me. I can do whatever the hell I want, whenever I want. You know, I''m the king of my domain. This is my life. If I want to do it, I''ll do it. If I don''t want to do it, I won''t. In other words, I am my own <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>, I am my own master. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is saying, no, I am your master. I gave you this life, I gave you this authority, I gave you these gifts of parents, I gave you these gifts of children. You know, I honored you with intellect and free will. I gave you position and power. I gave you health, wealth, strength. I gave you all these things so that you could recognize me and come closer to me, because I am your master. But I am a master who is unlike any other master you will ever come to know of. So submit to me with willing obedience and I will show you what true happiness is.</p>
<p>Willing and happily accepting our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُبْهُدِيَ</span> and submitting to our Master in sincere obedience and true worship is the greatest challenge that you and I will ever face in our lives.</p>
<p>And the only way to overcome this challenge is by recognizing and appreciating the love and mercy of our Master, and by completing the relationship of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو ضِيَاء</span> through a willing submission and obedience to our magnificent master. Because love engenders love, right? And if this incredible <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, if this incredible master does so much for us disobedient and ungrateful slaves, what would he do if we actually did become willingly obedient? Right, that''s something to think about. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذْ تَأَذَّنَ رَبُّكُمْ لَىِٕن شَكَرْتُمْ لَأَزِيدَنَّكُمْ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَىِٕن كَفَرْتُمْ إِنَّ عَذَابِى لَشَدِيدٌۭ</span>. And your master said, if you become grateful, I will give you more. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَىِٕن شَكَرْتُمْ لَأَزِيدَنَّكُمْ</span>. If you become grateful, I will give you more. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَىِٕن كَفَرْتُمْ إِنَّ عَذَابِى لَشَدِيدٌۭ</span>. But if you become ungrateful, then indeed my punishment is severe. Right, so may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> give us all the knowledge, the willingness, the capability to recognize His love, to move towards Him in complete submission, and, you know, be a good slave to this gracious master.</p>
<p>Okay, so we have a couple of questions. Okay, so the first question is, if our relationship with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is of master and slave, then what is the concept of freedom in Islam? Well, that''s an interesting question. What is freedom? Right? In Arabic, freedom is very different from, you know, in Arabic freedom is different from the definition of how we look at freedom in modern English, right?</p>
<p>So for instance, freedom in the pre-Islamic culture, right, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُرْ</span>, which means, you know, a free man, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُرْ</span> was in contradistinction to an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ</span>, a slave. In other words, a free person was somebody who was not an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ</span>. So the pre-Islamic people, they had the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُرْ</span>, which is the free man, and then the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ</span>, which is the slave. Now, one of the most amazing things about Islam is that it makes you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ اللهِ</span>. Right? It makes you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ اللهِ</span>, it makes you slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. Now, if you are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ اللهِ</span>, if you are the slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, then what does that make you in relationship to everything else? It makes you free. Right? Because now your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span>, your obedience and your servitude, is only to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. It is not to anything or anyone else. And so it sets you completely free.</p>
<p>So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُرْ</span>, right? So the Arab says, he says... The free man is a slave as long as he is covetous, as long as he is greedy, as long as he has wants and desires. And the slave is a free man if he is content. Right? So, a free man is a slave as long as he is covetous, and the slave is a free man if he is content, which gets to the essence of the Arab understanding of freedom before Islam.</p>
<p>That freedom in reality is moral freedom. It''s a freedom from want. It''s not a freedom to. See, in our culture, freedom has been almost entirely defined by freedom to. Freedom to do whatever I want. Freedom to say whatever I want. Freedom to have whatever I want, right? So it''s all about freedom to for us. But to the Arabs, it was freedom from. Freedom from desires, freedom from wants, freedom from needs. They saw all of these things as shackles. That''s how they perceive freedom.</p>
<p>So the Islamic concept of freedom comes from that powerful paradigm. Enslave yourself to the one to free yourself from everything else. Or move away from the one and become a slave of everything else. And you know, when we talk about enslaving oneself to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, it''s essentially just accepting that relationship of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْبِيَا</span>, right? Because we are slaves regardless, we already established that. We are property regardless of whether we accept it or not. So the concept of freedom is essentially just recognizing that relationship and drawing towards him, and in that process we achieve complete freedom and happiness. So I hope that answers the question.</p>
<p>Here''s the next one. If <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is so merciful, then why did Imam Hussain and his friends suffer in Karbala? Well, you''re saying why did Imam Hussain and his friends suffer in Karbala? I mean, that''s a matter of perspective, isn''t it? Did they really suffer? I mean, yes, apparently, you know, from how things played out, sure. But if you think about suffering, I mean, what are some of the signs of when somebody is in a state of suffering, what are some of the signs? Fear, right? Regret, depression, always trying to escape.</p>
<p>When we look at Imam Hussain and his companions, what do we find? Was there fear? No. Absolutely no fear. Were there any regrets? No. They couldn''t wait to die. Was there depression? Historically we find out that these people were actually, they went to the battleground smiling. The only reason, the only times when they were depressed, according to the stories that we read about Karbala — I''m sure if you''ve read the stories you would agree to that — anytime they were depressed was when Imam Hussain did not give them permission to go out in battle. That''s when they would cry and they would get depressed and they would be sad and they would plead with the Imam to say that, please let me go, that... what was his name?</p>
<p>That John, that black slave, right? He said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا إِمَام</span>, you don''t want me to...&quot; When he went to Imam Hussain and he asked permission to go out in the battlefield, Imam Hussain said that, &quot;Look, you''re a slave and you have completed your servitude to us, and I free you. Now you are not obligated to do this for me, you''re not obligated to kill, you know, martyr yourself for me.&quot; That''s what he says to him. And the slave John, he starts crying and he says, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا إِمَام</span>, you do not want a black man to lay amongst the white people.</p>
<p>You are all white and you are all, you know, your skin is white and shiny, and I am dark and I am black, and you just don''t want a black person to die along with white people, and you don''t want my smelly sweat to mix in with the fragrance of your people.&quot; I mean, you know, if he was suffering, he should have jumped at that opportunity. He should have run away.</p>
<p>I mean, he actually played the racist card. You know, the child that comes to the mother at the camp, when she sends out her son who just recently got married, she sends him out in the battleground and she says that, you know, do not, do not become a coward and do not shy away from, you know, becoming a martyr, and go and martyr yourself on Imam Hussain.</p>
<p>He goes out and then he fights and he comes back, and he''s all completely, you know, drenched in his own blood, and he comes back to his mother and he says, you know, &quot;Mother, are you happy with me now?&quot; And she says no. She says not until you sacrifice yourself for Imam Hussain. I mean, if this was suffering, why would she say that? I mean, Karbala in my opinion is the prime example of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span>.</p>
<p>It was a battle to defend the honor of the beloved. That''s what Karbala is to me. It''s a battle to defend the honor of the beloved. Yazid is, you know, he''s this guy who has challenged the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهِيَّة</span> of Imam Hussain''s beloved. And for the companions, he is somebody who has challenged the imamate of their beloved. And so he and his friends, they stood ground to defend the honor of their beloved and to set an example of true love for all of us.</p>
<p>They showed us that this is how you love. You pay any price, you pay any cost for your beloved. You sacrifice your food, your drink, your children, your family, even your own life, but you do not desert the beloved. Because what kind of love is it that flees at the first sign of trouble, right? And for a true lover, what good is life anyway without the beloved''s grace, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَلَيْتَكَ تَحْلُو وَالْحَيَاةُ مَرِيرَةٌ، وَلَيْتَكَ تَرْضَى وَالْأَنَامُ غِضَابُ، وَلَيْتَ الَّذِي بَيْنِي وَبَيْنَكَ عَامِرٌ، وَبَيْنِي وَبَيْنَ الْعَالَمِينَ خَرَابُ، فَيَا لَيْتَ شُرْبِي مِنْ وِدَادِكَ صَافِيًا، وَشُرْبِي مِنْ مَاءِ الْفُرَاتِ سَرَابُ، إِذَا نِلْتُ مِنْكَ الْوُدَّ فَالْكُلُّ هَيِّنٌ، وَكُلُّ الَّذِي فَوْقَ التُّرَابِ تُرَابُ</span>.</p>
<p>&quot;If I could just taste the sweetness of your happiness,&quot; the poet says, &quot;if I could just taste the sweetness of your happiness, it wouldn''t matter if life becomes bitter for me. If you are happy with me, who cares about the anger and the wrath of the world? You know, may things remain beautiful between us and let the entire world become ugly for me. You know, a sip from the wine of your love even makes the sweet water of Euphrates like a mirage for me. All I need is your love. If I have that, I have everything. Otherwise, even the greatest treasures of this world is just dust for me.&quot; So anyway, so yeah, I think that, you know, you''re looking at it the wrong way. That was not suffering, what we see in Karbala.</p>
<p>That''s just an expression of immense love.</p>
