UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ خَالِقِ الْوُجُودِ مِنَ الْعَدَمِ، وَجَاعِلِ النُّورِ مِنَ الظُّلَمِ، وَمُخْرِجِ الصَّبْرِ مِنَ الْأَلَمِ، وَمُلْكِ التَّوْبَةِ عَلَى النَّدَمِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَنَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى الْمَصَائِبِ كَنَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى النِّعَمِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنُصَلِّي عَلَى رَسُولِهِ الْأَكْرَمِ الشَّرَفِ الْأَشَمِّ، وَنُورِ الْأَتَمِّ، وَكِتَابِ الْمُحْكَمِ، وَكَمَالِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَالْخَاتَمِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلِّ يَدَيْ وَلَدِ آدَمَ الَّذِي بُشِّرَ بِهِ عِيسَى بْنُ مَرْيَمَ، وَدَعَا لَهُ بِآيَاتِهِ إِبْرَاهِيمُ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ، حِينَ كَانَ يَرْفَعُ قَوَاعِدَ بَيْتِ اللَّهِ الْمُحَرَّمِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ وَعَلَى أَصْحَابِهِ خَيْرِ الْأُمَمِ، الَّذِينَ بَارَكَ اللَّهُ بِهِمْ كَافَّةَ النَّاسِ، الْعَرَبَ مِنْهُمْ وَالْعَجَمَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَى أَمِيرِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ الْأَنْزَعِ الْبَطِينِ، وَقَائِدِ الْغُرِّ الْمُحَجَّلِينَ، غَالِبٍ عَلَى كُلِّ غَالِبٍ، مَطْلُوبِ كُلِّ طَالِبٍ، مَوْلَانَا عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ</span></p>
<p class="ar-block">‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَنزَلَ عَلَىٰ عَبْدِهِ ٱلْكِتَبَ وَلَمْ يَجْعَل لَّهُۥ عِوَجَاۜ</span> ‎</p>
<p class="ar-block">‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَقُلِ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًۭا وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ شَرِيكٌۭ فِى ٱلْمُلْكِ وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ وَلِىٌّۭ مِّنَ ٱلذُّلِّ ۖ وَكَبِّرْهُ تَكْبِيرًۢا</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ، الَّذِي نَحْمَدُهُ وَنَسْتَعِينُهُ وَنَسْتَغْفِرُهُ وَنُؤْمِنُ بِهِ وَنَتَوَكَّلُ عَلَيْهِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنْ شُرُورِ أَنْفُسِنَا وَمِنْ سَيِّئَاتِ أَعْمَالِنَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ يَهْدِهِ اللَّهُ فَلَا مُضِلَّ لَهُ، وَمَنْ يُضْلِلْهُ فَلَا هَادِيَ لَهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولُهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْسَلَهُ اللَّهُ تَعَالَىٰ بِالْهُدَىٰ وَدِينِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ وَكَفَىٰ بِاللَّهِ شَهِيدًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيَّ بْنَ أَبِي طَالِبٍ وَصِيُّهُ وَوَزِيرُهُ، فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَعَلَىٰ آلِهِمْ وَسَلَّمَ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرًا اللَّهُمَّ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَا إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ، وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَعُ، وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ، وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ أَمَّا بَعْدُ فَـ</span>...</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَأَمَّا بَعْدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَقُولُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى فِي كِتَابِهِ الْكَرِيمِ بَعْدَ أَنْ قُلْنَا أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ بِلَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ، وَاللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span></p>
<p>Once again, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>. So we were looking at some of these linguistic terminology that is used in the Quran, right? We began with understanding what the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰه</span> means and how it relates to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>We looked at the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, which is the primary and the most fundamental or essential attribute of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, and which is again a composition of these various meanings that we looked at. It comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَٰن</span>, which is the womb of a mother, and then we looked at its derivatives, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, and basically it''s a combination of unconditional love, concern, care and mercy or compassion.</p>
<p>So we have all these different, you know, we looked at all these different subterms or, you know, the components of this term in order to understand what the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> means. Then we looked at the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>, which is the action of the Creator, and which is also a composite word, and it is a combination of six things or six characteristics. Right, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> is a combination of all of these traits. He is the one with complete ownership, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيَّاد</span>, he is the one with absolute authority.</p>
<p>He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبِّي</span>, he is the one who nurtures, he is the one who guides, who sets to purpose. He is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">منعم</span>, he showers with undeserved gifts and blessings. And then he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيِّم</span>, he is the sustainer, the maintainer, the one who provides. So all of this together come, you know, they come together, these meanings, to give us an understanding of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>. Now, so now that we understand some of these primary terms, the next thing to understand is:</p>
<p>What is he a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> of? Right. And Surah Fatiha answers that question, in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎ All praise and gratitude belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, who is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>, and what is he a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> of? He is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span>. So in today''s session, we''ll begin looking at this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَمِين</span>, and then we will see what the interpretation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span> is and what it practically means for us. And there are many, many interpretations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>. You know, I''ll only talk about one, which I think is the most essential for us, it''s more practical, that''s just something in theory. So we''ll look at this term <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span> today.</p>
<p>But I''ll begin with first explaining the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span>, because it is not what we generally think of. Because if you look at any English translation of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَمِين</span>, you will see &quot;worlds,&quot; right, or &quot;universe.&quot; So, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, all praise and gratitude belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> who is the master of the worlds, who is the master of the universe. Now, this translation of &quot;world,&quot; it provides a weak, rather incomplete understanding of this word.</p>
<p>You know, just to break it down, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span> is the plural form of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَم</span>, right? So in order to understand the meaning of this word, let''s first, you know — so we have to look at actually two words. So that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَم</span>, and then we also have to look at its counterpart, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَمِ</span>. Okay? So if you look at these two words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَمِ</span>, you can see that they both have the exact same spelling.</p>
<p>Right? The only difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَمِ</span> is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span> on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَصْرًا</span> on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَمِ</span>. Other than that, they both share the exact same spelling. Right, if I remove the Arab on these words, you cannot tell which is which. So they have — but, and by now, you know, we''ve talked about this enough in the previous sessions, that you know that in Arabic, when two words share a common spelling, they have a deep connection in their meaning. And in this case, as you can see, they not only just share the spelling, they both come from the same root word, which is — and what does the word mean? It means knowledge. So everything, we know that everything that derives from a root word has a connection with that root word. So is the root word, and from that comes out <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span>. So they are all connected with the meaning of knowledge. So means knowledge, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span> is the one who knows, who comes to know, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span> is that which he comes to know of.</p>
<p>You understand? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالِم</span> is the one who knows, who is the cause, whereas <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span> is that which can be known, the effect. Right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالِم</span>, the knower, is the cause; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span> is the effect. And the device through which the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الم</span> learns about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الم</span> is the intellect, which in Arabic is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَقَلُّ</span>. Now, we as human beings, we can know many, many things, we can learn about many things, but even with all the knowledge that we have gained over the course of years individually, or over the course of centuries collectively, we still cannot know everything, right? We are knowledgeable about certain things, but at the same time we are also ignorant of other things.</p>
<p>As opposed to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, who knows everything there is to know. And the word for someone who knows everything about everything, the word for that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلِيم</span>. Okay? And again, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span> also comes from the same root word, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span>. It is the superlative form of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span>. So an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span> is someone who has knowledge, some knowledge, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span> is the one who has absolute and complete knowledge of everything. So now we have three words that stem from the same root word. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الٓمٓ</span> — you have the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ</span>, that which can be known, that which is waiting to be known, that which is waiting to be discovered, waiting to be researched, waiting to be understood, right?</p>
<p>That''s the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الم</span>. And then there is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الٓمٓ</span>, the one who studies it, the one who researches it, the one who discovers it. And in that process of discovery, in that process of research and learning, he comes to know about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الٓمٓ</span>. He comes to know about the one who knows everything there is to know, who is the source of all knowledge. Now, because we as human beings, because we are limited in our abilities of how we learn something, what the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلِيم</span> did, what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> did, is that He created the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span> for us, so that we can study it, so that we can reflect upon it, and through that reflection we become knowledgeable, so that we can become aware, so that we can recognize. Recognize who?</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آلِين</span>, you understand? So this is what I always refer to as the genius of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s design. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you know, think about it: if you are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, put yourself in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s shoes, if you are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you are unlimited in your capabilities, you have your, you''re about to create a creation that is going to be very limited in their abilities, and yet you want them, you want to provide them with a way to recognize you. How are you going to do it? So when you actually look at it from that perspective, you can actually truly appreciate the genius of this design, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is infinite in His capabilities, right? And infinite means without limits.</p>
<p>Imam Jafar Sadiq <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, he says, you know, when somebody asked him about the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>, he said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ مِنْ أَنْ يُحَدَّ وَيُحْصَى</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is great in the sense that He does not have a boundary. He cannot be confined to human comprehension. In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> cannot be defined. You see? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, anything about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> cannot be defined. But the reality is that as human beings, we are only capable of understanding things through what?</p>
<p>Through definition. Right? Definition is what defines or gives finiteness to something. It limits reality for us so that we can put it into a perspective. That is what the intellect does. It sets a boundary. It confines, right? It defines. You have to wrap, in order to wrap your mind around something, a concept, a definition, anything, you have to confine it first. So how did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> solve this problem for us?</p>
<p>The problem of understanding and recognizing an infinite Creator through finiteness. How do you solve that problem? By creating this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span>. Because as human beings, we are limited in our mechanisms of learning something, in that we have to define something before we can understand it. So how do we, as creatures of finiteness and definition, understand a Creator who is infinite and cannot be defined? How do we do that?</p>
<p>Through this intermediary of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span>, which essentially acts like an interpreter. Everybody knows what an interpreter is, right? If everyone in this group only speaks English and all I know is Urdu, then for me to understand what you''re saying, I will need a middleman, right? Someone who understands both languages, someone who can take your messages in English and translate them for me in Urdu, right? It''s that exact same concept with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span>, which in its creation is physical, but in its design reflects the attributes of its Creator.</p>
<p>It defines the attributes of the Creator''s glory and His magnificence, so that we can gain some understanding about our Creator by studying the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So in other words, this universe, it becomes a medium through which we can recognize <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. Because every sculpture necessitates the presence of a sculptor. Every sculpture necessitates the presence of a sculptor. The poet says, you know, the most convincing evidence that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> exists is the fact that every sculpture requires a sculptor. Similarly, everything that can be known requires and necessitates the existence of a knower.</p>
<p>This entire universe has the capability to be known, but what good is it without a knower? Because nothing can exist in a passive state, right? Therefore, the proof of how we know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, you know, the evidence that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> exists is through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّبْع</span>, through His signs, the signs and effects left behind in creation. We study these signs that He created for us in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span> in order to recognize the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالِم</span>.</p>
<p>Just as the Greek philosopher who asked that Bedouin, right? There was a Greek philosopher, he was passing through a desert and he saw this Bedouin walking, and he said, he had just heard that, you know, because apparently all the philosophy and everything Greek, and you know, they were really strong in their philosophy, so he just wanted to see how does, you know, how do the Arabs understand the existence of a God? So he just asked him, he said, how do you come, how did you come to know of your master? The Greek philosopher asked this poor Arab Bedouin, right? He said, how did you come to know of your master? And that Bedouin, he said, he said, the camel dung shows me that there was a camel here.</p>
<p>I see camel dung lying on the sand, that''s showing me that a camel passed by here. The donkey dung tells me that there was a donkey here. Footsteps in the sand tell me that someone walked by this place. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَالسَّمَاءُ ذَاتُ الْأَبْرَاجِ، وَالْأَرْضُ ذَاتُ الْفِجَاجِ، وَالْأَبْحَارُ ذَاتُ الْأَمْوَاجِ</span>. So when I look at the sky with its perfect constellations, when I see this earth with its perfect valleys and traverses, and when I look at the oceans with all of its waves, ...</p>
<p>... Do all of them... do they not indicate the presence of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَطِيف</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَبِير</span>? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>! It was that simple to him, see? We complicate, you know, we complicate things, but the truth is that the existence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the most clear and the most manifest truth there is, you know, for anybody whose <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِقْرَة</span> is intact. If that primordial essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِطْرَة</span> is intact, the question about the existence of the Creator does not even emerge.</p>
<p>And this is why when the atheist says, you know, I wish that there was more proof for the existence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the believer replies, I see nothing but the proof of his existence. It''s all around us. It''s in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَم</span>. So that''s the linguistic meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الم</span>. It is anything and everything that can be known by the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الٓمٓ</span> in order to enable him to recognize the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الٓمٓ</span>.</p>
<p>And this is why &quot;world&quot; is not the best translation for the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">العَالَمِينَ</span>. It limits the scope of its meaning. If <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> meant Master of the World, then it would just mean that, and nothing other than the world or universe, and in that case the plural form of the word, you know, of this word, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span> would have been used: ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span>. Because the use of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span>, it, you know, it, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span>, it encompasses a much broader spectrum than just this physical world. The statement <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>, it means that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not just the master of the world or the master of the universe, it means that He is the master of everything that can be known.</p>
<p>So I want you to change your definition of this verse. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎: praise and gratitude belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> who is the master of everything that can be known. Because everything that exists in creation is a reflection of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الٓمٓ</span>. It exists in a passive state, waiting for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الٓمٓ</span> to learn about it through reflection so that he can recognize his creator. So you see now the definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَغْبُ الْعَالَمِي</span>, it becomes much broader in scope than just master of the world or master of this universe.</p>
<p>He is also the master of all the different species. He is also the master of all the various groups in creation, right? Every group, every category, every concept, every idea, every thought, every postulate, every theorem, every physical matter, every chemical matter, every spiritual entity, everything in existence, physical or metaphysical, is now included in the definition of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span>, because they all exist for one and one reason alone:</p>
<p>so that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الٓمٓ</span> can study about it, come to know about it, and recognize his creator, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الٓمٓ</span>. Now, there are many explanations of what the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِ</span> means, there are many interpretations. Today I want to explore just one of those interpretations, which I think is the most lacking trait in our society today, and that is the trait of humility. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> points out that it is He who created different cultures and the different creations and the different civilizations.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the one who created people of different colors, race, languages, you know, social statuses. You know, He says in the Quran ... right, He says I gave you preference over other nations, and if you look ... and the word He uses for nations in this verse is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span>, and by that, over here, by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span> He''s talking about nations. So you can just see right here in the Quran that the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span> is not just restricted to world. So the idea is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the creator and the master of all nations. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>. So the implicit meaning in that is, you better treat these people properly, be careful how you interact with them, right, regardless of their race or religion, because don''t forget that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>. And why is that important to know?</p>
<p>you know, you know what the word means now, right? We have combined all of those definitions into one word, master, but it still means all of those things, right? Owner, the one with authority, the one who provides gifts, the one who nurtures, all of those meanings, but we''ve combined all of that in the word master.</p>
<p>So it''s important to understand this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>, because in our interactions with other people, if we hurt them, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is saying that if you hurt them, if you break their heart with their words, you know, if you abuse them with that temporary authority that I have granted you over them, you better not forget that I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>.</p>
<p>And this does not just apply to human beings, even animals, plants, this earth. So in that word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>, what the implicit message is, that before you''re cruel to any of my creation, you better remember that I am their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> too. Because when a slave gets hurt by someone, who gets offended? Ultimately the master. You know, in olden days wars would begin because the, you know, the member of one tribe hurt or killed the slave of another tribe.</p>
<p>So his master would take that as a personal insult. Why? He''s a slave. Nobody disrespected his property, you see. Slave is a master''s property and responsibility. So even if that slave was in the wrong and deserved to be punished, who should the complaint be taken to? His master, right?</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>, so be careful how you interact with my creation, no matter what group, what religion, what caste, what belief system, what country they belong to. You know, no matter what your relationship with them is on this earth, it doesn''t matter, because before he''s your son, he''s my slave. Before she''s your daughter, she''s my slave. Before he''s your father, before she''s your mother, they are my slaves. Before she''s your daughter-in-law, before he''s your son-in-law, they are my slaves. Before he''s your employee, before he''s your servant, before he''s your student, don''t forget that they are all my slaves. I am here, I am watching. If you treat them unjustly, if you hurt them, if you degrade them, if you misuse your authority over them, you will have to answer to me, because I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. And so, you know, in one of the sermons of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَهْجُ الْبَلَاغَةِ</span>, he explains that what this title of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span> does is that it brings all of us down on the same level in the eyes of the creator. It marks and categorizes all of creation as his slaves, you see, because when there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, there has to be an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَادٌ</span> to complete that relationship. We did an entire session on that. When there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, there has to be an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَادٌ</span>. So no nation, no group is better than another in terms of their race or their color.</p>
<p>If it is better, it is only by whose standards? If an individual is better or superior than another person, it has to be by whose standards? Who can be the judge of that? The master.</p>
<p>And the reason I''m saying this, is, you know, I''m bringing this topic up, is because this has become a huge issue in our community today. It has become a huge issue in our religion today. It has become a huge issue in the world today. You know, just because we are Muslims or we are Sulaimanis, we start thinking that we are better than the rest of the world. Yes, we are, I mean, we''re definitely superior in our knowledge of realities. Right? We are superior, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, in the reality, in understanding the realities through our various <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غُلُمٌ</span>, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَبْدَأً مَعَادًا</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقَائِق</span> and all of that. Yeah, absolutely. So I''m not taking that away. Right? So there''s no doubt about that.</p>
<p>But my point is that that does not give us the right to treat other communities or other religions with spite, or look down upon them, or, you know, because it''s this concept that has been developed now, that is like, you know, we are the old, you know, everybody is going to hell other than me. And that''s just not right, you know. It does not give us the license to look down upon others.</p>
<p>So I hope you understand what I''m saying, you know, because the last time I said this there was a huge riot in the session, in the previous group, when I said this, that, you know, oh, he''s saying that Sulaimanis are not on the right track and this. That''s not what I''m saying, right? I''m just saying that if you do believe, if we do believe as a group, that we are, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has blessed us with the knowledge of realities that he has not, you know, blessed others with, that is not a reason for us to look down upon them.</p>
<p>And this whole statement always drives me insane when people talk, say, you know, when people say that, oh, you know, he''s going to hell, he''s, you know, who are you? And that''s, in today''s world we are literally afflicted with this disease of superiority, and this feeling of having a chip on our shoulders, you know, whether we admit it or not. And it''s not just limited to religion or, you know, everything, money, social status, whatever.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is in this statement, this is why the statement of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span> is such a beautiful statement, because it brings everybody on the same, it levels the playing field. Don''t forget, I am the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>. You are all my slaves. Superiority in ranks can only exist through the eyes of the master. Superiority in ranks can only exist through the eyes of the master based on the servitude of the slave, not through the eyes of the slave.</p>
<p>This is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولَ اللهِ</span> said, the most honorable in the eyes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the one who is the most pious. That''s it. Not by any other standard. Piety is the only standard through which the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> measures the rank of the slave. Because even the highest ranking person is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s slave, and the lowest ranking person is also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s slave. I mean, look at what we recite in every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذَان</span>, five times a day.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُهُ وَرَسُولُهُ</span>. I testify that Muhammad is his slave and his messenger. Slave before messenger. Not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُهُ وَعَبْدُهُ</span>. It''s not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا رَسُولُهُ وَعَبْدُهُ</span>. No, it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُهُ وَرَسُولُهُ</span>. The highest ranking person who has ever walked this earth, we testify in every prayer that he is the slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Then what right do we have to claim superiority over each other?</p>
<p>Right? I mean, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has blessed us with some gifts, and the greatest, no doubt, the greatest gift of all that He has blessed us with is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, faith. My point is that that gift should make us more humble when in our interactions with other people, not more arrogant. Does Quran tell us the story of someone who became arrogant about his title and his rank? Do you guys remember the story of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْلِيس</span>?</p>
<p>I mean, what was his statement? I am better than him. I am created from fire. He is created from clay. Right? Who was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْلِيس</span>? He was an angel, right? From the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span> of angels. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آدَم</span> was a human being from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَان</span>. He looked down upon <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آدَم</span>. They were both slaves of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْلِيس</span> stood before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you know, he stood before the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span> and he said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَا خَيْرٌ مِنْهُمْ</span>.</p>
<p>I am better than him. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَقْتَنِي مِنْ نَارٍ وَخَلَقْتَهُمْ مِنْ طِينٍ</span>. You created me from fire, you created him from clay. So, I am better than him. I mean, how are we different when we say things like, you know, I am Muslim, you are ***** I am better than you. I''m going to heaven, you''re going to hell. How does that become any different from what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْلِيس</span> said to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>?</p>
<p>And look at the result, right? What do we learn in that story? What did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْلِيس</span> master do with him? Did his arrogance bring him closer to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> or did it get him removed from his divine presence? He got expelled from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>, we know that. So do we really think that an act that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْلِيس</span> was punished for, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> would let it slide for us? That would not be justice, right?</p>
<p>That he punishes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْلِيس</span> for his arrogance, removes him from his divine presence, but then reward us and bring us closer to him. That''s not possible. Because you have to understand, when you and I judge each other, you know, when you and I as slaves judge each other in terms of superiority, what position do we place ourselves in? We put ourselves in the position of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> has the right to judge.</p>
<p>Now, does the Quran tell us about someone who did that? Didn''t someone say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَا۠ رَبُّكُمُ ٱلْأَعْلَىٰ</span>, I am the greatest <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>? Who said that? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span>, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span> said that. How did he end up? And the reality is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span> knew that he was human. I mean, he knew that he was not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> because he was fully aware of his weakness. But despite that, despite knowing his true identity, he claimed a station that he was not worthy of.</p>
<p>Did you know that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span>, he did not like the fact that he needed to go to the toilet? Because obviously that is not befitting a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, right? So that bothered him. So he got all of his physicians together and he asked them that, how do I get rid of this problem? So they told him to eat bananas, lots of bananas. So he ate bananas for like 40 days.</p>
<p>And then he ended up with a much bigger problem. Anyway. So the reason I''m sort of enforcing this is, you know, because today this is not, you know, it''s not just a Pharaonic problem. This is a global problem, right? Everyone wants to become <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَه</span>, everyone wants to become <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْبُود</span>, everybody, at some level, everybody, you know, we have become people who want to be worshipped. But then we forget how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has defined us in the Quran, right?</p>
<p>You know, and we have created you from some chain of dirt, of mud and water mixed together. We created your progeny from a vile fluid. Who do you think you are? You know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> tells these people, you know, get down off your high horse, be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجُلٌ</span>. See, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجُلٌ</span>, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجُلٌ</span> is used for the one who has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجَلَ</span>, you know, who, as they say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجَلَ عَنْ فَرَسِهِ</span>, he is the one who comes down off his high horse and puts his feet on the ground. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجُلٌ</span>, someone who comes down off his horse and puts his feet on the ground. He doesn''t see himself raised above the earth, right? He comes down to the level that he came from, from dust. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجُلٌ</span>. But you know, we''re all...</p>
<p>we''ve all become people who at some level are just like playing games, trying to be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰه</span>. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says no, don''t be an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰه</span>, be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجُلٌ</span>, be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آدَم</span>, be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنِي آدَمَ</span>, be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آدَمِي</span>. That is truly accepting <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>. Humility, not arrogance, is the trademark of a believer. And this acceptance of our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُبْذِيَ</span>, this acceptance of our slavery, right, of our servitude to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, it''s a heavy burden that we as believers, we have to carry this on our back. When you carry a burden on your back and you''re trying to climb a mountain, if you have a burden on your back and you''re trying to climb a mountain, do you bend forward or do you walk upright?</p>
<p>You have to bend forward. The burden makes you bend. It makes you humble, doesn''t it? And this is why humility is the essence of spiritual progress. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِي</span>, he says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ تَوَاضَعَ لِلَّهِ رَفَعَهُ اللَّهُ لَهُ</span>. The one who becomes humble for the sake of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> raises him and elevates him in his ranks. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. I mean, this is why the highest ranking person to ever walk the face of this earth.</p>
<p>Right? Who was that? Our beloved prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَمَّدٌ مُصْطَفَى</span>. The greatest ranking man that this world will ever know of was the most humble in his character. He was marked by humility. You know, in the Bible, all previous religions, they have prophecies that predicted his arrival, right? His arrival is being predicted from the beginning of time. But in the Bible, that whole paragraph, you know, in which there are so many signs that have been described about his arrival, that he''s going to have all these traits, and the greatest sign that has been mentioned is humility.</p>
<p class="ar-block">The greatest mark of his prophethood that was mentioned, that was prophesized, was humility. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَجَعَلَ الصِّدْقَ يَنَاتَهُ لِبَاسَهُ، وَالْبِرَّ شِعَارَهُ، وَالتَّقْوَى ضَمِيرَهُ، وَالْحِكْمَةَ مَنْطِقَهُ، وَالصِّدْقَ وَالْوَفَاءَ طَبِيعَتَهُ، وَالْعَفْوَ وَالْمَعْرُوفَ خُلُقَهُ، وَالْحَقَّ شَرِيعَتَهُ، وَالْعَدْلَ سِيرَتَهُ، وَالْهُدَى إِمَامَهُ، وَالْإِسْلَامَ مِلَّتَهُ، وَأَحْمَدَ اسْمَهُ، هَدَى بِهِ بَعْدَ الضَّلَالَةِ، وَعَلَّمَ بِهِ مِنَ الْجَهَالَةِ، وَرَفَعَ بِهِ بَعْدَ الْخَمَالَةِ</span>.</p>
<p>... all these different predictions of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span>, of his arrival in the Bible. You know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> mentions all these beautiful signs, you know, I am going to send you a man from your own nation. ... his garment will be his peaceful temperament, his behavior will be kind, his conscience will be piety, his logic will be wise, his honesty and truth will be his nature, his dealings with humanity will be that of forgiveness, justice will be his character, all his actions will be just, you know, his nation will be called the nation of Islam, his name will be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحْمَد</span>.</p>
<p>So, all of these traits — you know, He will give a nation, you know, guidance, He will guide a nation and He will bring them out of error. He will give them knowledge and bring them out of ignorance. He will be raised after being belittled. He will become known after being unknown. And he will gain abundance after scarcity. He will become wealthy after being poor. You know, so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> mentions all of these — this is before the birth of the Prophet — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> mentions all of these signs in the Bible. He goes on mentioning all these traits about His beloved, but then, you know, after revealing all these beautiful traits and these incredible signs, He mentions the greatest sign of all. He mentions the last, the most prominent sign of the Prophet. And what is that? My beloved Prophet is going to be so humble that when he is going to walk among you in such humility that when he puts his feet on dry leaves, you will not hear the sound of them cracking under his feet.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. This is the prophet we follow. This is the example we follow. If you want to know why the slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> gained the honor he achieved from his master, it is through this one trait of humility. And that is what identifies a true believer from a hypocrite. Humility. And this is why I feel atheism is such a blatant form of arrogance, because the atheist is rejecting something that is the most obvious and fundamental aspect of his existence: that he was created. Because even an atheist cannot say that he was not created, he was not born. So now, if I say that my father created me and his father created him before him, that''s what they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَوْرٌ</span>, right?</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلْسُل</span>, because it keeps going on and on and on backwards. And then where do you stop? It stops at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آدَم</span>. Who created <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آدَم</span>? All of you came from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آدَم</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آدَم</span> came from dust. So arrogance does not defeat the dust. It does not befit the dirt, because it is constantly trampled upon, right? So that is recognizing this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span>, the magnificence. What are we in comparison to this universe? Our entire solar system is nothing more than a speck, a dot. So then who are we? So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span> is a sign that is showing us who our creator is, and what are we in comparison? We''re nothing. We''re dirt. We''re dust. So how does it befit us?</p>
<p>You know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِي</span> saw a man who was acting arrogantly, so he said to him, &quot;Who do you think you are? Do you not see your true essence? Your beginning is a vile, dirty fluid. Your end is a disgusting, decaying, smelling corpse.&quot; You know, so this is the first thing to know, you know, in the path of spiritual progress. This is the first thing to know: humility. To know our station in this world, to know who we are, where we stand. You know, we are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَرْبُوبٌ</span>, we are created, we will die, we will be raised up, you know. And this understanding, this understanding that we are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَرْبُوبٌ</span> — we are not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, we are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَرْبُوبٌ</span> — that is the station of a believer, of a true slave.</p>
<p>So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللَّهِ</span>, I give myself first of all this advice, and I give you all the sincere advice that be humble in all walks of life. You know, don''t be arrogant. Not about our religion, not about anything. It is the greatest challenge that we all face in our times today, because arrogance is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذَان</span>, it is the call of our times today. It has become the trademark of success in this world today. Schools, colleges, work, families, communities — it''s all about &quot;I''m better than you.&quot; And arrogance does not befit a slave. It does not befit an obedient slave. So may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> grant us all humility and guide us and enable us in walking in the footsteps of our beloved prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>.</p>
