UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِيمِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ خَالِقِ الْوُجُودِ مِنَ الْعَدَمِ وَجَاعِلِ النُّورِ مِنَ الظُّلَمِ وَمُخْرِجِ الصَّبْرِ مِنَ الْأَلَمِ وَمُمَلِّكِ التَّوْبَةِ عَلَى النَّدَمِ فَنَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى الْمَصَائِبِ كَمَا نَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى النِّعَمِ وَنُصَلِّي عَلَى الرَّسُولِ الْأَكْرَمِ ذِي الشَّرَفِ الْأَشَمِّ وَالنُّورِ الْأَتَمِّ وَالْكِتَابِ الْمُحْكَمِ وَكَمَالِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَالْخَاتَمِ سَيِّدِ وَلَدِ آدَمَ الَّذِي بَشَّرَ بِهِ عِيسَى ابْنُ مَرْيَمَ وَدَعَا لِبِعْثَتِهِ إِبْرَاهِيمُ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ حِينَ كَانَ يَرْفَعُ قَوَاعِدَ بَيْتِ اللَّهِ الْمُحَرَّمِ فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ وَعَلَى أَصْحَابِهِ خَيْرِ الْأُمَمِ الَّذِي بَارَكَ اللَّهُ بِهِمْ كَافَّةَ النَّاسِ عَلَى الْعَرَبِ مِنْهُمْ وَالْعَجَمِ وَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَى أَمِيرِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ عَلِيٍّ الزَّاهِدِ الْبَطِينِ وَقَائِدِ الْغُرِّ الْمُحَجَّلِينَ غَالِبٍ عَلَى كُلِّ غَالِبٍ مَطْلُوبِ كُلِّ طَالِبٍ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي أَنْزَلَ عَلَى عَبْدِهِ الْكِتَابَ وَلَمْ يَجْعَلْ لَهُ عِوَجًا</span></p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًا وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ شَرِيكٌ فِي الْمُلْكِ وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ وَلِيٌّ مِنَ الذُّلِّ وَكَبِّرْهُ تَكْبِيرًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي نَحْمَدُهُ وَنَسْتَعِينُهُ وَنَسْتَغْفِرُهُ وَنُؤْمِنُ بِهِ وَنَتَوَكَّلُ عَلَيْهِ، وَنَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنْ شُرُورِ أَنْفُسِنَا وَمِنْ سَيِّئَاتِ أَعْمَالِنَا، مَنْ يَهْدِهِ اللَّهُ فَلَا مُضِلَّ لَهُ، وَمَنْ يُضْلِلْهُ فَلَا هَادِيَ لَهُ، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولُهُ، أَرْسَلَهُ اللَّهُ تَعَالَى بِالْهُدَى وَدِينِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ وَكَفَى بِاللَّهِ شَهِيدًا، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيَّ بْنَ أَبِي طَالِبٍ وَصِيُّهُ وَوَزِيرُهُ، فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَعَلَى آلِهِمْ وَسَلَّمَ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَا إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ، اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا، اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَعُ وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَأَمَّا بَعْدُ، يَقُولُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى فِي كِتَابِهِ الْكَرِيمِ بَعْدَ أَنْ قَالَ أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِيمِ، رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَمَاتِ بِلَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ، اللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ، آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span> Once again, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>.</p>
<p>So in the previous session we looked at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>ic formula that allows us to gain some understanding about who our Creator is and, you know, what His intentions are. That <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>ic formula is declared in the verse <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَنُرِيهِمْ آيَتِنَا فِى ٱلأٓفَاقِ وَفِىٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ حَتَّىٰ يَتَبَيَّنَ لَهُمْ أَنَّهُ ٱلْحَقُّ</span>. So today I want to explore this verse a bit more in detail, just to point out some interesting <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span>cts about it.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, We will show them our signs in the universe and in their own selves, in their own souls, until it becomes absolutely clear to them that all of this is the ultimate truth. Or that it can also be understood as, so that it becomes absolutely manifest to them that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the ultimate truth. So I explained that the signs in the horizons tell us about the nature and the capabilities of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, and the signs in our soul, in our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>, it tells us about the intentions of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created us all in His own image, and therefore He only demands from Himself, or He demands for Himself rather, what we demand from others in terms of rights.</p>
<p>And this is how the analogy is between the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَاهِر</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَاطِن</span>, the horizon and the soul. This is how it works, right? So between the comparison of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَاهِر وَبَاطِن</span>, we find the insights into the inner workings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>, of how religion works. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, he said in a hadith, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ اللَّهَ أَسَّسَ دِينَهُ عَلَى مِثَالِ خَلْقِهِ</span>, that indeed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> founded His religion on the pattern of creation, to provide evidence for His religion through the world, and then to provide evidence for the world through His religion. Right? In other words, He created the physical world on the exact same principles and patterns as the spiritual world. And why did He do that? So that the human intellect could study the physical in the horizons and then be able to relate to those teachings with the spiritual realm.</p>
<p>So horizon and the soul, or the self, are the keys to learning about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. He dispersed, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> dispersed the knowledge of who He is in the horizons. And a simple way to understand this concept is the example of light. Right? So if you look at light, we know that light is... it''s white. Right? So if you look at this image, they call it white light because it doesn''t have any color.</p>
<p>But what happens when you pass that light through a prism? We know that a prism is just a medium. It''s a medium of glass, right? And when this white light, it hits this glass, it refracts, right? It bends. And the result of that, a whole spectrum of colors show up, from violet to red and everything in between. In the same way, when the light of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> hits creation, you know, because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> tells us that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the light of the heavens and the earth, right?</p>
<p>He is the light of the heavens and the earth. So what happens is that when this pure light of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s essence, when it hits creation, when it enters the realm of physicality, it refracts into the spectrum of these divine attributes. You see? This is why we get all these different manifestations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s essence in this universe. So this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الٓمٓ</span>, you know, you can look at it as a prism that is manifesting <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s various attributes in creations for us to observe.</p>
<p>So, in one instance, we see that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرِيم</span>, right? We see that He is generous. So, in another instance, the universe shows us that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَبَّار</span>, He is the compeller. In another instance, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span> reveals to us that He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَهَّار</span>, the subduer. In another instance, we see that He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَزِيز</span>, the powerful. In another instance, we see that He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُزِيل</span>, the humiliator. In another we see that He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْعِظَة</span>, the bestower of honor.</p>
<p>So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span> essentially serves as this colossal theater for the divine manifestations of these attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And that is how we come to know about Him. Now why did He set the stage up in this way? Because like I mentioned earlier, the human intellect, it learns by defining and limiting things. Right? Just as the body works by breaking down food. So if you look at how the intellect works, it works just like the body.</p>
<p>In order to digest knowledge, it has to take in the whole, break it down into parts, and then understand each part individually. Right? Only then it can sum those parts up again to understand the whole. So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span>, it presents us these parts in the form of these divine attributes so that we can reflect on them individually and then sum them up to put together a more complete understanding of our Creator.</p>
<p>So this is the essential theme of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. It invites us to reflect on the heavens and the earth, the horizons and the soul, because through that very reflection, we can recognize our Creator and His abilities. And then once we''ve done that, it turns our attention inward onto the soul, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>, to understand the purpose of our creation. Right? So now what is the purpose of our creation?</p>
<p>Scholars say that there are three reasons for the creation of human beings, and what''s beautiful about this distinction is that it allows for purposeful existence for not just Muslims but for people of other beliefs as well, or even people without belief. So he says that the first reason for creation of human beings, he says, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَارَة</span>. Right? Cultivation. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, We brought you forth from the earth to cultivate it.</p>
<p>That''s the first reason. The second reason, He says, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. We only created the jinn, which are beings of the unseen realm, and humans, which are beings of the seen realm, for the purpose of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, for willing worship. So Ibn Abbas said in a narration that the purpose behind the creation of human beings was also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لِعَارِفُونَ اللهِ</span>, to know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, and to know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is to love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>So those of you who have heard my session on those, on that stages of love, you know that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span> is one of the levels of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَحَبَّة</span>, right? It is one of the higher levels of love. Therefore He created the Jinn and the human beings to love Him. Right? So it can also be taken as, I did not create Jinn and man except to love me. So those are the two reasons.</p>
<p>The third and the last reason for creation of human being, he says, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَافَة</span>. We made you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلَفَاء</span>, because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيفَة</span>, the Caliph, is the one who stands in place of somebody else, right? He is the one who is left behind. He is left behind to stand in the place of someone else. In other words, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيفَة</span> is a steward. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created us as His stewards on this earth.</p>
<p>Why? So that He can witness what we do with all the external and internal gifts that He blessed us with. That''s the test. Now, there are two types of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَافَة</span>. There are two types of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلَفَاء</span>. We have been made a Caliph over ourselves and then over others. So for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَافَة</span> of the self, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, He said to the angels, I have decided to create a Caliph in this earth.</p>
<p>And then in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ صَاد</span>, He says, O Dawud, we have made you a caliph in the earth. So this is the second type of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَافَة</span> that is mentioned in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. So there are two types of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَافَة</span>. The first is to rule yourself and the second is to rule others. But the condition of ruling others is that you rule yourself first, that you take control and mastery of your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> first. And so here in this verse Dawud is being told we have put you in the earth to rule over others, because he was made a ruler over <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنِي إِسْرَائِيلَ</span>, right?</p>
<p>So, so that you can judge among them with the truth. And then He says, do not follow your own caprice, do not follow your own whims. In other words, you have to rule yourself first before you can rule others. You see, because if you do not do that, and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span> in the, you see, begins with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span> here is for causation. Because if you do not do that, if you do follow your caprice, if you do follow your whims, your appetites, then it will lead you astray from the path of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>So what are we as caliphs of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, and how are we doing as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s representatives on earth? So I''d like to spend a little time talking about this today, because I think it''s really important to put this into perspective. How are we doing as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s representatives? He says, We will show them our signs in the horizon and in our own souls. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will let us know through the signs of what we see around us.</p>
<p>He will let us know the reality of our existence. So let''s look at some of the signs that we see in the horizons today. Right? Let''s reflect on some of these signs to understand who we are and how we are doing as caliphs of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Just as a physician would look at the symptoms of a sick patient. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الرُّومِ</span>, He says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَهَرَ ٱلْفَسَادُ فِى ٱلْبَرِّ وَٱلْبَحْرِ بِمَا كَسَبَتْ أَيْدِى ٱلنَّاسِ لِيُذِيقَهُم بَعْضَ ٱلَّذِى عَمِلُوا۟ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَرْجِعُونَ</span> ‎. He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَهَرَ ٱلْفَسَادُ فِى ٱلْبَرِّ وَٱلْبَحْرِ بِمَا كَسَبَتْ أَيْدِى ٱلنَّاسِ</span>. Mischief, corruption, pollution has appeared on the land and sea because of what the hands of men have earned.</p>
<p>And again the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَمْ</span> here is for causation. So that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> can give them a taste of their deeds in order for them to turn back to Him. So we see that the reason <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> makes our negative actions have negative repercussions is in order for us to return back to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. This is why negative actions have negative consequences, so that, you know, it helps us return back to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>Scholars say that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَهَرَ الْفَسَادُ</span> is actually a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَقْصٌ فِي الْبَرِّ وَالْبَحْرِ</span>. So it means the diminished blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> on earth. You lose the blessings of the earth. And it also means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>, which is droughts and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span>mines and, you know, all the other harm that comes in agriculture, the fruit of the land. You know, the land stops growing crops. It also means that the benefits of the oceans disappear, from the fish and the other animals that live in the oceans, you know, like the whales. And all of this is going to happen because of what we earn from our own sinfulness. Because human beings, even though created in the best of forms, in the very image of its creator, you know, with all of His divine attributes, that human being is still capable of extreme corruption when he deviates from his divinity, when he deviates from his original state.</p>
<p>This is that very concern that the angels expressed when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> mentioned the creation of Adam. He said, this is what the angel said, right? Are you seriously considering creating a species in this earth who will create <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَسَادٌ</span> and who will spill blood? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَسَادٌ</span>, if you look at it in the English dictionary, it means rottenness, spoilness, corruption, decay, decomposition, putrid, depravity, wickedness, viciousness, iniquity, immorality, weakness, perversion, wrongness, incorrectness. All of these are meanings embodied in the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَسَادٌ</span>.</p>
<p>If you look at the word pollution in the English dictionary, it says, undesirable state of the natural environment being contaminated with harmful substances as consequences of human activities. You see, this is the result. So even in our language, pollution is a dominant problem on the planet. You know, we as human beings, we are responsible for the present state of the earth today. Our conditions of hardships in the form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span>mines and extreme weathers and earthquakes and floods, these are nothing more than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِمَا كَسَبَتْ أَيْدِى ٱلنَّاسِ</span>, from what our own hands have earned.</p>
<p>Many countries are in a major crisis because of fires that were started in forests, right? Climate changes are driving up temperatures. And as these temperatures rise, there are more incidents of fire. Another aspect of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَسَادٌ</span> is the air itself. In India, it has been estimated that half a million people die from air pollution, half a million people. We had 15,000 people die from terrorism and the US government had moved heavens and earth to have this war on terrorism.</p>
<p>Yet half a million people suffer and die from air pollution just in India. Where''s the war against that? Where''s the war against noise pollution? In Pakistan and India, look at the levels of noise pollution in the form of honking and yelling and screaming and the noise from rickshaws and taxis, you know, broken silencers. That''s all man-made <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَسَادٌ</span>. Today we know that the world is rapidly moving towards desertification, right?</p>
<p>This is because we''re losing what they call topsoil. Desertification is literally where a place that once had foliage, it turns into desert, and it has already happened in large portions of fertile land and this problem just continues to grow. I was reading this book the other day in which it said that there was once a time when all of North Africa was so fertile, you know, that before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنُو هِلَال</span>, before they got into Africa, he wrote that a squirrel could run from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِنْجَاب</span>, from Egypt all the way to Morocco without its feet ever touching the ground.</p>
<p>Imagine that. A squirrel could run from Egypt to Morocco without its feet ever touching the ground. The land was so fertile, but when these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنُو هِلَال</span>, when they entered North Africa, they were like locusts. They devoured all the resources. So, desertification is another example of ... from what our own hands have earned. Another aspect of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَسَادٌ</span>, as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, you know, in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, The earth has been put out for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَامْ</span>.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَامْ</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَوْمٌ</span>, which means sleep. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَامْ</span> are creatures that sleep. So this verse is actually talking about all sentient beings. It is not just about human beings. So it includes the animal kingdom. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, That there is no creature on the earth that walks the earth. And there is no bird that flies with its two wings, except that they have communities like unto you.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> tells us that the animal kingdoms are communities just like us. And then He says, That He did not leave anything out from this book. Even the animals are acknowledged in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. We know that there are animals that mate for life. We know that animals make huge pilgrimage and, you know, and vast migrations to places just like we do. Right? And today, because of the sins of mankind, you know, animals have all these problems.</p>
<p>I mean, dogs are on Prozac. In the United States, they put dogs on Prozac, which are antidepressants, because they are depressed in the houses of depressed and miserable people. Dogs are not naturally depressed animals, but there are so many houses in the US that are so sick that the dogs and the cats themselves get sick, because they are sentient beings, they are emotional creatures. They feel and experience emotions just like we do.</p>
<p>So there are all these examples of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَسَادٌ</span> that we see in the horizons. Look at the problem of endangered species caused by the hands of man. There are like, I think there are 25 critically endangered species of the world that are from the most beautiful and the most majestic animals. Think about that. These are animals that the ancient Arabs used to name their children after. Animals like, you know, lions and tigers, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسَد</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَهْد</span>.</p>
<p>Right? These are animals that the Arabs, they named their men after. They used to name them after these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُفْتَرِسًا</span>, you know, after animals that attacked their prey, that they would name the men after these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُفْتَرِسَاتٌ</span>, these animals that attack the prey. And then they would name their women after the prey itself, you know, like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَهَا</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَزَالَة</span>. You know, so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَهْد</span> goes after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَهَا</span> and she doesn''t stand a chance.</p>
<p>So that was the ancient Arab concept, right? You know, that is why we have all these beautiful names in Arabic. But what''s interesting is that if you look at the species that are endangered today, they''re not the cockroaches, they''re not the rats, they''re not the snakes — they''re flourishing. The animals and species that are endangered are the ones that represent the divine qualities within us that are disappearing. Right? The eagle, the peregrine, the lion, the tiger, the elephants. These are the animals that represent those great qualities in human beings that are disappearing today within your own souls. Why don''t you reflect? We''ve got plenty of cockroaches out there in human disguise.</p>
<p>We''ve got plenty of rats and snakes out there in human disguise, taking hold of the greatest offices in countries, becoming presidents and things like that. But what we do not find is the courage of lions. What we do not find today is the majesty of the eagles in human form. These are qualities that are extinct from human beings today. And I can go on and on, and I''m sure you can think of and add so much more.</p>
<p>But this is what we''re doing as caliphs and the stewards of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. We are destroying the very earth that sustains us and provides us with our livelihood. And then we complain that we have problems. You know, why doesn''t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> solve all our problems? Why should He? He''s not our janitor, He''s not our servant, He''s not our errand boy. No, He created us in His image so that we could create <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span> on earth, just as He created <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span> on heaven.</p>
<p>Why else do you think <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آدَمُ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span> was created in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>? Just for fun? If he was created for the earth, &quot;I am planning to create a caliph on earth&quot; — why, why did he need to be created in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span> when he was being created for earth? He could have been directly created in, on the earth, right? Then why did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> create him in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>? He was created in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span> so that he could see and experience the beauty of that place, so that when he comes to the earth he could cultivate that land and create that same <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span> on earth.</p>
<p>That is the purpose of a caliph, of a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيفَة</span>. He brought you forth from the earth in order to cultivate it, in order to create the same <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span> on this earth. And what did we do? And why did this happen? You know, how did things get so bad and so out of hand? Because of our corruption, because of our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span>cade, our deviation from the divine attributes and the majestic qualities that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created us with.</p>
<p>And so this is why he created the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَمِين</span> for us, so that we can study the signs of the horizons, the heavens and the earth, to learn the ideal conditions of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>, the ideal condition of the soul, through his divine attributes. But we as human beings, as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيفَةُ اللَّهِ</span>, we have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span>iled miserably, and we have proven that the angels did have valid grounds for their concerns. That''s what we have proved, that they clearly knew what they were talking about when they said, &quot;Do you really wish to create someone on this earth who will sow corruption and spill blood?&quot;</p>
<p>Obviously, they knew what they were talking about. So what is it that we have deviated from? We have deviated from the divine attributes. We have deviated from the character of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. The word for character in Arabic is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>, right? And the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span> comes from the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلُق</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span> is the plural, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلُق</span> is the singular. And this is really interesting, because we are called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْق</span>, right? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْق</span> means creation.</p>
<p>Sadaf, your mic is on. Can you please turn it off? No problem. Right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلُق</span> means character and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْق</span> means creation, which is related to the body. Right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْق</span> is related to the body, and the body exists in the horizons, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلُق</span>, which means character, it belongs to the soul, it belongs to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>, and they''re both made up of the same letters and in the same sequence.</p>
<p>So according to the science of Arabic, both these words are closely related in meaning. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْق</span> is related to the body, which is nourished by this physical world. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلُق</span>, character, it takes its nourishment from the spiritual world. You see? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْق</span> represents the existence of the body. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلُق</span> represents the existence of the soul. And both these words, they begin with the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خ</span>, which is a sound of friction in Arabic, right?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خ</span>. And friction takes effort. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خ</span> in Arabic is a sound of effort. This is because the body takes effort to develop, and so does character. Character takes a lot of effort to develop. Therefore, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْق</span> is the essence of the body, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلُق</span>, or character, is the essence of the soul. When we die, the body returns back into the earth with whatever it has acquired from the physical world, and the soul returns back to the spiritual world with its nourishment of character that it has developed in this world.</p>
<p>That''s our true earning. That''s our— our character is our true accomplishment. That''s our real acquisition. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلُق</span>, character. A soul that returns back to its master without a developed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلُق</span> is an impoverished soul. It is the soul that has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span>iled to fulfill its purpose of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَافَة</span>. It has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span>iled the purpose of its creation, and that''s why a weak soul cannot enter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>, because when the body is weak, it cannot benefit from the offerings of this physical world, right?</p>
<p>I mean, we know that this world has a lot to offer in terms of temporal pleasures, but if you''re extremely weak, if you can''t even get off your bed, you''re not going to be able to enjoy those pleasures, right? In the same way, a soul that is empty, that is bankrupt of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>, is not going to be able to benefit from the pleasures of the afterlife. It''s as simple as that.</p>
<p>Now, how is character developed? It is developed through revelation. As I said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is an— you know, if you look at it in an oversimplified statement, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is the explanation of divine attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. It explains the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and why He has those attributes. Right? He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَظِيم</span> because He created this massive universe. The size of this universe tells us that He''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَظِيم</span>. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَوِيّ</span>.</p>
<p>He''s all-powerful, because not only did He create this massive universe, He created it with a single command, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, be, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَيَكُونُ</span>, it was. So in essence, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is just the description of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s attributes, explaining why He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلَٰه</span>. He created all of us as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰه</span>, but then gave us, through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span>, He gave us these signs for us to recognize that He is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلَٰه</span>, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>Our hearts witness these divine attributes in the light of revelation, which then creates belief, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>. The more the heart understands revelation, the more we grow in our belief, which then nourishes and develops our character. And so character is nothing more than absorbing these divine attributes into the heart from revelation and then reflecting those same divine attributes into the rest of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s creation. That is what character is.</p>
<p>It is internalizing, developing, and training the heart in the light of these divine attributes. And then reflecting them in our interactions with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s creation. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>. And that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْلَام</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْلَام</span> is all about interactions. It is all about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُعَامَلَات</span>. Now, there are three types of interactions in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْلَام</span>. There is the interaction with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the rights of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. There is interacting with the self, which is, or the right of the soul.</p>
<p>And then interaction with the creations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, which is, the rights of others, the right of other slaves. So, I''ll just quickly talk about each of these so that you see how these are all related to the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. So, the first is the right of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Right? What right does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> have over us? That we recognize His attributes in the heavens and the earth, and we approach Him with love for everything that He has done for us.</p>
<p>And then out of that love we willingly obey and serve Him as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span>ithful slaves, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّةٌ</span>. So, it is the right of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> that we become <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدٌ</span>. That we worship Him recognizing the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span>ct that only He deserves to be worshipped. That is the right of the Creator over His creation. Right? Now, what is the right of the self? The right of the self is to reflect on the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم</span> through the teachings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, to learn those attributes, those qualities, basically to learn the character of our master, and to then internalize and harvest those qualities within our own hearts to develop our own character, our own <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>.</p>
<p>Because we have been created in the image of our master. We have been created to represent our master on this earth, which means we have to adopt the qualities and characteristics of our master. Right? That is what a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيفَة</span> is, a representative. You can''t represent unless you become like the one you are representing. Right? So, the second step of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقُّ النَّفْسِ</span> is that you train it in the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, in the character of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>So, for instance, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِيمٌ</span>. He is generous. Therefore, it is the right of my soul that I train it to be generous. Generous with who? Generous with myself. What is the greatest generosity that I can show myself? To try to protect it from sins and put it to work in good deeds. That is the greatest generosity for my soul. We know that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَقُّ</span>, He is truthful.</p>
<p>So it is the right of my soul that I train it to be truthful. With who? With myself. What is the greatest <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span>lsehood that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> is trapped in? The greatest lie that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> is trapped in is the delusion of this reality of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. So I must train myself on a daily basis to try to wake up from my delusions about this world. Right? I need to wake up to the reality that I''m only here for a short period of time.</p>
<p>Everything that I''m working for in the worldly sense will be left behind. I will be left with nothing other than this title of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَيَّتٌ</span>. You know, my loved ones, the people that I am slaving for day and night, they will be the first to dump me in a grave and throw dirt in my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span>ce. So I better not sacrifice my afterlife for this temporary life, you know, by eating <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, by... So that''s being truthful to the self.</p>
<p>So the right of the self is that I internalize the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and train myself in those attributes in order to develop my character so that I can realize the potential that I was created with. I was created in the image of God to act like God, and God is nothing other than just. This is why one of the divine names of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُؤْمِن</span>, which essentially means, you know, the giver of tranquility, the one who brings peace.</p>
<p>You know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span> comes from the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آمِين</span>, which means peace. So that is the right of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>, that I train it in the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And finally, there is the right of others. Now what are the right of others? So right of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> was recognizing His divine attributes in creation and submitting to Him willingly out of love in the form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. Right? Right of the self was to internalize those divine attributes.</p>
<p>I''ve already recognized them. The next step is to internalize those divine attributes to build my own character, to align it with the character of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, to become like the creator. Once that character has developed, only then the final right of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُقُوقُ الْعِبَادِ</span> comes into play, which is the idea of now reflecting those divine attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in interactions with creation. We trained ourselves to become generous with our own self.</p>
<p>Now it''s time to reflect that generosity onto others in our dealings with them. We trained ourselves to be truthful and sincere with our own <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>. Now it''s time to reflect that truthfulness, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدْق</span>, that sincerity in our dealings with others. We''ve recognized how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is forgiving with us, how He''s patient with us. We now have to reflect those attributes by being patient and forgiving with others. And that is not an easy task.</p>
<p>This is why one has to go through the training of fulfilling the previous two rights before he can fulfill this third right. And our problem today is that everyone is a preacher. You know, nobody takes the time to fix themselves, but when it comes to preaching, everybody is a teacher, everybody is enforcing things on others. I mean, I''m not an enforcer, I just share knowledge. So there''s a difference between teaching and between enforcing.</p>
<p>But today, you know, everybody''s the first, oh, you''re doing that wrong, you should be doing that, that, you know, you''re going to hell. That''s pretty much, you know, you can''t even put a shirt on today without getting some criticism from someone. Cannot even mop the floor without somebody telling you, oh, you have to go clockwise and not anti-clockwise, I mean, right? But people never take the time to actually develop their own character. The person saying that usually doesn''t have the most perfect character.</p>
<p>That person is full of flaws himself, but they are enjoying, but they, you know, force that knowledge on others. And that is why it''s not working. That is why we have this really big problem in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْلَام</span> today, within our community, within the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُلَيْمَانِي جَمَاعَة</span>, outside of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُلَيْمَانِي جَمَاعَة</span>, where children are developing this hatred for the religion, because it''s, you know, their first thing is, you don''t pray five times a day.</p>
<p>Why you keep forcing that prayer on me? So we first have to develop our own self, and that''s why this is so... because even dealing with people is not easy. You know, a man once came to the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and he said, O Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, I give him, but he withholds from me. I come to him, but he cuts me off. What should I do?</p>
<p>The Prophet said, that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةُ الرَّحِيمِ</span>. What you''re doing is the right thing. Continue meeting him even though he is cutting you off. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْلَام</span>, and that''s hard work. I mean, don''t think this is an easy religion. There are many easy religions in this world. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْلَام</span> is not one of them. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْلَام</span> demands hard work. It is not easy to become godlike. But then, we want to be people that strive, right? We want to be people that honor ourselves, that we don''t just take a handout, from even from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We want to earn our rewards, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created us as honored creatures, right? We have ennobled the children of Adam. We made you leaders over the land and the oceans.</p>
<p>We gave you a heart. We gave you an intellect. We nourished it through our revelation. We gave you the ability to overcome the worst aspects of yourself. And even if you capitulate from time to time, we''ve given you the means to renew and restore your soul through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>, through repentance. What a gift from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. So this is what we have to do. And we have to do it with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span>, with sincerity.</p>
<p>Only for the sake of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. But that''s not going to be easy. So these are the three dimensions of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. The right of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the right of the self, and the right of others. Recognizing the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in the heavens and the earth help us understand the rights of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Internalizing those divine attributes to develop our character is the right of the soul. It''s. Reflecting those divine attributes onto creation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the right of others.</p>
<p>It is the right... and because this last one is the most challenging of all, it cannot be accomplished unless and until the soul has first been illuminated with the character of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. This is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> said, adopt the character and the qualities of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. You know, there''s a narration in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> once saw a man talking to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِيّ</span> in a very disrespectful manner, right? And in return, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِيّ</span> responded back to this guy with complete kindness, complete humility.</p>
<p>But despite his kindness, this man continued to act in a disrespectful manner with him. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِيّ</span> kept responding with kindness over and over and over again. And then finally, when the man left, the Prophet asked him, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا عَلِي</span>, I saw this man talk to you in such a disrespectful manner. You know, he treated you in such a horrible way over and over again, and yet you kept showing him kindness and respect in return.</p>
<p>Why is that? And Maulana Ali said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللَّهِ</span>, if this man is not willing to give up his wretched and evil nature to interact with me, why should I give up my good character to interact with him? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ</span>, what a beautiful statement. If this man is not willing to give up his wretched and evil nature in his interaction with me, then why should I give up my good character in my interaction with him?</p>
<p>In other words, if this man continues to hold on to something ugly, why should I let go of something beautiful that I possess? That''s the beauty of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>. And this is why we follow the example of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, who according to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> reached the pinnacle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>. Through his character and nothing other than his character, he purified the most barbaric and the most vile group of people that ever walked this earth.</p>
<p>I mean, read the history of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْلَام</span>. You know, understand the mentality of these people he was sent to reform. Just think if you w' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'the horizons and the soul';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'ere sent to these people, just put yourself in his shoes. These were the worst; they were worse than animals. There was not a single virtue that existed in them. They were insane. They would eat the flesh of elephants and crocodiles. When they ate a goat, they would even boil and eat its leather.</p>
<p>They would burn their enemies alive in a bonfire. They would rip out their skin and wear it like a scarf while they were alive, the enemy. They would crack open their skull and use it as a cup to drink wine in. Their women would dye their clothes in the blood of their enemies and wear it. They were so barbaric that they would not just kill their enemy, they would tie that enemy''s camel to his grave and starve it to death.</p>
<p>That''s how barbaric these people were. Sons would marry their mothers. Fathers married their daughters. Daughters were buried alive. They used the children of their enemy for target practice. Even their prayers were barbaric. Can you imagine that? The prayers were horrific. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَبِّ فَلَا تَجْعَلِ الْحَيَاةَ مُذِلَّةً وَلَا مَوْتَةً بَيْنَ النِّسَاءِ نَوَائِحِهْ وَلَٰكِنْ قَتِيلًا تُمَزِّقُ الطَّيْرُ حَوْلَهُ وَتَشْرَبُ غِرْبَانُ دَمِي مِنْ جَوَارِحِهْ</span>. O Lord, do not disgrace me in my life by letting me die on a bed between grieving women.</p>
<p>Give me a death where birds are tearing out my flesh and crows are drinking the fresh blood gushing out of my limbs. I mean, this was their prayers. This was the state of the Arabs when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> was sent between them. Can you imagine that? This was the group he was sent to reform. And look what he did. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ</span>. He converted these people into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةُ اللهِ عَلَيْهِ</span>. Today we don''t even mention their names without saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةُ اللهِ عَلَيْهِ</span> after it.</p>
<p>May <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> have mercy on them. That is the kind of character he cultivated within them. And this is by their own admission, the admission of these own <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَاهِلِي</span> Arabs on the day of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَادِر</span> when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> asked, am I not more beloved to you than your own souls? What did they say? Indeed you are, a Prophet of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We were animals. You turned us into human beings.</p>
<p>And how did he do that? Through his impeccable <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>. By internalizing the divine attributes of his creator, by absorbing them to develop this immaculate character and then finally reflecting those very same attributes into creation with such intensity that the Creator Himself had to acknowledge in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. Indeed, no doubt about it, you have achieved the utmost and the most exalted level of character. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. May <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> grant us a few rays from the light of that sun that shined in the form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>. And may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you know, give us the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> to act in some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَ</span>shion as his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيفَة</span>, as his representatives. And the only way that can happen is through this training of adopting the characteristics of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِ سَيِّدِ مُحَمَّدًا خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَالصَّالِحِينَ وَآلِهِ وَأَصْحَابِهِ عَلِيِّ ابْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/86/718f2946-a675-46a9-ae87-0aaea1e7e6f8.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
