<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/86/0f665992-7d40-40ca-833b-ab1b223125cd.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'the horizons and the soul';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span>. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>.</p>
<p>Now that we understand these meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, now that we understand these terms, I want to share with you a beautiful spiritual representation that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> has created for us through the example of the womb, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>. To quickly reiterate these terms, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> is a combination of six things in classical Arabic. The definition is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّهُ الْمَلِكُ وَالسَّيِّدُ الْمُرَبِّي الْمُرْشِدُ وَالْمُنْعِمُ الْقَيِّمُ</span>. So a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> is an owner and he is the one with complete authority over its property. He is the one who nurtures his creation, guides it, benefits it by bestowing generous and undeserving gifts upon it, and he is the one who maintains it. So this is who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is, right? That''s His primary role with His creation, of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>. And all His other attributes, they basically center around and they stem from His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْبَرِيَّةِ</span>. So if someone asks, who is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>? The answer to that question is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>. Now, as for His nature, His nature is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيم</span>. So the manner in which He executes, or the way He carries out the functions of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْبَيَّة</span>, it is through intense and consistent love, compassion and mercy.</p>
<p>So now that we understand these concepts, I want to share this example that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has created for us through the womb. You know, by studying the womb, we can actually understand a lot about our master''s plan for us. Just by studying the relationship of the womb with its fetus. Because this womb, it is the first <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبَّ</span> of the fetus. And it demonstrates the exact same principles of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span> in its interaction with it. This is why there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span> in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, he said, this world is a womb for the afterlife. In other words, the story of our lives in this world, it tells a tale about the kind of life that we''re going to have in the afterlife, because this world, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> that we''re living in, is the womb, or acts as the womb, and we are the fetus in that womb. And just as the brief and temporary residence in a womb is actually preparation for this worldly life, our stay in this temporal world is nothing more than a short duration of preparation for the afterlife. Now, if you look at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, the womb, you''ll see all six principles of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبِّيَ</span> at work. It has ownership and it has authority over the embryo, right?</p>
<p>It nurtures the embryo, it helps the embryo grow and develop into a fetus. So the womb is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَٰلِكِ</span>, it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّد</span> and it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبِّي</span>. Then we see that the womb, it bestows these priceless gifts upon the fetus, to the development of its eyes, its nose, its ears, hands, feet, the heart, the intellect and all of its internal and external organs. So we can see that the womb is also the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْعِم</span>, the bestower of gifts. We also know that the womb, it manages the entire development process of the embryo from its conception all the way until it is fully developed. So the womb is also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيِّم</span>. So we can see that the womb, it actually acts as the very first physical <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبَّ</span> that we come in contact with. And this very first act, it actually demonstrates the design of how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> created this mechanism. It demonstrates the act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> by carrying out all six functions of the definition of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكٌ، سَيِّدٌ، مُرَبٍّ، مُرْشِدٌ، مُهَيْمِنٌ، قَيِّمٌ</span>. Now, what happens once the baby is delivered into this world?</p>
<p>Who is the baby handed over to? It is handed over to its loving mother, who becomes the next immediate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> of that baby. Not the father, the mother. It''s the mother that becomes the next <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>. Why? Because she feeds it. She takes care of it. There is this transference of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبِّيَ</span> that takes place. The mother now takes ownership of the baby. The mother now takes authority over it. The mother assumes the responsibility of nurturing it, helping it grow and develop, teaching it, guiding it, setting it to purpose, showering all these gifts and blessings upon it. She becomes the one who maintains that baby''s sustenance in all its various forms, physical, emotional, psychological.</p>
<p>So we can see that our mothers become the next immediate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>. Right after the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> of the womb, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَٰن</span>, it''s completed. Now father is also a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, but his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> gets executed through the mother. You see, the father always uses the mother as a means for her. He facilitates her functions of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>. That''s the role of the father, but the primary caretaker of the child is always the mother.</p>
<p>So now let''s study this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span> to better understand the underlying principles that are at work. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> , he said, . This temporal world is the womb of the afterlife. So let''s examine the fetus and the womb of its mother. Is it the womb actually doing all the work? Or is it the mother doing all the work behind the scenes and letting this womb act as an intermediary between herself and her child?</p>
<p>Is it the womb or the mother? We know it''s always the mother, right? The mother is the one taking care of the baby''s needs. She is the one eating all the right things. She is making sure she doesn''t bump into things so that her baby doesn''t get hurt. She''s the one getting all the ultrasounds done, taking all those painful but necessary blood tests, all those injections. So she is the one actually suffering for the baby. Baby is living comfortably in the womb, but it''s the mother doing all the work, right? The mother is the one doing all the hard work all along. But from the perspective of the fetus, who is doing all the work? The womb, not the mother. You see, the fetus has absolutely no knowledge of its mother.</p>
<p>All it knows, all it recognizes, all it understands is the womb. If I ask the fetus, who do you think is feeding you? Who do you think is nurturing you, taking care of you, sustaining you? Who is doing all the work that is necessary for your health and development? What will the fetus reply? The womb. The fetus has absolutely no knowledge of the existence of its mother. You see? What mother? Where''s the mother? I don''t see a mother. All I see is the womb.</p>
<p>There is no mother. It''s just cause and effect. When I tell this fetus, no, no, it''s not the womb, it''s the mother. She is the one feeding you. She''s the one taking care of you. This womb is only an intermediary. It''s a means. And the fetus says, you know, what are you talking about? It''s the umbilical cord that''s feeding me. That''s what gets me this blood that is carrying all the nutrients. I get fed by that, the umbilical cord. The umbilical cord is responsible for my development.</p>
<p>There is no mother. And so I explain, no, no, there is a mother. She is all around you. You are within the love, the care, the concern, and the mercy of your mother. This womb wouldn''t exist if she didn''t exist. And this womb that you''re in, it''s actually temporary. This is not the world you were created for. Believe me, this world is filthy, it is dark, it is immensely constricted. There is an amazing and a beautiful world that exists outside this womb that has been created just for you. And that world is so magnificent that you cannot even imagine its glory right now within this womb. Trust me, this womb is filth compared to that world. So if you think this womb is kind and merciful to you, let me tell you, it doesn''t even compare to the love and the mercy of your mother.</p>
<p>Because your mother is going to take care of you in the next world. She will shower you with so much love that you have absolutely no idea. You cannot even imagine that while you''re in this womb. But the fetus says, what are you talking about? I am really, really happy here. Everything I need is being taken care of for me right here. Why do I need to have another world? I don''t know why you keep talking to me about this mother and another world and an afterlife. There is no mother. There is no world outside this womb. You''re crazy. Okay, show me.</p>
<p>Show me this world that you keep talking about. Show it to me right now. I want to see it. Prove to me that there is another world. Prove to me that there is actually a mother. I''m not gonna believe you unless and until I see that world and until I see that mother. And I say, I can''t show it to you right now, but I promise when you transition out of this world, this womb, you''ll see both of them. Oh, so now I''m supposed to trust you. I''m supposed to trust your belief system, your intellect? What if you''re wrong? Now this is us with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> in this world. There are so many of us denying the existence of the mother, living in the comforts of this womb, of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>.</p>
<p>So now, I begin this logical path of explanation and I say, look, you have been given these eyes, but there is so little to see here. You''ve been given these ears, but there''s so little to hear. You''ve been given taste buds, but there is so little to taste here. You have a nose with this magnificent ability to smell, but there''s not a single fragrance here. You have arms, you have feet, you have all these amazing gifts, but you cannot benefit from them within this womb. So think about it. These are of no use to you inside this womb, which means there must be another world that is out there, a world where you will be able to use and benefit from these gifts. Think about it. Your only purpose in this womb, in this world, is to draw your sustenance from your mother and develop these organs. In other words, you''re here so that you can prepare them for the next world where you will actually be using them, where you will actually benefit from them. But does the fetus understand that?</p>
<p>No. It doesn''t. To the fetus, the concept of another world and the existence of a mother is just ridiculous. Just as the concept of a God and an afterlife is absurd to so many of us. So now what happens? This fetus continues to live in its ignorance and its arrogance until the nine months complete. Now what happens? Contractions begin. The fetus starts panicking. What''s going on here? What''s happening? This is what was promised to you.</p>
<p>I told you this day would come. I told you this world is temporary. So now with those contractions, this beautiful world of the fetus, this magnificent world it was living happily and comfortably in, it starts crumbling around it. It starts falling apart all around it. Now, isn''t that exactly the description of the Day of Judgment? When the earth is shaken up with its earthquakes. And the earth discharges its burden. And man says, what is wrong with this world? What is wrong with this earth? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمَئِذٍ تُحَدِّثُ أَخْبَارَهَا بِأَنَّ رَبَّكَ أَوْحَىٰ لَهَا</span>. On this day, it will report the news of which your master has commanded it. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمَئِذٍۢ يَصْدُرُ ٱلنَّاسُ أَشْتَاتًۭا لِّيُرَوْا۟ أَعْمَلَهُمْ</span> ‎.</p>
<p>On this day, people will separate out, and they will be shown the results of their deeds. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَمَن يَعْمَلْ مِثْقَالَ ذَرَّةٍ خَيْرًۭا يَرَهُۥ</span> ‎. So, whoever did an atom''s weight of good will see it. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَن يَعْمَلْ مِثْقَالَ ذَرَّةٍۢ شَرًّۭا يَرَهُۥ</span> ‎. And whoever did an atom''s weight of evil will see it. So the world of the fetus now comes to an end, and it is forced out of it, it is pushed out of it against its will. You see, does the fetus have a choice in whether it wants to stay in the womb or not? No. Whether it wants to or not, whether it''s ready or not, once its time is up, that''s it. It is pushed out into the next world. It dies in the womb in order to be born again in the next world, you see. Its death in the womb results in its creation in this world. That''s what the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">انْتَقَلَ</span> means. It comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَقَلَ</span>, which means a transference. It also means a copy or a replica. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَقَلَ</span> means, a copy or a replica.</p>
<p>Why? Because the next world is going to be a copy, it is going to be a replica of the first. If you did good in the world, in this world, you will find good in the next world. If you did evil in this world, you will find evil in the next world. It''s going to be a replica, it''s going to be a copy. If the fetus has not properly developed its eyes, its ears, its brain, its limbs in the womb of its mother, it is going to suffer the consequences of that in the afterlife of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So now this temporal world of the fetus is destroyed and it comes into this huge world that he was informed about, this world that was promised to him in the womb. He is now forced into this world, this afterlife that he chose not to believe in. And then, as soon as it''s out of the womb, who is the baby handed to? The mother.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. The mother that he said did not exist, the mother he denied his entire life in the womb. So you can see that the fetus goes through the exact same confusion of the seen and the unseen while it''s in the womb of its mother. That''s so many of us go through in this life. The fetus that accepts the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> of its mother through the womb comes out fully developed and is now capable of enjoying all the benefits that the afterlife has to offer, which is this physical world for the fetus. But the fetus that rejected the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> of the womb, it comes out underdeveloped, blind, deaf, mute, physically challenged, mentally challenged, incapable of benefiting from the joys and pleasures that this world has to offer. We are all going through this exact same process in life. Why? Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدُّنْيَا رَحِمُ الآخِرَةِ</span>, this world is the womb for the afterlife. And here we have our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> telling us, look, follow the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, keep yourself clean, keep yourself pure, do your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, complete your fast, pay your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>, fulfill your obligations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَجّ</span>. And what do we say?</p>
<p>Why? How is this going to help me? It''s not going to help you here. That''s not the purpose. The obligations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> is not meant for this world. It''s the sustenance for the development of our spiritual limbs. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> is. It''s a means like the womb. It''s a tool. It''s a mechanism, a means, an intermediary for the development of our spiritual limbs, for the development of our spiritual organs that are going to help us live a good life. After this world is over, that is where we will benefit from these fully developed spiritual organs. In the afterlife, all we''re doing here in this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> is developing them in order to use them in the next world, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>.</p>
<p>But how do you develop them? Through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span> is described as a world that is incredibly beautiful, a world that is governed by a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> who is intensely merciful, who is constantly merciful. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> is the umbilical cord providing the sustenance for the development of our spiritual existence, of our spiritual being. Now whether we believe in it or not, whether we want to or not, whether we execute it or not, that next world is inevitable. It''s coming. Right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْجَنَّةُ حَقٌّ، وَالنَّارُ حَقٌّ، وَالْمَوْتُ حَقٌّ، وَالْبَرْزَخُ حَقٌّ، وَالْمِيزَانُ حَقٌّ، وَالصِّرَاطُ حَقٌّ</span>. Paradise is a reality. Hellfire is a reality. Death is a reality.</p>
<p>Rising from graves is a reality. The scales in which our actions will be weighed, that is a reality. The straight path is a reality. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَنَّ ٱلسَّاعَةَ آتِيَةٌۭ لَّا رَيْبَ فِيهَا</span>. And that appointed moment, it is coming. There is absolutely no doubt about it. That is the world in which justice will be exacted. Every incident for which you said, where is justice? On that day we will see justice. The innocent people that suffered momentarily in this short span of life, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, this world, their momentary suffering will be balanced out with never-ending gifts and pleasures in the afterlife. And the one who caused those sufferings for fleeting gains and pleasures, their pleasures will be counterbalanced with grievous pain and everlasting torment. But that day of judgment is coming. These are all descriptions of the day of judgment, when this universe will be destroyed, this world that we are so comfortable in, it''s going to end. That day is coming when souls will be paired together, when you will finally meet those loved ones</p>
<p>Who left you behind in this world? And when that little girl who was buried alive will be asked, for what sin were you killed? This is the day where <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> will exact justice. This is the day of justice, not this world, not this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> that we''re living in. That day all the deeds will open up. And when the book of deeds will be made public, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذَا ٱلسَّمَآءُ كُشِطَتْ</span>. And when the sky will be stripped away, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذَا ٱلْجَحِيمُ سُعِّرَتْ</span>. And when that hellfire is set ablaze, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذَا ٱلْجَنَّةُ أُزْلِفَتْ</span>. And when the paradise is brought near, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلِمَتْ نَفْسٌۭ مَّآ أَحْضَرَتْ</span> ‎. Every soul will know what it has brought with it. All good will be rewarded on that day and all bad will be punished on that day. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>.</p>
<p>And the fact of the matter is that we have such a benevolent, such a merciful, such a loving master who is just dying to reward us for the smallest good deed that we do in this world. You know, it has been narrated by the Prophet that there will be a man on the Day of Judgment and all this man ever did was lend money to others. He was a money lender. And usually such people are black hearted people because of interest, and they use that as a mechanism to gain worldly profit. But when this man sent out his servants to collect the money that people owed him, if the person was having a hard time returning back that money, if that''s what the servant would return and tell him, he would say, forget about it. So on the Day of Judgment, this man comes before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and he does not have any good deeds to his name, except that he used to do that.</p>
<p>And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, He displays this man to everyone and He says, will I leave him to be more generous than me? Will I leave this man to be more generous than me? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. He used to forgive people. Do you think I am not going to forgive him today? This is the master, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> that we fail in our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو ضِيَاء</span> towards. On this day, every single good deed will be balanced with a reward from an immensely beneficent, from an incredibly loving and compassionate master. All our delusions of grandeur in this world, they will be gone. On that day, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُفْلِسٌ</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَنِيٌّ</span>, the destitute and the affluent, they will be separated.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> once asked his companions. He asked, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنِ الْمُفْلِسُ</span>? Who is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُفْلِسٌ</span>? Who is the destitute? They said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُفْلِسُ مَنْ لَا دِينَارَ وَلَا دِرْهَمَ عِنْدَنَا يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>. The destitute is the one who does not have any <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">درهم</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينَارٌ</span> to his name. And the Prophet said, no. He said, the greatest destitute of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> on the Day of Judgment, the greatest destitute of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> on the Day of Judgment, will be the one who comes with all of his prayers and all of his fasting and all of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَيَأْتِي قَطْ شَتَمَ هَذَا وَقَذَفَ هَذَا وَأَكَلَ مَالَ هَذَا وَسَفَكَ دَمَ هَذَا وَضَرَبَ هَذَا</span>, but in addition to all of these good deeds, he cheated this one, he lied to this one, he usurped the wealth of this one, he spilled the blood of this one, he struck this one. And so his good deeds will be taken from his account and put into the account of this one and this one and this one. And when his good deeds run out, before justice is fully served, the sins and faults of the remaining victims, they will be taken from their accounts and added to his accounts. And so despite living an entire life full of worship, full of prayers, full of fasting, paying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>, because of his injustices that he committed with other people, because of the hurt that he caused to other people in this world, this man will have to give up all his good deeds and he will have to take on all the horrible sins of his victims and he will finally be thrown into Hellfire.</p>
<p>This person, he said, will be the greatest destitute of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>. May <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> protect us all from being among these destitutes on the Day of Judgment.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرُ دَعْوَاهُمْ أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ</span> he <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِمَا الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ الْفَاتِحَة</span>.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'the womb of afterlife';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span></p>
<p>Now that we understand the linguistic meanings of the basic terms that I''ve explained so far in these sessions, now it''s time to start with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَسْمَلَة</span>, which is actually the first verse of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎. So Arabs had this way of shortening these commonly repeated statements. So if they wanted to refer to ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, they would, instead of saying the statement completely, they would just shorten it down to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَسْمَلَة</span>, right? And so, for example, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا حَوْلَ وَلَا قُوَّةَ إِلَّا بِاللَّهِ</span>, they just simply would call it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَوْقَلَة</span>. Similarly, they would use <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> for ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, and so on. So the term ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, it actually refers to the first verse of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, which is ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎.</p>
<p>Now, today what I want to focus on is this concept of being and attributes, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is a being, He''s an entity, and then He has all these various attributes. And this is quite a complicated topic, so, you know, I tried to make it as simple as possible, but there are literally books written on this, so it''s very difficult to condense this huge topic. But if you look at the statement of ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ from a grammatical point of view, you''ll see that in the context of mentioning <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, it uses a noun and it uses two adjectives, right? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ</span>, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is a noun representing an entity, a being, and then you have two attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>, right? These are the adjectives or the attributes describing the nature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, that He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and His nature is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>.</p>
<p>So let''s talk about this distinction between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s name and His attributes, which is essentially the distinction between the concepts of a noun and adjectives if you look at it from a grammatical point of view. Attributes or adjectives are tools that have been provided to us by language in order for us to understand something. In other words, it is through the combination of these two things, noun and adjectives, or name and attributes, that we actually understand an entity, right? So if you want to gain a complete understanding of an entity, you need to understand its attributes. In the Arabic language, this is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَاتٌ وَصِفَاتٌ</span>, being and attributes.</p>
<p>So for instance, we have a soul and we have a body, which together constitute our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span>, our being. But then soul has certain spiritual attributes, right? It can be kind, it can be brave, intelligent, generous, or it can be weak, it can be coward, it can be dumb, right? In the same way, the body has its own set of physical attributes: fat, thin, black, brown, white, tall, short. And once we understand these spiritual and physical attributes that are describing the entity, which is the soul or the body, we essentially come to recognize the nature of that being.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/152/92005ff6-ab51-4acb-a733-01fbbe1e5f0e.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>So the union of the two, they provide the identity and a complete description of that identity, which then enables our intellects to understand the nature of that being. But the thing is, attributes cannot exist without the being, right? It is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span> that gives existence to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِفَاتْ</span>. Attributes can only exist after the existence of the being itself. So if there is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, there cannot be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>. So that is why the name <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is greater in its rank than a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span> on the basis of its precedence.</p>
<p>So now if we start breaking down the statement of the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, the term <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> points to His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span>, to His being. You know, He is a deity named <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the title of His essence. As He says in the Quran, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّنِىٓ أَنَا ٱللَّهُ لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّآ أَنَا۠ فَٱعْبُدْنِى وَأَقِمِ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ لِذِكْرِىٓ</span> ‎, no doubt I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, and there is no one worthy of worship and obedience except Me. Now this noun <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has adjectives, which are His 99 <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِفَةُ سُورَةِ الفَاتِحَةِ</span>, His attributes describing the nature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, right? These are His various manifestations in His interactions with creation.</p>
<p class="ar-block">So now you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المَلِك</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقُدُّوسُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَام</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُؤْمِن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُؤْمِن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَزِيزُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْجَبَّار</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُتَكَبِّرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَالِقُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَارِئ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُصَوِّر</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَفَّار</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَهَّار</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَهَّابُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَلِيمُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكَابِدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَاسِطُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَافِضُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْفَاتِحَة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُعِزُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُذِلُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّامِر</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَصِيرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَكَمُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَكِيم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَدْل</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَللَّطِيفُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَلِيم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكَبِيرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَظِيم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَفُورُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْعَلِىُّ ٱلْكَبِيرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَافِظ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَسِيبُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكَرِيم</span>,</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُقِيم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّقِيبُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْجَلِيلُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُجِيبُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَسِيرْ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَدُود</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَجِيد</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَاعِثُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشَّهِيدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَكِيل</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْكَاوِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المَتِين</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَلِيُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمِيدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُحْصِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُبْدِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُعِيدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُحْيِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُمِيءُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَيُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَيُّوم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَاجِدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَجِيد</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَاحِدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصَّمَدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَاضِيل</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُقْتَدِرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُقَدِّم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُؤَخِّر</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَوَّلُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الآخِرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الظَّاهِرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَاطِنُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَلِيُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُتَعَالِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبِرُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التَّوَّاب</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُنْتَقِمُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَفُوّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّؤُوفُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المَلِك</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُلْك</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذُو الْجَلَالِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيُّ كَرَمٍ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُقْسِطُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْجَامِعَة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَنِيُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُكْنِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المَنِيَّة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذْدَر</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّفِير</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النُّور</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْهَادِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البَادِر</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البَاقِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الوَارِف</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّشِيدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصَّبُورُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَنَّانُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَنَّانُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدَّيَّانُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البُرْهَان</span>.</p>
<p>So now, these are all these attributes that describe the nature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. And to use the example of the prism and the light that I stated in one of the previous sessions, now you can think of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, as the term <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, as one pure light, right? He is the light of the heavens and the earth. Now, when this pure light of the name <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> comes into the physical realm, His divine attributes refract within creation. The light of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> splits into these 99 attributes within His creation.</p>
<p>So now we get all these different manifestations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s essence based on His interaction with His creation. So in one instance we see that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرِيم</span>, He is generous, but then in another instance we see He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَبَّار</span>, the compeller. In another instance we see He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحَرْ</span>, the subduer. In another we see that He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَزِيزُ</span>, the powerful. In another we see He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسِيل</span>, the one who humbles, but then He''s also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْمُعَوِّ</span>...</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>, is the one who bestows honor. So His essence manifests through all these different attributes within creation.</p>
<p>This world then becomes a theater for the divine manifestation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s attributes, enabling us to know who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is, not directly through His essence, but through the refraction of His attributes and His creations. This is why it is said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَفَكُّرَ فِي اللهِ، وَلَكِنْ تَفَكَّرْ فِي خَلْقِ اللهِ</span>, do not reflect upon <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, but rather reflect upon the creations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Why?</p>
<p>Because it is creation, it is nature that manifests all of His attributes for us. So if you want to understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, look outside yourself and look within yourself. We will keep showing them our signs within their own, within the horizons and within their own souls, until it becomes perfectly clear to them that all of this is from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَقّ</span>.</p>
<p>But then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is infinite, and so are His attributes. This is why, when we attempt to understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, we get bewildered by His attributes, because in a sense we cannot know what God is any more than we can know what God isn''t. You see, because when we speak about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, we are speaking about this infinite creator of the heavens and the earth. And the word infinite means without a limit. Now, the word in Arabic for definition is the same word that we use for a limitation or a boundary. A definition is what defines or gives finiteness to something, a concept. It limits it, it demarcates it, it puts it into perspective. So when we talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, we cannot define God. Because to define Him would mean to give Him a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُطّ</span>, a boundary, right?</p>
<p>We would have to place Him in a limit, which is impossible, because, you know, He is without any limit. The way the intellect works, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَقَالَ</span> means to create a boundary. Right? That''s the only way human intellect works. That''s the only way we know how to grasp or understand something, by creating a boundary, by creating a limit. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> cannot be limited. And this is why scholars say that all of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَقِيدَة</span>, this concept of creed, it can be reduced into two statements. He is unlike anything. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا يُشْبِهُ ذَاتَهُ الذَّوَاتُ وَلَا مُعَطَّلَةٌ تُوصَفُ الصِّفَاتُ</span>. That His essence is not like any other essence, and His attributes are not in any way metaphorical. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَنِيُّ عَنِ الْجَمِيعِ وَالْجَمِيعُ مُفْتَقِرٌ إِلَيْهِ</span>. He has no need of anyone, and everyone is in need of Him. So this statement actually negates need for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and it enforces need for the rest of creation. It negates independence for us.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/152/12b65610-07b1-45a5-be3a-0ebb449a5e2f.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>Now, here is where we get into another central problem with religion, and that is the problem of transcendence and immanence. Transcendence is when we speak about God without anything, you know, any reference in this world that can guide us to Him. There is nothing in the world for which we can say, you know, that is God or this is like God, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is above and beyond anything they ascribe to. So when we talk about transcendence, we''re talking about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> being above and beyond everything that we know of, right? That''s why we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَكَ</span>, which is in Arabic the word, it means transcendence, and it comes from which means to purify, to get rid of filth, you know, to transcend something, to be above and beyond something. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is transcendent.</p>
<p>But then pure transcendence has a lot of problems by itself. Because then now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> becomes the God of the scientist. Like Einstein believed in a God, but he did not believe in a personal God, right? He believed in a transcendent God. The God he believed in was the God of the deist, who has nothing to do with his creation. And then that again creates a major problem. But then there is also this concept of immanence, the idea of God being close to us in the world. So for example, like in Hinduism, right, the Brahman class, they don''t really believe in idols. You know, we generally, when we think of a Hindu, we think they are idol worshipers, but this class of Brahmans, right, they don''t really believe in idols.</p>
<p>It''s really a kind of trick to help all these stupid people of the lower class that they believe, who they feel that still need to evolve. So there''s this understanding in their tradition that the lower classes, or the caste system that they have, which they call the varna. So if you''re in a lesser varna, you cannot understand the Brahma. That''s what they believe, because he has gone through several lifetimes to get this ability to perceive. And it is something that only he can perceive, and only he can understand the Godhead, as they call it. Right, so Brahma understands the God that has no attribute, which they call the nirguna. But they have this idea that the lower class people actually need to see something in order to understand God, and so they gave them all these idols. It''s the Brahman class that gave idols, this concept of idols, to the lower classes, to the lower varna, who they actually knew were not gods. But they thought that it helps the common people understand God. And this is the problem of immanence in religion, which is creating likeness for God. You understand?</p>
<p>The way Christianity deals with this problem is obviously through Jesus, because Jesus becomes this incarnation of God, right? God enters into flesh. And in our tradition this is what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُلُول</span>, where God enters the flesh, and some of the deviant Sufis, they actually claim the state. So this is not something specific to Christianity. You know, we know that there was a group called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نُصِرَ</span> that claimed divinity for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِيّ</span>.</p>
<p>And it actually also happened for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامٌ جَعْفَرٌ صَادِقٌ</span> and other Imams as well. So now, how do we understand this concept of immanence in our religion? What is the relationship between transcendence and immanence? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَنْزِيهٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَجْرِيد</span>? Because you do need an access to your master, right? If you have a completely transcendent God, he becomes this cold, distant, uncaring, unloving God. On the other hand, if you give God the attributes of creation, you now have a whole new problem, and that''s what gives birth to these concepts of anthropomorphism, falling into this idea that God is petulant, that God has emotions, that God has anger, that he has pleasure in an anthropomorphic way. And this is something that, you know, penetrates the Muslim understanding as well. There are Muslims who think that when these words are used in the Hadith or the Quran, so for example when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says the one who faced the anger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, they think that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s anger is like our anger, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> becomes angry in an emotional sense. And that''s not true, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is above and beyond anything that is ascribed to him. But we do believe that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَب</span> has a meaning, and we believe that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِضًا</span> has a meaning. You know, we believe that these words that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> used in the Quran, like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَّضِىَ ٱللَّهُ عَنْهُمْ وَرَضُوا۟ عَنْهُ</span>, they were pleased with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> was pleased with them. But don''t think your pleasure with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the same as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s pleasure with you. Don''t think those two words mean the same thing. In the context they are being used, each means a specific thing in relation to that thing that it is predicating. So when we talk about the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, we have to be extremely careful. When we try to create an understanding of these attributes, we have to be extremely careful, because we in a sense can only say about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has said about Himself. We cannot, you know, any description that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> needed for our understanding, He described it Himself in the Quran. But then now here we enter into another problem, which is the problem of interpretation. Because even though <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is telling us things about Himself in the Quran, even though He is self-disclosing to us, He is self-disclosing through language.</p>
<p>And language is filtered through the human mind. And the human mind has the capacity to interpret what is being said in so many different ways. So now we have a whole new set of problems. And this is why we stick to the Prophet and his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">واسع</span>, right? Because they are the purest filter for us. And they''re still a filter, right? The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> is also a filter, but he is the purest filter. And this is why the prophets have a capacity that other human beings do not have, and that is that, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> has chosen them to be these filters for revelation. And that is why the best understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is the understanding that our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> has articulated to us. And this is what he said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ لَا نُحْصِي ثَنَاءً عَلَيْكَ أَنْتَ كَمَا أَثْنَيْتَ عَلَى نَفْسِكَ</span>. O <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, we are unable to praise you. You are as you praised yourself. In other words, I cannot truly praise you. Because one of the reasons that we praise is based on our knowledge of the praiseworthy, right? If you don''t know the qualities of someone or something, you cannot really praise it. You cannot really praise them.</p>
<p>And this is why in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s self-disclosure to us, He is describing for us the reasons why He is praiseworthy, the reasons why He is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمِيدٌ</span>. He has described to us, explained to us in the Quran the praiseworthy attributes that He has and why. This is how understanding the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> enables us to come to know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. But these attributes are known to us through our human experience. And that''s where you get into the problem of immanence. You know, there has to be some correlation between what we understand about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and what we understand about ourselves, or what we see in the world, because otherwise we just simply cannot know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. It just wouldn''t be possible. And so in dealing with the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, you are still working with things in this world, you see.</p>
<p>So when we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is, for instance, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَدِيم</span>, right? And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَدِيم</span> becomes a problematic word because it''s a technical word. It is not found in the Quran. It is not actually an attribute of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. It''s more of a technical word. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِدَم</span> is what they call a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> attribute, because it negates <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>.</p>
<p>I know this is getting complicated, but basically it negates occurrence. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَدِيم</span> means. It negates occurrence, it negates the fact that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدَثَ</span>, that He came into existence. No, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> did not come into existence. The idea of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِدَمٌ</span> is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is as He always was and He will always continue to be the same way, without any change or any time factor. And this is where pre-eternity becomes the best word in English that we can use. When we say pre-eternity, I don''t even know what that means. I really don''t. But it''s a word that, I mean, how can you describe pre-eternity? You cannot even describe eternity, because eternity means forever. And so how do you use pre with eternity? How can there be something before that is eternal, right?</p>
<p>So I really don''t know what that word means. I cannot. I have a sense of what that word means, but I cannot really describe it. But that''s the word that, that at best what we have come up with as a way of describing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s existence. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So you can see that we run into all these problems when we try to understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. And so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِيٌّ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, he gave us this simple, simple formula for recognition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَجْزُ عَنْ دَرَكِ الْإِدْرَاكِ إِدْرَاكٌ</span>. Well, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَحْثُ أَنْزَتِهِ كُفْرٌ وَإِشْرَاقٌ</span>. My inability to recognize <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is recognizing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. My inability to recognize <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, my inability of my intellectual powers to create a boundary, to create a definition around <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, the understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, is recognizing Him. And any attempt to discuss His essence, it will inevitably result in assigning and attributing partners with Him, creating likeness for Him. So accepting our inability to know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is our true recognition of Him.</p>
<p>And this is why the most essential act in worship in Islam is the act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِجْدَة</span>, putting our intellects on the ground and surrendering its capacity to recognize <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Now, Quran gives us an incredibly concise statement to help us understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Because the way in which we understand this world around us is through things, right? The Arabic word for a thing is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَة</span>, right? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَة</span>, it means thing, and this word, it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَاءَ</span>, which means will, the will of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Everything, every being, every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span> that exists in this world has its own individual set of attributes, right? Every noun has its adjectives, all of which points to what they point to, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ شَاءَ</span>, to the will and the purpose of their creator. So every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَة</span> is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span>, it''s a being, it''s an entity which has its own set of attributes, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِفَاتْ</span>. And they are a sign, they are a means, they are a tool for us to understand the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْشَأ</span> of the Creator, the will and the purpose of the Creator.</p>
<p>But when it comes to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> Himself, when it comes to the Creator Himself, there is no thing, there is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْء</span> that can be compared to Him in any way, shape or form. And so the Quran beautifully explains this concept to us through one single word in an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَةٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَمِثْلِهِ</span>. No matter what you do, no matter how high your imaginations fly, no matter how deep your thoughts dive, there is absolutely no way for you to understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, because there is no thing, no being, no attribute in existence, whether it''s physical or spiritual or metaphysical or conceptual or theoretical, there is nothing that is like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. There is absolutely nothing that you can use to compare it to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> or to create a likeness for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>Now I want you to take a moment to really let this paradox sink in. He created this magnificent universe all around us. Every single entity with its attributes is reflecting His divine existence. It''s reflecting His own attributes for us, so that we can gain a semblance of understanding of His powers, His glory, His majesty, His wrath, His love, His everything. And then once we do manage to get some feeble understanding of His attributes in creation, He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْسَ كَمِثْلِهِۦ شَىْءٌۭ</span>. Look, whatever little now that you think you know about Me, understand that I''m not like that. I''m way beyond it. And what''s so incredible is that He does this linguistically through the use of one single word. This is not a word that is commonly used, and it was not even used in classical Arabic. The ancient Arabs actually never used this word. This is a word that is used in only in eloquence of the Quran.</p>
<p>So from a grammatical standpoint, the word is actually two words. There is the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span>, and then there is the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثْلِهِ</span>. These are both words that are used for comparison, but they are never fused together in this manner. They are always used individually. So let''s try to understand the subtlety in the usage of this word ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎. It''s really beautiful.</p>
<p>So in Arabic, when you want to compare something in attributes — now you understand the difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِفَاتْ</span>, right? The noun and adjectives, being an attribute. So anytime you want to create a comparison in attributes, you add the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span> before it, o<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span>y? So for example, if I wanna compare the bravery of Zed to a lion, right?</p>
<p>So if I wanna say that Zed is brave like a lion, I will have to use the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span> to do that. So I''ll say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَيْدٌ كَالْأَسَدِ</span>. I''m saying Zaid is like a lion. Now what am I comparing here? I''m comparing the attribute of bravery of a lion with Zaid. So I''m using the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَيْدٌ كَالْأَسَدِ</span>. But when I need to establish a comparison in essence, I cannot use the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span>. You understand? Grammatically that would be wrong. I will now have to use the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثَالٌ</span>.</p>
<p>So for example, I will say . A cub is similar to a lion. The son of a lion is like the lion. Here I''m using the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثَالٌ</span>, because I''m not comparing in attributes. I''m actually comparing the essence of these two entities, the lion and the cub. So this is a rule of the Arabic language, that when you know when you''re establishing comparisons, both words <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثَال</span>, they are used in very precise ways. And we find this distinction all over Arabic poetry. So for example, there is this poet who is praising the generosity of a king and comparing it to an ocean, right? Now what is generosity? Is it being, or is it an attribute? It''s an attribute. So he''s comparing the generosity of this king to the ocean. So he has to use the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span>. So he says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَالْبَحْرِ يَقْضِي فُؤُولَ الْكَرِيِّ بِلَا عَلِيَّةٍ جُودًا وَيَبَّأُ صُولَ الْبَعِيدِ سَحَائِبَا</span>. He says, the king is generous like the ocean. When I move closer to him, he gives me pearls, and when I move away from him, he sends clouds filled with rain.</p>
<p>So here he uses the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span>, because he is comparing the attributes of generosity. But when the being or the entity needs to be compared, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثَالٌ</span> has to be used. So the poet says, I am a lion and this is my son, indeed the son of a lion is like the lion. So now, because the comparison is being done in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span>, in the entity itself, the poet is using the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثْل</span>. So I hope this gives you a much better understanding of this subtlety, because you know, that is what makes this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَةٌ</span> so beautiful.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says. To compare Himself, what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> does in this verse is that He fuses both the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثْل</span> together to create this one word, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَامِفْلِحْ</span>. Why is that? Because we just learned that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span> is used for attributes and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِسِّيلْ</span> is used for the entity itself. So what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is saying is that whether you try to compare me to attributes of creation, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span>, or if you try to compare me to the essence of creation, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِسِّيلْ</span>, in both cases, you will fail, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْسَ كَمِثْلِهِۦ شَىْءٌۭ</span>. There is nothing that exists in attributes, and there is nothing that exists in essence that you can use to compare to me. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Can you appreciate the beauty of this word?</p>
<p>It''s incredible. This is eloquence at its best. I mean... There is nothing comparable to Me in attributes. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثْل</span>. There is nothing comparable to Me in beingness, in existence. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَمِثْلِهِ</span>. There is nothing comparable to Me in attributes or in being. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْسَ كَمِثْلِهِۦ شَىْءٌۭ</span>. There is no thing, whether physical, metaphysical, spiritual, conceptual, theoretical, practical, there is simply no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْء</span> that you can use to create a comparison or a likeness for Me, not in attributes, not in essence. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>So now that we understand this concept, what the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ</span> does is that it condenses all the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> into two primary attributes: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>. In other words, the two attributes that will always be present in each and every single interaction of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> with His creation are the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيم</span>. Intense mercy, consistent mercy. So when we say ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, in essence we are remembering the essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Right? Because it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> who is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>. So we''re remembering, reminding ourselves of the essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> which is manifesting for us through these two primary attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>.</p>
<p>So what this does is that in a sense it implodes all of His remaining attributes. It sucks in all of His remaining attributes into this concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. You see, in other words, regardless of the way in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is interacting with us right now, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is present in that manifestation. Even if He is manifesting in my life right this moment as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْجَبَّار</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَهَّار</span>, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span>, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span>, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, His love, His care, His compassion, His concern, His mercy, it''s always present in His interaction with me. There is a lot more to be, you know, explained about this. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span> we''ll move this conversation forward in the next session.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتَمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِمَا الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/152/5b739499-b576-4bf4-b91f-f4f906ded16a.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'allah - being vs attributes';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span>. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَبَارِكْ وَسَلِّمْ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>.</p>
<p>In the previous session, we talked about the concepts of t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>nscendence and immanence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. So we learnt that in one way, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is immensely distant out of His loftiness and because of His magnificence. But then at the same time, He is extremely close to our hearts and our consciousness, as the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> says that He''s closer to us than our jugular vein. And what that ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically means is our consciousness, because when you cut the jugular vein, the first thing that you lose is your consciousness.</p>
<p>So in one instance He is extremely distant from us and in another He is extremely close. And there''s this beautiful verse in Urdu, which I really like, that I think that describes His immanence and His t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>nscendence in a really beautiful way, in which the poet says, Ke duri ki yahad hai, ki tasavur bi khata hai. And what the poet is saying there is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is so t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>nscendent, He is so distant from us that even imagining anything about Him is a sin. But on the other hand, He is so close to us that He is closer to me than my jugular vein. And I think that is a really beautiful way in which the poet expresses this concept of t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>nscendence and immanence.</p>
<p>We also looked at the distinction between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and His attributes, and we saw that how He is incompa<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ble by both of these. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْسَ كَمِثْلِهِۦ شَىْءٌۭ</span>, and the word ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, it''s actually a combination of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَـ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثْلِهِ</span>, one used for attributes, the other used for essence. And the verse reveals to us that He is incompa<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ble through attributes as well as through essence.</p>
<p>So now that we''ve covered this noun <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and His adjectives <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, today I want to talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِصَام</span>, right? Because it starts with ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎. The Alif is dropped because of a g<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>mmatical rule which I''ll talk about later, but that the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ is actually joined with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِصَام</span>. And the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِصَام</span> in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic, it means two things. It means a noun, you know, as in the g<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>mmatical term noun, but and it also means name, because every noun has a name. So the classical A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bs, they just used it interchangeably.</p>
<p>Now from a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>ic perspective, this concept of name is extremely important, because it is the names, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْمَاء</span>, that elevated Adam over the angels and made him worthy of their prost<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>tion. Also because these names were something that were directly taught to Adam by none other than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> Himself. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> taught Adam all the names. So the concept of name is actually extremely important in the human psyche, because we are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنِي آدَمَ</span>, we are the children of Adam.</p>
<p>Now, what is the name? A name is ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically the ability of symbolic thought. That is the essence of a name. So naming something is taking a symbol, which is linguistic in nature, and then applying that symbol to something which is either material or empirical, conceptual or even theoretical, right? Those are all entities that are actually named. We give a name to them. Or a name can be something that can be used as a means of abst<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ction in order to create sepa<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>tion in things.</p>
<p>So you can have a broad category called cars, and then you can further break them down, further abst<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ct each one of them by giving them additional names, right? Mercedes, Toyota, BMW, Audi, all of these. So now this is something that only we can do. Animals cannot do this. Animals cannot use symbolic language. This is something that only human beings are capable of.</p>
<p>It has been agreed by the A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>b scholars that human beings have ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically three dominant modes of symbolic language. We have linguistics, which is the highest form, and then we have mathematics, which, believe it or not, actually comes under linguistics. This has always been the mindset of the ancient people. It''s only the 20th century and the Western civilization that elevated mathematics over language. Today, language has taken a secondary place, and that is because people don''t understand the power of language, especially the A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic language.</p>
<p>And this is why even Muslims today, they try to place mathematics over the linguistics of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> when they want to try to prove it as a mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cle. And, you know, no doubt <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is mathematically perfect. I''m not, you know, discrediting that, and actually I''ve shown you a lot of examples of that in the sessions on is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> a mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cle. But the fact of the matter is that what makes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> a mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cle is not its mathematical perfection, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ther its linguistic perfection.</p>
<p>And this is something that as Muslims we really need to understand and appreciate. And, you know, this need to prove <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> mathematically, it actually comes from this feeling of inferiority before the god of modern science, which of course there is mathematics. You know, this comes from Newton and Einstein and all these other people who elevated mathematics and said that it is the only real way of understanding reality, right? And the reason they say that is because mathematics is precise and it is exact. But then these people did not study classical A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic, because A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic is precise and A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic is exact. In fact, it is precise and exact to a degree that mathematics cannot even come close to. And this is why the mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِعْجَاز</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, is not in its mathematics, it is in its linguistic perfection and eloquence.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is a linguistic mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cle. But the recognition of this, this concept of recognizing the fact that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is a linguistic mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cle, it actually comes from our ability to recognize things through names. Because names have meanings, right? They help us identify, they help us recognize things. So the actual power of recognizing the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> as a linguistic mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cle, it comes through our ability of understanding names. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اسْمٌ</span> is so important, to understand this concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اسْمٌ</span>.</p>
<p>And when it comes to classical A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic, even the letters become prominent, even the sounds become prominent. In fact, you know, if you take the A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic language, so when I say a tree, for example, in English, right, you see something that has a trunk, that has b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>nches and leaves. You know, that''s what comes to your mind when I say the word tree, right? If it''s a palm tree, it has a trunk and then it has these palm fibers. Or, you know, if it''s a fir tree, then it''s got all these spikes instead of leaves. But ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically there are three types of trees, right? You have the fir trees, you have the palm trees, and then you have these with leaves. So when we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span> in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic, what is the difference between a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span> and a tree? I mean, we could argue that they''re completely arbit<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ry, right? And this is a dominant opinion among most linguists in most languages, that names really have no connection to the thing that is named, other than the fact that they are ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically signs that human beings have collectively agreed upon.</p>
<p>So their opinion is that the A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bs saw the structure of a trunk with the b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>nches and leaves and they decided to call it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span>, whereas in English they decided to call it a tree. But the ancient A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>b would actually completely disagree with that. They would argue that there is a deep relationship between the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span> and this thing out there that we see. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span> is made up of three letters. This word, this name, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span>, is made up of three letters: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِيمْ</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>. Now, whenever you have the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, this is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرْفُ التَّفَشِّي</span> in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic. The letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> is always used for something that spreads out, that has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>diation, like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَمْسٌ</span>, the sun. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَعْر</span>, hair — hair has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>diation because it spreads out, it''s all spread all over the body. Shajar has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>diation because a tree has these b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>nches that spread out. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> is a very specific word and it has a very specific meaning. Then there is the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِيمْ</span>, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرْفُ الشِّدَّةِ</span>, right? It''s a strong letter.</p>
<p>It is used to represent things that are strong in their nature, because the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sis of a tree is strength. It is strong. It''s the trunk, right? That''s why it has the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِيمْ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِسْم</span>, the body, the main structure of the human being, strength. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَبَل</span>, a mountain, starts with the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِيمْ</span> because it''s powerful by its very nature, right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِيمْ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرْفُ الشِّدَّةِ</span>. Then there is the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>, which is the letter of repetition. Trees are constantly regene<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ting themselves, they''re repeating themselves. So the ancient A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>b would argue that there''s a deep relationship between the letters and the thing that is named itself. And this is something — I mean, these are philosophical de‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎tes about the nature of language — but the A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bs would definitely say that there is a secret in names. You know, in classical A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic names are not arbit<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ry. There is actually a very good reason why a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span> is called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span> and a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَمْسٌ</span> is called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَمْسٌ</span>, with those particular sounds in those particular order.</p>
<p>And this is why we know that these sounds, they have an effect. The sounds of the A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic letters have an effect on the human consciousness. There is no denying that. The recitation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> has a powerful effect on human consciousness, just by the very nature of its sounds. So even if you don''t understand a single word, just listening to a good recitation can deeply move your heart. And if you look up on the internet, you''ll find so many examples of people converting to Islam simply after hearing a recitation or hearing the adhan or something like that, because these sounds, they, you know, they''re not arbit<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ry. They have a very deep effect on the human consciousness. So A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic is an incredible, mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>culous language in every way, shape, or form, and it''s very different from other languages of the world.</p>
<p>But what I''m trying, the reason I''m explaining all this to you is because I want you to understand that all of its power, the power of this A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic language, it actually boils down to the human ability to understand names. You know, these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْمَاء</span> that are provided for us within the f<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>mework of language. So it''s no coincidence that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> started the statement with ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, and actually you''ll see, what I''ll explain as we go along, you''ll see the power of what that statement means. Now we understand the meaning of names, right? So let''s talk about the purpose of language. What is the purpose of language? The purpose of language gene<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>lly is communication, right? We want to be able to deliver communication, express ourselves. But if you actually analyze the purpose of language itself, it actually is unification. Language unifies.</p>
<p>This is the secret of the human being, that we have this ability to abst<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ct, differentiate, and identify things uniquely through names. We as human beings, we have this ability to see the one in the many. That''s the only way we can communicate. I mean, this is really profound. Think about it. The only way we can communicate is by seeing the one in the many. What am I talking about? So for example, if we talk about a lecture, let''s say, right? So for example, I tell you that there''s going to be a session next Sunday at 9:30 a.m. How do you know what the word session means in that context? How do you know what that statement means? The word session in that context, because the word session has many different meanings in different contexts.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/153/0afc12f6-52de-4003-bce7-6cefb9664849.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>So, I could say, you know, I''m recording a religious session, or the school is in session, right? The courtroom is in session. Yasmin had a great tennis coaching session. The Fede<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>l Act was passed in the session of 1908. So the word session can have so many different meanings in different contexts. So but when I say there''s going to be a session tomorrow, how do you know which meaning of the word session I''m talking about? The only way you know that is because you are ext<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cting that one particular meaning of that word within that context out of so many other possibilities. And this is something we all do instinctively all the time. This is just an ability that we human beings have, because we have this inherent capability to ext<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ct the one out of the many. And when we''re not able to do that, when we''re not able to zero in on one particular meaning, we are then forced to ask, wait a minute, I just want to make sure, did you mean this or did you mean that? Why do we do that? Because we''re trying to find that one particular meaning. You see that? So every sentence, every ph<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>se, every word, it needs to boil down to one particular thought from a sea of possibilities.</p>
<p>And so what language, and in particular names or these nouns, what they enable us to do is they enable us to speak intelligently about the oneness and the unified nature of words. Because that is who we are. We are all unifiers by our very inherent nature. This is what we as human beings were created to do. We are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَحِّدٌ</span> by our natures. We were designed to make one out of many through language. This is why Adam was given the gift of names, this gift of language, because using names we can unify. Language is a sign of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> within our existence, within the human existence, that that is what you have been created to do: to perform the ultimate unification, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, because that''s what the word unification means in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>. We were created for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, to single out the one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span>, the one true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, from all these possibilities and all these deities in our life posing as false gods. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So you can see how profound this concept of name or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> becomes.</p>
<p>So now that you understand that, the next question is where does the process of this recognition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> begin? It begins with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْمُ اللهِ</span>. It begins with the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>Now, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span>, linguistically, it comes from two root words. It comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> and it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسَمَّى</span>. O<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَـ</span>y, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span>, it actually comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسَمَّى</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, it means lofty, something very high, like the sky in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic is called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَمَا</span>, right? And the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسَمَّى</span>, it actually means a sign or a symbol. Alright, so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> is ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically a combination of two things. It is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسَمَّى</span>. So it is ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically a sign for those, it''s a lofty sign for those who can see. So if you break this word down, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> linguistically, it actually means a lofty sign, coming from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسَمَّى</span>, a lofty sign. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> is. It''s an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمَ</span>. It''s something you put onto something in order to signify it. So it''s essentially a signifier that indicates the signified, right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> is a lofty sign.</p>
<p>In fact, in Su<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>h <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الرُّومِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says, from His signs, the creations of the heavens and the earth.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/153/72fefb64-1e7f-4756-b3e4-8aa9ceacb74a.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>And in the variance, your tongues and your colors. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> relates the tongues to the heavens, right? Because you have the heavens first and then the earth, and He says, and then He says, you know, in the variance of your tongues and your skin color. So He relates tongues to the heavens and He relates the skin color to the earth. And this is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَبَق</span> in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic, which is this concept, it''s a type of antithesis where you use opposites to explain something. So He talks about heaven and earth and then He relates them to tongues and colors, like, you know, our skin color and our speech.</p>
<p>So there is a relationship between tongues and heaven, and there is a relationship between skin color and the earth. Because speech comes from heaven. You see, speech, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> taught human beings, or He taught Adam, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ther He imprinted within Adam language. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَّمَ</span> means to imprint, right? The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> imprinted within the human being the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. That''s lite<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>lly what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَّمَ</span> means, to imprint something. Language was imprinted within the human being. And this is why all human beings have this common ability to learn languages.</p>
<p>Now, complexions is something that comes from the earth, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> said that Adam was created from all the topsoil of the earth, right? From the white, the black, the brown, the yellow, the red. So you get all these variances in human complexions because Adam was created from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اُدْمَ</span>.</p>
<p>You see, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اُدْمَ</span> in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic, it means topsoil. And Adam means the one who was created from topsoil. So all human beings have these different complexions, just as there are all these colors of the soil. You have white soil and then you have pure white, right? You have red soil, yellow soil, black and then ebony black. So complexions are related to the earth, and language or speech is related to the heaven. Language is meaning, complexion is sensory. You see? Language is meaning, complexion is sensory. There''s a relationship between meaning and sensory. They''re both joined in the human being.</p>
<p>So what is significant and what is palpable, what we understand and what we can see, touch and feel. So the sensory and the meaning are gathered in these two phenomena, the phenomena of language and the body. Because the essence of human being is language. We are defined as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَيَوَانُ النَّاطِقُ</span>, the speaking or the articulating animal. Because what is the essence of language? It is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>tionality, right? Language is meaningful. So the essence of the human being is language. And that''s why, you know, when we judge a person by their skin color, it is just so wrong, because you''re judging them by something that is just incidental to their nature, right? But when you judge somebody by what they believe in, by how they speak, by what they say, by whether they''re truthful or not, whether they''re virtuous or not, whether they stand for the truth or not.</p>
<p>I mean, that''s how human beings should be judged. That''s why Dr. King, he talked about being judged by the content of cha<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cter. In one of his speeches, he said that we should judge people by the content of cha<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cter. And how do you know the content of cha<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cter? By the essence of that person. And what is the essence? Their belief, because that''s what''s going to manifest from them. So judging somebody by the skin color is just wrong at so many levels.</p>
<p>Anyway, so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> means a lofty or a heavenly sign. Now, the statement of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span> begins with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, the lofty signs pointing to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, because that is the only way we can know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, through the power of symbolic understanding, right? We cannot know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> as we know things. We cannot know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> the same way we know an MP3 player or a laptop or a computer because, you know, essences are hard to understand even with things, but when it comes to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, it''s absolutely impossible. So the only way we can know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is through symbolic language, which are these symbols within language and these symbols within existence, which he calls his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتٌ</span>, right?</p>
<p>So he has his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتٌ</span> within language, which is in the form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, and then he has his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتٌ</span>, his lofty symbols within existence in the form of the heavens and the earth, and everything in between. So now I''m bringing this entire concept all together, right? So you understand this idea of unification accomplished through language, and these signs, because if you look at the signs, there are numerous signs. There is a whole ocean, this vast ocean of signs around us, but they all condense down to one particular, they''re all pointing to one particular entity, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Right? So that''s what we find in the horizon. We will keep showing them our signs, our numerous signs within the horizons and within their soul, until it becomes absolutely clear to them that this is all coming from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَقُّ</span>, from the one truth, one reality, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>But and if you look at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, it directs us to his 99 divine names, right, as familiar linguistic symbols so that we can gain some concept of who he is. That''s why he uses these familiar names. So for instance, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, right? He is the owner or the king of the day of judgment. How are we able to understand that? How do we understand what that means? We only understand that because we understand the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, right? We understand the concept of a king. And this enables us to relate to and appreciate the position that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> will take on the day of judgment. He will assume the position of a king or the ultimate owner. But if you really think about it, we are only able to understand that because of the symbolic reference. But then we also learn that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is not limited to our concept of what a king means. He''s way more exalted than that. Right, so he''s — we get an idea that he''s a king, but then we also understand that it''s not like the kingship that we know of, but at least that concept of king, it gives us some understanding of his position and his relationship with us, right? It just, at the very least, it gets the point across.</p>
<p>So there is a distinction between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. There''s a distinction between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِصَام</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِصَام</span> are all these lofty signs and symbols that He created for us in the horizons and within our souls in various numerous dimensions. They enable us to know Him. That''s an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اسْم</span>. That is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْمُ اللهِ</span> means. A lofty heavenly sign or a symbol that helps us recognize <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. You see? Because I recognize you by your name, you recognize me by my name. So name is what helps us recognize.</p>
<p>So now that we understand the linguistic meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> as a lofty heavenly sign, what we can now appreciate about ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ is, you know, now I''m talking in the linguistic sense, that what we understand from it now is the lofty signs in the heavens and the earth and everywhere around us pointing to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and helping us recognize Him as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمٌ</span>. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, with the lofty heavenly signs that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has created for me in order for them to help me recognize that He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, the intensely merciful and the consistently merciful.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, in the next session we will look at this preposition ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ that is joined with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span>, and we will explore the depth of this entire ph<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>se, and we will see the power behind reciting ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, and you will see how perfectly this statement serves as the crown of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>. Right, it''s sitting on top of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> like a crown, because it really, it really deserves that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامٌ</span>.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتَمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ وَآلِهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِمَا الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/153/a12c19db-131b-4892-9838-c09bc76e3b15.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'the ism of allah';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ الرَّحْمٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ، اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ، وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ، كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ، وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ، فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ، أَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللهُ، وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُهُ وَرَسُولُهُ، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيًّا وَصِيُّهُ، وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>. Now that we''ve completed most of the terminology from a linguistic perspective in ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, today I want to talk about the letter ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ itself, which is a preposition, and what it means is &quot;with&quot; or &quot;in.&quot; So when you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>, what you''re actually saying is, I am beginning in the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. But you''re omitting the &quot;I am beginning.&quot; That''s just understood. It''s called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محذوف مُقَدَّر</span>, right, in grammar. But the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span> is actually connected to an omitted verb. And this is something very important to understand, believe it or not. Because verb or an action is essentially the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sis of all existence. You know, there are two schools of thought.</p>
<p>One believes that, you know, that the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sis of existence is a noun. And there''s another school of thought, which is the more dominant school of thought, which is also something that we subscribe to ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sed on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ</span>, is that the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sis of existence is not a noun, but rather a verb. Because if the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sis of existence is just a noun, then you''re simply talking about a state, right? Like, for example, if we look at our state of being, right, as a human being, we''re just simply here, like I''m being here, I''m existing here, and there''s no action involved in my being. But when you bring a verb into it, what you''re doing is you''re bringing the element of time into it, right? It''s either past, present, or future, because nouns do not have a past, present, future. A tree is a tree. But once a tree begins to blow in the wind, now there is a time associated with it, right? Did it blow in the past? Is it blowing in the present, or will it blow in the future when the wind comes? So the tree swaying is an action of the essence of the tree. Without that action, the tree is just a tree, right?</p>
<p>It would just be a being. So, and this is a very interesting de‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎te about existence itself. Is the root of existence movement and action, or is it being? Because if you think about it, this is also true about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> relationship with this universe, with us, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> has action, right? But His action is created. His actual essence, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَاتْ</span>, is passive. There''s no action in His essence when we talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is essence and attributes, but both essence and attributes are nothing more than states of being. But action is a substantive that moves into time. And that''s why the understanding in theology is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is beyond time and space. So His action is something that manifested within creation or bringing about creation, right? He brings things into existence through His action, through His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>.</p>
<p>But then that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا دَسَّ</span>. So, like as the Quran says, you know, when He decides an affair, what does He do? He says to it &quot;be&quot; and it becomes, right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> brings creation into existence through a command, which is actually a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>, it is an action. And so the preposition is actually connected to the omitted verb of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> action of creation. So when we recite the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>, what we''re saying, you know, when we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>, what we''re saying is that I am beginning with the recognition of the lofty signs within <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> creation. Because those signs are a witness and a proof of His intense and consistent love, compassion, care, and mercy, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. This is the context referring to the primordial act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the original creation. So this is the interpretation of that statement in the transcendent realm: that it is an acknowledgement of the fact that I''m beginning with the recognition of these lofty signs that exist for me within creation that are pointing to and that are bearing witness and acting as proof and evidence of His intense and consistent love and mercy. His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span>. So that is the interpretation of this verse in the transcendent realm. But what about its meaning in the imminent or the personal realm? Because as we talked about these two concepts earlier, there is the transcendent God and then there is the imminent God. So in the transcendent realm, we understand it because there are all these signs around us and they are depicting and illustrating <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love and mercy for us. But what about in my personal life?</p>
<p>How does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ</span> apply to me personally? So in order to understand that, let''s break down the statement into its grammatical components. And this may seem unnecessary or too technical, but actually there''s a very deep connection here. So first of all, when do we recite <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>? Let''s start there. When do we recite the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>? We recite it at the beginning of every action. So now let''s look at this statement from a grammatical point of view to understand this better. What are the various grammatical components of this statement? So it obviously begins with the letter ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, which I just explained as a preposition. Then we have the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span>, which is a genitive masculine noun.</p>
<p>This noun on its own, this noun would be written as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> with the alif, and meaning, you know, name. But because it''s joined with that preposition, the alif is dropped and the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ fuses with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> and it becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ</span>, making it an indefinite noun. Then we have the proper noun <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> itself, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>. So then we have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, which is the proper noun. And finally, the statement ends with two adjectives of mercy, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>. So we have a preposition, we have an indefinite noun, we have a proper noun, and we have two adjectives in the statement. What''s missing? From a grammatical point of view, what''s missing here to complete this sentence? A verb, you see? Because we know that grammatically a statement is incomplete without a verb. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> left the phrase ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ as an incomplete grammatical sentence. He omitted the verb. You see that? Why did he do that?</p>
<p>Because we conceptually complete the sentence by providing the verb through our action. You see, we complete the grammatical structure of the sentence by providing the verb through our action. This is why we recite the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ at the beginning of every action, every task, every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>, thus providing the verb to complete the structure of the statement. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. I mean, this is just incredible, to design a statement like this, right? So now if I''m about to read the Quran and I say ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ as I''m about to begin, my action of reading the Quran, of reciting the Quran, is what is now completing the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>. So now what I''m saying essentially is, with the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, in the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> who is intensely and consistently loving and merciful, I begin reading this book. And so now my action of reading the Quran, or regardless of what that action is, no matter what it is, right, it could be anything, but that action itself now gets empowered by the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>. But what''s even more amazing is that if we look at this linguistically, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sed on everything that we''ve learned, what I''m really saying, let''s say, let''s take the example of reciting the Quran, when I say ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ before reciting the Quran, what I''m really saying is that my ability to read and this Quran that I intend to read is a lofty sign pointing to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> incredible love and mercy, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span>. So what this does is that it puts <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love and His mercy right at the center of my focus as soon as I recite this phrase. That''s what triggers in my mind <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love and mercy, because whatever I''m doing is now becoming a sign for me, a lofty heavenly sign pointing to the love of my Creator. You see that?</p>
<p>My very act of reading, regardless, or not reading, whatever it is, it could be anything, any action, it becomes a lofty sign and a symbol. It becomes a witness and it bears testimony to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ</span>. And it completely transforms my perspective on that action that I''m about to do. And this transformation is now happening even before I''ve actually started doing it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So if I say ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ before I begin driving, that entire experience of driving, it becomes a reminder of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love and mercy for me: my ability to drive, the resources enabling me to own a car, my mental capacity to follow the signs, my brain power to remember what all these signs means, when to start, when to stop, my ability to assess traffic, my intuition, my instincts of when to stop, when to go fast, when to slow down, all the facilities that my car provides to me, the scenery outside the car. The wind coming in, the refreshing wind coming in through the windows of the car, every single thing that I can think of becomes a lofty reminder that points me directly to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> intense and consistent love, care, concern, and mercy for me. It is because of His mercy this action is happening. It''s all reminding me of the one who gave me this ability, provided me with all these resources in order to experience and enjoy His mercy. This is the power of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>It is incredible. I mean, it always blows my mind every time I think about it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> first act was ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sed on His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, right? He gave us this ability to act. And then every action that we do now becomes a witness to His love and His mercy. All of that so eloquently, so precisely, so concisely captured within the phrase ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎. I begin this task, this action, which is a lofty heavenly sign reminding me of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> intense and consistent love, care, concern and mercy. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. Now, what is the purpose of reciting the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span> before we begin any action? Why do we do it? From our perspective, like I said, it reminds us of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love and mercy and naturally makes us love Him ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ck. It''s our love manifesting in the form of worship and obedience.</p>
<p>That''s what the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, if you think about it in this way, the way I''ve explained to you, and if you actually become conscious of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love and mercy in our life, because this is what the statement does, it just puts that love right in front of us, and if we actually are able to experience it, it increases us in our love for Him. But what does it do from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> perspective? It fills our action with what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. That''s what the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ does. It fills our action with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا أَتَيْتُ رَضِيتُ وَإِذَا رَضِيتُ بَارَكْتُ وَلَيْسَ لِبَرَكَتِي نِهَايَةٌ</span>. When you obey me, it pleases me. When I''m pleased, I bless you with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, and there is absolutely no limit to my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>.</p>
<p>So now, what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>? It is generally translated as abundance. Right now, what has abundance got to do with the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎? What has it got to do with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الرَّحْمَٰنِ</span>, it is the last verse of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الرَّحْمَٰنِ</span> in which He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَبَرَكَ ٱسْمُ رَبِّكَ ذِى ٱلْجَلَلِ وَٱلْإِكْرَامِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَبَارَكَ اسْمُ</span>, so it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَبَرَكَ ٱسْمُ رَبِّكَ</span>, again due to the grammatical rules, they can convert, they converge, they fuse together, so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَبَرَكَ ٱسْمُ رَبِّكَ ذِى ٱلْجَلَلِ وَٱلْإِكْرَامِ</span>. The name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the name of your master, is glorious and benevolent. His name is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> does not mention in this verse which name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, right? He just says the name of your master is blessed. But which name? So what did the Surah begin with? It began with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>. And what is the first attribute of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> that is mentioned in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is telling us something about this attribute <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, that it is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>.</p>
<p>But then we also know that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> Himself is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. Right now we just talked about the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> being full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, but then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> Himself is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, because He says in the Quran, your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> who is the best of creators is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. So I want you to keep these two things in mind: the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, and the name <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> itself is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. So the first two words in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, they''re both loaded with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. Right?</p>
<p>Now what do you think will naturally happen to our action when we recite these two powerful words before beginning whatever it is that we''re doing? It will naturally, obviously fill it with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. In other words, by reciting ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ before performing an action, not only are we reminding ourselves of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> intense and consistent love and mercy through the act itself, but we''re also asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to fill that action with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>.</p>
<p>Now let''s look at this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> and understand what it means linguistically. So there is no word in the English language that can fully capture the meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. So we need to look at this like a composite word, like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> and all these other things that we''ve understood. So we''ll have to look at it through a combination of certain concepts. In classical Arabic, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> is defined as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البَرَكَةُ النَّمَاءُ وَزِيَادَةٌ</span>. It is growth and abundance. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> is defined as growth and abundance. Now the first word is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْعَمْتَ</span>, which means growth.</p>
<p>Something that rises, something that increases in size, makes something that progresses consistently, increasing in the results all the time, or something that goes further and further and increases. It increases beyond expectations. So if you plant a seed of one single fruit in the ground, how many fruits should you get in return? Just one. That would be fair because you planted the seeds of one fruit. But you take the seeds from that one fruit and after you plant it in the ground, you get this enormous number of fruits in return, each producing hundreds of thousands of seeds. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. That is the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, an unprecedented growth. You planted one seed and eventually got thousands of seeds as a result. It''s a growth beyond expectations. That is what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. Because if you think about it, what is the comparison of a tree with a seed?</p>
<p>The tree came from the seed, but those two things are completely beyond comparison. A seed is tiny, a tree is huge. A seed is delicate, a tree is firm. A seed has no value, but the tree bears hundreds upon hundreds of fruits, season after season, year after year. So if you really think about it, what we get from a seed, this tree that we get from a seed, when you compare it to what we actually invested there, there''s really no comparison. And that is the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, a ridiculous growth, an unlimited increase.</p>
<p>Then the Arabs say that referring to the rain that keeps on coming and coming and coming. So continuity is another characteristic of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. So it is growth, it is increase, but then there is a continuity associated with it. It doesn''t stop. It is extensively abundant. That is included in the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>.</p>
<p>Then the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> also includes the meaning of stability in the Arabic language. So for example, the Arab would say, you know, when it''s like when a camel just digs itself into the ground and it doesn''t move and it just doesn''t budge, that is also a quality of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. And this is actually a really beautiful concept, because if you think about it, when you grow something, right, and you grow it too much, so for example,</p>
<p>you know, my kids, they play with Legos, right? My son, he builds this tower, just block upon block upon block. He just puts this one huge tower. Now, as the tower is getting tall, does it become more stable or does it become less stable? It becomes less stable, right? The taller the tower is, the less stable it becomes. It will easily fall and break, right? And there''s not going to be any tower end left anymore. That''s how it works. So when something increases rapidly and it increases at a very fast pace, right, it becomes unstable. But when something has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> in it, that means it will increase in quantity, it will keep increasing in quantity beyond expectations, yet remain as stable as the camel sunk into the sand. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>In other words, that increase of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> is not going away. It''s not going to be unstable. It''s not an unstable increase. It''s a very consistent and stable increase. The more it grows, the stronger it will get. Because we find unstable increase all around us in this world, right? Our economy, I mean, goes up, crashes. Governments, they take over really fast, they disappear really fast. Fashion industry, right, there are all these trends that pick up really fast, disappear just as fast, so, you know, something else takes its place. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> is something that increases and increases and increases, but then it remains stable, it doesn''t go away. So that''s the linguistic understanding, the concept of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>.</p>
<p>So the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْمُ اللهِ</span>, is filled with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. The name <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> itself is filled with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. His blessed name is being mentioned since the day creation came into existence.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/159/300d20b6-e90b-4023-9d00-29a23096e187.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>And it only keeps increasing and growing with every single passing moment. That is an example of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> name. It keeps increasing and growing with every single second, and it will continue to grow till the very end of time, until there is no time left, right? I mean, worshipers come and go, but the one who is worshiped, he remains forever. The concept of worship has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> in it, because it remains forever. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has been worshiped from the beginning of time. People who glorify the names of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, they come and go. Many came before us, many will come after us, we will all be gone one day, but the glory of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will always remain forever, eternal. This is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> name.</p>
<p>So now, to summarize, when I recite ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ before each and every action that I do, my action itself turns into a reminder, a heavenly lofty sign pointing to the loving care and mercy of a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span> in my life. And what that does is it creates <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> in the results of my action, you see. It creates an abundance in my action. There''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثُ سُورَةِ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> in which he says, every single act is sterile or infertile without . Every act that is done without the remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will have no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> and no abundance in it. Now what does that mean?</p>
<p>In practical terms, what does that mean? What does abundance have to do with this? So think about it. If you have an abundance of anything, let''s say money, easiest example, right? Let''s say you have an abundance of money. What does it free you of? If you have a never-ending supply of money, what does it liberate you from? It frees you from ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎. It liberates you from the shackles of anxiety and depression, the two biggest killers of our time. It frees you from ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎.</p>
<p>Because you''re no longer concerned about that aspect of your life, of where''s the money gonna come from. So you''ll never feel anxious about it and you''ll never feel depressed. You get anxious about something ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sed on fear. Whether it''s going to happen or not. That makes you anxious. Loss creates depression. And if you have that abundant supply of money, at least as far as money is concerned, you will never be anxious and you will never be depressed. Right?</p>
<p>Now imagine if you have the abundance, a never-ending supply of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ</span>niya and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span> in your life. If you have a never-ending supply of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ</span> in your life, if you have this abundance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرُ اللَّهِ</span>, this close connection with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, will anything or any situation in life, no matter how ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎d it is, will it be able to affect you? Will it make you feel anxious or depressed? Impossible. Completely impossible. Because no matter the outcome, you''re experiencing the joy of closeness, and you''re experiencing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love simply by the act itself, being reminded by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>. It immediately puts that into focus for you, because this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>, all of these things that are working against us, make us forget <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. But this is why we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>, because if you think about it, if this is executed properly, we would be saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span> like a thousand times a day.</p>
<p>And every time we would say it, we would be reminded of this. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. And so what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, what he meant by that statement, that every state, every act is sterile and infertile without <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>. What he meant by that is, without the love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, without a focus on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, without a proximity to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, without the remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, no amount of physical abundance will satisfy you in this world.</p>
<p>And we find the greatest proof of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> when we look at this world around us. Millionaires, billionaires who are extremely anxious and constantly in a state of depression. Lottery winners committing suicides, actors, stars, rocks, these singers, all the money in the world, all the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> in the world, the best clothing, the most fancy cars, houses where they get lost, they can''t find an exit. Right? So much abundance of physical material, constantly anxious, constantly depressed, and they end up committing suicide. So physical abundance means nothing if there is no connection with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>On the other hand, on the flip side, we find these Muslims, these true Muslims and believers, these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِنِينَ</span>, who despite the worst of situations, they seem the most content and happy. I mean, I''ve personally met people like that. Just blows my mind, right? They''ve just, you know, two sets of clothes in their closet, drinking brown water, they cannot, they cannot even get clean water, but they''re content and happy like you won''t believe. Why? Because they''re connected to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>And that connection, it creates <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> in whatever little they have, to such a degree, to such an extent, that they are actually sincerely happy. They are content and so satisfied with what they have. So much so that they don''t even desire more. Imagine that. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. How do you take that away from someone? But that is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>.</p>
<p>May <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> fill all our hearts with His love and His mercy, and enable us that every time we perform an action we remember Him from this day forth, and every time may He make that experience of that action, whatever it is that we''re doing, turn it into an experience of joy and a remembrance of His love and mercy in our lives. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَغَرَضُ دَوَامٍ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ عَبْدِ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُوسَى وَالْأَقِي وَوَصِيِّ آلِ النَّبِيِّ تَعَالَى</span>...</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'in the name of allah the merciful';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ يَا اللَّهُ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span></p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span> So in the previous session, we learned that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created us as nouns, right? And just speaking in grammatical terms, we are all nouns in our essence, but then from the moment we wake up until the moment we go back to sleep, in fact, even while we''re asleep, what we''re constantly doing through our actions is creating verbs. So the power of ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ is that it provides a context for our verbs. It provides a context for our actions by turning them into these reminders, right? By turning our actions into these heavenly, lofty signs, these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, reminding us of His intense and consistent love, care, kindness, concern and mercy, which is His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span>. And once we experience His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>, right, once we experience His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> through our interactions with this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الم</span> that He created for us, because all of our actions are only happening because His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> enables and empowers us to do that. Right? So I''m able to drink water because He gave me this physical capacity to pour water into a glass. And then He made water subservient through His laws of nature so that it obediently falls into the glass when I pour it. It doesn''t refuse. It doesn''t change its direction, right?</p>
<p>It doesn''t just start rising upwards in mid-air. It follows and obeys these laws of gravity that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created, allowing me, enabling me to drink it. Now what is this if not a sign of the mercy and love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, where He enabled me and water to interact in this manner? This is His action, His proof of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> executed with intense and consistent love and mercy. So now once I recognize and, more importantly, once I experience this love, what should be my natural response to that love? It has to be ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎. All praise and all gratitude belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, the master of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِين</span>. This is why the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَسْمَلَة</span> directly connects with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>Now in today''s session, I want to do a linguistic study of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>, to demonstrate the perfection of the statement so that we can appreciate why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> used this particular word and why He used it in this particular fashion in this verse. You know, why didn''t He choose a different style for saying the same thing? So let''s begin with the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> itself, because this form of His diction, it veils many, many valuable lessons for us to learn from. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is actually a composite word that means two things.</p>
<p>It means praise and it means gratitude. Right? It''s praise plus gratitude. Now there are two other distinct words in the Arabic language for praise and gratitude. The commonly used word for praise is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span> and the word used for gratitude is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>. Now what''s the difference? Praise, as we all know, is a gesture of appreciation. When you are moved by something that is beautiful or colorful or scenic, you know, when something intrigues you in some way, when something impresses you, what do you do? You praise it. You verbalize your appreciation by praising it. That''s the very natural human response to something that our soul finds beautiful or impressive.</p>
<p>Now gratitude is something entirely different. Gratitude is done in response to a beneficial act that was done for you. Right? If somebody lends you money or does something nice for you, you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْرًا</span>. So gratitude is always done as a response to a beneficial act. So you see the difference? Praise is done when something impresses you, or when you appreciate something about someone. Whereas gratitude is done in response to a favor or a kindness that was done for you. Now even though these two terms are used interchangeably in our lives, they''re actually both mutually exclusive. Someone you thank is not necessarily someone you praise. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> clearly distinguishes between these two concepts.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/160/9f1d99bf-6c97-4f5b-baaa-b62c9928ac0a.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>So for instance, in the story of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span>, we know that he was adopted and raised in the house of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span>, right? And then years later when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span> returns to deliver <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s message to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span>, he says to him, &quot;How dare you? How dare you talk to me this way? Did we not raise you as a newborn in this house? Did you not spend many years of your life growing up here? How dare you speak to me like that?&quot; In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span> is saying, aren''t you grateful? I raised you for so many years and now you''re going against everything that I taught you? To that, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span> responds, yes, you did in fact show me that favor, and for that I am grateful. So even though <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span> is someone who will never be praised, especially not by a Prophet, but even when he does a favor, he will be thanked. So gratitude can exist without praise. You see?</p>
<p>In the case of parents, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ لُقْمَان</span>, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَوَصَّيْنَا ٱلْإِنسَنَ بِوَلِدَيْهِ حَمَلَتْهُ أُمُّهُۥ وَهْنًا عَلَىٰ وَهْنٍۢ وَفِصَلُهُۥ فِى عَامَيْنِ أَنِ ٱشْكُرْ لِى وَلِوَلِدَيْكَ إِلَىَّ ٱلْمَصِيرُ</span> ‎. And we have made it compulsory, we have enjoined upon human beings the care for their parents. His mother carried him increasing in weakness upon weakness, and then in his weaning, which is in two years, so be grateful to me and to your parents, because to me is the final destination. So be grateful to me and both of your parents. But then He says, but if your parents are working against you, in forcing you to disbelieve and commit <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> against me, then do not obey them. But you still have to be what? You still have to be grateful to them. In other words, if your parents are forcing you to disbelieve in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you don''t have to praise them, but you still have to be grateful to them. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْرَاهِيم</span> is a good example of this, right? He does not praise his father, who according to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> was an idol worshipper, but he is still grateful to him. So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَدَّاح</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشُّكْرُ</span>, praise and gratitude, these are two completely separate things. They are mutually exclusive. You can praise without being grateful, and then you can be grateful without praising someone or something. But what''s interesting about the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is that it necessarily means both things together. It''s mutually inclusive.</p>
<p>When <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, He is saying praise is for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and gratitude is also for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, both at the same time. You understand the difference? What the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> does is that it makes both these concepts of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span> mutually inclusive. One cannot exist without the other within <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>. It means praise and it means gratitude. So now let''s look at why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> did not use the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span> and instead used the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>. So let''s start with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span>. Why didn''t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> say all praise is for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>? The problem with the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span> is that it is a praise that does not necessarily have to be genuine. You see? Because we all know that praise can be insincere. Right? A kid walks in the house with a really bad report card, and as soon as he enters the house, he goes, &quot;Mom, oh my God, your food, it smells so delicious.&quot; And she hasn''t even started cooking yet. Right?</p>
<p>So he''s praising her, but it''s not a genuine praise. You get it? Because praise can be fake. There is fake praise done for kings, for bosses, for people in all these high positions all the time. Isn''t it? People falsely praise appearances all the time, right? Wife asks the husband, you know, &quot;Does this dress make me look fat?&quot; &quot;Oh no, you look beautiful.&quot; That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span>. This is why in classical Arabic, when you praise someone''s looks, you never say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمِدَ</span>. Because everybody knows that the person that is being praised did not make their own face. If they''re pretty, if they''re beautiful, if they''re handsome, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> made them that way.</p>
<p>So saying you''re so beautiful would just naturally be considered <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span>. Because it is understood in language itself that praising looks and all these external features, it is inherently a false praise. Now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>, on the other hand, cannot be a false praise. It cannot be insincere praise. Why? Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is generated in the heart by the awareness of the magnificence and the glory of the one that is being praised. I''ll say that again. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>, the praise element of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>, that occurs when the heart becomes aware of the magnificence and the glory of the one being praised. The heart and the intellect together recognize that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> truly deserves that praise. So therefore <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> can never be a false praise. Because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not dependent on a personal need.</p>
<p>It is never motivated by a personal agenda. You see, it stems simply from being impressed by who He is, not based on what He does for us. So I hope that makes it clear. Now let''s think about the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>. What if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had said, &quot;All gratitude is for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>&quot;? What would be the problem with that? Gratitude, or thanks, it originates from the acknowledgement of a favor. Someone does something for you that you appreciate, you thank that person for that favor. You have a flat tire, someone comes along and helps you, you feel grateful. And you express that gratitude by saying thank you. So thanks can only happen when you have a reason to thank someone, right? It is a reaction to a favor.</p>
<p>It''s never something you start with, right? You don''t just walk onto a street, see a stranger passing by and go, &quot;Hey, you, thank you,&quot; right? You never do that. When <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> uses the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>, which is a combination of both praise and gratitude, there are certain rules that apply linguistically. First rule is that the praise aspect of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> has to be genuine. It can never be fake. Second, the gratitude aspect of it is not reactionary. You see? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is sincere praise and it is not a gratitude that is in response to what He does for us. Therefore, linguistically, saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> is far more powerful than saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَمْدُ لِلَّٰهِ</span>, and it is far more powerful than saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشُّكْرُ لِلَّهِ</span>, because it is a lot more comprehensive than both of them.</p>
<p>Okay, so now what if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَدْحُ وَالشُّكْرُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>, &quot;Praise and gratitude belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>&quot;? What would be the problem with that? Even if we remove the shortcomings of both these words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>, the problem now is that when you put the word &quot;and&quot; in between the two things, which is the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَوْ</span>, right? When you add that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَوْ</span>, that &quot;and&quot; in the middle, the problem is that it separates both words even in meaning linguistically. So when I say linguistically, what I''m saying is that praise belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for certain things and gratitude belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for other things. They''re not always combined because the words aren''t combined. You see, this is a very cool feature of Arabic language: you never say what you don''t mean. So if we use the two words gratitude and praise and join them with an &quot;and&quot;, in classical Arabic that means sometimes I''m in the mood to praise Him but not necessarily thank Him. And then there are other times when I''m in the mood to thank Him but not necessarily praise Him. But when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, that means for every act that He does or doesn''t do, for every blessing He gives or holds back, genuine praise and unconditional gratitude is due to Him, both at the same time. We do not get to pick and choose which one.</p>
<p>So now if you own a nice car and you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, what have you just done? You''ve genuinely praised <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for giving human beings this knowledge and this ability to create cars, to design and manufacture them, and you thank <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for letting you own one. So now you''re praising and thanking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> both at the same time, and all of that is eloquently captured in the beautiful word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>. This is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is the attitude of a true believer, because it is optimistic by its nature. You know, sometimes you''re going through a difficult time and somebody says, &quot;How are you doing?&quot; and you say, &quot;Oh, you know, life sucks, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>.&quot; So you''re not really praising <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> or thanking Him when you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> that way, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> is not a phrase that believers just throw around, because it is the reminder of an attitude.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is never interested in what comes out of our tongues. He''s more interested in what''s in our hearts that is connected with what comes out of our tongues. So when a believer says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, regardless of the situation or circumstances, the sentiment that resides in his heart is that indeed, without a doubt, all praise and all gratitude belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> regardless of my situation. If I''m going through a difficult time and I say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, what I mean is, indeed my praise and my gratitude is for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, because no matter what I''m going through, it could always be worse. Someone right now in this world is going through a much harsher version of what I''m going through right now, and he can then say the same for somebody else, and that person can say the same for somebody else, because no matter what problem we go through, it could always be worse. So I''m grateful to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> that He is lenient with me. I''m grateful to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> that He has provided me with the resources to fix my situation, because there might be someone who just doesn''t have the same resources that I do. And I praise Him for His majesty. And this acknowledgement of how the one who gives help can take it away and then restore it whenever He desires. Because my pain is supposed to remind me of all the times <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had me living in comfort in which I forgot about Him. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, sincere praises and unconditional gratitude belongs to the one who reminded me of His grace and His blessings by temporarily or even permanently taking it away. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, sincere praises and unconditional gratitude belongs to the one who filled my life with undeserved blessings, even in my state of pain, even in my state of difficulty. So an understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> can really empower us, and it can completely transform our attitudes towards life itself as believers.</p>
<p>Now let''s look at this term from a grammatical perspective. In language, we have nouns and we have verbs, right? We all know the difference. Verbs, they have tenses, whereas nouns, they don''t. There''s no past tense nouns or present tense nouns or, you know, future tense noun. So now when I say I praise <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, I praise <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, am I using a noun or a verb? I''m using a verb. It''s a present tense verb. But when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says praise belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, is the word praise a noun or a verb? Here it''s a noun. You see, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> could have used a verb.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/160/c5cc90e2-d33d-4464-a9a9-070907dbb34e.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>He could have said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, I praise <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. He could have said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَحْمَدُ اللهَ</span>, we praise <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> did not use a verb. He used a noun. Why? Because when I use a verb, there''s obviously going to be a tense associated with it. So when I use a present tense to say I praise <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, then I''m only talking about the present. I''m not saying anything about the past or the future. You see? If I use the past tense, then I''m not guaranteeing anything about the future or the present. Because the verb limits and confines the action within time. It makes the action temporary.</p>
<p>But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> used a noun, which by its nature is permanent. Therefore, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s genuine praise and unconditional gratitude, it has been described with permanence. And what that means is that I may be praising <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> right now, but the praise of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has always been there and will always be there. I''m not going to be here in this world forever, but the praise and the gratitude of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is always going to be here forever. His praise and His gratitude is not dependent on me. It''s not dependent on you. It''s not dependent on this world. This is a huge reality encapsulated within <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, this concept of permanence in the praise and gratitude of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. It is a recognition of the fact that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is not dependent on my temporary existence. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is not because of me. See, when you use a verb, somebody always needs to do the verb, right?</p>
<p>It''s called the subject, or the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span> in Arabic. There has to be a doer for an action. You can''t just go into a conversation and say, failed the test. Who failed the test? Right? You can''t just say disappeared. What disappeared? Oh, your book disappeared. You understand? When you use a verb, you always need someone to do that work. But a noun doesn''t need a doer. A noun doesn''t need a subject. It is independent all by itself, right? An apple is an apple. It can exist all by itself, independent of any doer. So when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> used the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ</span>, He made it a noun. He made it independent, because He doesn''t need anyone to actually do the praising, and He doesn''t need anyone to be grateful. Had He used a verb instead, that would have meant that He needed someone to praise Him and He needed someone to be grateful to Him.</p>
<p>So He removed the construction of time and space by using a noun. Now what this means is that even if there was not a single creation in the world, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> would still be there. It would still exist. Even after this world, this universe fades into non-existence, His praise and His gratitude will remain. Because it does not require a doer or a subject. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. All of this is captured by one single word, used grammatically as a noun.</p>
<p>You know, sometimes we have these questions. Kids sometimes ask this question, like, why does He need to be praised? Why does He need our gratitude? So just the usage of this word, the grammatical way in which the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ</span> is used, clearly answers that question, that He does not need our praise and He does not need our gratitude.</p>
<p>Okay, now let''s look at some other possible variations, because I wanna—I''m just going over this just to show you the perfection that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has used in just one single verse. Imagine the entire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. Every single verse of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is perfect in this manner. And so just by appreciating one single verse, we can really appreciate the entire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَضْرَة</span> Ali Sahib used to give this beautiful example, that if you want to find out that, you know, if you''re cooking rice and you want to know that the rice has been properly cooked, you just check a couple of grains. You don''t check every single grain, right? So I love that analogy, because, you know, that''s exactly what we''re doing. We''re just looking at a couple of verses and studying its perfection, and that pretty much gives us an idea of the entire perfection of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>.</p>
<p>So let''s look at some other variations here. What if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had said, right, what if He used a command: praise <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the master of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِين</span>. Now wouldn''t that have been better? Because after all, He is a master.</p>
<p>So a command would just naturally make sense. So why didn''t He make it a command? I''ll give you an example to help you understand. I tell my daughter to bring me a glass of water. Right? I say, &quot;Hey Adam, bring me a glass of water.&quot; I give it a command. Now once she hears me, she has only two possible choices. She can either bring me the glass of water or she can bring me the glass of water, right? Those are the only two clear choices as far as I''m concerned. So now, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> commanded us to praise Him, you know, as a command, what are the two things that could have happened?</p>
<p>Some would praise Him, some would obey His command, and then there would be others who would disobey Him. Because when you tell someone to do something, the ball is now in their court. Right? It depends on them to comply with your wishes, with your command. Maybe they''ll choose to do it, maybe they won''t. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> did not talk about His praise and His gratitude in a way that is dependent on us. You see, He did not put the ball in our court. He said, whether you do it or not, whether you are grateful to me or not, whether you choose to praise me or not, my praise and my gratitude, my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>, is always there. It has always been there, it will be there forever. Human beings will come and go, nations will come and go, generations after generations will come and go, this universe itself will come and go, but the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will always be there. It is simply a matter of fact.</p>
<p>So now, what if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had said, certainly, for sure, absolutely no doubt about it, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>? Wouldn''t that sound a lot more powerful than just saying all praise and all gratitude belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>? Right? It does sound more powerful, doesn''t it? So how come <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> didn''t say so? If you remember from our sessions on the miracles of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, you know, when we had that topic of conversation, when we talked about conversation and communication itself, I told you there are two kinds of communications, right?</p>
<p>A communication can either be informative or it can be emotional, right? It can be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُمْلَةٌ إِنْشَائِيَّةٌ</span> or it can be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُمْلَةٌ خَبَرِيَّةٌ</span>. Either you have speech that is expressing your feelings and emotions, or you have speech that is communicating information. Those are the two kinds of speech that human beings are capable of. That''s it. Right? So for example, right now, as I''m teaching you about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ</span>, am I delivering information or am I expressing my emotions? I am delivering information. This is informative speech. But when I''m talking to my friend and I''m telling my friend how I feel about someone or something, what kind of communication is that? That is an emotional conversation.</p>
<p>So those are the only two types of speech that we as human beings are capable of. The thing is that in Arabic linguistics, when you use <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّا</span>, the statement can only be informative. You see, it can only be factual. It can never be emotional. That''s just a linguistic rule, that if you use <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّا</span> before a statement, it just becomes informative. Indeed, we sent this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> in the night of power. It''s information. It''s factual. It''s not an emotional conversation. Because the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ</span> removes the emotion out of the statement. But if you don''t use the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ</span>, then that communication can be informative, but it can also be emotional. You understand?</p>
<p>So by not using <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> made His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> an open statement. We can now talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s praise and gratitude with other people in order to teach them about it, to give them information about it, but we can also make it a statement to express our own emotions. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Can you appreciate the beauty of this? So using this style right now, by saying ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, I am sharing with you, I am teaching you about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>. A few hours later, when I''m standing in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span> before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and I use the exact same phrase, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, I will be expressing my praise and my gratitude to my master. The same statement will now become an emotional conversation for me. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So you can see how perfect the usage of this word is in the context of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>.</p>
<p>So I hope this really gives you a deeper appreciation of the perfection of this language and how one thing flows into another, how ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎ connects with ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, and how perfect the usage of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is. So, to summarize this entire concept of praise and gratitude in grammatical terms, we are nouns and we create verbs through our actions.</p>
<p>Right? We are nouns, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created us as nouns, as beings, as essence and we create verbs through our actions. Now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is also a noun and He created verbs through His action. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> creating us in His own image. Right? Now on a daily basis we are interacting with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and He is interacting with us, right, through His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبِّيَ</span>, over His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الم</span>, He is interacting with us and we through our worship and obedience and praise, we are interacting with Him. Now, in this interaction, listen to this carefully, in this interaction between us and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, for the recognition of the noun that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is, He is worthy of our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَاح</span>. He is worthy of our sincere and independent praise. And for all His actions influencing our existence, He is worthy of our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>, our ultimate and unconditional gratitude. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> covers both aspects of our existence and His existence. You see?</p>
<p>For the recognition of the noun that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is, for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْرِفَةٌ</span> of His essence, which is impossible, but as much as we can learn about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, our natural response to learning about understanding who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is, He becomes worthy of our sincere praise. And then for what He does for us, how He influences our existence, He becomes worthy of our unconditional gratitude. Both these concepts are beautifully, perfectly, eloquently encapsulated in this one single phrase: all forms of sincere praise belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for who He is. And all forms of unconditional gratitude, whether it''s on a personal level or a universal level, it belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for what He did, for what He does and for what He will do. Because His actions are constantly manifesting on the stage that we call the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الم</span>. Therefore, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتَمِ الَّذِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِمُ الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/160/80c720a9-7ae8-4b6b-b674-701c4dfd0b3e.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/160/00660fed-358b-498c-bb18-8cc7ff5c2952.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'linguistic perfection of hamd';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span></p>
<p>In the previous session, we did a linguistic study of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ</span>. We learned that it is a combination of sincere praise and unconditional gratitude that is owed to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> for His constant, relentless, never-ending <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوِيَّة</span> over His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span> that He created for us. And then it''s not just <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْدٌ</span>, but rather it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ</span>, which means the ultimate praise, and it means the absolute gratitude belongs to Him by virtue of Him being our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>.</p>
<p>So now the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَة</span> continues and He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيم</span>, the Abundantly Merciful and the Consistently Merciful. We''ve looked at this word in great detail in one of the previous sessions, so I''m not going to go into the meanings of it linguistically or otherwise so much, but rather today I want to explore some of the examples of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. But just a quick word on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> itself is, you know, generally we know that it means mercy. But it is actually a type of mercy that brings two things together. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is the type of love, the type of mercy that actually brings two things together in a way such that the principles of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> can be applied to that thing. Right? And this is why the womb of a mother is called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْم</span>, because it brings the baby, the fetus, and the mother together, so the mother can nurture it, so that she can shower her love over this fetus, so that she can help sustain the fetus and then make it grow. So the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is so powerful that, you know, our general understanding of these words like mercy, you know, they fall short in really capturing its essence.</p>
<p>So now in this next verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, He highlights His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> by dedicating an entire verse just to mention the quality of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>. So what according to Him is the essence or the defining attribute of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>? It is His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. It is His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمٰن</span>, His mercy, His love, His care, His concern that manifests through His abundance in His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and His consistency through His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span>. And this is why when we look around us, when we study this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ</span>, we find this one thread of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> flowing through the entire fabric of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آلم</span>. It is this glue that lovingly holds this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آلم</span> together. It''s that magical magnetic force that just naturally pulls us towards <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> and it keeps us connected with our Master. And so just to put this concept in visual terms, you can imagine <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> as this gravitational field, this force of love that pulls us towards Him. And what we experience from that pull is our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْدٌ</span>, our ultimate praise and our absolute gratitude that is owed to this magnificent, this abundantly and consistently merciful <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span> of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>.</p>
<p>Now, if you look at the gratitude aspect or the gratitude component of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ</span>, if we just focus on this one aspect of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>, what is the reality of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>? What is the manifestation of sincere gratitude? It''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, right? It is this constant awareness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s presence. It is the constant recognition and awareness of His blessings. And this is a very high station. This type of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>, it holds a very lofty <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَام</span>, and not everyone is truly capable of achieving these high ranks of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says in the Quran, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَقَلِيلٌۭ مِّنْ عِبَادِىَ ٱلشَّكُورُ</span>. Very, very few of My servants are truly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُورٌ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span> is very different from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَاكِرٌ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَاكِرٌ</span>, the grateful, is the one who is grateful for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s blessings. But the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span>, he''s the one who''s grateful for calamities and tribulations also, not just blessings. He actually feels gratitude when horrible things happen to him.</p>
<p>Because most people at best, they''ll become patient or resigned, but the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span>, they actually say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> as they are experiencing these calamities, because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span>, they know that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> only does good for the believers, right? There''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span> in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَجَبًا لِأَمْرِ الْمُؤْمِنِ فَإِنَّهُ أَمْرُهُ لَهُ كُلُّهُ خَيْرٌ</span>. How wondrous is the affair of the believer! His affair, all of it is good. So if you''re a true believer, then it''s all good. And that''s why if you really do believe, you enter into the state of constant gratitude, even for the tribulations. It''s always <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>. And always a good thing to remember is what Ibn Abbas <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللهُ</span> said, that no matter how bad it is, it could always be worse. And there are people like that. There are even in the non-Muslim community, you''ll find people like that, you know, they always look at the good side. Well, it could be worse. Think about this, you know, count your blessings. So there are people that just naturally, instinctively have this understanding. Now, in this verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says He is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span> that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>. How does His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> manifest in our lives? Today I just want to focus on how His love presents itself in our lives. How does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> demonstrate His love for us? Through His blessings, right?</p>
<p>Through His gifts, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِرْمَا</span>. When I as a parent, when I want to demonstrate my love for my children, how do I do that? By bestowing gifts upon them, right? Valuable, desirable, beneficial gifts. Gifts that the child understands that he or she could not attain by their own efforts. Now think about the blessings, the gifts that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has bestowed upon all of us. Incredible gifts, infinite gifts. If you attempt to enumerate the blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you will never be able to enumerate them. Gifts that are not only impossible to count, their value is just impossible to measure. Just look at one single <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمَ</span>. Look at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمَ</span>, for instance, of the eye. That is just one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمَ</span>, sight.</p>
<p>First of all, think about this, blinking. Just think about blinking. You don''t even notice that you blink. Now, if you didn''t blink, have you ever seen pictures of Aristotle Onassis? You know, when he got really old, the richest man, one of the richest men in the world, he owned his own private fleet of ships, he had to use band-aids to hold up his eyelids because he lost the function of the muscle that returns the eyelid and his eyelids just dropped all the time. So he used band-aids to hold them up, but then he had to put drops in his eyes all the time. Because if your eyelids don''t close, they get dry and you start getting dust in them. Because one of the things that blinking does is it brings the dust back down. You see, they''re like windshield wipers, right? They bring the dust down and then what happens is that the dust, it just moves to the side and accumulates in the corners of the eye. So when you wipe your eyes, you''ll get some of that dust out of the eye. And you don''t even think about that. That''s just one part of the eye.</p>
<p>But then if you think about the intricate network of these nerves behind the eye and the myelination of that sheath, the fact that there''s this constant synaptic responses, the fact that there''s neural transmission, because that breaks down for some people, right? They go blind, they lose the function of the sight. I mean, we don''t think about all of this. Think about the electric work that goes in the function of an eye. Colors, just think about colors. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> could have made everything black and white, right? Look at all the subtle colors that He put in this world that we can experience. Our eyes can experience an incredible variety of colors. I mean, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِرْمَا</span>. Then look how, you know, how some of these colors, they just give you pleasure in just seeing them. You don''t even think about it.</p>
<p>Today we know that there''s vibration. If you look at color, there are seven colors. All colors go down to seven primary colors. I think in color theory they go down to three, but basically everybody agrees that there are seven colors in a spectrum. If you look at visible light, each one of these colors has an effect upon the soul, right? Red has a certain vibrational resonance. It''s a lower resonance than violet. Violet is at the high spectrum, red is at the low spectrum. Red, when you see the color red, it stimulates you. You don''t even know that. They know that now in color theory because, you know, all the study that''s been done on people, that if you put them in red rooms, it has a different effect on them than if you put them in blue rooms. It''ll actually change their moods because these colors have a resonance.</p>
<p>there''s an electromagnetic radiation and it''s all having a psychological effect on us and we don''t even know it. Now, the thing about the world: if you go out and look at green, green will make you feel good, right? That''s why people love to look at grass, they love to visit these gardens, they love to look at trees. You know, it has a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُنَعِّش</span>, as the Arabs would say. Yeah, it''s called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَابِعَةٌ</span>, spring. This is why we say in our prayers that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> make the recital of Quran the spring of my heart: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلِ الْقُرْآنَ رَبِيعَ قَلْبِي</span>. What does that mean? Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَابِعَةٌ</span> is that thing which brings your heart back to life, because spring brings your whole being back to life. People start feeling good. People get this thing, they call it SAD, Seasonal Affective Disorder, SAD. There are people who get depressed in the wintertime. It''s an actual psychological state. But then spring comes around and they forget how depressed they were in the winter. They start feeling wonderful. Why are they feeling wonderful now? Green. You see, the color starts changing.</p>
<p>There''s photosynthesis that''s happening. All this chlorophyll is starting to come back to life. Flowers start blooming again. Flowers. Look at the flowers. I mean, what is that? A flower? Why do people like smelling a flower? That''s another name of scent. Just the sight of a beautiful flower. Just the sight, right? Beauty.</p>
<p>When the eye looks at something beautiful, why do we all respond the same way? When you see something beautiful, and most people, we all share a common taste in beauty, when we see something beautiful, we''re attracted to it, right? There''s an attraction. Why? It''s because beauty is an attribute of God, and what you''re seeing in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الٓمٓ</span> is a reflection of that divine attribute of your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>. So you''re essentially attracted to the divine, but you think you''re being attracted to the flower. No, it''s the beauty of the divine manifesting through the flower. That is real attraction. Real attraction is not in this ephemeral beauty that fades away. It''s the manifestation of the divine in creation that''s the real attraction. But we don''t realize that. So there are all these things about the eye that we just don''t think about. I mean, look at the eyelashes, the eyebrows. They''re serving a purpose, right? Eyelashes serve a purpose.</p>
<p>I mean, when anything comes near our eye, we instinctively close our eyelids to protect the eye. I mean, that''s just amazing. So there are all these things about just the eye that if we really start thinking about it, I mean, look at the abundance in just the eye, the abundance in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> of the eye. Just look at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, with one single gift. And that''s based on what we just know so far. There must be so many blessings that we don''t even know about yet. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. What about light? Right? The external factor that makes sight possible, that makes the eye useful, that makes the eye functional, which in turn makes people happy or depressed.</p>
<p>Why do people get depressed? You know, one of the reasons they think is because the serotonin level, as they drop, which is the key hormone in making us feel happy. That''s what they call serotonin. They call it the happy hormone, right? And one of its factors of release is based on light. So you''re taking in light during the day and you''re feeling good and happy and cheerful and you don''t even think about it. There are all these hormones that are related to melatonin. That''s based on light, right? Melatonin is what makes us sleepy. That''s why people that are constantly in artificial light, they have difficulty sleeping. But the eye is what''s doing all of that, right? The eye is dictating.</p>
<p>That''s why we get tired. It''s all based on the eye. So even our sleep patterns are being affected by the human eye. And we don''t even realize that. When we feel awake, when we feel drowsy, it''s all the result of the eye. And this is just the eye alone. We cannot even enumerate all the blessings of the eye, which is one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>. This is just <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةُ الْعَيْنِ</span> that we''re talking about here. We cannot even enumerate the blessings of this one organ. There are untold, infinite blessings in it. And it''s just one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِرْمَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>.</p>
<p>So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span> is the one who is constantly experiencing the state of gratitude, its presence and the lack of it. But this comes from this absolute certainty and this is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَام</span>, this is a station that is related to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِضًا</span>, which is complete contentment with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. It''s a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَحَبَّة</span>. It''s a state of this intense love because once you fall in love, you just cannot see anything else. I mean, there''s this incredible beauty of love. You know, when you''re in love, you can''t see faults, right? Even when they do exist, you just can''t see them. If you love Laila and anybody tells you, you know, Laila did this or she did that, you get angry. Why? What? Laila would never do that. No, no, no.</p>
<p>I saw Laila did this. You''re lying. You''re a liar. Laila would never do that. Why? Because he''s in love with Laila. No matter what you say about Laila, he''s not going to believe you. You know, that''s real love. People don''t even know that type of love anymore. So if you''re in love with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, He just cannot do anything that would harm you or hurt you. Impossible, nothing, because you''ll say it''s me. You see, I did it to myself. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> didn''t do that. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> would never do that. I did that to myself, or that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is doing it because, you know, like the father that punishes the child. You see, I mean, you see a father punishing the child. The father loves the child. Why is he punishing it? Because he knows that in that punishment is benefit for the child. He''s disciplining the child, right?</p>
<p>He''s bringing the child back on the right track. Through that punishment, he''s reminding the child of something worse. You know, if I see my son putting his hand in the fire, I''ll slap his hand. Why am I doing that? Because I want him to taste a light pain in order to avoid a much greater pain, you see? Because I know that the pain of this slap is nothing compared to the pain of the flame. And when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> gives us a tribulation, those are nothing compared to Hellfire. Nothing. So if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is purifying us to prevent us from doing something that He doesn''t want us to do, then the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span> realizes that, that this tribulation is the love of His Creator for him.</p>
<p>You know, the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he once saw a mother nursing her child. You know, and this is a man, as they say, you know, the man of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> takes discernment from everything, right? He takes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبْرَة</span>, a discernment, a lesson from everything. His glance is discernment and his speech is wisdom. So, there''s always a reason why he speaks. So, the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he once saw a woman nursing her child, and he said to his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span>, Do you think that this woman would throw her child into the Hellfire? And they said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا وَاللَّهِ يَا رَسُولَ اللَّهِ</span>. She would never do that. He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has more mercy for His servants than this woman has for her child. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَرْحَمُ بِعِبَادِهِ مِنْ هَذِهِ الْأُمِّ لِوَلَدِهَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has more mercy for His servants than this woman has for her child.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, you know, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْإِسْرَاءِ</span>, when He talked about His Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نُوحٌ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ عَبْدًۭا شَكُورًۭا</span>. Indeed, he was a grateful slave, because of the amount of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَعْوَة</span> that he did. You know, people today they get fed up after a week of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَعْوَة</span>, they say, you know, they give advice two times, three times and that''s it. Oh, you know, I give up, these people never listen. I mean, this is 950 years of doing what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> asked him to do. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span>. And one of the things mentioned in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السِّيرَةُ</span> is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نُوحٌ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, he never once went to relieve himself, except that he came back saying, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي أَذْهَبَ عَنِّي الْأَذَى</span>. Praise and gratitude be to the one who removed this harm from me. Even just having <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span> for something like relieving himself.</p>
<p>Because what a blessing that is, right? I mean, think about — you know, I don''t know if you''ve ever read these stories of Bahlol. As kids, we used to read them. And there''s this one story of Bahlol in which he went to visit Harun Rashid, the palace of Harun Rashid, who was a king at that time. And he asked Harun Rashid, you know, would you like anything? And Harun said, bring me back a glass of water. So they brought him water. As he was about to drink it, Bahlol asked, if you will refuse this water, right, you were dying of thirst, but you were refused this water, what would you give for this water? And he said, I would give half of my kingdom for it if I couldn''t have it. And then Bahlol said, if you drank this water and then you couldn''t release it, what would you give to release it? He said, I''d give the rest of my kingdom. So Bahlol asked, then what is the worth of your kingdom that is not worth a cup of water or the release of it as urine? In other words, what do you really possess? You know, if it, all this kingdom that you possess, it doesn''t even equal a cup of water or the release of it.</p>
<p>So think of that, the blessing of just eating, drinking, and digesting it. I mean, you don''t have to tell your hydrochloric acid to be released. You don''t have to push any buttons here, right? You eat something and if it''s protein, then it just breaks down into amino acids and your body then reassembles them as muscle tissue. If it''s fat, it breaks down into fatty acids, then your body reassembles them. If it''s carbs, it breaks them down, takes them to the brain, produces energy, right? If you have extra, then insulin is released, takes it to the liver, stores it as glycogen, so that when later on you''re out of energy, the liver can release that glycogen, break it down for you, and produce energy. You don''t have to do any of this. You don''t have to tell the body to do any of this, and yet it''s all doing that for us. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>.</p>
<p>I mean, that''s what — how to be grateful for the blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Islam is all about being grateful to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That''s what Islam is. We just, just the simplest of things. Right now, we''re all at 98.6 degrees.</p>
<p>Who do you think is maintaining our bodies at 98.6 right now? Seriously, who''s maintaining that? You know, right now, I could have a fever and I could be shivering. If I was sick right now, right? But if I was sick right now, even though it''s a moderate temperature in the room, I could be shivering, I could be feeling cold, and it wouldn''t even be cold. If I had a fever, I would be boiling up right now in a moderate climate. You''d all be fine, but if I had a fever, I would, you know, want to take off all my clothes and I''d be all hot and sweaty. Think about that. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is keeping us all at 98.6. Even if it''s 100 degrees outside, our bodies are releasing fluid as sweat in order to maintain this 98.6 on average. Even if it''s 100 degrees outside, why don''t we boil up to 100 degrees? What created that inner thermostat that keeps us all at this balance? If it''s cold out, why don''t we all drop down and freeze? Because we would if, you know, if we go out in ice cold temperatures long enough, we''ll freeze. But for a period of time, we won''t.</p>
<p>We''ll all stay at 98.6. Why? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. We go swimming in cold water and our temperature maintains itself. This is all from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. All manifestation of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَة</span> of our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>.</p>
<p>And the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span> is constantly aware of the abundance of these blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, but then they also look at those that are less fortunate than them in their worldly affairs. They use that as a resource to draw even more gratitude, right? There''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثُ سُورَةِ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> in which he said about this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> that you should always look at people who are less fortunate than you are in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, because you''ll always feel grateful. There''s always people who are less fortunate than you are today. I guarantee you, no matter how miserable you think you are, you''ll always find people in much more misery than you are right now. So you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>I mean, if you don''t have a lot of money, you know, in this country, the United States, or even most of us living in Pakistan and India, we''re considered well off, we''re considered, you know, decently rich. In biblical times, to put a jar of honey on your table was considered a sign of immense wealth. Think about that. A jar of honey on your table was a sign of great wealth. Really. The majority of people living in America today would be considered royalty in previous times. The houses that we live in, they would be considered palaces.</p>
<p>The facilities that we have, this idea of having running water, just running water. The fact that you can take a hot shower just by turning a knob, people don''t even think about this. There are places today in this world, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللهِ</span>, there are women who have the sole job of bringing water every day. It takes them about two hours to go get the water. It takes about an hour to fill up, you know, these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِرْبَة</span>, what they call. Loaded on the back of donkeys in these villages and then two hours to come back home. Five hours, five hours every day just to bring drinking water to their village, and that''s their entire life. Can you imagine what they must feel when they drink water? What do you think they would say after all that, five hours of hard work, when they take that first sip? What do you think they say? ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎.</p>
<p>That water, it''s brown. Most of them have kidney problems. Most of them have urinary problems because of the sediment in that water. People have forgotten this. We could have been born in that village. You and I could have been born in that village, drinking that brown water, having those kidney problems. I mean, we never think about these things, do we? Just think about the blessings, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمَ</span> of this house that we live in. Think about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمَ</span> of water, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمَ</span> of food on our table, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمَ</span> of clothes to cover up our nakedness, to keep us warm. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> of a bed. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> of just being healthy. I mean <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةُ الْعَافِيَةِ</span>, that you got up in the morning and you weren''t sick.</p>
<p>Think of how many people got up this morning and they had to empty out a bag that had their urine in it. A bag that they have to change every hour. Think of how many people that got up this morning that couldn''t go to the toilet or they bled blood. Think how many people there are with asthma. They couldn''t breathe this morning. They woke up gasping for air. Really? Think about how many people spend their last night coughing. They couldn''t sleep because of this cough. Really? That''s why, I mean <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>, showing gratitude to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, you really have to wonder about our lives, right? Just the blessings that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> has bestowed upon us and our complaints, my God! Our constant never-ending complaints against God.</p>
<p>What did I do to deserve this? What have I done to this? You exist. That''s enough as a crime, really. If you really think about this, that''s enough as a crime, to think that you exist beside <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. To think that we share existence with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We are criminals. Just for having that thought, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. There is nothing worthy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهِيَّة</span> except <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And yet here we are, right, walking around, just thinking, you know, these little contingent creatures living on this insignificant planet, in this insignificant galaxy, that are totally dependent for every breath, at every instant, on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, and we think we are independent. We think we are self-sufficient.</p>
<p>We think we are in control. If I''m in such a state of heedlessness with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, I''m a criminal. If I forget <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for one instant, I am a criminal and I stand condemned. And then anything that happens to me, I deserve it. Because I''ve forgotten who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is and who I am in relation to that. Oh man! What has deluded you about your master? What has deluded you about your Lord? Why are you in the state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غُرُور</span>? Total delusion. Thinking that you don''t need me at every instant of your life. Just to draw your next breath. Just to have your next heartbeat.</p>
<p>I mean heartbeat. What is a heartbeat? An electric impulse. Who''s activating it? It''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. You think it''s your brain. It''s not your brain. Your brain is not doing that. They don''t even know what''s doing it. I mean, you can cut all your nerves to the brain and the heart will still beat, right? What makes the heart beat? And yet, here we are, these insignificant creatures complaining about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, judging Him for His faults.</p>
<p>That''s a crime. Or we sit on our high horses, you know, right next to Him judging others. Our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span>, I mean just looking down upon others based on their knowledge that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has bestowed them with. That''s not why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> gave you that knowledge, to look down on others. He created us all as brothers to embrace each other. See, there''s two ways to embracing, right? There''s the embrace of an equal. And then there is the embrace of the superior. Did you ever think about that? What''s the embrace of an equal? You embrace somebody who''s the same as you. If I embrace you, you''re my brother.</p>
<p>We have an equal embrace. We''re the same height. I put my arms around you. You put your arms around me and we have an embrace. That''s the embrace of an equal. What''s the embrace of a superior to an inferior? How do you embrace a child? The child can put his arms around you. You pick them up, right? You lift them and you embrace them and you hold them. See, that''s Islam. That massage, that temperament, the temperament of Islam is to take all these other sects and religions and those who are inferior in knowledge and then just embrace them.</p>
<p>That''s the embrace of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. He is so great that He will even allow somebody that denies Him to exist. I mean, if you want to think about greatness, that is greatness. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. He''ll feed them, He''ll nurture them, give them rain, keep their bodies healthy, give them a wife, children that love Him. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> does all of that, even though that person denies His very existence. But all that does is show the greatness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>You know, there''s a story of Ibrahim <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ سَلَامٌ</span> that one night, it was famous about him that he never ate alone, right, Ibrahim <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ سَلَامٌ</span>? He always found somebody to eat with. But one night he couldn''t find anybody, so he went out looking for someone and then he finally found this man and he brought him home. And this man was a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُشْرِك</span>. So before this man ate his food he blessed it in the name of his idols, and when Ibrahim heard that he said, you know, you can''t eat any of my food, you''re an idol worshiper, get out of here. So the man left. But then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> revealed to Ibrahim, He said, Ibrahim, I have been feeding him for 40 years. And I''ve been patient with his idolatry for 40 years. You couldn''t be patient with him for just one meal. And so, Ibrahim, he went and he found that man, he apologized to him, invited him back to his table. And the man asked why? What suddenly changed? And he told him. And this man became a Muslim. That is Islam. You know. This ability to embrace everyone. I mean that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَعْوَة</span>.</p>
<p>What is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَعْوَة</span>? In Arabic, even in Urdu, you say, you know, what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَعْوَة</span>? What does that word mean? It''s an invitation, isn''t it? Now if I put a gun to your head and I say you''re coming to dinner tonight at my house, is that an invitation? Seriously, is that an invitation? It''s not. You''re not inviting someone, you''re forcing them to come. And you know, there''s no compulsion in religion. An invitation is only valid if you allow the person the possibility of refusing that invitation. Now, who would allow the person that possibility? A generous host, right?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has made a house, He called it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَارُ السَّلَامِ</span>, He invited all of His creation to it and He gave them the option to refuse to come to that house. So if people refuse it, why are we so fearful of them? Why are we so afraid of them? Right? See, the thing about people, you know, that when they are ignorant, they fear. That''s what a fundamentalist is, that''s what an extremist is. It is somebody who''s afraid of the other. He''s afraid of having his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> challenged. He''s so afraid that he won''t even allow for the other to exist because he can''t. He''s not going to allow that possibility. He wants them eliminated, my way or the highway. And we have this in our own community at our own levels, right? If you''re not going to follow what I''m saying, that''s it. And that''s really a disease. That''s not Islam. Islam is the only religion that''s ever allowed other religions, other ideologies to exist side by side with it, because it creates this environment of invitation. It allows the other people to experience something different and then be attracted to it.</p>
<p>But because we present this harshness in our behaviors and then call it Islam, no wonder people are getting turned off from Islam in multitudes. Because we have no care about a fellow human being. We don''t look at the other person as a human being. We look at them as either Muslim, non-Muslim, Christian, Jew, believer, non-believer, in agreement with me, in disagreement with me, my enemy, right? There''s no... One of the great scholars of Islam, he said that the door that he came to his Lord through was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعْظِيمُ الْمَخْلُوقَاتِ</span>, exalting the creation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. He said, ... I never saw a scholar except I exalted him because of the knowledge that he was carrying. But then it got to a point where <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا رَأَيْتُمْ مُؤْمِنًا أَوْ مُسْلِمًا مَهْمَا كَانَ حَالُهُ إِلَّا عَظَّمْتَهُ لِانْتِسَابِهِ الْعُبُودِيَّةَ لِلَّهِ</span>. I never saw a believer or a Muslim man, no matter what his state was, but I exalted him just for being a servant of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Just for attempting to submit to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>But then he said that I didn''t see anyone, even the non-Muslims, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَّا أَعْظَمَ طَاعَتِهِ لِأَنَّهُ مَحَلُّ الْإِرَادَةِ اللَّهِ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى حَيْثُ أَوْجَدَهُ بِقَوْلِهِ كُنْ فَيَكُونُ</span>. Except that I never saw anyone, anything in this universe except that I exalted it because through his existence, I saw the will of the divine manifesting before my eyes. So he said I never saw a rock or a tree or anything or anyone except that I saw it as a manifestation of the divine will. It was God that put that thing there. That''s what he was acknowledging. And then he said the one who exalts the sanctified things of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, he is the one who is most loved by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And what is more sanctified in this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الٓمٓ</span> than the creation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>? These things that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has created as His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَجَلِّي</span>, right? His divine manifestations that invite us all to marvel over it, praise it. They draw out from us the praise component of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْدٌ</span>. I mean, if you look at people, right? When you see people who can do something slightly amazing, you see this beautiful singer singing a song in his or her beautiful voice or playing an instrument in a concert. What do people do? They just clap, right?</p>
<p>They just stand up, whistle and clap. They do all these things. They give this artist a standing ovation, to praise him or her because of what''s happening in their hearts. They just see something extraordinary and they want to praise it. They cannot help themselves because they just saw something that affected their soul. So when they see somebody who mastered the piano, for instance, you know, who got up and played some extraordinary piece of Beethoven, they just stand up and they clap and they clap and they clap. And the point is to say what an extraordinary thing you''ve just done before my eyes, right? It was truly incredible. That''s what they feel like doing because they find it so amazing. And yet we''re here in this incredible existence, this theater of amazing creations, stars in the heavens, galaxies of them. You know, we''ve got Hubble telescopes that look into the furthest corners of the universe, into this massive expansiveness. We have flowers that bloom before our eyes that demonstrate these beautiful colors, this incredible scent. We watch flowers that move across as the sun moves in the sky, you know, flowers that follow the sun.</p>
<p>There are, the Arabs call them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ شَمْسٍ</span>, you know, slaves of the sun. We have animals that do the most extraordinary things. Elephants that can carry these extraordinary weight on their backs. But then ants, little tiny ants, the most extraordinary in their creation, that they can carry many times their own weight. All of these things are happening right before our eyes. We have women with the fetus developing in their wombs. You know, mitotic cell division happening at these rapid rates. All of these things happening everywhere, constantly within us, outside of us. And we don''t feel compelled to give <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> a standing ovation. To stand before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> at least five times a day and say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ، اللهُ أَكْبَرُ، اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. Really to stand in ovation before our master, like people stand before some poor soul who has perfected some little trick on a musical instrument, can just pipe out a tune, and we''re so compelled to jump up out of our seats and clap and, you know, Bravo, Bravo. And we can just get up to praise the master of this world, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, and just say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. I mean, really, what is wrong with us?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>What has deluded us? What has taken us away from the praise and the gratitude of our master? I just am so guilty of all of this. It''s so hard to even admit and acknowledge this, but we''re all in the same boat of ingratitude. And our Prophet constantly went into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَجْدَة</span> out of gratitude and praise for every little thing. He saw an eclipse. He went and gave God an ovation. He prayed. He stood up and, you know, he prayed, went into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَجْدَة</span> as a testimony that he was witnessing something incredible. He was witnessing a reminder of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span> of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>. Everything, every instant was a reminder for him. And yet here we are, blessed beyond measures.</p>
<p>May <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> strengthen our hearts to witness and to experience, and give us this ability to just marvel over the blessings of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span>. Because may He help us experience the joy of true and sincere praise and unconditional gratitude for not only everything that He has done for us, but also for what He has withheld from us. For not only the joy in our lives, but also for the grief and the sorrows that humble us, that remind us, that break our intoxication of this world, that sober us, that pull us off our high horses and just throw us down on the floor. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِ الْمُحَمَّدِينَ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَصَحْبِهِ عَلِيٍّ النَّبِيِّ طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ</span>.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'abundant and consistent mercy';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَبَارِكْ وَسَلِّمْ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span></p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span> In the last few sessions, we completed our discussion on these first three verses of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>: ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, all of which are a testimony to Allah''s abundant and consistent love and grace. He is the master of everything that exists in creation, and for that He is owed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَمْدُ</span>, which is sincere praise and unconditional gratitude.</p>
<p>Today we''re going to look at the next verse, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>. We''ll begin with a linguistic analysis of these three words to get a better understanding of exactly what this means. So this verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, there are two forms of recitation for this verse. There is the Kufi style of recitation, which is what we recite generally, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, but there''s another school of thought which recites this as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, and actually both forms are valid. So we''ll look at both variations.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, as we know by now, it means an owner, right? Even in Urdu the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> means owner. And it comes from these three root letters, mem, lam and kaf, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ</span>, and the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ</span> actually means property. So the one who owns property is called the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> of that property, right? Now there''s another word in Arabic that shares the same three root letters, and that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُلْك</span>, which means kingdom, and the one who rules over a kingdom is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, king.</p>
<p>So Allah talks about both in the Quran. There is this concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلْقِيَةٌ</span> and there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُلْقِيَة</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلْقِيَةٌ</span> means ownership and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُلْقِيَة</span> means kingship. And Allah ascribes both these attributes to Himself. So He is not only the owner, He is also the king. Because He is the one who judges, the one who decrees, who legislates, who has the ultimate authority over everything in His kingdom.</p>
<p>There is no one above the king that a plaintiff can plead his case with. So, Allah says in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الزُّمَرِ</span>, He says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا قَدَرُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ حَقَّ قَدْرِهِۦ وَٱلْأَرْضُ جَمِيعًۭا قَبْضَتُهُۥ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَمَةِ وَٱلسَّمَوَتُ مَطْوِيَّتٌۢ بِيَمِينِهِۦ ۚ سُبْحَنَهُۥ وَتَعَلَىٰ عَمَّا يُشْرِكُونَ</span> ‎ They did not give Allah a just estimation that was due to Him. And on the day of the resurrection, the entire earth will be grasped by His hand and heavens will be rolled up in His right hand. Now, what does He mean by having it all in His right hand? It basically means, it''s an ancient Arabic expression that actually means under His complete control, right? This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلْقِيَةٌ</span>, complete ownership.</p>
<p>In another verse, He says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلِ ٱللَّهُمَّ مَلِكَ ٱلْمُلْكِ تُؤْتِى ٱلْمُلْكَ مَن تَشَآءُ وَتَنزِعُ ٱلْمُلْكَ مِمَّن تَشَآءُ وَتُعِزُّ مَن تَشَآءُ وَتُذِلُّ مَن تَشَآءُ ۖ بِيَدِكَ ٱلْخَيْرُ ۖ إِنَّكَ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ شَىْءٍۢ قَدِيرٌۭ</span> ‎ So, this verse begins by saying that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلِ ٱللَّهُمَّ مَلِكَ ٱلْمُلْكِ</span>, declare that Allah is the king of all kingdoms. Right? Here He talks about Himself as being the king, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>. So you can see that both forms are used in the Quran. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> and He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>.</p>
<p>He is the owner of the day of judgment and He is also the king of the day of judgment. And both these terms have this implication that nobody will be able to question Him about any of His decisions, and He alone will pass all judgment and make all the final decisions. So that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>.</p>
<p>The next word is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْم</span>, which is actually quite an interesting word in Arabic, like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>. Now generally this statement is translated as a day, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْم</span> is translated as day, owner of the day of judgment. But linguistically, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْم</span> does not actually mean a 24-hour day. What it means is a time interval. It can mean any period of time. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْم</span> could be used for a moment. It could be used for an hour. It could mean a day, it could mean a month, a year. It could also mean several thousand years.</p>
<p>So, it is simply a duration of time allocated for, you know, a specific purpose. That''s what a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمِ</span> is. So, for instance, in the Quran, Allah says, ... The angels and the spirit ascend to Him during a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمِ</span>, the extent of which is 50,000 years. So, here Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> specifies <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمِ</span> as being equal to 50,000 years. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْمُزَّمِّلِ</span>, He says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمَ تَرْجُفُ ٱلْأَرْضُ وَٱلْجِبَالُ وَكَانَتِ ٱلْجِبَالُ كَثِيبًۭا مَّهِيلًا</span> ‎. That moment when the earth and the mountains will convulse and you will see that mountains will turn into a heap of sand that is being poured down. Here, He uses the same word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, for a moment. So the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْم</span> can be used for various periods of time, including time itself. So, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>. Owner of the time period of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>. Right?</p>
<p>So, now let''s look at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> in the Arabic language comes from the root letters <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَيْنٍ</span>, which has several meanings. One of the meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَيْنٍ</span> is, you know, it is a type of rain that falls over and over again until it brings the dead earth back to life. Another meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَيْنٍ</span> is to discipline something. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ دَنَا نَفْسُهُ</span>, whoever disciplines his soul. It also means a way of life. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَكُمْ دِينُكُمْ وَلِىَ دِينِ</span> ‎, you have your way of life and we have ours. Right? But the root meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَيْنٍ</span> actually means debt. Our religion is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينُ الإِسْلَامِ</span>, the Deen of Islam, and literally this can be translated as the debt of submission. In other words, the idea is that we have been endowed with innumerable and invaluable blessings by our Master.</p>
<p>And so for all those blessings, we owe our Creator a debt of submission. Allah says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَفَغَيْرَ دِينِ ٱللَّهِ يَبْغُونَ</span> (Do they seek other than the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> of Allah?) <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَهُۥٓ أَسْلَمَ مَن فِى ٱلسَّمَوَتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ طَوْعًۭا وَكَرْهًۭا</span>, while all creatures in the heaven and the earth have bowed down to Him in submission, willingly or unwillingly. So what Allah is asking here is that do they seek other than repaying this debt when in reality everyone and everything in existence is paying its debt to its Creator? They''re all in a state of submission, willingly or unwillingly.</p>
<p>Now, there are areas of submission that the human being recognizes. One of them is our call to nature. We have to do certain things that we just cannot avoid. And one of them is, for instance, to eat. That is part of our nature. When we get hungry we must eat or we die. We need to drink water when we are thirsty. If we don''t drink, we die. We have to relieve ourselves once we have eaten or taken drink. We don''t really have a choice in these matters, do we? We have to do them whether we want to or not. We have to sleep. When sleep comes upon us, we can fight it for a while, but eventually we have to submit or we go into acute psychosis.</p>
<p>So there is this idea of willing or unwilling submission to nature itself. But then there is also submission to our own beings, to our own physical bodies. We have limitations. We can eat, but there is only so much that we can eat. We can only drink so much. We can only do so much physical work before we get exhausted. Right? We have limitations in our movements because of the structure of our bodies. So there is a type of submission of the self, given that we are temporal creatures that are limited by our physical aspects.</p>
<p>Now the Arabic word for a boundary or a limit is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>. So all of creation have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُدُودٌ</span>, right? We are all confined within our limits, within our boundaries. We cannot go outside those boundaries. Water cannot flow upwards. A rock cannot fly. A bird cannot bark. A dog cannot sing. Honey will always stay sweet. Citrus will always stay sour.</p>
<p>So in the Quran, there is this idea of limitations, that everything is in a state of submission to its limitations. Every creature is confined within its boundaries, its <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُدُود</span>, and they are confined within it willingly or unwillingly. So in other words, they are all in a state of submission. And then Allah follows the statement with a reminder that they will all be returned back to their owner. And to Him is their return. Now if you deeply reflect upon creation itself, you''ll see that there are things that are emerging and then they are returning back into non-existence. Everything that has life comes into existence and then returns back into non-existence. Plants, vegetables, the animal kingdom, and ultimately even the mineral kingdom, they''re all returning to non-existence after they came into existence.</p>
<p>So in other words, everything that is in existence is paying back its debt to its creator, except the human beings, you see. It is only human beings who are not actively paying back their debt of submission, their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينُ الإِسْلَامِ</span>. But the difference here is that we are also not being asked about our debts right now. So when will we be asked? And who will ask us about these debts? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, the owner and the king of that time period when the debt of submission falls due. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمِ الدِّينِ</span> is that time period when the debt of submission falls due. It''s payback time. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَيْفَمَا تُدِينُ تُدَانُ</span>. How you transact with others is the way you will be transacted with. This is why this time period is also called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمُ الْحِسَابِ</span>, the time of accountability. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> said in one of his Hadiths, he said, take yourself to account before you are taken to account.</p>
<p>So there is a debt of submission that we owe Allah. But then there is also a debt that Allah will owe us on that day. You see? When a master issues a command to his slave, the fulfillment of that command is a debt that the slave owes to his master, because there are only two possibilities: he either obeys the command or he disobeys it. In both cases, a return or a recompense falls due upon the master. You see, if the slave obeyed his master and executed his command properly, then his master owes him a reward. But if he disobeyed his master''s command, the master now owes him punishment. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمِ الدِّينِ</span>, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمُ الْحِسَابِ</span>, the day when that debt falls due. This is why Allah calls upon people to actually owe him a debt. He says in the Quran, who will indebt Allah with a beautiful debt? So Allah is asking us for a loan. Who will indebt Allah with a beautiful debt?</p>
<p>Why is Allah asking us for a loan? So that Allah multiplies its rewards many, many times over and returns it back to his life. So in this verse, Allah is actually asking His slaves, who among you will give me a loan? Right? Who among you will indebt me with His willing submission of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span>, so that I can multiply its rewards many, many times over and pay it back to you. For He has the most noble rewards. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Even on that day of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِسَاب</span>, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> is full of love and mercy. So that is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span> linguistically means. The king or the owner of the time period when the debt of submission falls due. Now let''s look at some of the implications of this verse. The most important aspect of this verse is that it addresses this notion of an unjust world.</p>
<p>We know so many people today have given up faith and this belief in the existence of a creator based on the injustices that they see in this world, right? They look at all of this injustice that goes on around in this world and they use that as proof against God. Now this can be based on an injustice that happened to them personally or injustices that happens to another. And their argument is that if there is a God that is so loving and caring, then how come He let all of this happen? Now, what''s interesting about that, especially to us as a Muslim, is that nowhere in the Quran, Allah claims or promises to give us a good life in this world. Nowhere. In fact, what He promises every human being is quite the opposite. He says, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْبَقَرَةِ</span>, He says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَنَبْلُوَنَّكُم بِشَىْءٍۢ مِّنَ ٱلْخَوْفِ وَٱلْجُوعِ وَنَقْصٍۢ مِّنَ ٱلْأَمْوَلِ وَٱلْأَنفُسِ وَٱلثَّمَرَتِ ۗ وَبَشِّرِ ٱلصَّبِرِينَ</span> ‎. And no doubt about it, indeed, surely we shall test you with fear, with hunger, with loss of wealth, with loss of life, children, with loss of crops. But then he says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَبَشِّرِ الصَّابِرِينَ</span>, and for the patient ones there are glad tidings. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْمُلْكِ</span>, he says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَبَرَكَ ٱلَّذِى بِيَدِهِ ٱلْمُلْكُ وَهُوَ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ شَىْءٍۢ قَدِيرٌ</span> ‎. Blessed is the one whose hand has the ultimate dominion and is able to do everything.</p>
<p>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ ٱلْمَوْتَ وَٱلْحَيَوٰةَ لِيَبْلُوَكُمْ أَيُّكُمْ أَحْسَنُ عَمَلًۭا ۚ وَهُوَ ٱلْعَزِيزُ ٱلْغَفُورُ</span> ‎. Who created death and life to test you and see which of you is the best in action. According to the Quran, this world was never meant for justice. According to the Quran, this life is a test, and this world that we live in is an examination hall, and everyone is being tested. Some with poverty, some with wealth, some with sickness, some with health. In fact, the healthy and the rich are going through a much more difficult test than the sick and the poor. Because they will be held accountable for these gifts. You know, I gave you all this, show me what you did with it. And this is why it''s no surprise that the greatest man to walk the face of this earth, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ ﷺ</span>, he chose poverty over riches. But the point is that this world is imperfect and it is unjust by its very design. So now let''s take a moment to think about this perfect world. If I asked you to describe your perfect world to me, if I asked you to design a perfect world, what kind of world would you design? Think about it. What components would your perfect world comprise of?</p>
<p>We will all agree unanimously that in our perfect world there would be complete justice, there would be perfect harmony. No one in the world would sleep hungry. There would be no disease, there would be no pain, there would be no suffering. It would be a world where everybody is treated equally, justly. Love and peace will prevail. There will be absolutely no ugliness in that world. There will only be beauty. People would love and help each other. The proverbial lion and the deer would drink peacefully from the same river. Isn''t that the perfect world that we all dream of? Now if you think about it, what did I just describe to you? I just described <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>.</p>
<p>Isn''t it? Isn''t all of this exactly the description of paradise? So you can see that this world is simply one side of the equation. The problem with someone who is only seeing this world is that he is looking at just one side of the equation, and there are a bunch of imbalances on this side. If you do not believe in a Day of Judgment, in a day of accountability, then you are simply left with a world that is completely unfair. But if you believe in a Day of Judgment, all the injustices on this side of the equation will get balanced with perfect justice on the other side of the equation. So in fact, the injustices of this world is proof and testimony to the existence of a perfect world. Logically, just speaking logically, rationally as human beings, what we see in this world, we have to believe because it is our innate desire to have this perfect world. Where do we get this concept of a perfect world? How come even the atheist, the one who does not believe in God, does believe in the dream of a perfect world? Because that is the other side of the equation. So just this, the inferior version of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, is proof of the existence of a perfect world. So in this verse Allah talks about this transition that will take place from this world into the next.</p>
<p>There is a transition from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِيَّة</span>. From lordship to ownership or kingship. From the love, care, concern and mercy of Allah in this world to the one in which He takes on a different role. The role of the owner and the king of the day when the debt of submission falls due. So this verse actually makes the transition from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُخْرَى</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, in the next session we''ll look at some more implications of this verse and how it affects our lives. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ الْخَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ، وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ، وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِ الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ، وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'day of judgment - linguistics';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span>, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ يَا اللَّهُ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ، وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ، كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ، وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ، فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي، وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي، وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي، يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>.</p>
<p>So in the previous session, we learned that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, linguistically what it means is the owner, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, which is king. So the owner or the king of the time period when the debt of submission falls due. And we talked about the imperfections of this world which necessitate this idea of a perfect world which is the hereafter.</p>
<p>Today we look at this verse in some more detail and I want to help us understand its purpose and its relevance in the surah, especially from the point of view of this transition that we see taking place here. So if you look at the theme of the surah so far, we saw that from the beginning, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s love and His mercy was being highlighted, right? I begin with the lofty symbols of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> that are pointing to His immense love and mercy. All sincere praise and all unconditional gratitude is due to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for His mastery, His lordship over this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ</span>, which is everything that can be known in existence. So, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, the abundantly merciful, the consistently merciful.</p>
<p>So, if you look at this, right, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s love is being highlighted in the surah, and now suddenly there is this switch that we see in tone, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> changes His tone to say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, owner of the time when the debt of submission falls due. Right? Why this sudden change in tone? So scholars explain that this transition is extremely profound, and its purpose is to engender this desire for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> in our hearts. So it is what the Arabs called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَاعِث</span>, which is what basically stimulates you to do something, something that motivates you. It''s the motivating factor about something.</p>
<p>Because if you think about it, if love is not balanced with some system of accountability, it is human tendency to start taking advantage of that kindness. We see this all the time in our life. If you know anyone in your life who is just love, love, love all the time, someone who will let people get away with murder, what you''ll see is that as much as this person might be loved, they will never be taken seriously. In fact, they will be taken advantage of. Isn''t that the case? So understanding this weakness in human nature, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> changes His tone to remind us that look, even though I am a very merciful and a very loving God, but you have to take Me seriously.</p>
<p>Don''t think you can take undue advantage of My love, because I am also your creator, I am also your owner, I am also your king, and I have set a time period where I will take you to account for your debt of submission. Because nothing is happening right now to you in this world. You are free to do whatever you want, right? If you commit a sin right now in this world, lightning is not going to strike you down. So what that freedom allows human nature to do is that it allows us to become heedless, and this verse is basically reminding us not to be heedless. It creates a sense of accountability and it also creates a sense of urgency to make the most of whatever time we have remaining.</p>
<p>Because no one knows when and where he''s going to die, right? Except that we all know that we are going to inevitably die someday. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا تَدْرِى نَفْسٌۭ مَّاذَا تَكْسِبُ غَدًۭا وَمَا تَدْرِى نَفْسٌۢ بِأَىِّ أَرْضٍۢ تَمُوتُ</span>. No soul knows what he will earn tomorrow, and no soul knows in which land it will die. So this concept of an inevitable death and this idea of returning back to the creator for accountability, what it does is that it creates this awareness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in our hearts, which is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. This word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِقَايَة</span>, which means a strong sense of fearful awareness.</p>
<p>So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> is the awareness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s constant presence in our lives. So, scholars have stated that what this verse <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span> does is that it motivates us towards <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> in a number of ways. So, today I want to go over some of those motivating factors that have been outlined by scholars.</p>
<p>The first, they say, is the fear of punishment in this world. This is a worldly type of retribution which is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِقَاب</span>. So human nature is such that we just naturally tend to fear pain. In fact, fear of pain is probably a greater immediate concern than the desire for pleasure. There were these studies done that when given two choices and the possibility of one over the other were equal, people chose to avoid pain rather than acquiring pleasure. So the avoidance of pain, the fear of pain, actually supersedes the desire for pleasure. And so what this fear allows us to do is that it enables us to delay our gratification.</p>
<p>They did a study in the US which was, I think, a 30-year study. It was very interesting. What they did is that they took these five-year-old kids and they offered them a cookie. And they said that you can have this cookie right now, or if you wait 10 minutes you can have two cookies. And so the children were very different in their choices. And what these people did is that they monitored these kids over a 30-year period. And what they found almost consistently was that the children that delayed their gratification for a greater reward, the children that took one cookie and waited, that left that one cookie and waited 10 minutes to get the two cookies, basically delaying their gratification, they found that these kids were far more successful in their lives than the ones who wanted it and took it immediately. It was a very interesting study.</p>
<p>So the delay of gratification, right, this temporary deprivation for the hope of something greater, is very important. And this delay is the basis of a lot of what is going on in the Quran. This idea of delaying gratification, because the people of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, they desire instant gratification. Right? No, you love this, the instant gratification, the present life of this world, and you keep delaying, you keep leaving the Hereafter. So basically you put off what''s coming and you just want this immediate pleasure.</p>
<p>And this is something we all are guilty of. We all fall into lust. It''s an immediate pleasure. It''s instant gratification. It''s an immediate satisfaction. And we often end up sacrificing doing the right thing to gain this immediate pleasure. And we don''t think of repercussions. But that''s the way we are, that''s just human nature.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span> is essentially what the scholars say, you know, it is the fear of punishment in the next world. One of the things about the Islamic and essentially all Abrahamic faiths is that there''s an idea that just as you have physics, you also have metaphysics. So physics are laws that we can discern from this palpable world, right? This world, this sensory world that we can touch and feel, it has certain laws.</p>
<p>And these are the laws of cause and effect. If you do something in this world, so every action has to have a reaction. So for instance, if I hit this table, there''s going to be the sound, right? If there was no sound, then there''s a suspension of a physical law and we''d have to look into it. Like, why did that happen? Why was there no sound? Because according to the laws of physics, my striking this table should produce a sound. So there are these laws of, if you jump off a building, there''s going to be an effect from that cause. Right? So just as there are physical laws, there are also metaphysical laws. There are laws of cause and effect that transcend the physical world. And they move into the spiritual world or the psychic world.</p>
<p>So for instance, if I shout at you, if I scream at you, there is going to be a psychic effect. It''s going to have an emotional effect. Where are emotions? We don''t truly understand where emotions lie, except that we just know that they''re part of our consciousness. But if I shout at you, you will feel something inside. You will either feel depressed or you will become angry. And that''s coming from another realm, right? It''s not from the physical realm. That''s from the realm of emotions. So just as there are physical effects, there are also spiritual effects, there are also psychic effects. So for instance, the Arabs, they have this term, what they call which literally means water of the face. But the idea is that it''s a kind of purity or a kind of impurity that people have on their face.</p>
<p>You''ll see this, people who have sinned a lot in their lives, their evil begins to show up on their faces and you get a type of metaphysical darkness in their face. In the same way, people that do a lot of good deeds, you''ll see that their faces are affected by that. They have a kind face. You see this luminance or this brightness radiating from their face. And this is why the Arabs used to call a face, they would call it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصَّحِيفَة</span>, which means the register, because it registers everything. Somebody once said that by 50, everybody has the face that they deserve. So if you''re 50 years old, you have the face that you deserve. If you''ve had a good life, it''s going to show up on your face. So that fear of another worldly punishment is one of the things that will compel a person to do good and stay within boundaries within this world by delaying gratifications. The next motivating factor for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> is desiring a benefit. That''s the next one.</p>
<p>So just as people fear punishment, they also desire benefit. This is at the most basic human foundation, right? Stick and carrot. This is how children are disciplined, stick and carrot. It''s how societies work, stick and carrot. So really much of society is based on these principles. And that''s why the highest people, the elite, the most intelligent people are the ones that actually transcend these stick and carrot factors and they move into another realm. People like Maulana Ali, who often said in his prayers, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا إِلَهِي، مَا عَبَدْتُكَ بِشَوْقِ جَنَّتِكَ، وَلَا مِنْ خَوْفِ نَارِكَ، بَلْ وَجَدْتُكَ مُسْتَحِقًّا لِلْعِبَادَةِ فَعَبَدْتُكَ</span>. My master, I do not worship you out of a desire for your paradise, nor do I worship you out of fear of your hellfire. Instead, I truly found you worthy of my worship, and for that reason alone I worship you. And this is a very high station of worship that completely transcends this carrot and stick philosophy. It''s the ideal form of worship. But the majority of people are at this base level, and because the Quran speaks to all of humanity, it talks in this general manner. There''s a Hadith in which the Prophet said, move according to the level of the least amongst you. Move according to the level of the least amongst you.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So you have to move with the least amongst you. You don''t speed up and leave people behind. Right? No, that''s not the way of Islam. And this is why if you carefully study this religion, you''ll see that most of it is designed to protect the weakest people. Right? Because there are people who can stand on their own and they don''t have a problem with that, but then there are also people that cannot do that and they have their human weaknesses. And so the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, it protects those people by preventing them from certain things. And this is why the Quran speaks against things like gambling, for instance, right? The Quran says that there is good in gambling, actually, it does say that.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَسْـَٔلُونَكَ عَنِ ٱلْخَمْرِ وَٱلْمَيْسِرِ قُلْ فِيهِمَآ إِثْمٌۭ كَبِيرٌۭ وَمَنَفِعُ لِلنَّاسِ وَإِثْمُهُمَآ أَكْبَرُ مِن نَّفْعِهِمَا</span>. They ask you concerning alcohol and gambling. Tell them in them is a great sin and also a benefit for mankind, but the sin is far greater than their benefit. So Quran actually says that there is some good in gambling. I mean, there are lessons to be learned in gambling, right? Risk is an interesting phenomenon in commerce. There''s a strong relationship between gambling and commerce. But the harms of gambling are far greater than its benefits. And it is a known social science, this fact that around 3% of people that go into gambling casinos, they become addicted and it destroys their lives. And this is why if you''ve ever been to a gambling casino, I mean, I''ve been inside this casino at Atlantic City many years ago, and it was one of the most frightening things I ever saw. It was like a circle out of Dante''s Inferno. And you just see all of these people in this zombie-like state, just sitting on their seats, putting in quarters into the machine one after the other and pulling this lever. I mean, it''s a scary sight. And then there was this pawn shop in the middle of that casino with this big sign outside that said, We take wedding rings. That is just so sinister.</p>
<p>What kind of people are these? How do you justify a job like this, just depriving people in this manner, just taking advantage of human weakness? But that''s the reality of our world, right? And gambling is based on the carrot phenomenon. It''s a carrot phenomenon. You know, gamblers think that if I put in money and I pull the lever, and it''s all designed so that winning will happen enough times to keep you there, right? That''s part of the design. So when people leave, they say, oh, you know, I made such and such. They never actually think about how much they lost. They''ll always tell you how much they won. So that''s the phenomenon of the carrot, you know, and it''s used a lot, and that''s one of the worst things about it.</p>
<p>But then obviously from the worldly things, you know, these things that are pleasurable that we find in the slide, that give you instant pleasure, they''re always followed by a far greater pain, because that''s just the design of it, isn''t it? I mean, in our case, we all can give examples of that. And this is why the Prophet, he said, Paradise was surrounded by distasteful things and fire was surrounded by delightful things, pleasurable things. So if you''re walking towards paradise, you will inevitably encounter distasteful things. It''s just part of the design. And so now it''s up to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَقِيل</span>, this person of intellect, to recognize this, and that''s why the Quran frequently talks about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدَبُّر</span>, which means to reflect deeply upon something. You know, Quran frequently asks us ... Why don''t you reflect deeply? It actually uses the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدَبُّر</span> a lot, which is deep reflection. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدَبُّر</span> comes from a root which means to take something to its end. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُبُر</span> is the behind of something.</p>
<p>It''s the end of something. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَدْبَار</span> is what''s behind you. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدَبُّر</span> is going through something until you get to its very end. So that''s why when you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدَبُّر</span> as a quality, you just look down the road, right? You think about the consequences. You think about where this action will end up. Whereas this person who''s just stuck in this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَجَلَ</span>, this haste of instant gratification, doesn''t really just go beyond the present, and they''re just stuck right there and they don''t think about the future. And there''s this interesting theory in theology about this divine experience versus a demonic experience. And the way you differentiate between a true divine experience and a true demonic experience is that a demonic experience is always accompanied with an expansion of the self. In other words, it is very exciting and it''s a very joyful experience, the demonic experience. But then it is always followed with despair.</p>
<p>On the contrary, a true spiritual experience, a true divine experience is exactly the opposite. The initial experience is incredible constriction and it''s incredible fear and dread, but then it is followed by a sense of expansion and joy. So, you know, anyway, so the benefit, acquiring a benefit is the second motivating factor for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. The next motivating factor, scholars say, and benefits of this verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, the other benefit that it provides is that it gives us the grounds for the fear of a reckoning. That you''re going to be reckoned on the Day of Judgment. And so this fear of reckoning, it should instill in our hearts this desire to do good. You see, anyone who has a business, they have what they call internal audits. If you have a business and you live in a country like the US or England, for instance, where they have these revenue services, these are external agencies that will come in and they will examine your books, they will examine your business. And if you''ve been fudging the books, then you''ll get penalties. It''s not just that you will pay what you owe in back taxes and things like that. They will actually add penalties and they will add interest and they will do all these kinds of things.</p>
<p>So companies that are worried about that external reckoning or that external auditing by the revenue services, what they do is that they conduct an internal audit, and so if there''s anything that needs correcting, they will correct it internally. And that''s what''s called in our terms, it''s called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَاسَبَة</span>, and it''s part of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, where you actually take your own self to account. The hadith says: Take yourself to account before you are taken to account. And this is why it has been said that when a true believer, when he lies down to sleep at night, he reflects on his actions for the day. And the things that he found that he did wrong, he asks <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for forgiveness and he makes a determination to correct those things in the next day. So this is why this concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَاسَبَة</span> is an extremely important concept within Islam.</p>
<p>The next benefit of this verse is that it creates this concept of shame or modesty from the awareness of God. That <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is aware of our actions, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is watching us. So we should have this shame or this modesty before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَيَّا</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَيَا</span> in Arabic is probably better translated as modesty, which is related to shame. There''s a sense that you don''t want to do something in front of a person or in front of your Lord out of love and respect. There are certain things that children won''t do in front of their parents out of respect. The Prophet, he said: Every religion has a peculiar characteristic, and the characteristic of my religion is modesty. Anthropologists, they divide cultures into two types of cultures.</p>
<p>They divide it as shame based cultures and then guilt based cultures. So Western cultures generally tend to fall into the guilt culture. And a guilt culture, it cultivates within people moral components inside of themselves where If they do something wrong, they feel bad about it on the inside. And so one of the things a lot of people know about people in the West is that they will often find it very difficult to do wrong things without actually feeling bad. In fact, there''s a whole industry in America to help people overcome this feeling of guilt, self-help books, self-improvement courses. There''s a whole industry that capitalizes on this idea of helping people overcome guilt. So in guilt cultures, there''s an internal mechanism. In shame-based cultures, the mechanisms are external. And so people, they don''t do wrong things out of fear of others. They don''t do wrong things out of fear of others.</p>
<p>which generally creates hypocrisy in cultures. And India and Pakistan and many Asian countries are very much rooted in the shame based culture where people don''t have an internal component and they''re only controlled from external factors. So when such a person is in his own country, in his own community, he finds it shameful to do certain things, but when he goes to another country, where nobody knows him it''s no longer shameful to do those same things right they start doing them and and they don''t really feel any guilt inside or any shame inside and this is why you know a lot of kids from India and Pakistan when they come to the US for studies or things like that or you know any other Western country they go completely crazy they start drinking they start partying they do drugs and even though they never did those things when they were back home It just becomes completely acceptable overnight because they''re no longer in the culture where to do those things was shameful. And also, a different country or a different city, it provides anonymity, right? When people move into different countries, into different environments, because back home, everybody knows you, right? They know your parents. So if you do anything, the entire community is going to hear about it. The entire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَمَاعَة</span> is going to hear about it. In a different city or a different country, there is this anonymity. So it''s difficult to sense that shame, which is why guilt cultures are very important in advanced societies and shame, it works very well at the tribal level, right?</p>
<p>It works quite well in small cultures, but when you move to advanced cultures like the West, it breaks down. now Islam is rooted in a shame based culture but what it does is that it transcends the shame that is rooted in people and it grounds it in shame before God so it''s not so much of an internal factor it is still an external factor right meaning it''s more shame based but it has to do with this modesty, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَيَّا</span> before God, before the prophet, before the angels, right? Because you have these two angels on each side recording everything that we do. So the idea is that you become aware of the spiritual presence that then prevents you from doing wrong things and this is called which is a very high station it is a station of vigilance or this constant awareness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s presence based on his word in the Quran indeed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> sees everything and is aware of everything The use of this term is so beautiful. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقِيب</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقِيب</span> is the jealous lover. You know, like if you''re in love with someone and that person is in love with another person, so that other person becomes your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقِيب</span>, right? And it is the nature of a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقِيب</span> that you''re constantly gonna spy on them, constantly be aware of every move they make, right? That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقِيب</span>.</p>
<p>the jealous lovers. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is watching you like a jealous lover. He''s watching every single move of it. The metaphors, the analogies are so beautiful in the Quran. So there is this idea of shame. There''s this very interesting story over a hundred years ago when British ruled India, they say they lived a pickpocket, right? He was a Muslim, but his job was just picking pockets of people. He was a thief. In fact, he was a member of a gang who did this. There was an entire gang dedicated to just, they would go out and then they would pick pockets all day and then bring all the earnings in the night. So one day, this man, he picked the pocket of an Englishman, of a Britisher, and that wallet had a lot of money in it. So he was quite happy initially.</p>
<p>But then he thought of something, and then he went and returned that wallet back to that Englishman. And that night when he returned back to his gang, the leader would ask everyone how much did they earn that day. And so when his turn came, he said, &quot;You know, I did pick the pocket of this rich Englishman, this Christian, but then I decided to return it back.&quot; And when asked why, he said, &quot;You know, I thought that on the Day of Judgment, if Christ came to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> and said, A man of your nation picked the pocket of a man from my nation, my Prophet would have to feel shame before him on my account. And I just cannot be the reason to put my Prophet to shame before Christ. And so I returned his wallet to him.&quot;</p>
<p>Right? And I mean, this was the level of awareness of just people a hundred years ago, because they were concerned about putting our Prophet to shame. And in his last sermon to his nation, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, in his last <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُطْبَة</span>, one of the things he said was ............................................. put me to shame on the Day of Judgment. You know, when a classroom produces a bad result, the teacher feels shame, right? Isn''t he asked, &quot;What''s the matter? Why did so many children fail in your class today?&quot;</p>
<p>And our Prophet, his last request to us was, &quot;My people, do not shame me before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> on the Day of Judgment.&quot; There is this verse in Urdu poetry, which I love so much. It''s wojo geet tum neso na nahi, mere umre bhar kari astha. Mere dard ki tivo das tanjis at tum haseema ragai.</p>
<p>So there''s the story about — I don''t know if this story is true or not, but nevertheless it demonstrates this beautiful love that this man had for his Prophet, and it was grounded in shame, because he felt ashamed at the idea of being the reason for the Prophet''s shame. So it was still an external factor, but it transcended the realm of people and it was rooted in a higher dimension. And may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> give us all this awareness of understanding that, just having this awareness of not just being ashamed before the Prophet, but also putting the Prophet to shame before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, based on our — you know, this man who strived twenty-three years of his life for us, and we cannot even produce the most basic result for him.</p>
<p>The next motivating factor for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> is gratitude over the blessing of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, for all the blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, by being obedient. So the next thing that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, this verse, helps us with is obtaining <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> through the recognition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s blessings and demonstrating the gratitude for those blessings through obedience. So it''s this awareness that every blessing that you have, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> gave it to you. If God gave you sight, the gratitude for that sight is to use it for the sake of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. If He gave you hearing, the gratitude for hearing is to use it for the sake of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. If He gave you physical strength, everything should be used for what it was made for.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, He did not create His creation and desire disobedience from them. He enables disobedience, which is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِذْلَان</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the enabler. He allows disobedience to happen, but He''s not content with it. He does not want His servants to be in a state of ingratitude, even though He allows it. So it''s very important to have gratitude for the blessings by using those blessings in obedience. That is the definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>: to use the gifts that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has given you to perform things that are pleasing to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>The next motivating factor is knowledge. So knowledge actually provides a reason to have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْلَا</span>. Now, if you notice, there is this ascension that is taking place in the motivations here.</p>
<p>We started off with the base levels, which is the fear of punishment and the desire for a reward. Then it transcended, it went higher into this idea of a reckoning, and then just gratitude through obedience, and now a higher realm of knowledge, which is the next motivating factor for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْلَا</span>, which is based on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, right? Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, &quot;Indeed the ones who are truly fearful of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, who have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُشُوعٌ</span>, they are the scholars.&quot; The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُشُوعٌ</span> is a type of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> that comes from a fearful awareness of God through knowledge. It comes from awe before God that is based on knowledge of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is. And it is amongst His creation, amongst His servants, it is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> that have this knowledge.</p>
<p>And then the magnification of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s majesty, right? And this is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامُ الْهَيْبَةِ</span>, which is the station of awe, and which is a higher station than, you know, just even just basic gratitude. Because it comes from this awe that is produced by the knowledge of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is, right? It''s called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامُ الْهَيْبَةِ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحِبٌّ</span> is someone who has this awe for a greater, like for the Sultan, right? There''s an awe that goes with it.</p>
<p>so you know when you go visit a Sultan, there''s a whole way that they have made things in order to instill this awe in your hearts for him. So for instance, if you go to the White House to meet the President, you''ll see these Marine guards with guns standing outside that have these big bayonets. I mean, there''s a reason why the bayonets are there. I mean, they could have just had the guns, right? Why do you need those bayonets? But the bayonet is there to instill this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, right? Bayonet is this little sword-like thing, this knife-like thing that is attached to the gun, to the barrel of the gun. Why do they need that? In case of an event, they''ll probably shoot rather than stab, but why is there that knife? Because it''s meant to instill this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> in our hearts. It''s the same with just swords outside the palace of a king in Arabia. It creates <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>. And then you go in and then there are all these long corridors made of marble and this expensive stuff, and they make you walk through all of these magnificent corridors with all of these elegant decorations. Why? The purpose is to humble you, because you''re about to meet the Sultan, you''re about to meet the President, the Prime Minister, the King. And so this whole experience that they make you go through is to produce this kind of humility within your heart, to put you in your place, you see?</p>
<p>And no matter where you go, they all have the same type of rituals. Whether you go to the Queen''s palace or you go to the White House or to go to the Sultan''s palace, they''re all designed to create this awe in your heart for the Sultan, which is important. Because if the Sultan does not generate this awe, then why should people obey him in the first place? And this is also why traditionally <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> dressed a certain way. This is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> encouraged <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> to dress in this certain particular way so that it instills this awe, this respect in the hearts of the audience. Otherwise, why is anybody going to listen to them? This is why we find the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِسْوَة</span> on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكَعْبَة</span>, that black cloth, because it''s a sanctified place, right? That <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِسْوَة</span>, it illustrates the magnificence of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكَعْبَة</span>. It creates this awe within our hearts. So it''s a very important aspect of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعْظِيم</span>. And then the final stage, you know, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدْقُ الْمَحَبَّةِ</span>, the sincerity in one''s love for God, right?</p>
<p>It gives this ultimate form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, which is the highest motivating factor for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. And this is, you know, one of the poets, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محْمُودٌ الوَرَّاقُ</span>, he said, &quot;You disobey <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> but then you claim that you love Him? This is impossible and a very strange type of logic that I don''t understand. Had your love been sincere, you would have been obedient.&quot; Because a lover always obeys his or her beloved. So the nature of love is that you want to obey the one that you love. Then he says, a woman asked about the state of the one who was in this burning passion for her, right? The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَاشِقٌ</span> is the one who is in this burning passion for the beloved. So she''s asking this man about her lover, and she says, &quot;So for the sake of God, tell me about his state. And don''t stint in your description and don''t exaggerate.&quot; In other words, I want to hear the truth. So she asks this man, tell me about his love. Tell me what kind of love does he have for me. And so this man says, if this man knew that he would die from thirst, and if you said don''t touch that water, he wouldn''t touch it.</p>
<p>So if your lover is gonna die of thirst and you said don''t touch that drink of water, he won''t touch it. He''d rather die than disobey you. So the point of that, what the poet is emphasizing, is that it is the nature, obedience is the nature of love. The very nature of love is to be obedient. So the more you love God, the more you will obey God. And that''s the idea. And so the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> have stated these, think of it like a staircase, right? And think of all of these motivating factors as rungs. So it begins with the fear of punishment, then the next step is desiring the rewards, then it is having this concept of reckoning, this accountability before God, right? And then shame, the next level is having shame before God, His prophet and His angels. Then the next level is gratitude, then the next is knowledge, and finally you reach the final stage of love, which is sincere love manifested through obedience.</p>
<p>So it''s a very, you know, it''s a very beautiful and a very powerful verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, and its purpose is to motivate us towards <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. And so you have, you know, with ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, from these three verses, we get a complete picture of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is. Right? So it provides a complete description, a very concise description of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is.</p>
<p>The next, the following verses that we will study in future sessions, so these next verses, they give us an understanding of what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> wants from us and what we want from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And so this is how the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> is split into these two parts. You have the first, these beginning three verses, which tells us in third person who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is. And then, from there, it begins this transition of what we are asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for. So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, we''ll explore those verses in the following sessions, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ، وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيٍّ النَّبِيِّ طَالِبٍ شَرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ، وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِمَا الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ، وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'motivating factors for piety';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>.</p>
<p>We completed the linguistic study of the first four verses of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>. So now we have, after giving us an introduction of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is through the first, or rather the beginning verses, then he says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎. It is only you that we worship or willingly enslave ourselves to. And it is only you that we seek help from. Now here, there''s something really interesting in this verse. If you notice, there is a complete change of tone over here. So the previous verses, they were all in the third person, right? They were informational. So ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, begin with the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, extremely merciful, intensely merciful. All praise and glory belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, who is the master of all universes or everything that is known, the abundant, the abundantly merciful, the consistently merciful, owner of a time when the debt of submission falls due. So these are all statements. If you notice, they''re all in the third person, in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is just basically informing us about who He is.</p>
<p>But then, He suddenly changes the tone and He turns it into a second person conversation. He turns it into a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>, as if we are now speaking directly with Him. It is only you we worship and it is only you we turn to for help. The tone of this verse is not that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the one that we worship and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the one that we turn to for help. It''s not in the third person. It''s as if we are in direct conversation with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> at this point. And this is a beautiful linguistic tool in the Arabic language that is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الِٱلْتِفَاتُ</span>, which is a sudden change in the pronoun of the speaker, or the one that is being spoken to. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> constantly changes the conversation in the Quran from third person to first person, then to second person, and then back to third person, right? And you can see this in almost every single <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>. He sometimes refers to Himself using I, sometimes He uses we, right? Sometimes he uses him. These are all linguistic tools to not only keep the reader alert and awake, but also a mechanism to highlight what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> wants from us. So in the case of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, you can see that the tone now suddenly changes to a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>, it changes to a supplication. It is only you we worship and it is only you we turn to for help.</p>
<p>And who is the one saying that? It is you and I that are saying that. So we are now in direct conversation with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And this style of switching suddenly from third person to second person is a means of letting us know that this is in fact what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> wants from us. You understand? When we say ... because this is all revelation coming from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, right? We are not the authors of Quran. So this is all revelation coming from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. So now imagine a book like Quran, something so meaningful. How is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to have this conversation with us where suddenly now He wants us, while He''s describing Himself, He wants us to know what He wants from us. So by changing his tone to ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is expressing His intention of wanting us to say that we willingly worship you and we freely give up our freedom and desires to become your slaves.</p>
<p>In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not commanding us into slavery by saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَعْبَدُوا أَنْفُسَكُمْ لِ</span>, enslave yourselves to Me. Instead, He wants us to say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span>, it is only you that we want to worship. In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is asking us to reach this conclusion of our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُضِيءَ</span> on our own, through the study of the previous three verses. The idea is that when we truly understand the meaning of ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, when we truly understand the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, and when we truly understand the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, and when we recognize what an incredibly loving, merciful, and powerful <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اللَّهُ</span> is, our natural and logical conclusion to that understanding has to be . You see? Our natural response has to be a willing desire of submission. So the idea here is that if you haven''t reached that conclusion all by yourself, then don''t say it. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not forcing us to say it. This is us saying it to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> out of our own free will. So I wanted to start the explanation of this verse by making that distinction.</p>
<p>Now let''s look at this verse linguistically. The first word is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>, which means only you. So, linguistically, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span> excludes everything and everyone except the object of the conversation. So, think of it like a clause of exclusivity. So, what we''re saying is you, only you, and no one else but you, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>.</p>
<p>The next word is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعْبُدُ</span>, which means we worship. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعْبُدُ</span> comes from the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, which we all know means worship, which in turn comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, which means slave. Now, what is the connection between worship and slavery? What''s the relationship there? The relationship is that worship is a demonstration of one''s slavery. Worship or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is a demonstration of one''s acceptance of slavery. It is a proof of one''s slavery. It is an act of devotion and dedication that establishes one''s slavery.</p>
<p>So I''ll give you a very basic example. If I stood on the top of a building and I screamed at the top of my lungs for two hours that, you know, I''m a slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, I''m a slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, I''m a slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, but then when it''s time for prayer, if I do not pray, I can say that I''m a slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> all day long, but did I back it up? Did I prove that statement? No.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/166/23fd8cf4-d085-43b5-a0e9-eb75f7b8751d.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>So our worship, our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, is actually proof and evidence of the fact that we are in fact the slaves of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Otherwise it''s just an empty claim. That''s how these two words are connected together. So it means it is only you and no one else but you that we worship or willingly enslave ourselves to.</p>
<p>Now, what''s really powerful about this concept is that by desiring this enslavement of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, we are essentially desiring freedom from everything else. Becoming the slaves of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> provides freedom from every other slavery of this world. So essentially what we''re recognizing through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span> is that we do not want to become the slave of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. We want to become the slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and in the process of that slavery, through the process of that slavery, we want to free ourselves from everything else. So by recognizing our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمُّ الْقُرْآنِ</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, what we get is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُرِّيَة</span>, which is true freedom. In completing the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمُّ الْقُرْآنِ</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, we are no longer slaves to ourselves and that''s a truly free person. If I can control myself, then I''m a slave to myself. Somebody can say, I''m free, I''m free.</p>
<p>But then when the food shows up, they just cannot stop themselves. And that''s not freedom as far as the Muslim is concerned, right? He''s not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ اللهِ</span> at that point. He''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ الْهَوَى</span>. He''s a slave of his own passions. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ اللهِ</span>, the slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, is that when the situation arises, he has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. He has that mindfulness of the presence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. It''s not that the temptation goes away, right? The desire is still there because we''re all humans, so the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهْوَة</span> might still be there, but the slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ اللهِ</span>, can control it because he''s not the slave of his passion, he is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّد</span> of it, right? He''s not the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ</span> of his passion, he is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّد</span> of his passion, he is the master of it. That is someone who is really free, you know, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدَّهْرُ</span>. But this level of mastery can only be attained through the slavery of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>So, you know, there are many kinds of slaveries in that respect, right? The tongue is the same, right? There are so many people who just can''t stop backbiting. They just can''t stop gossiping. I mean, you can tell them, you can point it out to them a million times, but they just can''t stop. I mean, it''s just amazing. And in most cases, they''re not even aware of it. I mean, I''m sure I do things like that all the time, but because this just exists within us, this inability to control our tongues, to speak badly about others, to say things that we really shouldn''t be saying. And I''m just guilty as the next person. But this is the human weakness that people find it hard to control. We find it hard to control these things. So learning to control the tongue is also a major thing in Islam.</p>
<p>So when we say &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span>,&quot; it is only you we desire to enslave ourselves to, what''s implicit in that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> is that we are essentially desiring freedom from the enslavement of everything else. Everything that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>: money, houses, cars, children, food, these branded clothes, friends, relatives. I mean, there are millions and millions of shackles that we wear around ourselves, and you know, and we don''t even realize it. And what the slavery of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> does is that it frees us from all those shackles.</p>
<p>The next thing that I want to point out about this verse is the fact that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, he uses the plural form here. He says, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span>,&quot; it is only you we worship. Now, let me ask you a question. The majority of the time when we pray, are we praying by ourselves or in a group? Generally, we''re praying by ourselves, right? So then, can I say when I''m praying by myself, can I just say, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ أَعْبُدُ</span>,&quot; it is only you that I worship? I mean, wouldn''t that be more logical?</p>
<p>So then can I change it to the singular form when I''m just praying by myself? No, I cannot. It''s not permissible. It''s not allowed. I cannot change it. I have to use the plural form. So that''s very interesting, right? Why did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> fix the statement in the plural form? Scholars explain that by fixing the statement in the plural form, what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is emphasizing is this importance of unity. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is emphasizing this concept of unity, that even when I pray by myself, I do not speak just for myself. I speak on behalf of the whole <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>, on behalf of my entire community.</p>
<p>I say, ... because the ultimate way to establish unity is through the worship of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That''s why they say that a family that prays together stays together, a community that prays together sticks together. So the message in this verse and the following verses is to be united, to stay united as one unit, as one family, one community, one nation. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has forced this idea of this togetherness, you know. So when I''m doing my prayers, I am just naturally praying for you. When you''re doing your prayer, you''re praying for me, even when you''re praying alone. So this plural form, this unity, this, the sense of brotherhood, the sense of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلَّة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>, right, all of this is just present in this plural form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعْبُدُ</span>.</p>
<p>So we say &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span>,&quot; it is you that we worship. And the next thing we say is &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span>,&quot; it is only you that we seek assistance from. Now the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَسْتَعِينُ</span>, it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِعَانَة</span>, which in turn then comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَوْن</span>, and it means assistance. Now, there are many other words in Arabic for help, right? There is one common word is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصْر</span>. There is also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسَاعَدَة</span>, there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَجْدَة</span>. There are all these different kinds of help. But these are all different kinds of help.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصْر</span> is a type of help that has a greater sense of urgency to it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصْر</span> is the kind of help that we ask for. It''s the kind of help that we seek in emergency situations. Major, major things, right? When the help of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> arrives at the most difficult of times, understand that victory will be near. When the help of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> arrives and you are victorious in battles, right? Again, major help. Keep us steadfast and help us over the nation of disbelievers. Major emergency situation, major challenge. So when I''m in a situation where I cannot do anything to get myself out of that, then I need somebody else to do it for me. And in those kind of emergency situations, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصْر</span> would be used. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَوْن</span> is used for small matters. It''s the help that I need in little, little things, right?</p>
<p>So, I need a glass of water. I can get up and get it myself, but if you''re just right next to the cooler, I can say, &quot;Hey, can you get me a glass of water?&quot; And if you bring me that glass of water, what you did is you assisted me, right? That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَوْن</span>. It''s seeking assistance in daily tasks, little, little things. So why is this distinction made in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> where the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَوْن</span> is used instead of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصْر</span>? It''s because a true believer is someone who turns to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> all the time. That''s the quality of a true believer. He turns to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for everything, for all the little, little things in his life, you understand. He doesn''t just think of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in major situations. He remembers <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in the little, little things all throughout his day, you know, like a lover that remembers the beloved all the time.</p>
<p>And this is exactly what we find in the daily routine of our Prophet. He woke up in the morning. What was the first thing he did? The second he woke up, he praised <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي أَحْيَانَا بَعْدَ مَا أَمَاتَنَا وَإِلَيْهِ النُّشُورُ</span>&quot; He went to the restroom to relieve himself. &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الْخُبْثِ وَالْخَبَائِثِ</span>&quot; he sought the protection of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>. Right? He got on his horse.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ الَّذِي سَخَّرَ لَنَا هَذَا وَمَا كُنَّا لَهُ مُقْرِنِينَ وَإِنَّا إِلَى رَبِّنَا لَمُنْقَلِبُونَ</span> Before he would start eating his food, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ As soon as he finished eating his food, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎ He stepped out of his house to leave somewhere. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ تَوَكَّلْتُ عَلَى اللَّهِ وَلَا حَوْلَ وَلَا قُوَّةَ إِلَّا بِاللَّهِ الْعَلِيِّ الْعَظِيمِ</span> It is used for seeking assistance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> constantly, in even in the most tiny matters. So the first reason for using our own is this idea of constant remembrance.</p>
<p>The second reason we constantly seek His assistance is because we recognize that we are not the ideal slaves, right? We understand that we are weak human beings who desperately require assistance every step of the way, in every little thing that we do. Because we are dominated by our desires of this world. We are challenged by innumerable distractions every single waking moment of our lives. So we are constantly walking this slippery slope. So we know that it''s not going to be easy to maintain our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدِيَّة</span>, because we are surrounded by the worst four enemies that are constantly working against us.</p>
<p>First is our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>, our soul that is weak to begin with. Then we have this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, the temptations of this world. Then we have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوَىٰ</span>, which is our overwhelming desires and passions. And then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> himself. So we are surrounded on all sides by these powerful enemies. And we recognize that, and so we admit that our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو ضِيَاء</span> and our servitude is not going to be worthy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>. But we''re willing to try, right? We''re still willing to try. And in that trying, we''re going to need our master''s assistance in every little thing that we do. So when we fail over and over again, we will still constantly need <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to pick us up, because only He can protect and assist us. So, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎. It is only You that we worship, but then it is only You that we seek assistance from in every little thing that we do. So the statement is made up of these two parts.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span> is making that willing commitment to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That yes, I do want to become your slave so that I can be free from everything else. I desire freedom. The reason I am making this commitment to you is because I desire freedom. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span>. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> is asking Him for assistance in getting that job done. So that''s the second reason. And the third reason is that we seek the closeness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We desire the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْب</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. This is also the reason why this conversation switched from third person to second person, right? Because when we say we are now directly speaking with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. So it''s a more intimate conversation.</p>
<p>And we''re using that intimate conversation for two things, for worship and for assistance. So, what scholars tell us is that what this verse teaches us is that we all want to be near and we all want to be dear to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We all want to be loved by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We want to be His beloved. Right? But the ultimate way to establish that nearness, that love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, is by worshiping Him and by calling out to Him for assistance. That''s why in a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ قُدْسِيٌّ اللَّهُ</span> very explicitly says: my slave does not get closer to me more so than by doing what I have told him to do, and that is the five times daily prayer. So if you want to be close to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, pray five times a day and keep calling Him for assistance, because there is no better time to call Him for assistance than with prayer.</p>
<p>The next topic that I want to talk about is the sequence, the sequence in which these two things are used, these two words are used, right? So if you look at it, the first thing that is mentioned is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. Worship is mentioned first, and that is followed by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ</span>, which is the assistance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Now why is that important? Because the sequence that you find in the Quran between things, that is never coincidental, right? It always has a very specific purpose. It''s always very precise, because this is divine, this is divine revelation. So like nothing is just arbitrary in the Quran. So why is worship mentioned before seeking assistance? Because the best way to seek the help and the assistance from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the best way to ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for anything and everything that we may need or want, is to ask after worshipping Him.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ اللهِ</span> ibn Masood, he would tell his students, &quot;Save your needs and your necessities for the obligatory prayers.&quot; So whenever you need something or whenever you''re dealing with something difficult, a difficult situation, a challenge in life, don''t worry. There is going to be an obligatory prayer right around the corner. Use that as an opportunity to ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to help you solve your problems. Not to say that you cannot remember <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> during the entire day, which obviously we should, that''s the idea of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ</span>, right? But the proper sequence, the proper protocol would be to worship <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and then seek His assistance.</p>
<p>The last thing I want to point out is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ</span>, the wisdom in the repetition of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>. So you can see that the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span> is used twice here, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span> and then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span>. So the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span> is repeated twice. Now grammatically speaking, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> omitted the second <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>, and if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had just said ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎, it is only you we worship and seek assistance from.</p>
<p>Right? Just eliminate the second <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>. That would have been grammatically correct. So then why did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> repeat Himself when it''s not grammatically necessary? Does this mean that the second <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span> is not absolutely necessary to the meaning? No. And let''s look at that. So there''s a wisdom behind that. So when we say ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎, when we remove the second <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>, both words <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعْبُدُ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَسْتَعِينُ</span>, they connect back with the same <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>. Which means they become associated with that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>. But when I say ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎, they both now have their own <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>.</p>
<p>So what that does grammatically is that it makes both independent of one another. It makes worship and assistance independent of one another. Why is that important? Why did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> do it this way? Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> said worship and then seek assistance, right? That''s the optimal method. That''s the right protocol. That would be ideal. That''s how we''re supposed to do it. But then there might be some of us who might have lost our way. There might be some people who are not praying to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. They''re not worshipping <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>And yet, because of life, they just run into some difficulties or some tragedies. And in that difficult moment, they need <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s assistance. They need <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s help. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> very subtly, He separates the two, just a little bit. He makes them independent for the sake of the people to let them know that even if you aren''t worshipping Me, even if you aren''t praying to Me, don''t worry, you can still seek My help and My assistance. My door is still open to you. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ</span>. So worshipping Me and then seeking assistance would be the ideal way, in submitting yourselves to Me, willingly, freely enslaving yourselves to Me and then seeking My assistance, that would be the more logical way of doing things. But even if you''re not doing that, even if you''re not worshipping Me or enslaving yourselves to Me or submitting to Me, I will not deny My assistance to you. You can still always turn to Me. You can still always come to Me, talk to Me.</p>
<p>I will still be there for you. That''s the beauty of ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎. Now, considering the relationship between a master and a slave, what is the slave constantly in need of from his master? Commands, instructions, right? If there are no instructions from a master, if there are no commands coming from a master, then technically that means you''re free. So as the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ</span>, we need some instructions from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, right? Now the Arabic word for instructions is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِدَايَة</span>, which comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span>. So the next logical progression in this line of thought is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا اللَّهُ</span>, now that I have enslaved myself to You, now that I''m committed to You, now that I''m depending on Your assistance to fulfill my commitment, what is it that You want me to do? How can I pay off this debt of submission that I owe You? How can I move towards You? How can I get closer to You? Give me some instructions, give me some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span>. And so, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> progresses into . Guide us, instruct us towards the straight path.</p>
<p>Scholars point out something very important here in this sequence of declarations. So we just learned that is a commitment, right? It''s a declaration, it''s a promise, it''s our sincere as we call it. Right? That is followed by a call for assistance, which is then followed by asking for instructions. So if you think about the sequence here, we''re not saying give me the instructions first, if I think I''ll be able to do them, if they make sense to me, if I feel like, you know, I''m able to find the time to do them, then I will do them. No. All of that has already been covered in the earlier verses. Right? Starting from we are just making the commitment.</p>
<p>We''re making that promise of that we will do whatever you tell us to do, because that''s all part of being a slave. Right? You don''t become a slave and then say, you know, I''ll do it when it makes sense to me. The making sense of it was all done in the previous three verses. Those were the verses that make us realize that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is worthy of our . Those are the verses where we get convinced of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s mastery over us. Right? Those were the verses where we completed our homework, where we did all our research, they complete the theory portion of this whole message. Starting from now, it''s practical time, it''s all action.</p>
<p>And the reason this is important is because people ask this question, you know, why do we have to take ahead when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السُّلَيْمَان</span> is, when we take, when we make this commitment, people say that, we know, why do I have to make this promise, why is this so important? Because that is the action of commitment. You can think and ponder and plan all you want, but until you actually commit to something, you cannot produce a change. I mean, isn''t that true in every aspect of our lives? Weight loss, the biggest problem of our times.</p>
<p>We can read all about weight loss. In fact, according to a study, obese people are usually the ones who know the most about losing weight, how they need to lose the weight, because they''ve read up so much about it, they''ve watched so many videos on this. But until they commit to a lifestyle of good diet and a lifestyle of exercise, they cannot actually lose the weight, right? So you have to make a commitment to produce change.</p>
<p>And this is why we have I commit only to you and I seek only your assistance, and then we have guide us, give us instructions. So the more sincere we are in our commitment, the more we can expect <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to help us on our journey towards him. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, in the next session we will look at the remaining verses. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِ الطَّاهِرِينَ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span>.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/166/2f3e642f-eccf-48a3-8184-e170c2c0a613.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/166/1bd391c8-bf59-4171-9cfd-5c663dd0f904.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'worship and assistance';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ يَا اللَّهُ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَبَارِكْ وَسَلِّمْ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span></p>
<p>We''re studying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, and we''ve completed the first four verses: ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>. So now in this session we''re going to look at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>. This verse is commonly translated as &quot;guide us to the straight path,&quot; right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>, guide us to the straight path.</p>
<p>Now let''s look at what does that mean. Normally we think about it like, you know, so when I say to you, &quot;guide me to your house,&quot; what am I asking for? I''m asking for instructions, right? Directions. That''s what guidance essentially means. It means instructions or directions. Now, how are you going to give me these instructions? What are the different ways in which you can give me instructions or directions? So for instance, you can say, take this particular road and go three miles on it and then turn left, right? That is how typically you will give instructions for a particular destination. You''ll just list it out for the person. Another way you can do that, you can tell me, you can guide me by saying, you know what, just stand at this bus stop, and take this particular bus and then get off at this particular stop. So that''s another way you can guide me. This is guidance through the bus, right? It is guidance through a medium or through an intermediary.</p>
<p>So what you''re saying in the second mode of instructions is that you don''t have to do anything. The bus will do all the work for you. You''re telling me that all you have to do is make sure that you''re at the right bus stop at the right time and you catch the right bus, and then just let the bus get you to your destination. That''s the second way of giving instructions, through an intermediary or through a medium. The third way is that you can say, look, I''m headed home right now, why don''t you just jump in the car with me and I''ll take you all the way, right? Those are the three ways by which you can guide me or direct me to your house. First is through a set of instructions. Second is through a medium or an intermediary. And the third is by literally taking me to that destination yourself. It''s very important that you understand these three, the differences between these three modes of instructions.</p>
<p>Now, what is so cool about this verse, and listen to this carefully, is that asking for guidance through a set of instructions or through a set of directions in Arabic, it is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا إِلَى صِرَاطِ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا إِلَى صِرَاطِ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. Guide me through a set of instructions. Asking for guidance using a medium would be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا بِالصِّرَاطِ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. The letter ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ would be included with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاط</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا بِالصِّرَاطِ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. Asking for completely assisted guidance, which is the third form, in which you are literally holding my hand and taking me to the destination, that would be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا عَلَى صِرَاطِ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. You see?</p>
<p>Now, what''s interesting about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span> is that we are not asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> for instructions by saying, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا إِلَى صِرَاطِ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. We''re not asking Him for instructions by saying, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>. And we''re not saying, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا عَلَى صِرَاطِ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. We are not using any of those words. We are just simply saying .</p>
<p>You see that? We''re omitting the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>, we''re omitting the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, and we''re omitting the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span> in our request for our instructions. Now why is that relevant? Because according to the rules of Arabic linguistics, when you don''t use any of these words, what you actually mean is all of them at the same time. You see, the principle of Arabic is that if you are using none of these words, you''re linguistically using all of them, otherwise you would have to use these separate sentences.</p>
<p>So now when we say , what we''re really saying is, direct me to the straight path through your instructions, which is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. We''re saying, guide me to the straight path through your intermediaries, through the mediums, through the example of people like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> , <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِنَا عَلِي</span> , the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَإِيمَا</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>, and then finally, hold my hand and walk me along this path until I reach my destination. Grant me your constant companionship as I walk along this path. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. It''s amazing.</p>
<p>See, a school counselor gives us guidance, guides the students, right? That''s the job of a school counselor. His guidance is ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically information, right?</p>
<p>A traffic officer''s guidance is information. The GPS guiding you in your car is information, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s guidance is more than just information. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s guidance has to do with the personal choices that we have to make every single moment. So we''re not just asking for directions, we''re also asking Him for the strength and His assistance in helping us make those right decisions. These are two very different things. Because sometimes we can have all the right information and we can still make the wrong decisions.</p>
<p>Also, if you think about it, you know, guidance, what did we say guidance is? It''s just a set of instructions, right? How many times do we need information? One time. You know, if I''ve been given the right information, I don''t have to ask for it again. Once I know the direction to your house, that''s it. I''m not going to keep asking you again and again. But how many times do we ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> for guidance? Over and over again, as a standard, right? It''s like drinking water. You cannot say I drank water yesterday, so I''m never going to need it again. It doesn''t work that way. If you want to survive, you have to drink again and again, every single day.</p>
<p>![](images/167/7500cce9-7ea7-43ad-a8ea-d087e3‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎3830.jpg)</p>
<p>So just as our bodies become thirsty for water and food, our heart keeps starving for guidance. This is why we keep coming ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ck to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> in every single prayer with this request of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>.</p>
<p>Now let''s look at this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا</span>. So guidance we already talked about. But what''s really interesting is that if you look at the root word for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا</span>, it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span>. So in this context, the derivative is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span>, which means guidance, which I just explained to you. But what''s interesting is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span> is also the root word for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَدِيَّة</span>. Now what does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَدِيَّة</span> mean? It''s also used in Urdu. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَدِيَّة</span>, it means a gift. Now for an Arab living in the desert when he''s lost, right? Think about an Arab who lives in the desert and he got lost in the desert.</p>
<p>What is the greatest gift that you can give him? Even more than water. Because water is not going to be good enough. It will last him maybe one more day. Food is maybe going to last him a couple more days. So from an Arab''s perspective, especially an Arab who is lost in the desert, what is the greatest of all gift for him? Guidance, directions ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ck to his home, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ck to his family. That''s the ultimate gift. So we''re asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> not just for any gift, we''re asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> for the ultimate gift. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s guidance is the ultimate gift for a believer. So it becomes so much more than just instructions or informations, you see? It completely changes the perspective of a believer from just simply a set of rules.</p>
<p>It creates the sense of gratitude because instructions and directions are now perceived as a gift, a gesture of love, a gesture of care. That''s when you give a gift to somebody, right? You don''t give a gift to somebody you hate. You always give a gift to somebody you love. So now these instructions and this set of information of how to live our life, it no longer feels like this burden that we have to bear, but rather it feels like a gift. That is the perspective that changes when you understand the linguistics of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span>.</p>
<p>Now let''s look at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>. What is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>? So it is generally translated as a path, right? But in the Arabic language, there are certain rules that go with the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>. The first rule is that it can only be used for a straight path. Okay? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الفَاتِحَةِ</span>, it can only be used for a straight path. If a road is bumpy, or if it curves, or if the road is wavy, it cannot be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الفَاتِحَةِ</span>.</p>
<p>Right? So by definition, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الفَاتِحَةِ</span>, it means that the path has to be straight. So the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الفَاتِحَةِ</span>, right, just with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضُمَّ</span> at the top, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَة</span>, it is actually used for a perfectly straight sword. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَة</span>. If the sword is curved, it is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيْف</span>. You know some of these swords that we see in old movies? So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِنَا عَلِي</span>''s sword <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذُو الْفِقَار</span> is a good example of that, right? It''s, we know that it''s curved, right? The shape of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذُو الْفِقَار</span> was curved. So what, what is that, what did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> say? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا فَتَى إِلَّا عَلِيٌّ، لَا سَيْفَ إِلَّا ذُو الْفَقَارِ</span>. Not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا سُورَةَ إِلَّا ذُو الْفَقَار</span>, right? He said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا سَيْفَ إِلَّا ذُو الْفَقَارِ</span>, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَة</span> by definition is a curved sword, right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاط</span> means a perfectly straight path. That''s the first thing. The second rule is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span> can only be used for a wide path. By definition, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span> cannot be used for a narrow path.</p>
<p>It has to be a wide path. A path that allows multiple people to travel on it simultaneously. So in our world, just think of it like a major highway with multiple lanes, except that it''s just a perfectly straight highway. You understand? Now, this is something very cool. There are other words in Arabic that can be used for a path. There are various different ways. So, for example, there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبِيل</span>, which means a path. There is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>, which means a path. There is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَارِق</span>, which means a path. Now, what''s really interesting about these words is that there are plurals ava<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>ble for all these words.</p>
<p>So, for instance, for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبِيل</span>, there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبُل</span>. Right? The plural of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبِيل</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبُل</span>. For <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَارِق</span>, the plural is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَوَارِق</span>. For <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>, the plural is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِجَاج</span>. What was really interesting is that there is no plural for the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>. It is a singular word. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاط</span> also means the only ava<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>ble path. So imagine, just imagine these two islands that are connected by a single bridge, right? There is water under the bridge and the water between these two islands, it''s very turbulent. Think about it that way, right?</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/167/8d9fab28-3712-415b-982e-31adf5393271.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>You cannot take a boat to go from one side to the other or one island to the other. The only way for you to get from point A to point B in this case is this one single wide bridge. There is absolutely no other path for you to take except this bridge. You understand? So in this case where there is no alternate route ava<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>ble, then that bridge, that one singular wide path would be called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span> linguistically has these peculiar characteristics. It has to have, it has to be a wide path, it has to be a straight path, and it has to be the only path to that destination.</p>
<p>So now what does all of this mean for us? It means that there can be many, many paths ava<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>ble to us in terms of how we choose to live our lives. There can be many religions, there can be many belief systems, all of which can be correct. But the path that directly leads to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, it can only be one. It means that even though there are many different religions, many different philosophies, all explaining how to reach God, all explaining how to live your life, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الفَاتِحَةِ</span> indicates that there can only be one true religion, the perfect religion.</p>
<p>Think about it like that. The perfect religion. So there is good, there is better, that is best, that is perfect. If you start breaking it down in that way, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span> indicates the perfect religion. Right? It''s a religion, a path that will be the shortest distance between point A and point B, connecting the slave to his master.</p>
<p>Now, if this was not interesting enough, which obviously it is, what really blows my mind is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> did not just say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span> in this verse, he said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّرَاطَ</span>, right? He used the definite article, the. This, the path, the particular path. Now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span> already meant one single path by its definition, then why say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّرَاطَ</span>? Because even though <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is the one true religion, there are many, many sects within <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>, and by making this word a definite, by adding the definite article to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الفَاتِحَةِ</span>, making it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّرَاط</span>, what is being taught here, that there can only be one perfectly true sect within <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> itself. Isn''t that incredible? So just by understanding the linguistics of the word, it can answer all of our questions, you know, because people ask this all the time. I used to ask this question all the time, you know, why does there only have to be one true religion?</p>
<p>Why can''t every religion be a true religion? It can''t because the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> does not have a plural. So it can only be singular. It must be straight, which means it must take the shortest route to connect us with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Right, because the shortest distance between two points is a straight line. Also, it must be a wide path, which means there has to be acceptance for others along the way. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>, but then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّرَاطَ</span> means that there is only one sect, one lane that will, the fastest lane, right? Think about it on a highway, you have the slow lane, you have the fast lane, so this is the fast lane that will directly lead to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> using the shortest distance. It''s the most direct path to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ</span>. All of these meanings are encapsulated in the single word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّبْعُ</span>...</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّرَاطَ</span>.</p>
<p>And this is not to say that the rest of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> and all the other sects are all gar‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ge. No, of course not. All it means is that it''s going to take them longer to get there, but they will eventually get there. Everybody is going to eventually get there. And this is why all Muslims of all sects, regardless, right, they pray for this knowledge of this direct path to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, because all Muslims are praying and saying, including us, I mean, we just, you know, knowing the straight, we, so even if I say that is the, is the, is the fastest path, is knowing that path and actually walking the path are two different things. So that is why we''re also saying, we''re also constantly praying for, for, because if you think about it, if we, if we strongly believe, we truly believe in our hearts that we are on the right path, on the right, we are the right sect within the world of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>, then why do we have to constantly keep asking for this guidance? Why do we have to keep every prayer, why do we have to keep saying, we already are on the right path? But being on the right path and walking the path are two completely different things.</p>
<p>Just by standing on the path, I do not get to my destination. I have to walk the path, right? So there''s so many other nuances within this which I''m skipping over, but I''ve given you the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sics, and if you really think about it and if you really reflect on it, it''ll open up so many more, it''ll open up a world of understanding for you. Anyway, so that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّرَاةُ</span>.</p>
<p>Now the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَقِيمَ</span>, it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>, right? How is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَقِيمَ</span> translated generally? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>, guide us to the straight path. But we already established that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>, it already implies straight, right? Because if it wasn''t straight, it wouldn''t be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>. So now why is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> repeating Himself? He''s not. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَقِيمَ</span> does not just mean straight, it actually means upright.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَقِيمَ</span>, it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَامَ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقَامَ</span>, which means to stand up. Not so much to go forward, but to go upward, right? It''s from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامٌ</span>, which means to stand upright. So in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, in our prayers, you know, we have ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, we have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُكُوْر</span>, we have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُجُود</span>, right? So what does ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ mean? Means to stand up straight. That''s what ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ is. So when we say we are asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> for guidance on a path, which heads which way? Straight up it is. We''re asking guidance for a path of spiritual elevation.</p>
<p>Now think about this. What happens when I travel on a path that puts me on an elevation? The more I travel on it, the higher I will rise. Isn''t that true? Imagine this: a path that is just a physical path. Think of it as a ladder, like this ladder that is going from the ground all the way up to the sky.</p>
<p>As I''m climbing this ladder, what am I rising above? I''m rising above this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, isn''t it? And as I rise higher, who am I getting close to? I''m getting closer to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Now, what happens to the view as you rise higher? It becomes better, it becomes broader, right? You start getting a better perspective on things. We already established that walking this path is all about gaining knowledge. So the more we learn, the more knowledge we acquire, the more aware we become of the realities of this world and of the afterlife. This path of spiritual elevation helps mold and form our perspective.</p>
<p>But then there''s a negative side to this as well, because the higher you climb, the more danger there is of falling. So if you are, you know, one foot high and you fall off from a stool, you''ll be okay, right? If you''re 20 feet high and you fall, you''re not going to be so okay. If you''re 100 feet high and you fall, right? So in other words, the person who is traveling on this path for a long time, is he in less danger or is he in more danger?</p>
<p>He''s in more danger. And this is why you''ll see that the higher the rank of the person, the more <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُشُوعٌ</span> this person shows in his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, it just manifests, the more, because he has so much more fear in his heart, the, you know, the more <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُشُوع</span> he has, the more he cries on his prayer mat during <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>. And this is why we find the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> exercising extreme caution at every single step of their way. Not because they love to be miserable, which is the impression we get sometimes reading these books of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَة الْفَاتِحَة</span>. I mean, at least I used to think that way. I''m like, what is wrong with these people? Like, why? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has not asked us to be miserable in life. He has asked us to enjoy life and have, lead a good life and all of that. Then why are these people suffering or putting themselves through these hardships of constant prayer, constant fasting? Just, right? Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> never demanded that. Yet we find, if we read the books of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, we find these people just striving to the nth degree, and it never made sense to me until I understood the linguistics of this. Why did they do that?</p>
<p>They did that because of this caution and this superior perspective. You know, we look at them and we think that they are suffering. But if we had a chance to ask them, they would say that they were enjoying it, they were loving it, because they were excited about their destination. And so the work to get there no longer felt like work, you understand? So if you have a goal, if there is something that you want to achieve and you''re really crazy about achieving it, you''re really motivated about getting there, the effort to get there is actually enjoyable. You don''t really care about it. Think about a rocket. A rocket that is headed towards space.</p>
<p>That''s literally... it is on a path that is directed upward. Now, we know that this rocket, it needs a lot of fuel, it needs a lot of force, it needs a lot of energy to overcome the gravity of this Earth. But once it breaks through the gravitational field and it enters space, there is no effort required for any kind of movement. All it needs is a bit of thrust here and there, and it''ll just glide to wherever it wants to go. So that''s what the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> were aiming for. That''s why they put so much work, because then once they perfected it, once they rose above the perspective of this inferior <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, once they became aware of the unlimited and infinite space, the infinite possibilities of the solar system, g<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>xies, this universe, then this insignificant <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> became worthless to them.</p>
<p>So if you really grasp this concept, and if you start looking at it from this perspective, you know, you can really reconcile a lot of their behavior in this world. So we say... when we say it in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, when we pray, what we''re actually saying is: grant us the gift, the gift of guidance. Grant us the gift of true knowledge through your book, through the practical examples of your beloved and chosen people. And then, as we begin our spiritual journey towards you, following the footsteps of your chosen ones, accompany us every single step of the way.</p>
<p>![](images/167/e1618e1c-a163-4415-9af3-b9d008a48‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎0.jpg)</p>
<p>Support us, assist us as we walk through this path that is straight, that is wide, and that is the only path that elevates us towards You. Bring us into this fast lane. Help us move along this fast lane. Help us see things clearly as we rise above this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> and move towards You, and then protect us from falling ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ck into this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. This is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُنْ</span>, the assistance that we beg for daily in our five daily prayers.</p>
<p>Then we say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>, the path of those whom You bestowed Your favors upon. Now, what''s interesting is that this verse, it actually uses the past tense. Right? On the path of those on whom you bestowed your favours. So why the past tense? The reason is because all these role models, they exist in the past.</p>
<p>So you know, if you''re a student and you want to get some good career advice, who do you seek that advice from? Do you seek it from your fellow students or do you seek that advice from graduates? Obviously the graduates, right? You''re not going to ask your friend who is studying with you in the same school, in the same class, about career advice. So if you want to become a doctor, you''ll take advice from graduate doctors, you know. What course did you take? How did you get into this program? What are the things that I need to focus on?</p>
<p>So the verse says, the path of those on whom you showered your blessings, the path of the prophets, the path of the messengers, the path of the martyrs, all these righteous people, right? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْعَمْتَ</span> actually comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمْ</span>, which means something that is smooth and soft, right? There''s this word in Arabic for cattle, you know, cows and sheep. In Arabic they''re called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْعَمْتَ</span> because they walk very slowly, they walk in a gentle manner. So this verse is identifying and specifying the path that was requested in the previous verse. You know, when we said, oh <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, guide us on this path, towards this path of spiritual elevation, what is that path? It is the path of those who were successful in reaching you, those who got through the trials of this world in a soft and smooth manner and achieved spiritual elevation.</p>
<p>That is... then we say... not on the path of those who received your wrath, your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبٌ</span>, who became the recipients of your anger, and not on the path of those who are lost. Now this is really interesting, because when the verse talked about blessings, right, when it talked about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>, the verse reads, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>, the path on whom you showered your blessings, right? But when it came to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>, the ones who receive the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبٌ</span>. You see that? It leaves out the subject. It just says those who received your anger. Not those that you were angry upon, but those who received your anger. See, when I say he got beaten, the question that comes up is who did the beating? It could be one, it could be many, right?</p>
<p>So what this is telling us is that those who receive <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s anger, they''re not just receiving <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s anger, they''re receiving the anger of messengers, the anger of angels, the anger of other believers, the anger of the heavens, the earth, right? They''re receiving the wrath and the anger from all directions. And so this verse, it does not mention <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> when it talks about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَب</span>, but it specifically mentions <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> when it talks about blessings, the ones you showered blessings upon. But it is not those who you became angry with, because anger and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبٌ</span> was never the intention of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> to begin with, because He is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>. So whatever they did, they did it to themselves. You see, they earned it for themselves. But the ones who received blessings, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> helped them along the way towards the right path, you know, with the help of instructions, with the help of guidance, through messengers, through all of these intermediaries, and then also by helping them along the way with His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span>, with His knowledge.</p>
<p>So in other words, it means on the path of those who you granted blessings. And what is that blessing? We talked about this earlier, that''s knowledge. So essentially this is saying on the path of those that whom you granted knowledge, the knowledge of realities, and then the ones whom you granted the examples of how to put that knowledge into action through role models like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِي</span>, through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِمَامُ</span>, through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدُّعَاءُ</span>, and then that personal divine companionship along the way. All of that is encapsulated in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>. Not on the path of those whom you gave knowledge but they chose not to act upon it, and not on the path of those who act but they have no knowledge of what they''re doing. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضَالِّينَ</span> means the one who is lost, who does not know any better. And notice that the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِرَاطَ</span> is not used with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ</span> and it is not used with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضَالِّينَ</span>. You see, it''s only used with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>, because that is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>, the one that has the blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>Now, let me try to explain this difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضَالِّينَ</span> with a simple example. So I have two children, right? I have Eram and Amir. Eram is 13, Amir is 7. So, you know, they both love chocolate chip cookies, right? So I buy a fresh box from the store and, you know, I give that box to Eram and I say, both of them, I put that box in front of both of them and I say, look guys, I know this is your favorite cookie and, you know, can you see how nice this is and how fresh it looks? Right here, smell it, smell it, right? So I put that in front of them and then I tell them, look, all of this stuff is so cool, but you cannot have them. I''m going to place this right here on the table right in front of you. And then I''m going to go downstairs in my ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sement to record this session on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>. And when I come ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ck, we will eat these together because I brought it for all of us to share together. But until I return, don''t touch these cookies. I''m telling Eram this, right, because she''s the older one.</p>
<p>And I''m also telling her, make sure Amir does not eat them as well, because he''s still a kid. Right? So I explain all of this to Eram and then I go in the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sement, I do my session, right? And then I come ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ck upstairs and what do I find? I find these two cookie monsters sitting on the couch, right? They''re both just wolfing all of these cookies down. Now, who am I going to get mad at? Eram or Amir? Obviously Eram. Why? Because I explicitly, very clearly told her not to eat those cookies. I did not tell Amir.</p>
<p>He doesn''t know what''s going on, right? But I specifically told Eram not to eat the cookies. So obviously, I''m going to be a lot more angry with her because, you know, she knew better. I''ll tell her that, I''ll say, I clearly told you not to eat these cookies and you still disobeyed me. Right? And while I''m shouting at her and scolding her, Amir is looking at me innocently and he''s still like eating these cookies. But you understand the difference, right? The difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضَالِّينَ</span> is that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْضُوب</span> are the ones who were given the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَة</span> of knowledge. They were shown the right path and they still chose to disobey <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. They still chose to do the wrong thing. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضَالِّينَ</span> are the ones who had no clue that they were doing the wrong thing. It doesn''t mean that they''re innocent.</p>
<p>It doesn''t mean that they will not be taken to account, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> gave all of us intellects, right? Which is a proof and a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُجَّة</span> for and against each and every one of us. Right? It is a proof that we need to seek knowledge. So, &quot;Oh, I just didn''t know&quot; is not a good enough excuse, because that''s what the intellect was given to you for, so that you would gain knowledge. So the are the people who should have strived to gain the necessary knowledge of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and then do the right thing. They should have made that effort to know better. So they''re not off the hook by any sense, but they''re still better than . which are the people who absolutely knew what the right thing to do was and they still chose not to do it. So that''s the difference between these two terms. So this is the real message of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>, this b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>nce between knowledge and action.</p>
<p>Right? So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ مَالِكِ يَوْمِ الدِّينِ</span>. These verses are all about knowledge. These are all about seeking knowledge of our Master, getting to know Him, getting to understand Him, understand what He wants, why He created us, what does He want from us? What''s this concept of mastery? What''s this concept of slavery? And what are the advantage of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span>? And how can we achieve freedom through this? And this idea that we are the ones in need of Him, because He has something that He can give to us that we want, rather than Him wanting us to do something. So that is all encapsulated in these verses. And then we have . This is our recognition and our acceptance of this knowledge being the one true knowledge.</p>
<p>You know, and us making a commitment to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, and after that we have: guide us to the only straight and wide path of knowledge that elevates us and brings us closer towards You. Right? Guide us to the path of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, give us the ultimate gift of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> through the path of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>, which is Your ultimate blessing. This path that is this religion that is the perfect b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>nce between knowledge and action. So guide us along this path through the examples of role models of the people of the past that You have favored, the ones who were wise, who had wisdom. You remember the definition of wisdom: to have correct knowledge and then to act upon it. That''s a wise person, who has the knowledge and then acts upon it. So make us among those people, of these role models of the past, that when they were provided knowledge they acted upon it. Do not make us among those who gain knowledge but then do not act upon that knowledge, because those are the people who will receive Your wrath from all directions on the Day of Judgment, because of the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎d decisions that they made. And do not make us from among those who act without knowledge, because they are the ones who are lost. So the essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> is this b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>nce between these two things. It is b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>ncing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْد</span> with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَلٌ</span>, knowledge with action. It is, you know, b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>ncing, it is the b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>nce between gaining the knowledge of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, right, striving to acquire the knowledge of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, making a commitment, this promise, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَهْدِ</span>, which has to come before the action, and only after that comes the instructions, right, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِدَايَةٌ</span>.</p>
<p>And then we ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> to make us among those who created this b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>nce between knowledge and action. And this is why Maulana Ali says knowledge without action is a c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>mity on the soul, and action without knowledge is pure ignorance, it''s perversion. So success, it lies in the middle. It lies at the center of this b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>nce between knowledge and action. That is the true manifestation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبْهُدِيَّة</span>. A slave who obeys through knowledge, who strives to understand the wisdom of his master''s commands and then practically acts upon it, realizing that this is for his own benefit. And that is the entire essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ، وَالْآلِ وَالْوَصِيِّ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ، وَإِلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِ الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ، وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'guidance to the straight path';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span> So now that we''ve completed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> I wanted to do a session on this idea of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span> which is remaining steadfast on the straight path and this is one of the major challenges of walking the path and it is something that we all probably can relate to we all as Muslims we want to do the right thing we want the straight path but we''re not always able to for various different reasons and then we end up getting frustrated with ourselves and sometimes with religion itself. Now this is something that I have personally struggled with extensively in my life. But then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> I came across some material and some perspectives that has helped me a lot. And so I thought I would like to share that with you hoping and praying that it will do the same for you. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> we request <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> for guidance we say guide us towards the straight path now this verse is the essence of the Surah the initial verses they are basically building up to this one right giving us an understanding of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ مَالِكِ يَوْمِ الدِّينِ</span> but this is it is called the prayer of all prayers. Guide us, guidance. Guide us through instructions, role models and then through your divine companionship. Now the question is what exactly is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> actually defines it for us in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ يَاسِين</span>.</p>
<p>where he says and no doubt about it you should worship me for that is the straight path so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span> the willing submission is how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> defines the straight path The person who willingly submits himself or herself to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the one who has found the straight path, which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> we all have. But now the big question is how do we walk the path? Because we never get to our destination by just finding the correct path. We get to it by actually walking it, right? So how do we walk this path? And what is the term? Is there a term for walking this path? There is. And that term is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, which is a strong mindfulness for the presence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in our lives. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, commonly translated as piety, is essential for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو دِيَّة</span>, for attaining the ranks of servitude. And that is exactly what we asked for when we said, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎ It is only you we worship Again, worship being the straight path.</p>
<p>And it is only you we seek help from. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اسْتَعَانَ</span>, right? What is this method of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اسْتَعَانَ</span>? Seeking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s help? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> explains that in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْبَقَرَةِ</span>. He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَسْتَعِينُ بِالصَّبْرِ وَالصَّلَاةِ</span> and seek my help through patience and prayer and the same word is used <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْتَعِينُ</span> so we have now just solved this equation right we all desire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span> the straight path <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَقِيم</span> as I explained earlier a path that has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقَامَ</span> built into it right a path of spiritual elevation How is that achieved? Through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span>, willing servitude to the will of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. How do we walk this path? By developing piety, by developing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, an acute awareness of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is, by establishing a connection with Him. And how do we connect with Him?</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/168/afef5a3f-8851-4eea-9ec8-f3a9bdc1d0ee.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِالصَّبْرِ وَالصَّلَاةِ</span>, through patience and through prayer. Now we all know about the daily prayers. But what does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْر</span> mean here in this context? It means to be steadfast and consistent with our prayers. And then the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتٌ</span> continues to say, And no doubt about it, for sure, this is truly an extremely difficult task for most people. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَّا</span>, except, those who possess the knowledge and who possess the knowledge and the awareness of the majesty of their master. People who have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. So it all ultimately boils down to consistency and being steadfast, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقَامَة</span>. Now what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقَامَة</span>? It comes from the same root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَا</span> That was used for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَقِيم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطُ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>, the same root word, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَامَ</span>.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقِمْ</span> means to stay firm on something. And how do we stay firm? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> answers that in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> when He says, . He says, establish the prayer, be firm, stay firm on your prayer, stay firm on your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span> is to establish something firmly or to be solid in something, to be firm in it. Right? So resilience is one of the words that is used. Grit, some people use the word grit. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span> is essentially the ability to stand firm and be consistent in our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, in our worship and our belief. To give you sort of a visual representation from the physical world of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>, think about a solid tree. A solid tree, it stands firm even when the wind blows, right? Even if a storm hits it, it stays its ground.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/168/59d175a9-8e91-4862-8d53-491dab98e134.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>Now, the question we''re trying to address is, how can we be like that? How do we develop the ability to stay strong on this path despite the storms of our situations, of our challenges, of our environments? So you must have noticed if you read the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> that in many verses <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, He always mentions two things when He talks about faith. He always couples <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَلْ</span>. He couples faith with action. So we understand that there are two parts to what we are supposed to do. There is the inner part and then there''s the outer part. What''s the inner part? It''s the belief, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, the faith. Another way we can think about this in terms of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is that there is a worship for the heart and then there is a worship for the limbs. The body, the tongue, the ears, the eyes. You understand?</p>
<p>There are the internal <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَات</span> and then there are the external <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَات</span>. The internal has to do with our belief system, with our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>. How do we think of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>? How do we view Him? How do we respond internally with the things that He has asked us to do? And why He has asked us to do those. That''s all internal. It has to do with our certainty or with our doubt, right? So certainty is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَقِين</span>, doubt is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَقَّ</span>. So we are either certain about it or we have doubts about it. But that all has to do with our internal mechanism, our internal worship system. What are the external <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَات</span>?</p>
<p>Those are the obvious ones, the five time prayers, reading <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, you know, guarding our ears, eyes, all of that, you know, these are all external type of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَات</span>. Now, if I asked you, what do you think is essential for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>? Is it the internal or the external? What would you say is essential for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>? Our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> in a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span>, he stated, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ فِي الْجَسَدِ مُضْغَةً، إِذَا صَلُحَتْ صَلُحَ الْجَسَدُ كُلُّهُ، وَإِذَا فَسَدَتْ فَسَدَ الْجَسَدُ كُلُّهُ، أَلَا وَهِيَ الْقَلْبُ</span> Indeed, within the body, there is a lump of flesh. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا صَلُحَتْ صَلَحَ الْجَسَدُ كُلُّهُ</span> If it is set right, if it is set straight, then the entire body will be set right.</p>
<p>But if it is corrupted, then the entire body will be corrupted. Indeed, beware it is the heart. So what we''re learning from this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span> is that the master of the body isn''t actually the mind. Which sounds a bit strange because many people believe that the mind is what is in control. Well, news flash. It''s actually your heart that is in control. It''s your heart that controls your mind. Now what do I mean by that? The type of heart that you have will control your thoughts. It''ll control your mind and eventually it''ll control your actions. So whatever your heart truly desires, your mind will think of ways to fulfill those desires. And then the limbs will actually make it happen.</p>
<p>They will execute it. You see? So the heart is the king. What the king wants needs to happen. Who helps the king figure out how to make it happen? That''s the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَزِير</span>, that''s the mind, right? That''s the brain. And then there is the army, which is the limbs that actually carry out and execute what the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَزِير</span> comes up with for the king. So the mind actually becomes a tool, an instrument to execute whatever is in the heart. And this is why our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he said if you take care of this lump of flesh, which is the heart. Now everyone be clear that when I say the heart, I''m not talking about the physical heart, right? I''m talking about the spiritual heart, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَلْب</span>. So if this heart is purified, when you take care of that heart, when you feed the heart, when you rectify the heart, then all your actions will automatically become pure. But when the heart is corrupted, when it is starved, when it''s not taken care of, when it is deprived of its spiritual sustenance, then the rest of the actions will just naturally get corrupted. And what''s really interesting, that if you look at, like, if you want to see this theory in its practical form, let''s look at the chronological theme of revelation. Just think about how the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> was revealed.</p>
<p>What were the first verses to be revealed? They were not about drinking alcohol and they were not about preventing people from committing fornication. They were not about the rules of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>. The first verses to be revealed were all about building <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. They were all about building an attachment with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, about understanding the Day of Judgment, realizing and learning that we will be taken to account for our actions, that we''re not just living in this life without a purpose like animals, that there is a purpose to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيدٌ</span>, there is a purpose to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s oneness. So the initial verses of revelation were about, you know, the Day of Judgment, they were about retribution, they were about loving <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, they were about understanding what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has done for us, it was about understanding what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> wants from us, about life and death, about, you know, basically who He is and what He wants. Why? Because all those initial verses of revelation, they were building a foundation for us, they were setting the foundation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>.</p>
<p>So now if we go back to the example of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاة</span> tree, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> says in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ إِبْرَاهِيمَ</span>, when He gives the example of a good word, He says that a good word is like a good tree. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ تَرَ كَيْفَ ضَرَبَ ٱللَّهُ مَثَلًۭا كَلِمَةًۭ طَيِّبَةًۭ كَشَجَرَةٍۢ طَيِّبَةٍ أَصْلُهَا ثَابِتٌۭ وَفَرْعُهَا فِى ٱلسَّمَآءِ</span> ‎. Do you not see how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> sets forth the example of a good word, which is like a fruitful tree? Its roots are firmly fixed into the earth and its branches rise up to the sky. So in other words, its foundation is solid. That''s what the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> uses. And then its branches rise up towards the sky. See, you can become elevated. Right, you can grow, you can get higher, you can force yourself into a path of spiritual elevation, but if you don''t have solid roots, it''s not gonna last long, and this is what happens with so many people.</p>
<p>And this is something that I have struggled with myself, so I can actually speak from experience, that I can, I have forced myself into the prayers, fasting and this and that, and I do it for a while and I push myself and I push myself and I just grit my teeth and I do it, but doesn''t last. Something happens and suddenly everything goes out the window. And this was happening for a while until I finally understood these things, because what I realized is that the taller a tree gets, the deeper its roots have to go. So if you study the way <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has designed a tree, you''ll see that the taller it is, the more deep and wide its roots are spread within the earth. In fact, even mountains we know have foundations, and the taller it is, the more it is grounded within the earth. Everything, anything in existence, in order for it to be solid and firm and to have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>, it must have a solid foundation.</p>
<p>And so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is telling us, and even in the training that He gives us, He did not start out with the rules, He started out with building our foundation. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, which is essentially the summary of the entire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, right? It''s a description of what you are about to find in the remaining 113 <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>. It begins, look how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> begins, by creating an understanding of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ، مَالِكِ يَوْمِ الدِّينِ</span>. These are all verses about knowing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, about setting this foundation. And once this foundation is built, which will naturally engender <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَكُّل</span>, which is, you know, our reliance and trust in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, only then we are taught to ask for the rules and guidance. But look at the order of these verses, right? The beginning verses are designed to build a solid foundation.</p>
<p>So that when the rules are revealed, we can just have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>. You understand? If we look at the example of the tree again, when a wind blows, if the roots of the tree are deep and they are firm within the ground, what happens to the tree? Well, its leaves might move, the branches might shake and break, but the tree stays standing. Right? It''s solid. But what happens to a tree that does not have deep roots? It easily gets knocked over. And I believe that the problem with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span>ic training these days is that we are raised in such a way that we just don''t have solid roots. Our training method is the rule shaming method. It''s this rule oriented or rule shaming method. This is the method where you grow up as a kid and the only thing you are really taught about is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> and hellfire.</p>
<p>And the emphasis is on if you do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, you will go to hellfire. And that''s how many of us were raised and that''s how many of us are raising our children. Well, it is true that there are things that are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>. It is true that there is hellfire. It is true that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has anger and wrath. This is all true. But why are children being taught that above His mercy? Why are we not teaching our children that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the most merciful? And that even if you make a mistake, if you make a mistake and you repent, you can always try again. Why is this not the message that is being taught? And the answer, unfortunately, is because this is to control the behavior of the child. So many of us use fear as this control mechanism.</p>
<p>The only problem is that this might have worked maybe somewhat in my generation, more so in the previous generation, but in the new generation, it just backfires. When we''re using false fear or false shame, unhealthy shame to control someone''s behavior, we''re not building solid roots. What we''re building is avoidance. Imagine that you grew up in a household where you''re always being told, let''s take police as an example, right? You''re always being told that you better watch out for the police. They''re going to throw you in jail. Don''t make a mistake. You better not do this. You better not do that because, you know, the police is going to catch you and they''re going to throw you in jail. They''re going to punish you. So you are basically taught to be afraid of the police.</p>
<p>Why? Because police is made out to be this very wrathful and scary entity. Now, if you grow up with this kind of training, years and years of this kind of material being fed to you, best case scenario, you will learn to avoid the police. That''s the best case scenario. But will you want to be close to the police? Will you want to be best friends with the police? Will you confide your problems to the police? Will you turn to the police for help when you''re in trouble? No, because you don''t trust the police and you''ve been told that they''re just there to punish you. You see? And so what''s happening is that this type of training is not building <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقَامَ</span>. It''s not building any form of stability in our youth and in our own selves.</p>
<p>It''s building avoidance. So many people, when they are raised this way, when they grow up, they don''t want to have anything to do with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. They actually want to avoid <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and religion completely. Because they say, you know what, everything is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, no matter what I do, I''m going to end up going to hell anyway. Everything I do is wrong, so what''s the point in trying? You know, and to make it worse in our teachings and these, you know, there are some, I''ve heard someone say that, you know, if you don''t do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَمَاز</span> this way and if you don''t do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُكوع</span> this way and such that your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَمَاز</span> will be thrown in your face. I mean, this is, you know, a child making an effort to actually stand up for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَمَاز</span>. And then you tell him that, okay, so everything I do is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> and I''m going to get punished for it, but the good things that I do are not good enough. You tell me, why should anyone come to a religion like that?</p>
<p>So what happens is that everybody starts avoiding religion and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> together, all together, because it''s backfiring. And the reason it''s backfiring is because we are trying to grow this tree of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span> within ourselves and our children without creating any solid foundation. So even miraculously if this tree sustains long enough to grow, it then is not able to survive the smallest earthquake, you see? It cannot stand the smallest storm. So we need to focus on building a solid foundation so that when life hits us, because we are going to be shaken in life, there''s no doubt about that, I mean, look at the times we live in with all these temptations around us, these desires going crazy, right, the tree, this tree of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span> within our hearts, it will be shaken from time to time. We will be tested through trials.</p>
<p>That''s not something that should come as a surprise to anyone, right? That is something that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> clearly says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. Do you really think that you will enter paradise without going through that which those who came before you went through? They were struck with calamity, they were struck with adversity, they struggled, and then they were shaken. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُلْزِلُوا</span> in Arabic, it actually comes from the same root as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِلْزَال</span>, which means an earthquake. So they were shaken like an earthquake, and they were tried so much and they were shaken so much that even the messengers and those with the messengers, they started asking, when will the help of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> arrive? Indeed, the help of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is near. The help of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> isn''t just there. It doesn''t just exist as a concept. It''s there right now with you as you''re going through the test. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says that we are closer to you than your jugular vein. And what''s interesting about that, you know, a lot of people don''t understand what that means. That''s the verse.</p>
<p>What does that mean? It means that we are closer to you than your own consciousness. See, jugular vein, you cut the jugular vein, what''s the first thing that the person loses? Consciousness, right? So what that means is that we know what you are about to think before you think it. It''s that kind of proximity. So we are going to be shaken from time to time. Now, how does a building withstand an earthquake? Again, the answer all goes back to the type of foundation it has. That is what will determine its <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>. So the question we have to ask ourselves is what type of foundation do we have with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>?</p>
<p>When I am tested through pain or through pleasure, will I be able to withstand it? Will I be able to be firm on the straight path? And if the honest answer to that is no, that means I need to work on my relationship with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, not work on the rules. Not try to increase my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span> or my fasting, and I need to first work on my relationship. The rules, the actions will just follow. And this is why scholars have said that the first verses that were revealed, they did not say give up alcohol and give up fornication, because people would have said, you know, we''ll never give up those things, and they would never have if it started with that, because they had not yet built a foundation with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. They had not yet built the roots. So if you come to a person from the very start and you give them rules, they''re just not going to follow it, because it goes against what they want. And that is the very mistake that many of us make with ourselves and with our children. I made that with myself. So we''re not building a foundation, we''re constantly throwing rules at them. We''re not talking about building an attachment with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, we''re not teaching them to love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, we''re not teaching them what it really means to put our reliance on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Where is that being taught? The only thing being taught to our children is that, you know, this is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> to eat this, it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> to drink that, it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> to dress like that, it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> to say this. I mean, that''s it, right?</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/168/dbd071d9-0c31-4c3e-9250-c83d94f7a905.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>And the problem with that method is that everything about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span> is being rejected. And that is exactly what the scholars said, right? That if you start off with the rules, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had started off with the rules, even with the companions, these were the best people that existed, but even they would have rejected the rules. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> did not start with the rules. I mean, look at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُزْءُ عَمَّ</span>, right, the 30th chapter of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, it''s basically Meccan, majority of the verses in that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُزْء</span> were revealed in the initial stages of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span>, in the first 10 years of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span>. Where do you find rules in that? Do you find any rules? Not really. It talks about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, it talks about His love, His mercy, it talks about the Day of Judgment, it talks about human beings, it talks about their relationships with themselves, among themselves, with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, it talks about, you know, human psychology, it talks about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, it talks about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَكُّل</span>, it talks about retribution, it teaches about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>, it teaches about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَهَنَّم</span>, right, it focuses, you know, on all these things that are relationship building, it''s all foundation building stuff in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُزْءُ عَمَّ</span>. Because if you want <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>, you have to build a foundation in the love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>See, this brings me to an interesting point about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, that when we generally talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, we think in terms of shame and fear. Right? And you might say, well, that is a good thing, isn''t it? I mean, what''s wrong with fear? Well, interestingly, today, psychologists, they are telling us that there is healthy fear and then there is unhealthy fear.</p>
<p>Okay, there is healthy fear and then there is unhealthy fear. Let me give you an example. You know when you do something really bad and you''re really scared of your parents or your loved ones finding out? Sometimes what do we do? Do you run to the parent or your loved one or do you run away from them? You run away from them and you hide, right? So there''s basically two ways to respond when you hurt someone or when you make a mistake. So imagine that you just hurt your closest friend or you hurt your spouse. There are some people who feel so bad about it, so ashamed about it, what they do is that they hide. They try to avoid the person. They try to hide from the issue. They try to avoid the conversation.</p>
<p>They distance themselves from the person, right? Now is that a healthy way to deal with it? Does that actually solve the problem or does it make it worse? It makes it worse. And this is exactly what we do with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. When many of us feel like we''ve made a mistake and we''re so ashamed, instead of going towards <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, we run away from Him. We try to hide. So we don''t want to pray anymore because, you know, we feel so ashamed. And we don''t want to talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, we don''t want to talk about religion, because it makes us feel ashamed. But then what''s a healthier way to deal with this? When you''ve hurt someone, what''s the healthiest way to fix it? To go to the person and what? Apologize. To go to the person and say, I''m sorry, I regret it. I admit it. I made a mistake. I''m going to try to do better.</p>
<p>Give me another chance. I will try to do better. And if I fail, I will try again. And I will try again. And I will try again. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْتَغْفِرْ</span>. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>. That is what we''re supposed to do. Because that is healthy fear. That is the healthy response when you make a mistake. That instead of trying to hide from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, instead of saying, you know, I just don''t want to do anything with religion, I want nothing to do with God, I''m not even gonna try, I''m not gonna pray because, you know, I''ve committed so many sins, you know, I just feel so ashamed to face <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That''s an unhealthy type of shame. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> is not unhealthy fear. It is not unhealthy shame.</p>
<p>It is healthy fear. It is the kind of healthy shame that will help you try to stay away from what displeases or what angers <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And when you do what angers <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you will rush to apologize and you will rush to repent. You will try to make things better.</p>
<p>And the reason I''m mentioning all of this is because I feel what''s happening is that we are just feeding the wrong kind of fear and shame. Because, you know, when you talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> or you think about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> like a scary policeman, I mean, you''re encouraging yourself, you''re encouraging your children and the people around you to actually avoid <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. You''re subconsciously teaching them to run away from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, not to run towards Him, not to apologize, to fix it. So healthy fear will motivate us to get closer to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and repent. Unhealthy shame will make us run away from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and it''ll make us avoid Him out of shame. So in order for us to have this ability to stay firm on our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, to have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقَامَ</span> on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>, we all need to learn to develop this healthy fear of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, which is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. Now how do we do that? How do we build healthy fear?</p>
<p>You know what the seed of healthy fear is? What the seed of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> is? This is interesting. Actually, let me put it this way. Let''s back up. What''s the seed to unhealthy shame? We know that a seed is the essence, right? It''s the seed that grows into a tree. But it all starts at the seed level. So if you want to address something, we should always look at the root cause, right? The seed. Now, if you look at the seed of unhealthy shame, you know what it is?</p>
<p>It''s thinking that you are supposed to be perfect. It''s this myth of perfection that lies at the root of our problems. It is when you''re taught and you start believing that you''re actually supposed to be perfect. And if you believe that, then you will react with unhealthy shame when you make a mistake. Why? Because you feel like I was supposed to be perfect. And I''m not perfect and I''m so ashamed of myself, I''m not even going to try anymore because this is too hard. So what that does is it leads us to despair. But what was the root cause? What was the seed? That you thought that you were supposed to be perfect. Because that''s what you were taught by your family, your teachers, your culture, whatever.</p>
<p>Now let''s go to the root or the seed of healthy <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> and healthy <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْتَغْفِرُ</span> and healthy <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>, you know, going back to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Do you know what the root is? Knowing, understanding that all human beings will make mistakes. It''s actually the opposite. It is realizing that the human being is imperfect. Understanding that we have been created as imperfect human beings. When you know that, when you understand that, then you can easily respond with repentance, because you know that you''re human and so you messed up. Okay, I messed up. I screwed up. I screwed up big time. But I''m gonna fix it. I''m gonna try and repent. I''ll try again. I''ll try harder. Even if I make the same mistake over and over and over again and I keep repenting over and over and over again, I will not give up trying to do better. That is the essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>. Imperfect human beings trying to take one tiny step towards perfection. That''s what we are trying. Trying to make tomorrow better than yesterday.</p>
<p>That''s what we are supposed to do. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he said, all children of Adam will make mistakes, but the best of them are the ones who repent. So we have to let go of this crazy myth of perfection, because it''s the root cause of so many of our problems. It is the reason why we keep, you know, falling into despair. Because if you''re told you''re supposed to be perfect and you end up realizing that you''re not perfect, or you cannot be perfect, or it''s just too difficult to be perfect, that is frustrating, that is irritating. But when you understand that we are created with this, you know, this design of being imperfect. How can you go against the divine design? Tell me that. Just tell me that. How can you go against the divine design? How can anyone do that? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says that He created us imperfect. Look at the descriptions of human beings in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>.</p>
<p>I mean, seriously. This, like, you know, clay, pottery clay. We created man weak. We made him impatient. Indeed, man has been created anxious. Look at, I mean, these are some descriptions of human being in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. Weak, built from filthy liquid, hasty, impatient, anxious. So how can we go against that? It''s impossible. And when you try to go against that, you will end up hitting with despair. But then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, he balances out our weakness by telling us, oh my servants, my servants, my beloved servants. What type of servants? Who have transgressed completely against their soul by their constant sins?</p>
<p>Do not despair of the mercy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. You know, when we commit sins, okay, everybody get this straight, when we commit sins, when we do something that has been forbidden, we are not hurting <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Just be very clear about that. When we do something good, we''re not doing any favors to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. When we do something bad, we''re not hurting <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We''re only hurting ourselves, because we cannot wrong <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, right? We cannot hurt <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We cannot take anything away from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. His kingdom is infinite. Everybody be very clear about that. And by doing anything good, we''re not doing him any kind of favors. So, you know, by doing, when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> forbids us from something, it''s for our own benefit. It''s for the benefit of our spirituality. It''s for us to move along the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُرَاطُ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. So by doing wrong, by committing what is forbidden, we are only hurting our own souls. So be very clear about that. You know, it''s like if you drink poison, are you hurting yourself or the doctor? The doctor is telling you don''t drink poison because the doctor understands that what you''re drinking is poison. You''re drinking it because it tastes good. It feels good. It makes you high. But if you drink that poison, are you essentially hurting yourself or the doctor? If you refuse then to take the medicine, the antidote for that poison, are you hurting yourself or are you hurting the doctor? You''re hurting yourself.</p>
<p>So just realize that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not the one that we are wronging by committing a sin. We are hurting none other than our own souls. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, tell my slaves, my beloved slaves, the ones that I have created with love and compassion, and these people who are hurting themselves by doing wrong, who are transgressing against their own selves, who are transgressing against their own souls, do not despair in the mercy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is telling us not to despair, but many of us, we despair, because we have this wrong concept about ourselves and this wrong concept about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Our egos just cannot bear the fact that we are not perfect. So we think of ourselves as, you know, that I''m supposed to be perfect, I better be perfect, and if I''m not perfect, then I''m worthless. So we all have this, you know, this all or none mindset. I mean, I either have to be an angel, otherwise I am going to be the devil.</p>
<p>This is something I thought only I used to struggle with, but I found out that this is very common, because I used to always think like that. There''s no way I can be good, there''s no way I can be an angel, but yeah, I can be a devil, that comes easier to me, so I''m just going to go that way. In my head it was all black or white. But we have to stop that. It changed my life when I stopped thinking like that. You know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنِي آدَمَ</span> is not one or the other. The children of Adam, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنِي آدَمَ</span>, were not angels and were not a devil.</p>
<p>A believer is actually someone who is flawed, contrary to everything that we''ve been taught so far. A believer is actually someone who is flawed but who continually strives to repent, and who tries really hard to get it right, who doesn''t give up, who constantly strives to get back up after he falls, who never gives up. That is a believer, that is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span>, not somebody who is flawless. This is why the Prophet said that all children of Adam will make mistakes, but the best are those who repent.</p>
<p>Letting go of this false sense of perfection will allow us this ability to get back up when we fall. And you want to hear something else that''s really counterintuitive? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> feeds off this false sense of perfection. I mean, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> really does a number with it. He uses it as a weapon against us. You know how? When you make a mistake or when I make a mistake, he''ll come to us and he''ll say, look at you. How shameful is you? You''re gonna pray now after you did this? What kind of a terrible person are you? Do you not have any shame? Look what a hypocrite you are.</p>
<p>Just look at you. Now you''re gonna stand before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> after doing this. He''s not even gonna look at you. Who would look at you? Would you look at somebody else who did that to you? Would you be merciful on somebody who did something like that to you? Of course not. So then if you wouldn''t be merciful towards somebody like that, then why do you expect <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to be merciful to you? You don''t deserve His mercy. You don''t deserve His love. Does that sound familiar? These kind of thoughts?</p>
<p>This is how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> uses this tool of false perfection against us. He''ll put us through this unhealthy shame. He''ll drag our face through this unhealthy fear and shame, and then when we''re done shaming ourselves and bashing ourselves up, then he''ll come to us from a different angle and he''ll say, do you really want to believe in a God who puts you through this mental torture? I mean, think about it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created you as imperfect human being and He''s now demanding perfection of you. What kind of a horrible master is this? I mean, what kind of ridiculous religion is this? Look at the contradiction in it. Just leave it, right? Look how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> works, right? He''s the one who put us through this unhealthy shame, then he comes at us from a different angle. How could <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> do this to you?</p>
<p>How could He put that alcohol in your presence? Didn''t He know that you''re weak in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>? He says that He knows everything. He says He''s closer than your jugular vein. He knew that you would drink that alcohol. Why did He put it in your presence? Whose fault is it? Why would He do that to you? It''s not your fault you drank that alcohol. It''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s fault, clearly. Look at that. It''s just logic. I mean, look at this religion. How stupid is that, you see? And you know what we do? We say, you know what, that makes perfect logical sense. If really <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you know, He should have known better, so you know what, screw religion. But if we realize that we are not perfect and that we were never created to be perfect, then when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> comes to us in these ways, and he gives us all these brilliant ideas, we say, you know what? Thank you for that. Yes, I know I made a mistake, but my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is Most Merciful.</p>
<p>My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not like me. My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, I may not forgive another person if he does something wrong to me. I may not forgive this other person if he does it again. I will definitely not forgive him if he does it again and again and again, the same thing. But I am not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. But my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not like me. My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is way more merciful than I can ever be, than all of humanity combined can ever be. Because my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is, He is the Most Forgiving. He will forgive me if I try again, and even if I fail after that, and if I repent again, He will forgive me again. But I will keep on trying until I get it right. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> wants, us to have hope in His mercy.</p>
<p>There''s this beautiful <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> in which, it''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُدْسِيّ</span>, in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is talking to us through the Prophet, and He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا ابْنَ آدَمَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, O son of Adam, as long as you call upon Me and ask of Me, I shall forgive you for what you have done and I will not even care about it. I won''t even care what you did. That''s how easily I will forgive you. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا ابْنَ آدَمَ، لَوْ بَلَغَتْ ذُنُوبُكَ عَنَانَ السَّمَاءِ ثُمَّ اسْتَغْفَرْتَنِي غَفَرْتُ لَكَ</span>. O son of Adam, were your sins so big, so many, to reach the clouds of the sky, and were you to then ask forgiveness of Me, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَرْتُ لَك</span>, I would instantly forgive you.</p>
<p>I don''t have time to get into the linguistics of this, but it''s so beautiful. Instantly forgive you. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا ابْنَ آدَمَ، إِنَّكَ لَوْ أَتَيْتَنِي بِقُرَابِ الْأَرْضِ خَطَايَا ثُمَّ لَقِيتَنِي لَا تُشْرِكُ بِي شَيْئًا لَأَتَيْتُكَ بِقُرَابِهَا مَغْفِرَةً</span>. O son of Adam, were you to come to Me with sins as great as this earth, and were you then to face Me, ascribing no partnership with Me, I would bring you forgiveness as great as your sin is. That''s who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is. I mean, according to this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says that if someone goes to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> with sins that fills the area between the heavens and the earth, I mean, do you know how much that area is? I mean, think about it, the area between the earth and the sky filled with sins. Who has that many sins?</p>
<p>That''s a' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'staying steadfast on the path';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || ' lot of sins, right? That''s a lot. And what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is saying, that if anyone comes to Him with sins filling the area between the heaven and the earth, and then they sincerely repent to Him, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will give them forgiveness which is as big or more. So that is who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is. He is not a policeman who is waiting to throw us in jail. He is the Most Merciful. But the problem is that we are not teaching His mercy. We''re just teaching His wrath. We''re not teaching any attachment to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We''re just teaching rules. We''re not talking about the roots, we''re talking about the leaves and the branches. You have to eat like this, you have to dress like this, you have to walk like this, you have to eat, sleep and spit like this.</p>
<p>I mean this is allowed, this is not, you know, these micronutrients, this micro-ingredient is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span> within the pizza. I mean, it''s kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span>, but it''s not 100. I''ve actually seen a bottle of water that says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span> certified on it. A bottle of water that says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span> certified on it. It''s water. *<strong> </strong>** it. I mean, this is missing the fruit, what do we call it, the forest for the trees, right? This is it. This is missing the forest for a leaf on a tree. We''re not focusing on the roots. So what ends up happening is we end up creating either this aversion and this hatred for religion, you know, completely abandoning it, or then there is this external shell, this lifeless shell of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> that just crumbles to the ground with the first trial. I mean, don''t get me wrong, I do not belittle the importance of the outer worship.</p>
<p>Of course, it''s a combination of both things. That''s how I started by saying that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> is the outer worship coupled with the inner worship. That''s why I began with that, right? It''s both <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آمَنُوا</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَمِلَ صَالِحَاتٍ</span>. But what I''m saying is that you cannot do one and not the other. The problem, what I''m saying is that many of us are focusing only on the outer aspects, and if you just focus on the outer, it''s like growing a tree without roots. So when the wind comes, it''ll just knock it down. We all need to have solid roots, which is the relationship with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>So, how do we build these roots practically? First, I told you, we remove this myth of human perfection from our minds. We have to set a different understanding, that we as human beings, we are not perfect, we were not created to be perfect, and a true believer is somebody who acknowledges that imperfection and then takes the step to better himself. So we are created as human beings to keep repenting and keep trying again and take small tiny steps every day towards that perfection, which we may or we may never achieve, which is fine.</p>
<p>But at least we''re walking in the right direction. We''re not walking in the opposite direction. I mean, can you imagine a child that is learning to walk for the first time, this child, right? He''s learning to walk and he falls down and he says, you know what? I''m just going to stop trying. This is too hard. I''m just going to sit here and that''s it. I''m never going to walk. That child will never learn how to walk. Can you imagine a person driving and the moment he makes a wrong turn, they just switch off their car. You know, they put it in park and they just say, you know what, forget it. I''m not going to drive.</p>
<p>This whole car is stupid. This whole route is stupid. I''m done. That''s it. I made a wrong turn. Imagine, okay, imagine your GPS saying you made a wrong turn. Sorry, right? You turn left instead of right and your GPS goes, you know what? You made a wrong turn. Just stop trying. I''m not giving you any directions. Turn off the car.</p>
<p>Your trip is over. Does the GPS do that? No. What does it do? It does something completely different. What does it say? Recalculating, rerouting. What is that in Arabic? That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>. That''s actually literally <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> means to return, return. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> is our internal mechanism rerouting us back to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That''s the process of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْتَغْفِرُ</span>.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> in Arabic literally means to turn around and return. How many times does the GPS of your car reroute you? Just once? If you take several wrong turns, what does the GPS say? You know what? You''re an idiot. It''s pointless trying to give you directions. You just keep making the same wrong turns. I''m not going to guide you anymore. You know, does the GPS do that? No, it keeps rerouting you. How many times?</p>
<p>Millions, billions of times if it has to. Infinite number of times. Tell me I''m wrong. A GPS, a man-made machine, is capable of doing that. You think <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> cannot do that? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So we have to change the way we think. We have to be able to get back up after we fall. We have to learn to repent and we have to learn to keep going and keep trying. That is the first thing for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقَامَ</span>. Now what we can do to develop this foundation</p>
<p>is actually very simple. It''s a simple three-ingredient recipe. The first is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>. That''s like the oxygen of the heart. That is where we connect with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That''s where we have that communication with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That''s where we go to the person we''ve wronged and we say sorry. It''s what keeps us spiritually, psychologically and mentally connected with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. So no matter what you do, even if you''re doing the worst things possible, don''t ignore your prayers. Keep getting there, keep connecting. If you can do five, do three. If you can do three, do two, do one. At least try to pray once a day, and then rejoice in that prayer. Be really happy and proud of that prayer. I mean, no matter how distracted or imperfect you think it was, you know what, if you did a fast and furious kind of prayer.</p>
<p>This is another problem we face, that again stems from this myth of perfection. If I asked you to list out for me all your sins and all your mistakes that you committed today, you''ll probably be able to give me a long list. But if I asked you to list out for me all your good deeds of the day, I bet you''re going to struggle with that.</p>
<p>Can I write this down? Is it good enough? Does that count? First of all, you won''t even remember half the things you did. Because we don''t appreciate the good that we do as much as we beat ourselves over the wrong that we do. You see? So from now on, every good deed that you do, from tasting that salt before, if you remember to taste that salt before your food, to wearing your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَة</span>, to, I don''t know, to smiling at someone, every little thing. I want you to be grateful for that. I want you to acknowledge it. I want you to be happy about it. I want you to remember <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> when you do that. I want you to, you know, make the angels write that in your book by saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>. Keep reminding yourself of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> after every good deed, and then feel really happy about it and really proud about it. Just for remembering to say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ</span> before something, you know, from saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ</span> to saving somebody''s life and everything in between, I want you to be happy and rejoice in every good deed that you do. You did a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, you did one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, just the first, and you were Amazon shopping while you were doing that. You know what, you did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَمَاز</span>, you did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, you stood up to remember <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you dropped whatever you were doing and you connected with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Be happy about that. Try to do better next time, but be happy. Don''t crap on what you did. Don''t discredit the effort that you made.</p>
<p>This is how you build relationship with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, slowly, small chunks, by thinking about Him, by remembering Him, consciously realizing that this is something you''re actually doing to please Him every time you do it. So be easy on yourselves. Enjoy what you''re doing. Enjoy the good that you are doing to make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> happy. That''s the first. Second is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>, the daily, the remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> through supplications. That''s the GPS rerouting us. And the third is the recitation and understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. I mean, you have to stay connected to it daily, even if one verse a day, I don''t care. If you do this daily and you understand it and you''re consistent with it, you will build <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>. You will build this resilience on the straight path, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّرَاطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>. I mean, these are things that have really, really helped me in life, and I really hope it''ll do the same for you. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ وَبِالْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ وَعَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ، وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ، وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِمَا الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ، وَسَلِّمْ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَةُ</span></p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'staying steadfast on the path';
